// change to src/data/curriculum/, and commit the result here.
window.__BASIS_LESSONS__ = {"t1-m1-l1":{"id":"t1-m1-l1","title":"What Is a Share?","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"A share is a fractional ownership stake in a company. Buy one and you own a small slice of the business — entitled to a share of its success, and exposed to its risks.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Ownership, not a loan","body":"When a company issues shares it sells pieces of itself to raise capital. As a shareholder you become a part-owner with a **residual claim** — you are entitled to what remains after the company pays its debts and other obligations. You are not lending money (that would be a bond); you own **equity**.\n\nYou can profit two ways: **capital appreciation** (the share price rises) and dividends. If the company underperforms, your shares can fall in value or become worthless.","icon":"document-text"},{"heading":"Dividends","body":"A **dividend** is a distribution of profits to shareholders, usually paid in cash. Dividends are discretionary: the board decides whether and how much to pay. Mature, profitable companies often pay steady dividends, while fast-growing companies frequently pay none, reinvesting profits to grow instead.","icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Voting rights","body":"Ordinary shares usually carry voting rights — the right to vote at the company’s general meetings on matters such as electing the board of directors and approving major decisions. Typically one share equals one vote, so larger holders have more influence.","icon":"checkbox"},{"heading":"Ordinary vs preference shares","body":"Ordinary (common) shares carry votes and variable dividends, with full exposure to upside and downside. Preference shares rank ahead of ordinary shares for dividends — often a fixed amount — and ahead in a liquidation, but usually carry little or no voting power. In short, preference shares trade some control and upside for priority and income stability.","icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Dilution","body":"If a company issues new shares, the total share count rises and each existing share represents a smaller percentage of the company. That reduction in ownership is called **dilution**. The opposite is a **buyback**, where the company repurchases its own shares, reducing the count and concentrating ownership in the remaining holders.","icon":"water"}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"What does a share represent?","options":["A loan to the company that must be repaid","A fractional ownership stake in a company","A guarantee of fixed annual income","A claim on revenue before any costs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A share is a unit of equity ownership — you own a slice of the business, not a debt claim against it."},{"id":"t1-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"How do preference shares typically differ from ordinary shares?","options":["They always pay a lower dividend than ordinary shares do","They carry extra voting power on every corporate matter","They rank ahead for (often fixed) dividends, usually without votes","There is no real difference from ordinary shares at all"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Preference shares get priority on dividends (often fixed) and in liquidation, but usually carry little or no voting power. Ordinary shares vote and receive variable dividends."},{"id":"t1-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"What do voting rights give an ordinary shareholder?","options":["The right to personally set the share price each day","A say on corporate matters such as electing directors","A legally guaranteed dividend paid every single year","The unilateral power to force a company buyback"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Voting rights let shareholders vote at general meetings on matters like electing the board and approving major decisions."},{"id":"t1-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"What is a dividend?","options":["A penalty that shareholders are charged each quarter","The fixed interest a company pays out on its bonds","The market price you pay to buy one share today","A distribution of profits to shareholders"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A dividend is a portion of profits paid out to shareholders — usually in cash, at the board’s discretion."},{"id":"t1-m1-l1-q5","prompt":"What is dilution?","options":["Issuing new shares, reducing existing owners’ percentage","A company buying back its own shares on the open market","A sustained fall in the company's quoted share price","A reduction in the per-share dividend the board pays"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Issuing new shares raises the total share count, so each existing share represents a smaller slice of the company — that’s dilution."}]},"t1-m1-l2":{"id":"t1-m1-l2","title":"Reading a Stock Quote","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"A stock quote is a snapshot of a company’s market price and recent trading activity. Learn to read the key fields and you can size up a stock in seconds.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Bid, ask, and the spread","body":"The bid is the highest price a buyer is currently willing to pay. The ask (or offer) is the lowest price a seller is willing to accept. The ask is always at or above the bid, and the gap between them is the bid/ask spread — effectively the cost of trading immediately. Liquid stocks have tight spreads; thinly traded ones have wide spreads.","visual":"bid-ask"},{"heading":"Volume","body":"Volume is the number of shares traded over a period (e.g. a day). High volume signals strong interest and **liquidity** — it’s easier to buy or sell without moving the price. Volume often spikes around news and earnings."},{"heading":"Day’s range and 52-week range","body":"The day’s range shows the low and high price so far today. The 52-week range shows the lowest and highest price over the past year. The 52-week high/low frames where the current price sits relative to its recent history — a quick gauge of momentum and positioning."},{"heading":"Market capitalisation","body":"**Market capitalisation** (market cap) = current share price × shares outstanding. It is the market’s **valuation** of the company’s total equity. A higher share price alone doesn’t make a company “bigger” — you must multiply by the number of shares."},{"heading":"The P/E ratio","body":"The price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio = share price ÷ **earnings per share**. At a headline level it tells you how much investors are paying for each £1 (or $1) of the company’s earnings. A higher P/E generally reflects higher growth expectations."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"What do the bid and ask prices mean?","options":["Bid is what sellers want; ask is what buyers pay","Bid is what buyers will pay; ask is what sellers will accept","They are always equal","Bid is always above ask"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The bid is the highest price a buyer will pay; the ask (offer) is the lowest a seller will accept. The gap between them is the spread."},{"id":"t1-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"What does trading volume represent?","options":["The number of shares traded over a period","The company's total revenue booked over the period","The total number of registered shareholders on the books","The annual dividend expressed as a percent of price"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Volume is how many shares changed hands in the period — a gauge of liquidity and investor interest."},{"id":"t1-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"What is the 52-week high/low mainly used for?","options":["Calculating the dividend the company will pay out","Setting the bid/ask spread quoted by market makers","Framing where the price sits in its recent range","Determining the company's trailing P/E multiple"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The 52-week range frames the current price against the past year — a quick reference for momentum and positioning."},{"id":"t1-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"How is market capitalisation calculated?","options":["Total revenue minus all of the company's costs","The share price divided by earnings per share","Total assets minus the company's liabilities","Share price × shares outstanding"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Market cap = current share price × shares outstanding — the market’s valuation of the company’s equity."},{"id":"t1-m1-l2-q5","prompt":"At a headline level, what does the P/E ratio tell you?","options":["How much you pay per £1 of current earnings","The company's profit divided by its book equity","The cash dividend paid out on each single share","The gap between the current bid and ask prices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Price-to-earnings divides price by earnings per share — how much investors pay for each £1 of earnings."}]},"t1-m1-l3":{"id":"t1-m1-l3","title":"Market Capitalisation","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"Market capitalisation is the market’s estimate of a company’s total equity value: shares outstanding × current share price.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The formula","body":"Market cap = shares outstanding × share price. Example: 100 million shares at £20 each = £2bn market cap. Because it multiplies by share count, two companies with the same share price can have wildly different market caps."},{"heading":"Size buckets","body":"Companies are loosely grouped by market cap. Conventionally: large cap is roughly above $10bn, mid cap about $2–10bn, and small cap about $300m–$2bn (with micro cap below that). Thresholds vary by index provider, but the language is universal on a trading desk.","visual":"market-cap"},{"heading":"Market cap vs book value","body":"Market cap is a forward-looking market price of equity — it reflects what investors will pay today, driven by expectations and sentiment. Book value is the accounting value of equity (assets minus liabilities) from the **balance sheet**, based on historical figures. The two rarely match: profitable, fast-growing firms usually trade well above book value."},{"heading":"Buybacks and share count","body":"When a company buys back its own shares, shares outstanding fall — the opposite of **dilution**. With fewer shares, ownership concentrates in the remaining holders and **earnings per share** typically rises. (Total **equity value** also falls by the cash spent, but each remaining share owns a bigger slice.)"},{"heading":"Free float vs market cap","body":"Market cap is based on all shares outstanding. The **free float** is only the portion of shares available for public trading — it excludes locked-up holdings by insiders, founders, or strategic investors. Index weightings are often based on free float rather than total market cap."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"What is the market cap formula?","options":["Net income × P/E","Shares outstanding × share price","Total assets − total liabilities","Revenue × margin"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market cap = shares outstanding × share price."},{"id":"t1-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Roughly, what defines a \"large cap\" company?","options":["Below $1bn","Around $300m–$2bn","Typically above ~$10bn","There are no conventions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Conventionally: large cap ~>$10bn, mid cap ~$2–10bn, small cap ~$300m–$2bn. Thresholds vary by provider."},{"id":"t1-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"Why does market cap differ from book value?","options":["They are one and the same figure, identical for every listed company on the market","Book value already includes the live share price","Market cap simply ignores the shares outstanding","Market cap is what investors pay today; book value is accounting equity"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Market cap is a forward-looking market price of equity; book value is historical accounting net assets. They rarely match."},{"id":"t1-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"What happens when a company buys back its own shares?","options":["Shares outstanding fall, concentrating ownership in fewer shares","Shares outstanding rise, spreading value more thinly","The dividend per share is automatically increased","The company's book value can never change at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Buybacks reduce the share count (the opposite of dilution), concentrating ownership and often lifting earnings per share."},{"id":"t1-m1-l3-q5","prompt":"How does free float differ from market cap?","options":["They are the same measure under a different name","Float is the shares available to trade publicly; market cap values all shares","Float counts only the shares held by company insiders","Market cap deliberately excludes the current live share price from its whole calculation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Free float is the portion of shares freely tradable (excluding locked-up insider/strategic holdings); market cap is based on total shares outstanding."}]},"t1-m1-l4":{"id":"t1-m1-l4","title":"The P/E Ratio","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"The price-to-earnings ratio is the most widely quoted equity valuation multiple. It tells you how much investors will pay per unit of current earnings.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The formula","body":"P/E = share price ÷ **earnings per share** (**EPS**). Equivalently, market cap ÷ total **net income**. A P/E of 20 means investors are paying £20 for every £1 of annual earnings. On its own a P/E is just a number — it only means something in comparison (to peers, to history, to growth)."},{"heading":"Forward vs trailing","body":"Trailing P/E uses the last 12 months of actual reported earnings. Forward P/E uses analysts’ estimated future earnings (e.g. next 12 months). Forward P/E reflects expectations and is usually lower than trailing for a growing company, because earnings are expected to rise."},{"heading":"Why high P/Es?","body":"A high P/E typically signals that the market expects strong future earnings growth, so investors will pay more per current £1 of earnings. This is why many technology companies trade at high P/Es — the price is anchored to anticipated growth, not just today’s profits."},{"heading":"Limitations","body":"P/E says nothing about growth rate, debt (**leverage**), or earnings quality, and it can be distorted by one-off items. It’s also not comparable across very different industries. That’s why analysts pair it with other measures (growth-adjusted PEG, EV/**EBITDA**, **free cash flow**)."},{"heading":"Negative P/E","body":"If a company is loss-making, EPS is negative, so the P/E is negative — and generally treated as “not meaningful.” You can’t sensibly value a company on a negative P/E; analysts switch to other metrics for unprofitable firms."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m1-l4-q1","prompt":"What is the P/E formula?","options":["Annual earnings divided by the current share price","The share price divided by the company's revenue","Share price ÷ earnings per share","The share price divided by book value per share"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"P/E = price per share ÷ earnings per share (EPS)."},{"id":"t1-m1-l4-q2","prompt":"What is the difference between forward and trailing P/E?","options":["Trailing uses past 12-month earnings; forward uses estimated future earnings","Forward P/E uses the past twelve months of earnings","They are identical, just labelled differently by data providers","Trailing P/E is built from forward analyst estimates"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Trailing P/E uses the last 12 months of actual earnings; forward P/E uses forecast earnings, so it reflects growth expectations."},{"id":"t1-m1-l4-q3","prompt":"Why do many tech companies trade at high P/E ratios?","options":["They currently pay unusually large cash dividends","Investors expect rapid future earnings growth","They carry essentially no earnings risk whatsoever","Their accounting book value per share is very high"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A high P/E typically signals the market expects strong earnings growth, so investors pay more per current £1 of earnings."},{"id":"t1-m1-l4-q4","prompt":"Which is a real limitation of the P/E ratio?","options":["It perfectly captures a company's true intrinsic value in essentially every situation","It fully accounts for every balance-sheet risk","It can never be compared across different companies","It ignores growth and leverage and breaks down with volatile earnings"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"P/E says nothing about growth rate, debt, or earnings quality, and is misleading when earnings are negative or one-off."},{"id":"t1-m1-l4-q5","prompt":"What does a negative P/E mean?","options":["The company has negative earnings (a loss)","The company's quoted share price has gone negative","The company has chosen to pay no dividend this year","The stock is effectively a risk-free investment now"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A negative P/E arises when EPS is negative — the company is loss-making — so the ratio is generally not meaningful."}]},"t1-m1-l5":{"id":"t1-m1-l5","title":"IPOs and Secondary Markets","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"An IPO is a private company’s first sale of shares to the public. After that, those shares trade between investors on the secondary market.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Primary vs secondary market","body":"The **primary market** is where new securities are issued and the company itself raises capital — an **IPO** is a primary-market event. The **secondary market** is where investors subsequently trade existing shares among themselves (e.g. on a stock exchange); the company receives no new money from those trades."},{"heading":"Who buys in an IPO","body":"In a typical IPO, newly issued shares are allocated to investors — institutions (funds, pensions) usually dominate via the bookbuild, sometimes alongside a retail tranche. Allocation is decided by the underwriters, not bought freely on an exchange."},{"heading":"The bookbuild","body":"Bookbuilding is the process where underwriters collect indications of demand from investors across a price range. That “book” of demand lets them set the final offer price and decide allocations. Strong demand can push the price to the top of the range or higher."},{"heading":"Underwriting","body":"Underwriters are the investment banks that manage the offering. They commit to buy or place the new shares with investors, taking on the risk that the issue might not sell — in exchange for fees. They also advise on pricing, timing, and marketing (the “**roadshow**”)."},{"heading":"Lock-up period","body":"A lock-up period (commonly 90–180 days after the IPO) contractually restricts insiders and early investors from selling their shares. This prevents a flood of selling right after listing and supports an orderly market. Share prices sometimes wobble when a lock-up expires."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m1-l5-q1","prompt":"Who is allocated shares in an IPO?","options":["The stock exchange itself buys the whole offering","Investors — often institutions first, via the bookbuild","Only the company's own employees are allowed to buy","Only retail investors, never large institutions, buy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"New shares are allocated to investors; institutions typically dominate the bookbuild, sometimes alongside a retail tranche."},{"id":"t1-m1-l5-q2","prompt":"What is a bookbuild?","options":["The company's internal accounting ledger of trades","A published list of the company's board members","Underwriters collecting investor demand to set the IPO price","The historical record of secondary market trading"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bookbuilding gathers demand at various prices from investors so underwriters can set the offer price and allocations."},{"id":"t1-m1-l5-q3","prompt":"What distinguishes the primary from the secondary market?","options":["Primary issues new securities; secondary trades existing ones","The primary market is used for issuing bonds only","The secondary market is where firms raise new capital","They are the same market under two different names"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The company raises capital in the primary market (issuance); thereafter shares trade between investors in the secondary market."},{"id":"t1-m1-l5-q4","prompt":"What is a lock-up period?","options":["A temporary trading halt triggered by high volatility","The number of days it takes for a trade to settle","A blackout window when the firm cannot pay dividends","A post-IPO window when insiders cannot sell their shares"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Lock-ups (often 90–180 days) restrict insiders and early investors from selling right after the IPO, supporting an orderly market."},{"id":"t1-m1-l5-q5","prompt":"What is underwriting in an IPO?","options":["When banks agree to buy/place the new shares, taking on placement risk","When ordinary investors buy insurance policies to protect their whole portfolio from losses","The listing fee the exchange charges the company","A particular type of dividend paid to new holders"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Underwriters (banks) commit to buy or place the issue and manage the offering, taking on the risk of selling the shares."}]},"t1-m2-l1":{"id":"t1-m2-l1","title":"Bond Basics","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"A bond is a loan you make to an issuer. They borrow your money, pay you interest along the way, and return the principal at the end.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Debt, not equity","body":"A bond is a debt instrument. The issuer — a government, corporation, or supranational like the World Bank — borrows money from investors and is contractually obliged to pay interest and repay the principal. Unlike a shareholder, a bondholder is a lender: no ownership, no votes, but a higher-priority claim than **equity** if things go wrong."},{"heading":"Face value (par)","body":"The **face value** (or par value) is the principal amount the issuer will repay at **maturity**, and the base on which interest is calculated. It’s commonly quoted as 100 or 1,000. The market price can trade above or below face value, but the redemption amount at maturity is the face value."},{"heading":"Coupon","body":"The **coupon** is the periodic interest payment, usually expressed as a fixed percentage of face value (e.g. a 5% coupon on £1,000 pays £50 a year, often split into semi-annual payments). The coupon is set at issue and, for a fixed-rate bond, does not change."},{"heading":"Maturity","body":"Maturity is the date the bond’s life ends — when the issuer repays the face value to the holder, typically alongside the final coupon. Bonds range from a few months (bills) to 30 years or more. Longer maturity generally means more interest-rate risk."},{"heading":"Bond vs equity","body":"Bondholders are lenders with contractual interest and repayment, ranking ahead of shareholders if the company is wound up. Shareholders are owners with unlimited upside but the last claim on assets. So bonds are generally lower-risk, lower-return; equity is higher-risk, higher-potential-return."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"What is a coupon?","options":["The eventual repayment of the bond's principal","The periodic interest payment a bond makes","The bond's fluctuating price in the open market","A penalty charged for selling the bond early"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The coupon is the periodic interest the issuer pays bondholders, usually a fixed % of face value."},{"id":"t1-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"What is face value?","options":["The current market price","The total interest paid","The principal repaid at maturity","The issuer’s credit rating"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Face (par) value is the principal returned at maturity and the base for coupon calculations — commonly 100 or 1,000."},{"id":"t1-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"What is maturity?","options":["The date the principal is repaid and the bond ends","The date on which the bond is originally issued","The date the very first coupon payment is made","The date the issuer's credit rating is reviewed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Maturity is when the issuer repays the face value and the bond’s life ends."},{"id":"t1-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"How does a bond differ from equity?","options":["Bonds grant their holders full voting rights, whereas ordinary equity shares do not at all","Equity has to be repaid in full at a maturity date","Bonds and equity are treated as legally identical","A bond is debt with priority claims; equity is ownership with residual claims"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Bondholders are lenders with contractual interest and repayment, ranking ahead of shareholders — who are owners with upside but the last claim."},{"id":"t1-m2-l1-q5","prompt":"At maturity, the issuer repays the bond’s face value to the holder.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Correct — at maturity the issuer returns the principal (face value), typically alongside the final coupon."}]},"t1-m2-l2":{"id":"t1-m2-l2","title":"Yield and Price Relationship","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"The single most important idea in fixed income: bond prices and yields move in opposite directions.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Why they move inversely","body":"A bond’s **coupon** is fixed at issue. If market **interest rates** rise, newly issued bonds pay higher coupons, making your older, lower-coupon bond less attractive — so its price must fall until its return is competitive. If rates fall, your higher-coupon bond becomes more valuable and its price rises. Hence: yields up, prices down; yields down, prices up.","visual":"price-yield"},{"heading":"Yield to maturity (YTM)","body":"YTM is the total annualised return you’d earn if you bought the bond at its current price and held it to **maturity**, collecting all coupons and the final principal. Technically it’s the **discount rate** that makes the present value of all future cash flows equal the price. It captures both coupon income and any capital gain/loss to par."},{"heading":"Current yield","body":"Current yield = annual coupon ÷ current market price. It’s a simpler measure that ignores any capital gain or loss between now and maturity, so it differs from YTM whenever the bond trades away from par."},{"heading":"Discount and premium","body":"A bond trading below its **face value** is said to trade at a discount — typically because its coupon is below prevailing market yields. A bond above face value trades at a premium (its coupon beats current yields). At maturity, either way, the holder receives par."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"Why do bond prices fall when yields rise?","options":["New bonds offer higher coupons, making existing lower-coupon bonds less attractive","Issuers immediately cancel their older outstanding bonds","The coupons on existing bonds automatically rise to match the newly issued higher-coupon bonds","The remaining maturity of existing bonds shortens"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When market rates rise, newly issued bonds pay more, so older bonds must fall in price until their yield is competitive."},{"id":"t1-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"What is yield to maturity (YTM)?","options":["Simply the stated coupon rate that is printed on the face of the bond certificate itself","The total annualised return if held to maturity, given price, coupons and par","The annual dividend yield paid on the bond instead","The change in the bond's price over a single day"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"YTM is the internal rate of return that equates the bond’s price to the present value of all its future cash flows."},{"id":"t1-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"A bond \"trading at a discount\" means its price is…","options":["Above face value","Below face value","Exactly par","Zero"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A discount bond trades below par, typically because its coupon is below prevailing market yields."},{"id":"t1-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"What is current yield?","options":["Annual coupon ÷ current price","The coupon divided by the bond's face (par) value","The bond's current price divided by its coupon","The yield to maturity minus the coupon rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Current yield = annual coupon ÷ current market price. It ignores any capital gain/loss to maturity (unlike YTM)."},{"id":"t1-m2-l2-q5","prompt":"Bond prices and yields move in the same direction.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False — they move inversely: when yields rise, prices fall, and vice versa."}]},"t1-m2-l3":{"id":"t1-m2-l3","title":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"Duration tells you how sensitive a bond’s price is to changes in interest rates — the key measure of interest-rate risk.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"What duration measures","body":"**Duration** estimates how much a bond’s price moves when **interest rates** change. It’s also interpreted as the weighted-average time to receive the bond’s cash flows, expressed in years. The bigger the duration, the more the price swings for a given rate move."},{"heading":"Modified duration in practice","body":"Modified duration approximates the percentage price change for a 1% (100 basis point) change in yield. A bond with a modified duration of 5 will lose roughly 5% if rates rise 1%, and gain roughly 5% if rates fall 1%. It’s the everyday tool for sizing interest-rate risk."},{"heading":"Maturity drives duration","body":"All else equal, longer-**maturity** bonds have higher duration because more of their value sits far in the future. So a 10-year bond has greater duration — and greater interest-rate risk — than a 2-year bond. Higher coupons slightly reduce duration (you get cash back sooner).","visual":"duration"},{"heading":"Why pension funds care","body":"Pension funds have long-dated, rate-sensitive liabilities (future payouts to retirees). They focus on duration to match the interest-rate sensitivity of their assets to their liabilities — a practice called liability-driven investing — so that when rates move, assets and liabilities move together and funding stays stable."},{"heading":"Convexity","body":"Duration assumes a straight-line relationship, but the true price/yield relationship is curved. Convexity measures that curvature — how duration itself changes as yields move. It refines the price estimate for larger rate moves, and is generally beneficial to bondholders."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"What does duration measure?","options":["The time remaining until the first coupon is paid","A bond’s price sensitivity to interest rate changes","The credit risk that the issuer might default","The size of the coupon the bond pays each year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Duration estimates how much a bond’s price moves for a given change in rates (and is a weighted-average time to receive cash flows)."},{"id":"t1-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"Which has higher duration, a 2-year or a 10-year bond (all else equal)?","options":["The 2-year","They are equal","The 10-year","Neither has duration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Longer maturity means higher duration and greater price sensitivity to rate changes."},{"id":"t1-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"What is modified duration used for?","options":["Estimating the % price change for a 1% change in yield","Calculating the size of the next coupon payment","Finding the exact date the bond finally matures","Assigning a credit rating to the bond's issuer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Modified duration approximates the % price change for a 100bp move in yield (duration 5 ≈ −5% for +1%)."},{"id":"t1-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"Why do pension funds focus heavily on duration?","options":["Purely in order to maximise the total coupon income the investor receives over the years","To avoid ever having to hold bonds at all","To increase the investor's corporate voting rights","To match the rate sensitivity of assets to their long-dated liabilities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Pension liabilities are long-dated and rate-sensitive; matching asset and liability duration immunises funding against rate moves."},{"id":"t1-m2-l3-q5","prompt":"What is convexity?","options":["The curvature in the price/yield relationship that duration alone misses","The frequency with which the coupon is paid out","The probability that the issuer eventually defaults","The size of the bid/ask spread that dealers quote on the bond in the open secondary market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Convexity captures how duration itself changes as yields move, refining the price estimate for larger rate moves."}]},"t1-m2-l4":{"id":"t1-m2-l4","title":"Credit Ratings","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"Credit ratings grade the chance an issuer defaults. The grade drives the yield investors demand.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The big three agencies","body":"Three agencies dominate credit ratings: Moody’s, S&P Global Ratings, and Fitch. They assess the probability that a bond issuer will fail to make its payments (default) and publish letter grades that the market relies on heavily."},{"heading":"Investment grade vs high yield","body":"Bonds rated BBB- or above are **investment grade** — judged to have relatively low **default risk**, so they pay lower yields. Bonds rated BB+ and below are **high yield**, or “junk” — higher default risk, compensated by higher yields. The investment grade / high yield (IG/HY) boundary is a major dividing line: many investors and funds are restricted to one side of it."},{"heading":"Downgrades and prices","body":"If an issuer is downgraded, investors perceive more risk and demand a higher yield — which pushes the bond’s price down. Upgrades work in reverse. Ratings changes can be significant market events, especially around the IG/HY boundary."},{"heading":"Credit spread","body":"The **credit spread** is the extra yield a risky bond pays above a comparable risk-free benchmark (typically a **government bond** of similar maturity). It’s the market’s price for taking on **credit risk**: wider spreads mean more perceived risk or fear; tighter spreads mean confidence."},{"heading":"Fallen angels","body":"A “fallen angel” is a bond that was investment grade when issued but has since been downgraded into high yield. These can be forced sellers’ situations — some funds may only hold IG — which can pressure prices around the downgrade."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m2-l4-q1","prompt":"What is \"investment grade\"?","options":["Any bond that carries a rating below BBB-","Bonds rated BBB- or above, signalling lower default risk","Only bonds issued directly by national governments","Bonds that carry no formal credit rating at all"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Investment grade means BBB- or above — lower default risk, lower yield. Below that is high yield, or \"junk\" — higher risk compensated by higher yields."},{"id":"t1-m2-l4-q2","prompt":"What typically happens to a bond’s price when it is downgraded?","options":["It always rises","It is unchanged","It typically falls","The coupon increases"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A downgrade signals higher default risk, so investors demand a higher yield — pushing the price down."},{"id":"t1-m2-l4-q3","prompt":"Who are the \"big three\" credit rating agencies?","options":["Goldman, Morgan Stanley, JPMorgan","SEC, FCA, ESMA","ICE, CME, LSE","Moody’s, S&P, and Fitch"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The three dominant agencies are Moody’s, S&P Global Ratings, and Fitch."},{"id":"t1-m2-l4-q4","prompt":"What is a credit spread?","options":["The extra yield over a risk-free benchmark for taking credit risk","The bid/ask spread quoted on the bond in the market","The bond's coupon rate minus its current price","The yield gap between two different maturities"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The credit spread is the yield premium a risky bond pays above a comparable risk-free (e.g. government) bond."},{"id":"t1-m2-l4-q5","prompt":"What is a \"fallen angel\"?","options":["A bond downgraded from investment grade to high yield","A newly issued junk bond sold below investment grade","A government bond that has already gone into default","Any bond currently trading above its par value"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A fallen angel was investment grade at issue but has since been downgraded into high yield."}]},"t1-m2-l5":{"id":"t1-m2-l5","title":"The Yield Curve","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"The yield curve plots yields against maturity for bonds of the same credit quality. Its shape is one of the most watched signals in markets.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"What it shows","body":"The **yield curve** plots yield (vertical) against **maturity** (horizontal) for bonds of the same credit quality — most often government bonds. It lets you see, at a glance, what investors earn for lending over different time horizons."},{"heading":"Normal (upward sloping)","body":"A normal curve slopes upward: longer maturities yield more than shorter ones. Investors demand extra yield to lend for longer (tying up money, bearing more uncertainty). An upward slope is consistent with expectations of ongoing economic growth.","visual":"yield-curve-normal"},{"heading":"Inverted curve","body":"An inverted curve is downward sloping — short-term yields exceed long-term yields. It often reflects expectations that the **central bank** will cut rates as growth slows. Historically, inversion has preceded recessions, making it a closely watched warning sign. The US curve inverted before the 2008 financial crisis.","visual":"yield-curve-inverted"},{"heading":"Steepening and flattening","body":"Steepening means the gap between long and short yields widens (long yields rise relative to short, or short fall faster). Flattening means the gap narrows. These shifts signal changing expectations about growth and central-bank policy."},{"heading":"The 2s10s spread","body":"The “2s10s” spread is the 10-year yield minus the 2-year yield — a popular summary of the curve’s slope. A positive 2s10s is a normal, upward slope; a negative 2s10s means the curve is inverted, which markets treat as a **recession** signal."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m2-l5-q1","prompt":"What does a normal yield curve look like?","options":["Perfectly flat across every maturity on the curve","Downward sloping, with short maturities yielding more","Upward sloping — longer maturities yield more","Vertical, with all maturities at a single yield"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A normal curve slopes upward: investors demand more yield to lend for longer, consistent with growth expectations."},{"id":"t1-m2-l5-q2","prompt":"What does an inverted yield curve historically signal?","options":["Strong imminent growth","A possible recession ahead","Zero inflation","A market holiday"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Inversion (short > long yields) has historically preceded recessions, reflecting expectations of future rate cuts/slowdown."},{"id":"t1-m2-l5-q3","prompt":"What is yield curve \"steepening\"?","options":["The gap between long and short yields widens","The curve flattens toward a single common yield","All yields across the curve fall by equal amounts","Short-dated yields rise above long-dated yields"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Steepening means long-term yields rise relative to short-term (or short-term fall faster), widening the spread."},{"id":"t1-m2-l5-q4","prompt":"What is the \"2s10s\" spread?","options":["The 2-year yield minus the 30-year bond yield","The spread between two bonds' coupon rates","The bid/ask spread quoted on the 10-year bond","The 10-year yield minus the 2-year yield"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"\"2s10s\" is the 10-year minus 2-year government yield — a closely watched gauge; negative values indicate inversion."},{"id":"t1-m2-l5-q5","prompt":"The US yield curve inverted before the 2008 financial crisis.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — the curve (e.g. 2s10s) inverted in 2006–07, ahead of the 2008 recession, consistent with its historical record."}]},"t1-m3-l1":{"id":"t1-m3-l1","title":"Options — Calls and Puts","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"An option gives its buyer the right — but not the obligation — to trade an asset at a set price. That asymmetry is what makes options powerful.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Calls and puts","body":"A **call option** gives the buyer the right to buy the **underlying** asset at a fixed price (the strike) before or at expiry. A **put option** gives the right to sell at the strike. The buyer pays a premium up front for this right. Calls profit when the asset rises; puts profit when it falls."},{"heading":"Buyer vs seller (writer)","body":"The buyer holds the right and can choose whether to use it. The seller (writer) takes on the obligation: if the buyer exercises, the writer must deliver (sell to a call holder, or buy from a put holder). For accepting that obligation, the writer keeps the premium. So the obligation always sits with the seller."},{"heading":"In-the-money and intrinsic value","body":"**Intrinsic value** is what an option is worth if exercised right now. A call is in-the-money when the spot price is above the strike (intrinsic value = spot − strike); a put is in-the-money when spot is below strike. If exercising wouldn’t pay off, intrinsic value is zero and the option is out-of-the-money. Any premium beyond intrinsic value is **time value**.","visual":"call-payoff"},{"heading":"Exercising","body":"To **exercise** an option is to invoke its right — actually buying (call) or selling (put) the underlying at the strike. Note this differs from simply selling the option contract to someone else, which is “closing” the position. Many **options** are closed for a profit/loss rather than exercised."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m3-l1-q1","prompt":"What is the difference between a call and a put?","options":["A call is the right to buy; a put is the right to sell — at the strike","A call is the right to sell, and a put the right to buy","Both a call and a put are obligations to buy the stock","Both a call and a put are simply rights to sell it"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A call gives the right to buy the underlying at the strike; a put gives the right to sell — before or at expiry."},{"id":"t1-m3-l1-q2","prompt":"What is an option’s intrinsic value?","options":["The upfront premium the option buyer originally paid","The in-the-money amount — what exercising now is worth","The amount of time remaining until the option expires","The strike price written into the option contract"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Intrinsic value is the immediate exercise value (for a call, max(spot − strike, 0)). The rest of the premium is time value."},{"id":"t1-m3-l1-q3","prompt":"When is a call option \"in-the-money\"?","options":["When it has expired","When it has no premium","When the spot price is above the strike","When the spot price is below the strike"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A call is in-the-money when spot > strike (a put when spot < strike) — exercising would be profitable before costs."},{"id":"t1-m3-l1-q4","prompt":"In an options contract, who carries the obligation?","options":["The option buyer, who paid the premium upfront","Both the buyer and the seller, equally, at all times","Neither party carries any obligation whatsoever","The seller (writer)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The buyer holds the right; the seller (writer) takes on the obligation to fulfil if exercised, in exchange for the premium."},{"id":"t1-m3-l1-q5","prompt":"What does it mean to \"exercise\" an option?","options":["To invoke the right to buy (call) or sell (put) at the strike","To sell the option contract on to another investor","To deliberately let the option expire worthless","To pay the option premium to the contract's writer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Exercising means using the option’s right — transacting the underlying at the strike. Selling the option itself is \"closing\", not exercising."}]},"t1-m3-l2":{"id":"t1-m3-l2","title":"Futures Contracts","trackId":"t1","trackTitle":"Markets & Instruments","color":"#6366F1","premium":false,"intro":"A futures contract locks in a price today for a trade that settles in the future — and unlike an option, both sides are obligated.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Both parties are obligated","body":"A **futures** contract obligates both the buyer and the seller to transact a specified asset at a predetermined price on a future date. This is the key contrast with options: an option buyer has a right they can walk away from (losing only the premium), whereas both sides of a future must fulfil the contract."},{"heading":"Long and short","body":"Going long a future means you’re obligated to buy the **underlying** at the agreed price at **expiry** — you profit if prices rise. Going short means you’re obligated to sell — you profit if prices fall. Futures are used both to hedge (lock in a price and remove risk) and to speculate."},{"heading":"Margin","body":"You don’t pay the full contract value up front. Instead you post initial margin — a good-faith deposit, a fraction of the contract value — to open the position. This is collateral, not a part-payment, and it’s why futures provide **leverage**."},{"heading":"Marking to market","body":"Each day, futures positions are marked to market: that day’s gains or losses are credited to or debited from each party’s margin account. If the balance drops below a maintenance level, you get a **margin call** to top it up. Daily settlement is what keeps **counterparty** risk low on an exchange."},{"heading":"Where futures are used","body":"Futures trade across many asset classes: commodities (oil, gold, wheat), equity indices (e.g. the S&P 500), **interest rates** and government bonds, and currencies. That breadth makes them a core **hedging** and trading tool for producers, investors, and speculators alike."}],"questions":[{"id":"t1-m3-l2-q1","prompt":"What is the key difference between options and futures?","options":["A future obligates both parties; an option gives the buyer a right, not an obligation","Futures are pure rights, while options are obligations","Futures and options are functionally identical financial products in almost every respect","Options are always cash-settled at their expiry date"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Both parties to a futures contract must transact; an option buyer can choose not to exercise, losing only the premium."},{"id":"t1-m3-l2-q2","prompt":"What is a \"long\" futures position?","options":["A legally binding agreement to sell the underlying asset at a set price on a future date","A right, but not an obligation, to sell the asset","An agreement to buy the underlying at the set price on the future date","Simply a cash deposit lodged with the exchange"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Going long futures means you are obligated to buy the underlying at the agreed price at expiry — gaining if prices rise."},{"id":"t1-m3-l2-q3","prompt":"What is \"marking to market\" in futures?","options":["Daily settlement of gains/losses to each party’s margin account","The process of setting the contract's strike price","The final physical delivery of the underlying asset","Assigning a credit rating to the counterparty"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Futures are marked to market daily: gains/losses hit margin accounts each day, reducing counterparty risk."},{"id":"t1-m3-l2-q4","prompt":"What is initial margin?","options":["The full value of the contract, paid upfront in cash","The good-faith deposit required to open a futures position","The commission the broker charges to open the trade","The coupon the futures contract pays each period"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Initial margin is a fraction of contract value posted as collateral to open the position; variation margin then covers daily moves."},{"id":"t1-m3-l2-q5","prompt":"Which set of asset classes commonly use futures?","options":["Commodities, equity indices, and interest rates","Only physical real estate and property assets","Only private equity stakes in unlisted companies","Only rare collectibles such as art and wine"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Futures are widely used across commodities (oil, gold), equity indices (S&P 500), rates/bonds, and currencies."}]},"t2-m1-l1":{"id":"t2-m1-l1","title":"The Income Statement","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The income statement shows how much a company earned and spent over a period, ending in profit or loss. It reads top to bottom, revenue down to the “bottom line.”","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Top line to bottom line","body":"The **income statement** (or P&L) covers a period — a quarter or a year. Revenue (sales) sits at the very top, which is why it’s called the “top line.” You then subtract costs step by step until you reach **net income** at the very bottom — the “bottom line.”"},{"heading":"Gross profit","body":"The first subtraction is cost of goods sold (**COGS**) — the direct cost of producing what you sold. Revenue − COGS = **gross profit**. This shows how profitable the core product is before overheads, interest, and tax. Gross margin = gross profit ÷ revenue."},{"heading":"Operating profit","body":"Next, subtract operating expenses — things like salaries, marketing, R&D, and rent (often grouped as SG&A). Gross profit − operating expenses = **operating profit** (also called operating income or **EBIT**, earnings before interest and tax). This reflects how well the core business operates."},{"heading":"Down to net income","body":"From operating profit, subtract interest (the cost of debt) and taxes to arrive at net income — the profit attributable to shareholders. **Earnings per share** (EPS) = net income ÷ shares outstanding."},{"heading":"Profit is not cash","body":"Net income is an accounting figure, not the cash generated in the period. It includes non-cash items such as **depreciation**, and it’s based on accrual accounting (revenue and costs are booked when earned/incurred, not when cash moves). Actual cash is tracked separately on the **cash flow statement**."}],"questions":[{"id":"t2-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"What does the \"top line\" refer to?","options":["Net income","Revenue (sales)","Gross profit","Operating expenses"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Revenue sits at the top of the income statement — the \"top line.\" Net income at the very bottom is the \"bottom line.\""},{"id":"t2-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"How is gross profit calculated?","options":["Revenue minus every operating and non-operating expense","Revenue minus the cost of goods sold (COGS)","Net income with taxes added straight back on","Operating income after interest has been deducted"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Gross profit = Revenue − COGS. It shows core product profitability before operating costs, interest, and taxes."},{"id":"t2-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"What does operating profit (EBIT) represent?","options":["Profit remaining after both interest and tax are paid","Total revenue booked before any costs are deducted","Profit from core operations, before interest and tax","The cash currently sitting in the company's bank"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Operating profit = gross profit − operating expenses. It’s earnings before interest and tax (EBIT), reflecting core operations."},{"id":"t2-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"What is earnings per share (EPS)?","options":["Net income divided by the shares outstanding","Total revenue divided by the shares outstanding","The share price divided by the firm's earnings","Total dividends divided by reported net income"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"EPS = net income ÷ shares outstanding — the bottom-line profit attributable to each share."},{"id":"t2-m1-l1-q5","prompt":"Net income is the same as the cash a company generated during the period.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False — net income includes non-cash items (e.g. depreciation) and uses accrual accounting. Cash is tracked on the cash flow statement."}]},"t2-m1-l2":{"id":"t2-m1-l2","title":"The Balance Sheet","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The balance sheet is a snapshot at a point in time of what a company owns and owes — and it must always balance.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The accounting equation","body":"Every **balance sheet** obeys one rule: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Everything a company owns (assets) is financed either by what it owes (liabilities) or by its owners (equity). It “balances” by definition."},{"heading":"Assets","body":"**Assets** are resources the company controls that have value: cash, accounts receivable (money owed by customers), inventory, and long-term assets like property, plant, and equipment. They’re usually split into current (expected to convert to cash within a year) and non-current."},{"heading":"Liabilities","body":"**Liabilities** are what the company owes to others: accounts payable (money owed to suppliers), debt/loans, and other obligations. Like assets, they split into current (due within a year) and long-term."},{"heading":"Equity","body":"**Equity** is the owners’ residual claim — assets minus liabilities. It includes money raised by issuing shares (share capital) and **retained earnings** (cumulative profits kept in the business rather than paid out as dividends). Retained earnings is part of equity, not a liability."},{"heading":"Snapshot, not a period","body":"Unlike the **income statement** (which covers a period), the balance sheet is a snapshot at a single date. It tells you the financial position at that moment — useful for assessing solvency, leverage, and **liquidity**."}],"questions":[{"id":"t2-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"Which equation must always hold on the balance sheet?","options":["Assets = Liabilities + Equity","Assets = Revenue − total Expenses","Equity = Assets + all Liabilities","Liabilities = Assets + Equity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The fundamental accounting equation: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Assets are financed by debt or owners’ equity."},{"id":"t2-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"Which of these is a liability?","options":["Cash","Accounts payable","Inventory","Retained earnings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Accounts payable is money owed to suppliers — a liability. Cash and inventory are assets; retained earnings is equity."},{"id":"t2-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"What is equity on the balance sheet?","options":["The company's total revenue booked for the year","The owners' residual claim: assets minus liabilities","Exactly the same thing as the company's cash","The total money the company owes to its lenders"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Equity = assets − liabilities — the owners’ residual stake, including share capital and retained earnings."},{"id":"t2-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"What are retained earnings?","options":["A liability the company owes back to its shareholders","Cumulative profits kept in the business (part of equity)","The revenue the company has booked this year alone","The outstanding money the company owes its suppliers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Retained earnings are accumulated profits not paid out as dividends — a component of equity."},{"id":"t2-m1-l2-q5","prompt":"The balance sheet shows a snapshot at a single point in time.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — unlike the income statement (a period), the balance sheet is a position at one date."}]},"t2-m1-l3":{"id":"t2-m1-l3","title":"The Cash Flow Statement","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The cash flow statement tracks the real cash moving in and out, reconciling profit to the change in the bank balance across three sections.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Why it exists","body":"Because **net income** includes non-cash items and **accruals**, a profitable company can still run out of cash. The **cash flow statement** strips that away and shows the actual cash generated and used over the period, split into three activities."},{"heading":"Cash from operations (CFO)","body":"Operating cash flow is the cash generated by the core business. It typically starts from net income and adds back non-cash expenses (like **depreciation**) and adjusts for changes in **working capital** (receivables, inventory, payables). Healthy companies generate consistent positive CFO."},{"heading":"Cash from investing (CFI)","body":"Investing activities cover buying and selling long-term assets: capital expenditure (**capex**) on equipment and property, acquisitions, and purchases/sales of investments. Heavy capex makes CFI negative — often a sign of a company investing for growth."},{"heading":"Cash from financing (CFF)","body":"Financing activities cover cash exchanged with investors and lenders: issuing or repaying debt, issuing or buying back shares, and paying dividends. Raising capital is a cash inflow; repaying debt, buybacks, and dividends are outflows."},{"heading":"Putting it together","body":"CFO + CFI + CFF = the net change in cash for the period, which ties to the movement in the cash line on the **balance sheet**. A useful derived figure is **free cash flow** ≈ operating cash flow − capex: the cash left over after maintaining and growing the asset base."}],"questions":[{"id":"t2-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"What are the three sections of the cash flow statement?","options":["Revenue, total costs, and resulting profit","Operating, investing, and financing activities","Assets, liabilities, and shareholders' equity","Current, long-term, and total classifications"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cash flow is split into operating (CFO), investing (CFI), and financing (CFF) activities."},{"id":"t2-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Buying new equipment (capex) appears in which section?","options":["Operating","Financing","Investing","It does not appear"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Capital expenditure is an investing activity — cash used to acquire long-term assets."},{"id":"t2-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"Paying a dividend is classified as which type of cash flow?","options":["Operating","Investing","Financing","Non-cash"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dividends paid are a financing outflow — cash returned to shareholders."},{"id":"t2-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"A common approximation of free cash flow is…","options":["Net income added on top of total revenue","Operating cash flow minus capital expenditure","Total revenue with COGS subtracted from it","Total assets minus the company's liabilities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Free cash flow ≈ operating cash flow − capex: cash left after maintaining/growing the asset base."},{"id":"t2-m1-l3-q5","prompt":"Why can a profitable company still run out of cash?","options":["Net income uses accruals and non-cash items, so profit ≠ cash","Reported profit is essentially always a negative figure","Cash and accounting profit are one and the same thing","It is entirely down to the dividends that get paid out"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Accrual accounting and non-cash items mean reported profit can diverge from actual cash — hence the cash flow statement."}]},"t2-m1-l4":{"id":"t2-m1-l4","title":"Connecting the Three Statements","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow statement aren’t separate — they’re three views of the same business, tightly linked.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Net income flows everywhere","body":"**Net income** from the income statement is the starting point of the **cash flow statement** (operating section), and it also flows into the balance sheet: net income minus dividends increases **retained earnings** within equity. So this period’s profit shows up in next period’s equity.","visual":"three-statements"},{"heading":"Cash ties to the balance sheet","body":"The bottom of the cash flow statement — the net change in cash — updates the cash balance on the **balance sheet**. Ending cash = beginning cash + net change in cash. This is the most direct link between two statements."},{"heading":"Depreciation appears twice","body":"**Depreciation** is an expense on the **income statement** (reducing profit) but it’s non-cash, so it’s added back in operating cash flow. It also reduces the value of property, plant & equipment on the balance sheet over time. One item, three footprints."},{"heading":"Working capital connects P&L and cash","body":"Changes in balance-sheet items like **receivables**, inventory, and **payables** adjust operating cash flow. For example, if receivables rise (customers haven’t paid yet), revenue was booked on the income statement but the cash hasn’t arrived — so cash flow is lower than profit."},{"heading":"Why it matters","body":"Because the statements interlock, you can’t change one without consequences in the others — which is exactly why three-statement models are the backbone of financial modelling. A change in assumptions ripples through all three consistently."}],"questions":[{"id":"t2-m1-l4-q1","prompt":"Net income links the income statement to the balance sheet via…","options":["Accounts payable owed to the firm's suppliers","Retained earnings (net income − dividends)","Total revenue booked over the period","Inventory still sitting in the warehouse"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Net income minus dividends adds to retained earnings within equity on the balance sheet."},{"id":"t2-m1-l4-q2","prompt":"The net change in cash on the cash flow statement updates which balance-sheet line?","options":["Equity","Cash","Revenue","COGS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Ending cash = beginning cash + net change in cash — the cash flow statement updates the balance-sheet cash line."},{"id":"t2-m1-l4-q3","prompt":"Why is depreciation added back in operating cash flow?","options":["It is treated as a genuine cash inflow","A non-cash expense that reduced net income","It directly increases the top-line revenue","It is classified as a financing activity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Depreciation lowers net income but uses no cash, so it’s added back to reconcile profit to operating cash."},{"id":"t2-m1-l4-q4","prompt":"If accounts receivable rise during the period, operating cash flow is…","options":["Somewhat higher than the reported net income","Lower than net income (cash not yet collected)","Completely unaffected by the timing difference","Exactly equal to the top-line revenue figure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rising receivables mean revenue was booked but cash hasn’t arrived, reducing operating cash flow relative to profit."},{"id":"t2-m1-l4-q5","prompt":"The three statements are interconnected, so a change in one affects the others.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — that interlock is why integrated three-statement models are central to financial analysis."}]},"t2-m1-l5":{"id":"t2-m1-l5","title":"Key Financial Ratios","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Ratios distil the statements into comparable measures of liquidity, leverage, profitability, and efficiency.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Liquidity","body":"**Liquidity** ratios test whether a company can meet short-term obligations. The current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities; above 1 means current assets cover current liabilities. The stricter quick ratio excludes inventory."},{"heading":"Leverage","body":"**Leverage** ratios measure reliance on debt. Debt-to-equity = total debt ÷ equity shows how aggressively the company is financed. Interest coverage = **EBIT** ÷ interest expense shows how comfortably **operating profit** covers interest payments — higher is safer."},{"heading":"Profitability","body":"Profitability ratios measure how efficiently the company turns revenue and capital into profit. Net margin = **net income** ÷ revenue. Return on equity (ROE) = net income ÷ equity shows the return generated on shareholders’ money."},{"heading":"Efficiency","body":"Efficiency (activity) ratios show how well assets are used. Inventory turnover = **COGS** ÷ average inventory indicates how quickly stock is sold; asset turnover = revenue ÷ total assets shows revenue generated per unit of assets."},{"heading":"Context is everything","body":"A ratio is only meaningful in context — compared to peers, to history, or to industry norms. A “good” current ratio for a supermarket differs from a software firm. Always compare like with like."}],"questions":[{"id":"t2-m1-l5-q1","prompt":"What does the current ratio measure?","options":["The company's long-term profitability outlook","Short-term liquidity: current assets ÷ current liabilities","The size of the dividend the company pays out","The rate at which company revenue is growing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities — a gauge of ability to meet short-term obligations."},{"id":"t2-m1-l5-q2","prompt":"Debt-to-equity is a measure of…","options":["Liquidity","Leverage","Efficiency","Growth"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Debt-to-equity (total debt ÷ equity) is a leverage ratio — how reliant the company is on debt financing."},{"id":"t2-m1-l5-q3","prompt":"Return on equity (ROE) is calculated as…","options":["Net income ÷ equity","Revenue ÷ assets","EBIT ÷ interest","Current assets ÷ current liabilities"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"ROE = net income ÷ equity — the profit generated on shareholders’ invested capital."},{"id":"t2-m1-l5-q4","prompt":"Interest coverage (EBIT ÷ interest) tells you…","options":["How fast the company sells through its inventory","How comfortably operating profit covers interest payments","The company's overall net profit margin percentage","The current level of the company's share price"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Interest coverage shows how many times operating profit covers interest — higher means safer debt servicing."},{"id":"t2-m1-l5-q5","prompt":"A ratio is most meaningful when compared to peers, history, or industry norms.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — ratios need context; a healthy level varies widely by industry and business model."}]},"t2-m2-l1":{"id":"t2-m2-l1","title":"Time Value of Money","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"The foundation of all valuation: money has a time value, so future cash must be discounted to compare it with money today.","xp":20,"questionCount":5},"t2-m2-l2":{"id":"t2-m2-l2","title":"Free Cash Flow","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"A DCF values the cash a business actually generates for its investors — free cash flow — not accounting profit.","xp":25,"questionCount":5},"t2-m2-l3":{"id":"t2-m2-l3","title":"WACC","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"WACC is the blended rate of return a company must earn on its investments — and the rate used to discount unlevered cash flows.","xp":25,"questionCount":5},"t2-m2-l4":{"id":"t2-m2-l4","title":"Terminal Value","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"You can’t forecast forever. Terminal value captures all the cash flows beyond the explicit forecast period — and it usually dominates a DCF.","xp":25,"questionCount":5},"t2-m2-l5":{"id":"t2-m2-l5","title":"Building a DCF","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"Now assemble the pieces: forecast cash flows, discount them, add terminal value, and bridge from enterprise value to a share price.","xp":30,"questionCount":5},"t2-m3-l1":{"id":"t2-m3-l1","title":"Why Companies Merge","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"Mergers and acquisitions are driven by a search for value — usually synergies, scale, or strategic positioning.","xp":25,"questionCount":5},"t2-m3-l2":{"id":"t2-m3-l2","title":"Deal Structures","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"How a deal is paid for and legally structured shapes the risk, tax, and returns for both sides.","xp":25,"questionCount":5},"t2-m3-l3":{"id":"t2-m3-l3","title":"LBO Mechanics","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"A leveraged buyout uses a large amount of debt to acquire a company, aiming to amplify equity returns.","xp":30,"questionCount":5},"t2-m3-l4":{"id":"t2-m3-l4","title":"Accretion/Dilution Analysis","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"Accretion/dilution analysis answers a simple question acquirers obsess over: will this deal increase or decrease the acquirer’s earnings per share?","xp":30,"questionCount":5},"t2-m3-l5":{"id":"t2-m3-l5","title":"Deal Process","trackId":"t2","trackTitle":"Corporate Finance","color":"#10B981","premium":true,"locked":true,"intro":"Deals follow a structured process — from first pitch through diligence to signing and closing — often run as an auction.","xp":30,"questionCount":5},"t3-m1-l1":{"id":"t3-m1-l1","title":"What Central Banks Do","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Central banks manage a nation’s money and credit. Their decisions ripple through every market on earth.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Core mandate","body":"A **central bank**’s primary job is usually price stability — keeping inflation low and steady. Many also pursue maximum employment (the US Federal Reserve has a “**dual mandate**” of stable prices and full employment) and safeguard financial stability. They are typically independent of day-to-day politics so policy isn’t driven by the electoral cycle."},{"heading":"Setting interest rates","body":"The headline tool is the policy interest rate — the rate that influences borrowing costs across the economy. Raising rates cools demand and inflation; cutting rates stimulates borrowing, spending, and growth. This is the lever markets watch most closely."},{"heading":"Lender of last resort","body":"In a crisis, the central bank acts as **lender of last resort** — providing emergency **liquidity** to solvent but illiquid banks to stop panics and bank runs from cascading through the system. This role is central to financial stability."},{"heading":"Other functions","body":"Central banks also issue the currency, oversee the payment system, and often regulate or supervise banks. They manage foreign-exchange reserves and, in some countries, conduct currency interventions."},{"heading":"Why independence matters","body":"Operational **independence** lets central banks make unpopular decisions (like raising rates) for long-term stability. Credible, independent central banks tend to anchor **inflation expectations** better — which itself makes controlling inflation easier."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"What is most central banks’ primary objective?","options":["Maximising the government's total tax revenue","Price stability (low, stable inflation)","Actively pushing the domestic stock market higher","Setting the country's headline income tax rates"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Price stability — keeping inflation low and steady — is the core mandate of most central banks."},{"id":"t3-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"The US Federal Reserve’s \"dual mandate\" is…","options":["Growth and exports","Stable prices and maximum employment","Low taxes and high wages","Strong dollar and trade surplus"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed’s dual mandate is stable prices and maximum (full) employment."},{"id":"t3-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"Raising the policy interest rate is intended to…","options":["Stimulate borrowing","Cool demand and inflation","Increase the money supply","Weaken the banking system"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Higher rates raise borrowing costs, cooling demand and inflation; cuts do the reverse."},{"id":"t3-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"What does \"lender of last resort\" mean?","options":["Providing emergency liquidity to solvent banks in a crisis","Lending money directly to ordinary consumers","Buying company shares to prop up the equity market","Setting the government's overall tax policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In crises the central bank supplies emergency liquidity to stop panics and bank runs spreading."},{"id":"t3-m1-l1-q5","prompt":"Central bank independence helps anchor inflation expectations.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — credible independence lets banks make tough calls and keeps expectations anchored, easing inflation control."}]},"t3-m1-l2":{"id":"t3-m1-l2","title":"Monetary Policy Tools","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Beyond simply “setting rates,” central banks use a toolkit to steer money and credit through the economy.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The policy rate","body":"The main tool is the policy (or base) interest rate — e.g. the Fed funds rate, the ECB’s deposit rate, or the Bank of England’s Bank Rate. It anchors short-term **interest rates** throughout the economy, influencing everything from mortgages to corporate borrowing."},{"heading":"Open market operations","body":"Central banks implement rate decisions through **open market operations** — buying or selling government securities to add or drain reserves in the banking system. Buying securities injects money (eases policy); selling withdraws money (tightens). This keeps market rates near the target."},{"heading":"Reserve requirements","body":"**Reserve requirements** set the minimum reserves banks must hold against deposits. Lowering them frees banks to lend more; raising them restricts lending. Many modern central banks rely less on this tool today, but it remains part of the toolkit."},{"heading":"Forward guidance","body":"Words are a tool too. **Forward guidance** is communication about the likely future path of policy — signalling, for example, that rates will stay low for a long time. By shaping expectations, it influences longer-term rates today, even before any rate change."},{"heading":"Balance-sheet policy","body":"When rates are already near zero, central banks turn to balance-sheet tools — large-scale asset purchases (**quantitative easing**) — to ease further. These “unconventional” tools became mainstream after 2008 and are covered in a later lesson."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"What are open market operations?","options":["Buying and selling government securities to steer reserves and rates","Setting the national rate of income tax","Commissioning and issuing new currency designs","Directly regulating and supervising the national stock exchange and its members"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"OMOs add or drain bank reserves by buying/selling securities, keeping market rates near target."},{"id":"t3-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"Lowering reserve requirements tends to…","options":["Restrict bank lending","Free banks to lend more","Raise the policy rate","Reduce the money supply"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Lower reserve requirements let banks hold less against deposits, enabling more lending."},{"id":"t3-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"What is forward guidance?","options":["Communication about the likely future path of policy to shape expectations","Imposing a mandatory minimum reserve requirement on all commercial banks in the system","Granting a temporary tax break to households","A newly issued type of government bond"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Forward guidance signals future policy, influencing longer-term rates today via expectations."},{"id":"t3-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"When a central bank buys government securities in OMOs, it…","options":["Drains money from the system","Injects money (eases policy)","Raises taxes","Cancels the debt"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buying securities injects reserves/money, easing policy; selling drains money, tightening."},{"id":"t3-m1-l2-q5","prompt":"What is the central bank’s main tool in normal times?","options":["The policy interest rate","The exchange rate peg","The income tax rate","Stock buybacks"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The policy (base) rate is the primary conventional tool, anchoring short-term rates economy-wide."}]},"t3-m1-l3":{"id":"t3-m1-l3","title":"Inflation Targeting","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Most major central banks aim for around 2% inflation. That target shapes nearly every policy decision.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What inflation targeting is","body":"**Inflation** targeting is a framework where the **central bank** publicly commits to a specific inflation rate and sets policy to achieve it over the medium term. The Fed, ECB, and Bank of England all target around 2%."},{"heading":"Why not zero?","body":"A small positive target (not zero) provides a buffer against **deflation**, which is dangerous — falling prices can cause households to delay spending and make debts harder to repay. A 2% cushion also gives the central bank room to cut real rates in a downturn."},{"heading":"Anchoring expectations","body":"A credible target anchors inflation expectations: if workers and firms believe inflation will be ~2%, they set wages and prices accordingly, which helps make 2% self-fulfilling. Losing that **credibility** — letting expectations “de-anchor” — makes inflation far harder to control."},{"heading":"Symmetric, medium-term","body":"Modern targets are symmetric (overshooting and undershooting are both unwelcome) and medium-term (the bank looks through temporary, one-off price moves rather than reacting to every monthly wiggle). This avoids over-steering on noisy data."},{"heading":"The trade-off","body":"To bring high inflation down, central banks raise rates, which slows the economy and can raise **unemployment**. **Inflation targeting** is thus a balancing act between stabilising prices and the cost to growth and jobs."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"What inflation rate do most major central banks target?","options":["0%","Around 2%","Around 10%","As high as possible"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed, ECB, and Bank of England all target inflation of around 2%."},{"id":"t3-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Why target a small positive rate rather than 0%?","options":["A buffer against dangerous deflation, with room to cut rates","Purely to maximise the government's own revenue","Because a zero-percent inflation target is illegal","Deliberately to weaken the domestic currency abroad"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A ~2% cushion guards against deflation and leaves policy room to ease in downturns."},{"id":"t3-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"Why are inflation expectations so important?","options":["If people expect ~2%, they set wages and prices to match, making it self-fulfilling","Inflation expectations have no real effect on the actual path of the economy at all","Inflation expectations directly set the tax rate","They determine only the level of the stock market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Anchored expectations feed into wage and price setting, reinforcing the target; de-anchoring is dangerous."},{"id":"t3-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"Why is deflation (falling prices) considered dangerous?","options":["It makes people delay spending and raises the real burden of debt","It reliably boosts economic growth every single time it appears in the data","It pushes workers' wages up almost instantly","It carries essentially no downside at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deflation can depress spending and raise the real value of debts, risking a downward spiral."},{"id":"t3-m1-l3-q5","prompt":"Inflation targeting focuses on the medium term, looking through temporary price spikes.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — targets are medium-term and symmetric, so banks don’t over-react to one-off, temporary moves."}]},"t3-m1-l4":{"id":"t3-m1-l4","title":"QE and QT","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"When rates hit rock bottom, central banks turn to their balance sheet — buying assets (QE) or letting them roll off (QT).","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Quantitative easing (QE)","body":"**Quantitative easing** is large-scale asset purchases — mainly government bonds — funded by newly created central-bank reserves. QE is used when policy rates are already near zero and the bank wants to ease further. It expands the **central bank**’s **balance sheet**."},{"heading":"How QE works","body":"By buying bonds, QE pushes their prices up and yields down, lowering long-term **interest rates** across the economy. It also injects **liquidity** and can push investors toward riskier assets (the “portfolio balance” effect), supporting borrowing, spending, and asset prices."},{"heading":"Quantitative tightening (QT)","body":"QT is the reverse: the central bank shrinks its balance sheet, usually by letting bonds mature without reinvesting the proceeds (or, less often, selling them). This drains liquidity and puts mild upward pressure on longer-term yields."},{"heading":"When each is used","body":"QE is an easing tool deployed in crises and downturns (notably after 2008 and in 2020). QT is a tightening/normalisation tool used when the economy recovers and the bank wants to unwind the stimulus, typically alongside higher policy rates."},{"heading":"Not “printing money” for spending","body":"QE creates reserves to buy financial assets — it’s not the government printing cash to fund spending directly. The reserves sit in the banking system. Whether QE feeds into consumer prices depends on how much it boosts lending and demand."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m1-l4-q1","prompt":"What is quantitative easing (QE)?","options":["Large-scale asset purchases that expand the balance sheet","Raising the central bank's headline policy rate","Cutting back on total government expenditure","Selling down the country's foreign reserves"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"QE is buying assets (mainly bonds) with newly created reserves to ease policy when rates are near zero."},{"id":"t3-m1-l4-q2","prompt":"QE typically pushes bond yields…","options":["Up","Down","To zero exactly","Unchanged"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buying bonds raises their prices and lowers yields, reducing long-term borrowing costs."},{"id":"t3-m1-l4-q3","prompt":"What is quantitative tightening (QT)?","options":["Shrinking the balance sheet by letting bonds roll off","Launching a fresh new round of large bond buying","Cutting the central bank's main interest rate","Imposing an across-the-board increase in taxes"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"QT reverses QE — the balance sheet shrinks, draining liquidity and nudging long-term yields up."},{"id":"t3-m1-l4-q4","prompt":"When is QE typically deployed?","options":["When policy rates are already near zero and more easing is needed","When inflation across the economy is running very high","When the economy is clearly overheating fast","Primarily as a way to raise extra tax revenue"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"QE is used as additional easing once conventional rate cuts are exhausted (e.g. post-2008, 2020)."},{"id":"t3-m1-l4-q5","prompt":"QE creates reserves to buy financial assets, not cash handed directly to the government to spend.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — QE swaps newly created reserves for financial assets; its inflation impact depends on lending and demand."}]},"t3-m1-l5":{"id":"t3-m1-l5","title":"The Fed vs ECB vs BoE","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"The world’s most-watched central banks share tools but differ in mandate, structure, and the economies they serve.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The Federal Reserve (US)","body":"The Fed sets US **monetary policy** with a dual mandate: stable prices and maximum employment. Rate decisions are made by the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC). Because the US dollar is the world’s **reserve currency**, Fed decisions move global markets more than any other **central bank**’s."},{"heading":"The European Central Bank (ECB)","body":"The ECB runs monetary policy for the euro area — a single currency shared by many countries with one central bank but separate national governments and fiscal policies. Its primary mandate is price stability. Setting one rate for diverse economies (e.g. Germany vs Greece) is a core challenge."},{"heading":"The Bank of England (BoE)","body":"The BoE sets UK policy via its Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), with an inflation target (around 2%) set by the government. It’s a single-country central bank, broadly comparable to the Fed in structure but for one economy and the pound."},{"heading":"Shared toolkit","body":"All three use the same core tools — a **policy rate**, **open market operations**, **forward guidance**, and balance-sheet policy (QE/QT). The mechanics are similar; the differences lie in mandates, governance, and the economies and currencies they manage."},{"heading":"Why divergence matters","body":"When these banks move in different directions — say the Fed hiking while others hold — interest-rate differentials shift, driving currency moves (e.g. a stronger dollar) and global **capital flows**. Markets watch the relative path of policy as much as any single decision."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m1-l5-q1","prompt":"Which committee sets US interest rate policy?","options":["The FOMC","The MPC","The ECB Governing Council","The Treasury"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) makes the Fed’s rate decisions."},{"id":"t3-m1-l5-q2","prompt":"A unique challenge for the ECB is…","options":["Setting one policy rate for many diverse member economies","Operating with no interest-rate tool of any kind","Targeting the level of employment and nothing else","Choosing to issue no physical currency at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The ECB runs one monetary policy for many countries with different economies and separate fiscal policies."},{"id":"t3-m1-l5-q3","prompt":"The Bank of England sets UK rates via its…","options":["The FOMC committee","Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)","The bank's Governing Council","The nation's Supreme Court"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The BoE’s Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) sets UK policy against a government-set inflation target."},{"id":"t3-m1-l5-q4","prompt":"Why do Fed decisions have outsized global impact?","options":["Because the US dollar is the world's reserve currency","Because the Fed happens to be the oldest central bank","Because the United States has essentially no inflation","Because the Fed is the body that sets global tax rates"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The dollar’s reserve-currency status means Fed policy ripples through global markets and funding."},{"id":"t3-m1-l5-q5","prompt":"When central banks’ policies diverge, a key market effect is…","options":["Shifts in interest-rate differentials that drive currency moves","They have no effect on currencies whatsoever","They force interest rates to be identical everywhere","They bring about the end of currency trading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Diverging policy changes rate differentials, moving exchange rates and global capital flows."}]},"t3-m2-l1":{"id":"t3-m2-l1","title":"GDP and Growth","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Gross domestic product is the broadest measure of economic activity — the headline number for an economy’s size and pace.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What GDP measures","body":"**GDP** is the total value of all final goods and services produced in an economy over a period. It’s the standard yardstick for the size of an economy and, in its growth rate, the pace of economic activity."},{"heading":"Real vs nominal","body":"**Nominal** GDP is measured at current prices, so it rises with both higher output and higher prices. Real GDP strips out inflation to show the true change in the volume of output. When economists talk about “growth,” they mean real GDP growth."},{"heading":"The expenditure approach","body":"A common way to break down GDP: consumption + investment + government spending + net exports (exports − imports). In most developed economies, consumer spending is the largest single component."},{"heading":"Recession","body":"A common rule of thumb defines a technical **recession** as two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP. (In the US, the NBER uses a broader judgement of activity, but the “two negative quarters” shorthand is widely used.)"},{"heading":"Why markets watch it","body":"GDP shapes expectations for corporate earnings, employment, and central-bank policy. Strong growth can mean higher rates (to contain inflation); weak growth can mean cuts and stimulus. It’s a backward-looking but foundational gauge."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"What does GDP measure?","options":["The total value of final goods and services produced","The size of the government's annual budget","The overall market value of the stock market","The economy's headline unemployment rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"GDP is the total value of all final goods and services produced over a period — the size of the economy."},{"id":"t3-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"What is the difference between real and nominal GDP?","options":["Real GDP strips out inflation; nominal is at current prices","Nominal GDP is the measure that removes inflation","Real and nominal GDP are one and the same thing","Real GDP entirely ignores the economy's output"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Real GDP adjusts for inflation to show true output change; nominal GDP is at current prices."},{"id":"t3-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"In most developed economies, the largest component of GDP is…","options":["Government spending","Consumer spending","Net exports","Investment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Consumption (consumer spending) is typically the biggest single component of GDP."},{"id":"t3-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"A common rule-of-thumb definition of a recession is…","options":["Two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP","A single month of falling equity-market prices","Any rise at all in the headline inflation rate","One unexpectedly weak monthly jobs report"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth is the widely used technical definition."},{"id":"t3-m2-l1-q5","prompt":"When economists refer to \"growth,\" they usually mean…","options":["Nominal GDP growth","Real GDP growth","Stock market growth","Population growth"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Growth refers to real GDP growth — the inflation-adjusted change in output."}]},"t3-m2-l2":{"id":"t3-m2-l2","title":"Inflation Measures","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Inflation is the rate at which prices rise. Different gauges measure it differently — and central banks care which one.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What inflation is","body":"**Inflation** is the percentage rate at which the general price level rises over time, eroding the purchasing power of money. It’s measured by tracking the price of a representative basket of goods and services."},{"heading":"CPI","body":"The Consumer Price Index (**CPI**) measures the average change in prices paid by consumers for a fixed basket of goods and services. It’s the most widely cited inflation gauge and is often used to index wages, benefits, and contracts."},{"heading":"Core vs headline","body":"Headline inflation includes everything; **core inflation** excludes food and energy, which are volatile. Central banks watch core closely because it better reflects the **underlying**, persistent trend rather than temporary swings in oil or food prices."},{"heading":"PCE","body":"The Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) price index is the US Federal Reserve’s preferred inflation measure. It covers a broader set of spending than CPI and adjusts for consumers substituting between goods as prices change, so it often reads slightly lower than CPI."},{"heading":"RPI","body":"The Retail Prices Index (RPI) is an older UK measure that uses a different formula and includes some housing costs differently from CPI. It tends to run higher than CPI and, though officially downgraded, still appears in some contracts, rail fares, and index-linked bonds."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"What does CPI measure?","options":["The average change in prices consumers pay for a basket","The economy's headline unemployment rate, measured across the whole labour force","The rate at which real GDP is growing","The currency's prevailing exchange rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Consumer Price Index tracks the average change in consumer prices for a fixed basket."},{"id":"t3-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"How does core inflation differ from headline?","options":["Core CPI excludes volatile food and energy prices","Core CPI includes only food and energy prices","Core and headline CPI are exactly identical","Core CPI excludes every category of goods"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Core strips out volatile food and energy to reveal the underlying inflation trend."},{"id":"t3-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"Which inflation measure does the US Federal Reserve prefer?","options":["CPI","PCE","RPI","GDP deflator only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed targets the PCE price index, which is broader and adjusts for substitution."},{"id":"t3-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"What does inflation do to the purchasing power of money?","options":["Increases it","Erodes it","Has no effect","Fixes it permanently"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rising prices mean each unit of money buys less — inflation erodes purchasing power."},{"id":"t3-m2-l2-q5","prompt":"Compared with CPI, the UK’s RPI tends to…","options":["Run higher","Run lower","Be identical","Be negative"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"RPI uses a different formula and tends to run higher than CPI, though it’s been officially downgraded."}]},"t3-m2-l3":{"id":"t3-m2-l3","title":"Employment Data","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Jobs data tells you how much slack is in the economy — and few releases move markets like the US payrolls report.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The unemployment rate","body":"The **unemployment** rate is the share of the labour force that is without a job but actively seeking one. Crucially, it counts only those in the labour force — people who have stopped looking aren’t counted as unemployed, which can flatter the number."},{"heading":"Nonfarm payrolls","body":"In the US, nonfarm payrolls (NFP) measure the net change in jobs added (excluding farm work) each month. Released on the first Friday of the month, it’s one of the most market-moving data points, shaping expectations for Fed policy."},{"heading":"Participation rate","body":"The labour force participation rate is the share of the working-age population that is either working or actively looking. It provides context: a falling unemployment rate can be “good” (more jobs) or partly an artefact of people leaving the labour force."},{"heading":"Wage growth","body":"Average earnings (wage growth) matters for inflation: rapidly rising wages can feed into prices (a wage-price dynamic), so central banks watch it closely. Strong wage growth with low unemployment can signal a tight, possibly inflationary, labour market."},{"heading":"A lagging-ish indicator","body":"Employment tends to respond after the economy turns — firms hire and fire with a lag. Still, because jobs drive incomes and spending, labour data heavily influences both consumer confidence and central-bank decisions."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"The unemployment rate counts people who are…","options":["In the labour force, without a job, and actively seeking one","Everyone without a job, including those not looking","Only part-time workers","Retired people"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"It measures jobless people who are actively seeking work, as a share of the labour force."},{"id":"t3-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"What is \"nonfarm payrolls\"?","options":["The monthly US net change in jobs, excluding farm work","The total number of operating farms in the US","The total value of unemployment benefits paid","One of the standard measures of national GDP"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"NFP is the closely watched monthly change in US jobs excluding agriculture."},{"id":"t3-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"Why does the participation rate add useful context?","options":["A falling unemployment rate can reflect people leaving the labour force, not job gains","The unemployment rate is really just an alternative measure of the inflation rate","The unemployment rate is what sets interest rates","The unemployment rate counts only retired people"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Participation shows whether unemployment moves reflect real hiring or people exiting the labour force."},{"id":"t3-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"Why do central banks watch wage growth?","options":["Rapidly rising wages can feed through into inflation","Wage growth has no bearing on prices at all","Wage data is what sets the currency's exchange rate","Wage figures are really a measure of total exports"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Fast wage growth can push up prices (a wage-price dynamic), a key inflation signal."},{"id":"t3-m2-l3-q5","prompt":"Someone who has stopped looking for work is still counted as unemployed.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False — only those actively seeking work are counted; discouraged job-leavers fall out of the measure."}]},"t3-m2-l4":{"id":"t3-m2-l4","title":"PMI and Business Surveys","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Surveys like the PMI ask businesses how things are going right now — giving a fast, forward-looking read before the official data lands.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"What a PMI is","body":"The Purchasing Managers’ Index (PMI) surveys purchasing managers at companies about activity — new orders, output, employment, supplier deliveries, and inventories. It’s compiled into a single index for manufacturing and for services."},{"heading":"The 50 line","body":"The PMI is a diffusion index centred on 50. A reading above 50 signals expansion (more firms reporting improvement than deterioration); below 50 signals contraction. The distance from 50 indicates the pace of change."},{"heading":"Why it’s timely","body":"PMIs are released quickly — often before official **GDP** or production data — and are forward-looking because they capture new orders and current sentiment. That makes them a valuable early signal of where the economy is heading."},{"heading":"Soft vs hard data","body":"PMIs and confidence surveys are “soft” data — based on opinions and sentiment — versus “hard” data like GDP and industrial production, which measure actual activity. Soft data leads; hard data confirms. Markets watch both, and divergences between them can be telling."},{"heading":"Other surveys","body":"Similar business and consumer surveys (e.g. consumer confidence indices, regional Fed surveys, the German Ifo) round out the picture. As a group they help analysts anticipate turning points before the official statistics catch up."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m2-l4-q1","prompt":"What does a PMI survey?","options":["Purchasing managers, about orders, output and employment","Ordinary households, about their spending plans","National governments, about their tax take","Commercial banks, about their reserve levels"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The PMI surveys purchasing managers on activity such as new orders, output, and employment."},{"id":"t3-m2-l4-q2","prompt":"A PMI reading above 50 signals…","options":["Contraction","Expansion","No change ever","Recession"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Above 50 means expansion; below 50 means contraction — the index is centred on 50."},{"id":"t3-m2-l4-q3","prompt":"Why are PMIs valued by markets?","options":["They are timely and forward-looking, often leading official data","They are only released several years after the fact","They measure past GDP with perfect precision","They are the tool used to set interest rates"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"PMIs come out fast and capture new orders/sentiment, giving an early read on the economy."},{"id":"t3-m2-l4-q4","prompt":"PMIs and confidence surveys are examples of…","options":["\"Soft\", sentiment-based survey data","The category known as \"hard\" data","Government tax-collection data","Corporate balance-sheet data"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Surveys are soft data (opinions/sentiment); GDP and production are hard data (actual activity)."},{"id":"t3-m2-l4-q5","prompt":"A PMI of exactly 50 indicates…","options":["A period of strong economic expansion","No change at all from the prior period","A period of deep economic contraction","A gap caused by missing survey data"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A reading of 50 is the neutral line — neither expansion nor contraction on balance."}]},"t3-m2-l5":{"id":"t3-m2-l5","title":"Reading an Economic Calendar","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Markets move on surprises, not just numbers. An economic calendar helps you anticipate the releases that matter and how they’re likely to be read.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"What the calendar shows","body":"An economic calendar lists upcoming data releases and events — **GDP**, inflation, jobs, central-bank meetings — with their date, time, and importance. High-importance items (like US payrolls, **CPI**, and Fed decisions) are flagged because they move markets most."},{"heading":"Consensus and the surprise","body":"Each release has a consensus (the average analyst forecast) and the prior reading. Markets largely price in the consensus beforehand, so what moves prices is the surprise — how far the actual number deviates from expectations, not the absolute level."},{"heading":"Beats and misses","body":"A “beat” is data stronger than consensus; a “miss” is weaker. The reaction depends on context: strong data can lift stocks (growth) or hurt them (fear of higher rates), so the same number can be read differently depending on the macro backdrop."},{"heading":"Revisions matter","body":"Early data is often revised later as more information arrives. A strong headline can be undercut by a downward revision to the previous month. Analysts watch revisions, not just the latest print, to judge the true trend."},{"heading":"Using it in practice","body":"Traders use the calendar to manage risk around key events — reducing exposure before a major release, or positioning for a likely surprise. Even long-term investors track it to understand **volatility** and the evolving policy picture."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m2-l5-q1","prompt":"What primarily moves markets on a data release?","options":["The surprise relative to consensus expectations","The absolute headline number on its own","The colour scheme used on the data chart","Only the time of day it was released"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Markets price in the consensus; it’s the deviation (surprise) from expectations that moves prices."},{"id":"t3-m2-l5-q2","prompt":"What is \"consensus\" on an economic calendar?","options":["The average analyst forecast","Last year’s figure","The central-bank target","The market close"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Consensus is the average of analysts’ forecasts — the benchmark the actual is judged against."},{"id":"t3-m2-l5-q3","prompt":"A data \"beat\" means the actual figure was…","options":["Weaker than consensus","Stronger than consensus","Exactly consensus","Not released"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A beat is stronger-than-expected data; a miss is weaker than consensus."},{"id":"t3-m2-l5-q4","prompt":"Why do revisions matter?","options":["Early data is often revised, changing the true trend","Data revisions essentially never actually happen","Revisions are what set the consensus forecast","Revisions apply only to stock-market figures"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Initial readings are frequently revised; a strong print can be undercut by a prior-month downward revision."},{"id":"t3-m2-l5-q5","prompt":"Strong economic data can sometimes hurt stocks (e.g. by raising rate-hike fears).","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — context matters: strong data may stoke fears of tighter policy, so \"good news\" can be \"bad news\" for markets."}]},"t3-m3-l1":{"id":"t3-m3-l1","title":"Exchange Rate Basics","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"An exchange rate is simply the price of one currency in terms of another — and it’s quoted in a specific way you need to read correctly.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Base and quote currency","body":"A currency pair like EUR/USD has a base currency (EUR, the first) and a quote currency (USD, the second). The rate tells you how many units of the quote currency one unit of the base buys. So EUR/USD = 1.10 means €1 buys $1.10."},{"heading":"Appreciation and depreciation","body":"If EUR/USD rises from 1.10 to 1.15, the euro has appreciated (and the dollar depreciated) — each euro now buys more dollars. A currency appreciates when it strengthens relative to another, and depreciates when it weakens."},{"heading":"What moves exchange rates","body":"In the short run, interest-rate differentials and **capital flows** dominate: money tends to flow toward higher-yielding currencies, lifting them. Growth, inflation, trade balances, risk sentiment, and central-bank policy all feed in.","visual":"supply-demand"},{"heading":"Why it matters","body":"Exchange rates affect the price of imports and exports, the value of overseas earnings for multinationals, and the returns on foreign investments. A stronger currency makes imports cheaper but exports less competitive — and vice versa."},{"heading":"Floating vs pegged","body":"Most major currencies float freely, set by the market. Some are pegged — fixed to another currency (often the dollar) by the **central bank**, which buys/sells reserves to hold the rate. Pegs offer stability but require defending and can break under pressure."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m3-l1-q1","prompt":"In the pair EUR/USD, which is the base currency?","options":["USD","EUR","Both","Neither"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The first currency (EUR) is the base; the second (USD) is the quote. The rate is quote units per base unit."},{"id":"t3-m3-l1-q2","prompt":"EUR/USD = 1.10 means…","options":["$1 buys €1.10","€1 buys $1.10","They are equal","€1.10 buys $1"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"One unit of the base (€1) buys 1.10 of the quote ($1.10)."},{"id":"t3-m3-l1-q3","prompt":"If EUR/USD rises from 1.10 to 1.15, the euro has…","options":["Depreciated","Appreciated","Stayed flat","Been pegged"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A higher EUR/USD means each euro buys more dollars — the euro appreciated (the dollar depreciated)."},{"id":"t3-m3-l1-q4","prompt":"In the short run, a major driver of exchange rates is…","options":["Interest-rate differentials and capital flows","The number of banknotes printed that day","The colour of the currency","Corporate dividends only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Money tends to flow to higher-yielding currencies, so rate differentials strongly drive short-run FX moves."},{"id":"t3-m3-l1-q5","prompt":"A pegged currency is one that…","options":["Is fixed to another currency by the central bank","Floats entirely freely against other currencies","Has effectively no value of any kind","Cannot legally be traded in any market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A peg fixes the rate to another currency; the central bank buys/sells reserves to maintain it."}]},"t3-m3-l2":{"id":"t3-m3-l2","title":"Purchasing Power Parity","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Purchasing power parity is the idea that, over the long run, exchange rates should equalise the price of the same goods across countries.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The core idea","body":"**Purchasing power parity** (PPP) holds that identical goods should cost the same across countries once converted into a common currency. If they don’t, exchange rates should, in theory, adjust over time until they do — the “law of one price” applied to whole baskets."},{"heading":"The Big Mac Index","body":"The Economist’s Big Mac Index is a light-hearted PPP gauge: it compares the price of a Big Mac across countries. If a Big Mac is much cheaper in one country, its currency may be undervalued versus the dollar on a PPP basis — and vice versa."},{"heading":"A long-run, not short-run, guide","body":"PPP is a long-run anchor, not a short-run predictor. Exchange rates can deviate from PPP for years, driven by **capital flows**, rate differentials, and sentiment. PPP suggests the direction of eventual adjustment, not next week’s move."},{"heading":"Why it deviates","body":"Many goods and services aren’t traded across borders (e.g. haircuts, rent), transport costs and tariffs create wedges, and taxes differ. These frictions mean prices never fully equalise, so real-world rates persistently depart from strict PPP."},{"heading":"Using PPP","body":"Economists use PPP to compare living standards and the “real” size of economies (PPP-adjusted **GDP**) more fairly than market exchange rates, which can be volatile and distorted. It’s a tool for comparison, not trading."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m3-l2-q1","prompt":"What does purchasing power parity (PPP) say?","options":["Identical goods should cost the same across countries","Currency exchange rates essentially never change","Every currency in the world is formally pegged","The rate of inflation is always exactly zero"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"PPP applies the law of one price to baskets: rates should adjust so identical goods cost the same."},{"id":"t3-m3-l2-q2","prompt":"The Big Mac Index is a playful measure of…","options":["PPP and currency over- or under-valuation","Prevailing market interest rates","The economy's rate of GDP growth","The headline unemployment rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"It compares burger prices across countries as an informal gauge of PPP-based currency valuation."},{"id":"t3-m3-l2-q3","prompt":"PPP is best understood as…","options":["A long-run anchor rather than a short-run predictor","A precise daily forecast of exchange rates","One of the central bank's main policy tools","A particular type of government bond"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rates can deviate from PPP for years; it indicates long-run direction, not short-term moves."},{"id":"t3-m3-l2-q4","prompt":"Why do real exchange rates deviate from PPP?","options":["Non-traded goods, transport costs, tariffs and taxes create wedges","Because purchasing power parity is actually illegal","Because every good is freely traded across borders","Because currency exchange rates never move at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Frictions like non-traded services, shipping costs, tariffs, and taxes prevent full price equalisation."},{"id":"t3-m3-l2-q5","prompt":"PPP-adjusted GDP is used to…","options":["Compare living standards and economy sizes more fairly","Set the central bank's main policy interest rate","Price options and other financial derivatives","Measure the headline rate of inflation only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"PPP adjustment gives a fairer cross-country comparison than volatile market exchange rates."}]},"t3-m3-l3":{"id":"t3-m3-l3","title":"Interest Rate Parity","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Interest rate parity links exchange rates and interest rates: differences in rates between two countries are reflected in their forward exchange rate.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The no-arbitrage idea","body":"Interest rate parity (IRP) is a no-**arbitrage** condition: you shouldn’t be able to make a risk-free profit by borrowing in one currency, converting, investing in another, and locking in the future **exchange rate**. If you could, traders would exploit it until the gap closed."},{"heading":"Spot vs forward rates","body":"The spot rate is the exchange rate for immediate delivery. A forward rate is an agreed rate today for exchanging currencies at a set future date. IRP determines the relationship between the two, given the two countries’ **interest rates**."},{"heading":"How the differential shows up","body":"The currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward discount (it’s expected to be weaker in the forward market), and the lower-rate currency at a forward premium. This exactly offsets the interest advantage, so there’s no free lunch on a hedged basis."},{"heading":"Covered vs uncovered","body":"Covered interest parity uses a **forward contract** to lock in the future rate — it holds tightly because it’s arbitrage-enforced. Uncovered interest parity assumes the expected future spot rate adjusts instead (no **hedge**); it’s a theory about expectations and holds far less reliably in practice."},{"heading":"The carry trade","body":"In reality, uncovered parity often fails: borrowing a low-rate currency to invest in a high-rate one — the “carry trade” — has historically earned returns, because high-rate currencies don’t depreciate as much as theory predicts. But it carries crash risk when sentiment turns."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m3-l3-q1","prompt":"Interest rate parity is fundamentally…","options":["A no-arbitrage condition linking rates and exchange rates","One of the formal statutory mandates handed to the central bank by government","A specific corporate tax accounting rule","A particular category of government bond"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"IRP says you can’t earn a risk-free profit from rate differences once the forward rate is accounted for."},{"id":"t3-m3-l3-q2","prompt":"What is a forward exchange rate?","options":["An agreed rate today for exchanging currencies later","The rate quoted for immediate spot delivery","The central bank's headline policy interest rate","Simply last year's average exchange rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A forward rate locks in today the price for a currency exchange that settles in the future."},{"id":"t3-m3-l3-q3","prompt":"Under IRP, the higher-interest-rate currency typically trades at a forward…","options":["Premium","Discount","Peg","Par exactly"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The higher-rate currency trades at a forward discount, offsetting its interest advantage — no free lunch."},{"id":"t3-m3-l3-q4","prompt":"Covered interest parity differs from uncovered because it…","options":["Uses a forward contract to lock in the future rate","Simply ignores interest rates altogether","Applies only to equities, never currencies","Assumes exchange rates are permanently fixed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Covered IRP hedges with a forward and holds tightly; uncovered relies on expected spot moves and holds weakly."},{"id":"t3-m3-l3-q5","prompt":"The \"carry trade\" involves…","options":["Borrowing a low-rate currency to invest in a higher-rate one","Trading only in formally pegged currencies","Deliberately avoiding any and all exposure to interest rates across the portfolio","Simply buying and holding physical cash"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Carry exploits the historical failure of uncovered parity, but carries crash risk when sentiment reverses."}]},"t3-m3-l4":{"id":"t3-m3-l4","title":"EM vs DM Currencies","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Emerging-market and developed-market currencies behave very differently — in yield, volatility, and how they respond to global risk.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"DM vs EM","body":"Developed-market (DM) currencies — the dollar, euro, yen, pound, Swiss franc — belong to large, stable, liquid economies. Emerging-market (EM) currencies — like the Brazilian real, South African rand, or Turkish lira — come from faster-growing but less stable economies."},{"heading":"Higher yields, higher risk","body":"EM currencies typically offer higher **interest rates** to compensate investors for greater risk: higher inflation, political and policy uncertainty, and weaker institutions. That yield attracts carry-trade flows in calm times."},{"heading":"Volatility and liquidity","body":"EM currencies are generally more volatile and less liquid than DM currencies. They can move sharply on local politics, commodity prices (many EMs are commodity exporters), and shifts in global risk appetite."},{"heading":"Risk-on, risk-off","body":"In “risk-on” periods, investors chase EM yield and EM currencies tend to strengthen. In “risk-off” periods (crises, fear), capital flees to safe havens — the dollar, yen, and Swiss franc — and EM currencies often fall hard. EM is high-beta to global risk."},{"heading":"Safe havens","body":"Certain DM currencies act as safe havens, appreciating when markets panic: the US dollar (**reserve currency** and deep markets), the Japanese yen, and the Swiss franc. This flight-to-safety behaviour is the mirror image of EM weakness in stress."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m3-l4-q1","prompt":"EM currencies typically offer higher interest rates because…","options":["They compensate investors for higher risk and inflation","Their underlying economies essentially never grow","They are all formally pegged to the gold price","They experience no domestic inflation at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Higher yields offset greater risks — inflation, political/policy uncertainty, weaker institutions."},{"id":"t3-m3-l4-q2","prompt":"Compared with DM currencies, EM currencies are generally…","options":["Rather less volatile than developed markets","More volatile and less liquid than peers","Always formally pegged to the US dollar","Effectively risk-free for global investors"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"EM currencies tend to be more volatile and less liquid, moving on local and global risk factors."},{"id":"t3-m3-l4-q3","prompt":"In a \"risk-off\" period, capital tends to…","options":["Flee to safe havens like the USD, JPY and CHF","Pile aggressively into emerging-market currencies","Stay perfectly still and make no moves at all","Rotate exclusively into the equity markets"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Fear drives a flight to safety — the dollar, yen, and Swiss franc — while EM currencies often fall."},{"id":"t3-m3-l4-q4","prompt":"Which is considered a safe-haven currency?","options":["The Turkish lira","The Japanese yen","The Brazilian real","The South African rand"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The yen — alongside the US dollar and Swiss franc — strengthens in risk-off, safe-haven episodes."},{"id":"t3-m3-l4-q5","prompt":"EM currencies tend to strengthen in \"risk-on\" periods when investors chase yield.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — in calm, risk-on conditions, yield-seeking flows support EM currencies; risk-off reverses this."}]},"t3-m3-l5":{"id":"t3-m3-l5","title":"FX and Trade Flows","trackId":"t3","trackTitle":"Macro & Monetary Policy","color":"#F59E0B","premium":false,"intro":"Exchange rates and trade move together: currencies shape competitiveness, and trade and capital flows shape currencies.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Currency and competitiveness","body":"A weaker domestic currency makes a country’s exports cheaper for foreign buyers and imports more expensive at home — boosting export competitiveness. A stronger currency does the reverse: cheaper imports, but exports become pricier abroad."},{"heading":"The current account","body":"The **current account** records a country’s trade in goods and services (plus income flows) with the world. A surplus means it sells more than it buys; a deficit means it buys more than it sells. Persistent imbalances influence currency values over time."},{"heading":"Trade flows vs capital flows","body":"Trade flows (paying for imports and exports) create currency demand, but **capital flows** (cross-border investment in bonds, stocks, and assets) are now far larger and often dominate day-to-day FX moves. A country can run a **trade deficit** yet have a strong currency if it attracts enough capital inflows."},{"heading":"The adjustment mechanism","body":"In theory, a trade deficit can weaken a currency, which then makes exports cheaper and imports dearer, gradually shrinking the deficit — a self-correcting loop. In practice, capital flows and policy can delay or override this adjustment for long periods."},{"heading":"Why it matters","body":"Currency moves feed back into inflation (via import prices), corporate earnings (exporters vs importers), and policy. Central banks and governments watch the **exchange rate** as both a cause and a consequence of the trade and capital picture."}],"questions":[{"id":"t3-m3-l5-q1","prompt":"A weaker domestic currency tends to make exports…","options":["More expensive abroad","Cheaper for foreign buyers","Illegal","Unchanged"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A weaker currency lowers the foreign-currency price of exports, boosting competitiveness (imports get dearer)."},{"id":"t3-m3-l5-q2","prompt":"What does the current account record?","options":["Trade in goods and services, plus income, with the world","The size of the government's annual budget","The central bank's headline policy rate","Total returns from the domestic stock market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The current account captures trade in goods/services and income flows; surplus = sells more than it buys."},{"id":"t3-m3-l5-q3","prompt":"In day-to-day FX moves, which now often dominate?","options":["Capital flows, meaning cross-border investment","Only the physical trade flows in goods, and nothing else across the border","Just international tourism receipts","The printing of new banknotes"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Capital flows dwarf trade flows in size and frequently drive short-term currency moves."},{"id":"t3-m3-l5-q4","prompt":"How can a country run a trade deficit yet still have a strong currency?","options":["If it attracts enough offsetting capital inflows","It cannot do so under any circumstances","By simply printing more of its own money","By imposing an outright ban on all imports"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Strong capital inflows can support a currency even alongside a trade deficit."},{"id":"t3-m3-l5-q5","prompt":"Why do currency moves feed into inflation?","options":["A weaker currency raises import prices","Currencies have no effect on prices","Only exports affect inflation","Inflation is fixed by law"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A weaker currency makes imports more expensive, feeding into domestic inflation (imported inflation)."}]},"t4-m1-l1":{"id":"t4-m1-l1","title":"Diversification","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Diversification is the only free lunch in finance: combining assets that do not move in lockstep reduces overall portfolio risk without necessarily sacrificing return.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Why diversification works","body":"When one asset in your portfolio falls, another may rise or hold steady. If two assets are perfectly correlated (they always move together), combining them offers no benefit. But in practice, different assets respond differently to news — adding them together smooths the overall ride. This is **diversification**: risk reduction through combination.","icon":"shuffle"},{"heading":"Correlation — the key concept","body":"**Correlation** is a number between -1 and +1. A correlation of +1 means two assets move in perfect lockstep — no diversification benefit. A correlation of 0 means their moves are unrelated — combining them reduces risk. A correlation of -1 means they move in opposite directions — a perfect hedge. Real-world assets tend to have correlations between 0.2 and 0.8, providing partial but meaningful diversification.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Systematic vs unsystematic risk","body":"Unsystematic (or idiosyncratic) risk is specific to a single company or sector — a CEO scandal, a product recall. This risk can be diversified away by holding many companies. Systematic risk (market risk) affects all assets and cannot be diversified away — it includes recessions, rate shocks, and panics. Diversification only eliminates unsystematic risk.","icon":"filter"},{"heading":"How many holdings?","body":"Studies show that most of the diversification benefit kicks in with roughly 20–30 uncorrelated stocks. Beyond that, adding more holdings offers diminishing risk reduction. Owning 500 stocks does not meaningfully reduce systematic risk vs owning 30 well-chosen ones — but it does increase complexity and may dilute your best ideas.","icon":"list-circle"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"What type of risk can diversification eliminate?","options":["Systematic, market-wide risk affecting all stocks","Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Interest-rate risk driven by central-bank moves","Inflation risk that erodes investors' real returns"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Diversification eliminates unsystematic (idiosyncratic) risk — risk specific to individual companies or sectors. Systematic risk affects all assets and cannot be diversified away."},{"id":"t4-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"If two assets have a correlation of -1, combining them in a portfolio:","options":["Doubles the total risk","Provides no diversification benefit","Can eliminate portfolio risk entirely","Only works for bonds, not equities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A correlation of -1 means the assets move in perfectly opposite directions. In theory, the right combination can reduce portfolio risk to zero — making it a perfect hedge."},{"id":"t4-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"Beyond roughly how many stocks does additional diversification offer diminishing returns?","options":["5","20–30","100","500"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Most of the diversification benefit from equities kicks in with 20–30 uncorrelated stocks. Adding more holdings beyond this provides minimal additional risk reduction."},{"id":"t4-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"A correlation of 0 between two assets means:","options":["They always move in the same direction","They always move in opposite directions","Their movements are unrelated to each other","One is always more volatile than the other"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A correlation of 0 means the assets' movements are statistically unrelated — combining them provides meaningful diversification benefit."},{"id":"t4-m1-l1-q5","prompt":"Why is diversification called the \"only free lunch in finance\"?","options":["It completely eliminates all investment risk from a portfolio","It reduces risk without necessarily sacrificing expected return","It guarantees a positive return in every single year","It is available only to large professional investors"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Diversification reduces risk through combination — without necessarily giving up expected return. Most other risk-reduction strategies cost you something in expected return. Hence the \"free lunch\" label."}]},"t4-m1-l2":{"id":"t4-m1-l2","title":"Risk & Return","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Every investment decision is a risk-return trade-off. Higher expected return almost always comes with higher risk — understanding exactly what \"risk\" means is the foundation of professional investing.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What is risk?","body":"In finance, risk is typically measured as **volatility** — the standard deviation of returns. A high-volatility asset has returns that swing widely around its average; a low-volatility asset delivers more predictable returns. Volatility captures uncertainty, not just downside. A stock that might return +50% or -40% in a year has high risk even if its average return is attractive.","icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Expected return vs realised return","body":"Expected return is the probability-weighted average of all possible outcomes — what you anticipate earning on average over many periods. Realised return is what actually happened. In any single period, realised returns can deviate wildly from expected returns. The **risk premium** is the extra return you expect for bearing risk versus holding a risk-free asset like a **government bond**.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"The risk premium","body":"Investors demand compensation for bearing risk. The equity risk premium is the extra return equities must offer above the risk-free rate to attract investors. Historically, equities have returned about 4–6% per year above cash or short-dated government bonds — the long-run reward for tolerating volatility, bear markets, and the occasional wipeout.","icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Standard deviation in practice","body":"If a portfolio has an expected annual return of 8% and a standard deviation of 12%, the \"one-sigma\" range of outcomes is -4% to +20% in any given year. Two-thirds of outcomes should fall within one standard deviation of the mean. Doubling the standard deviation captures about 95% of outcomes. This is why volatility is the dominant risk metric in portfolio management.","icon":"bar-chart"},{"heading":"Time horizon matters","body":"Risk looks different across time horizons. A 50% **drawdown** is devastating if you need the money next year; it is a blip if you are investing for 30 years. Long-horizon investors can take more equity risk because they have time to wait out volatility. Matching risk to time horizon is one of the most important decisions in portfolio construction.","icon":"time"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"In finance, risk is most commonly measured as:","options":["The chance of losing all your money","The standard deviation of returns","The maximum possible loss","The correlation with the market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Standard deviation of returns — volatility — is the dominant risk measure in portfolio management. It captures the dispersion of returns around the average."},{"id":"t4-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"The equity risk premium refers to:","options":["The extra return equities must offer above the risk-free rate","The tax advantage of holding equities","The dividend yield on a diversified equity portfolio","The volatility of equity returns relative to bonds"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The equity risk premium is the extra expected return equities offer investors to compensate for their higher risk compared to holding a risk-free asset like a government bond. Historically around 4–6% per year."},{"id":"t4-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"A portfolio with an expected return of 8% and standard deviation of 12% should have returns within the range of -4% to +20% approximately:","options":["50% of the time","68% of the time","95% of the time","99% of the time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"One standard deviation above and below the mean captures approximately 68% of outcomes in a normal distribution. So -4% to +20% (8% ± 12%) should occur about two-thirds of the time."},{"id":"t4-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"Why can long-horizon investors generally afford to take more risk?","options":["They are simply smarter at picking individual stocks","They have time to wait out volatility and recover from drawdowns","They pay a noticeably lower rate of tax on their gains","They have privileged access to far better information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Long time horizons allow investors to ride out market downturns without being forced to sell at a loss. The longer your horizon, the more volatility you can absorb — historically, equity markets have always recovered and gone on to new highs given enough time."},{"id":"t4-m1-l2-q5","prompt":"The difference between expected return and realised return is:","options":["The compensation known as the equity risk premium","It is essentially always zero over the long run","The result of actual market outcomes deviating from forecasts","Exactly the same thing as the standard deviation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Expected return is the probability-weighted average outcome. Realised return is what actually happened. In any given period, actual outcomes can differ significantly from expectations due to randomness and unforeseen events."}]},"t4-m1-l3":{"id":"t4-m1-l3","title":"The Efficient Frontier","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The efficient frontier is the set of portfolios that offer the highest expected return for each level of risk. Portfolios below it are suboptimal — you could do better without taking more risk.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Plotting risk vs return","body":"Imagine plotting every possible portfolio on a chart: expected return on the vertical axis, standard deviation (risk) on the horizontal axis. Some portfolios are obviously inferior — they offer less return for the same risk as another option. The efficient frontier is the upper-left edge of this cloud of possibilities: the set of portfolios you cannot improve without accepting more risk.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Dominated portfolios","body":"A **portfolio** is dominated if another portfolio offers a higher expected return for the same (or less) risk. Rational investors never hold dominated portfolios — they shift to the dominating option. The efficient frontier contains only non-dominated portfolios. Any portfolio below the frontier is inefficient by definition.","icon":"close-circle"},{"heading":"The minimum variance portfolio","body":"At the far left of the efficient frontier sits the minimum variance portfolio (MVP) — the combination of assets that delivers the lowest possible **volatility**, regardless of return. It is the starting point of the frontier. Adding more expected return from this point requires accepting more risk.","icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Where does the optimal portfolio lie?","body":"The efficient frontier tells you which portfolios are available; it does not tell you which to choose. That depends on your risk tolerance. A conservative investor picks a portfolio closer to the minimum variance point; an aggressive investor picks one further right and up. No single portfolio is universally optimal.","icon":"star"},{"heading":"Adding a risk-free asset: the Capital Market Line","body":"When you add a risk-free asset (like a **government bond**) to the mix, the efficient frontier becomes a straight line called the Capital Market Line (CML). The tangency portfolio — where the CML touches the curved frontier — is the optimal risky portfolio. Any rational investor should hold the tangency portfolio mixed with the risk-free asset in proportions matching their risk tolerance.","icon":"trending-up"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"The efficient frontier represents portfolios that:","options":["Guarantee a positive return","Offer the highest expected return for each level of risk","Have the lowest possible fees","Are only available to institutional investors"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The efficient frontier is the set of portfolios offering the highest expected return for each given level of risk. Portfolios below it are suboptimal — you could get more return without taking more risk."},{"id":"t4-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"A portfolio is described as \"dominated\" if:","options":["It happens to have the single highest return in the whole market","Another portfolio offers higher return for the same or less risk","It is passively managed by a low-cost index fund","It holds a portfolio of more than fifty different stocks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A dominated portfolio is one where another option offers a higher return for the same risk (or the same return for less risk). Rational investors avoid dominated portfolios."},{"id":"t4-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"The minimum variance portfolio is:","options":["Simply the portfolio with the highest expected return of all","The portfolio with the lowest possible volatility regardless of return","A portfolio that is invested entirely in cash and equivalents","The single optimal portfolio suitable for every investor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The minimum variance portfolio (MVP) is the combination of risky assets that produces the lowest possible standard deviation — it sits at the far-left tip of the efficient frontier."},{"id":"t4-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"Adding a risk-free asset transforms the curved efficient frontier into:","options":["It traces out a perfect circle on the chart","A straight line called the Capital Market Line","It forms a downward-opening parabola shape","It traces out a distinct inverted U-shape"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When a risk-free asset is available, all efficient portfolios lie on a straight line — the Capital Market Line (CML) — that is tangent to the curved risky-asset frontier."},{"id":"t4-m1-l3-q5","prompt":"Which investor should hold a portfolio closer to the minimum variance point on the frontier?","options":["An aggressive investor with a 30-year horizon","A pension fund with very long liabilities","A conservative investor with low risk tolerance","A hedge fund seeking maximum alpha"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The minimum variance portfolio minimises risk at the expense of return. It is most appropriate for conservative investors who prioritise capital preservation over growth."}]},"t4-m1-l4":{"id":"t4-m1-l4","title":"Beta & Market Risk","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Beta measures how much a stock or portfolio moves relative to the overall market. It is the central risk concept in the Capital Asset Pricing Model — the most widely used framework for pricing assets.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What beta means","body":"**Beta** (β) measures the sensitivity of an asset's returns to market returns. A beta of 1 means the asset moves in line with the market.\n\nA beta of 1.5 means it moves 50% more than the market in each direction. A beta of 0.5 means it moves half as much. A negative beta means it tends to move opposite to the market — gold and some defensive stocks can have low or negative betas.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"High-beta vs low-beta stocks","body":"High-beta stocks (tech, small caps, leveraged companies) amplify market moves in both directions — exciting in bull markets, brutal in bear markets. Low-beta stocks (utilities, consumer staples, healthcare) hold up better when markets fall but lag in rallies. Investors with lower risk tolerance often tilt toward low-beta assets to dampen **volatility**.","icon":"swap-vertical"},{"heading":"The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)","body":"CAPM formalises the beta concept: Expected Return = Risk-Free Rate + Beta × (Market Return - Risk-Free Rate). The term (Market Return - Risk-Free Rate) is the equity **risk premium**. CAPM says you should only be compensated for systematic (market) risk — not unsystematic risk, which can be diversified away for free.","icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Portfolio beta","body":"The beta of a **portfolio** is the weighted average of its component betas. If you hold 60% in a stock with beta 1.5 and 40% in a stock with beta 0.5, the portfolio beta is (0.6 × 1.5) + (0.4 × 0.5) = 1.1. Portfolio managers use this to understand and control how much market exposure they are running at any time.","icon":"pie-chart"},{"heading":"Limits of beta","body":"Beta is backward-looking — it is estimated from historical price data and may not predict future sensitivity. It also assumes a linear relationship between asset and market returns, which breaks down in extreme markets. And it only captures systematic risk; alpha — the excess return above what CAPM predicts — is not captured by beta.","icon":"alert-circle"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m1-l4-q1","prompt":"A stock with a beta of 1.5 would be expected to:","options":["Return 1.5% per year regardless of the market","Move 50% more than the market in each direction","Have a 50% chance of beating the market","Be 1.5× more diversified than a benchmark"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta of 1.5 means the stock is expected to amplify market moves by 50% — up and down. If the market rises 10%, the stock would be expected to rise 15%; if the market falls 10%, the stock would fall 15%."},{"id":"t4-m1-l4-q2","prompt":"According to CAPM, investors should only be compensated for:","options":["Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Systematic (market) risk","Both systematic and unsystematic risk","Volatility that exceeds 15% per year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CAPM says unsystematic risk can be diversified away for free, so the market only rewards investors for bearing systematic (market) risk — captured by beta."},{"id":"t4-m1-l4-q3","prompt":"If you hold 50% in a stock with beta 2.0 and 50% in a stock with beta 0, the portfolio beta is:","options":["0","1.0","1.5","2.0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas. (0.5 × 2.0) + (0.5 × 0) = 1.0. The portfolio moves in line with the market on average."},{"id":"t4-m1-l4-q4","prompt":"Which type of stock typically has a low or negative beta?","options":["High-growth technology companies","Leveraged small-cap companies","Utilities and consumer staples","Emerging market equities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Utilities and consumer staples sell things people buy regardless of the economic cycle (electricity, food). Their earnings are stable, so their stock prices are less sensitive to overall market swings — giving them low betas."},{"id":"t4-m1-l4-q5","prompt":"A key limitation of beta is that:","options":["It simply cannot be calculated for individual stocks","It is backward-looking and may not predict future sensitivity","It completely ignores the dividend income a stock pays","It is really only relevant when analysing bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta is estimated from historical data and is backward-looking. A company's sensitivity to the market can change significantly over time, making historical beta an imperfect predictor of future behaviour."}]},"t4-m1-l5":{"id":"t4-m1-l5","title":"The Sharpe Ratio","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"The Sharpe ratio is the most widely used risk-adjusted performance metric in finance. It answers a simple but powerful question: for each unit of risk you took, how much excess return did you earn?","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The formula","body":"Sharpe Ratio = (Portfolio Return − Risk-Free Rate) ÷ Portfolio Standard Deviation. The numerator (excess return above the risk-free rate) measures what you earned for taking risk. The denominator (standard deviation) measures how much risk you took. A higher Sharpe ratio means better risk-adjusted performance.","icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Interpreting Sharpe ratios","body":"A Sharpe ratio above 1 is generally considered good. Above 2 is excellent. Above 3 is exceptional and rare — often a sign of a strategy with unusual characteristics or a short track record. A Sharpe below 0 means the strategy underperformed the risk-free rate after accounting for **volatility** — a poor outcome. Most long-only equity funds have Sharpe ratios between 0.3 and 0.8 over long periods.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Why it matters","body":"Two portfolios may have the same return, but if one achieved it with half the volatility, it is clearly the better outcome — a higher Sharpe ratio captures this. Equally, two portfolios with the same volatility can have different Sharpe ratios if one earned more excess return. It is the right way to compare managers who run different risk levels.","icon":"trophy"},{"heading":"Limitations","body":"The Sharpe ratio penalises upside and downside volatility equally — but investors care more about downside risk. The Sortino ratio addresses this by using only downside deviation in the denominator. Sharpe also assumes normal distribution of returns; strategies with fat tails or option-like payoffs can game Sharpe by suppressing measured volatility while hiding **tail risk**.","icon":"alert-circle"},{"heading":"Information ratio vs Sharpe ratio","body":"The information ratio (IR) is a related concept used in **active management**: IR = (Portfolio Return − Benchmark Return) ÷ **Tracking Error**. Where Sharpe measures excess return vs the risk-free rate, the IR measures excess return vs a chosen benchmark per unit of active risk. A good fund manager is expected to have a consistent IR above 0.5.","icon":"swap-horizontal"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m1-l5-q1","prompt":"What does the Sharpe ratio measure?","options":["Total return over a given period","Excess return per unit of risk taken","The correlation between a portfolio and the market","The percentage of winning vs losing months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Sharpe ratio = (Return − Risk-Free Rate) ÷ Standard Deviation. It measures how much excess return you earned for each unit of volatility risk — the classic risk-adjusted performance metric."},{"id":"t4-m1-l5-q2","prompt":"A Sharpe ratio of -0.2 means:","options":["The portfolio delivered a slightly negative total return","The strategy underperformed the risk-free rate after adjusting for volatility","The portfolio was around 20% more volatile than the benchmark","Returns happened to be negative in 20% of the months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A negative Sharpe ratio means the excess return (return minus risk-free rate) was negative — the strategy would have been better served by holding a risk-free asset."},{"id":"t4-m1-l5-q3","prompt":"Portfolio A returns 12% with 20% volatility. Portfolio B returns 10% with 10% volatility. The risk-free rate is 4%. Which has a higher Sharpe ratio?","options":["Portfolio A (Sharpe ≈ 0.4)","Portfolio B (Sharpe ≈ 0.6)","They are equal","Cannot be determined without more information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Portfolio A Sharpe = (12-4)/20 = 0.4. Portfolio B Sharpe = (10-4)/10 = 0.6. Portfolio B has the better risk-adjusted return despite the lower absolute return."},{"id":"t4-m1-l5-q4","prompt":"The Sortino ratio differs from the Sharpe ratio in that it:","options":["Uses the market return instead of the risk-free rate","Only uses downside deviation instead of total standard deviation","Is calculated monthly rather than annually","Is used exclusively for bond portfolios"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Sortino ratio replaces standard deviation with downside deviation — only penalising for below-target returns. This is more intuitive since investors care more about downside volatility than upside."},{"id":"t4-m1-l5-q5","prompt":"The Information Ratio measures:","options":["How much data a fund manager analyses","Excess return vs a benchmark per unit of active risk (tracking error)","The ratio of good to bad information used in stock picking","Total return divided by total fees charged"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Information Ratio = (Portfolio Return − Benchmark Return) ÷ Tracking Error. It measures a manager's skill at generating active return per unit of active risk taken relative to a benchmark."}]},"t4-m2-l1":{"id":"t4-m2-l1","title":"Asset Class Basics","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Every portfolio is built from a mix of asset classes: equities, fixed income, alternatives, and cash. Understanding how they behave — and how they interact — is the foundation of portfolio construction.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Equities (stocks)","body":"Equities represent ownership in companies. They are the highest-returning major asset class over the long run — global equities have averaged 7–10% per year historically — but also the most volatile. They are most appropriate for long-horizon investors who can tolerate bear markets (typically 30–50% drawdowns every decade or so).","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Fixed income (bonds)","body":"Bonds are loans to governments or corporations that pay regular interest. They are less volatile than equities and provide a more predictable income stream. Investment-grade government bonds have historically delivered 3–5% per year. In a diversified portfolio, bonds act as a stabiliser — they often rise when equities fall, providing ballast during market stress.","icon":"document-text"},{"heading":"Alternatives","body":"The \"alternatives\" bucket includes anything outside traditional stocks and bonds: real estate, commodities (gold, oil), **private equity**, **hedge** funds, infrastructure, and more. These assets often have lower correlations to equities and bonds, making them valuable diversifiers. The trade-off: they are typically less liquid, harder to value, and often require higher minimum investments.","icon":"grid"},{"heading":"Cash and cash equivalents","body":"Cash and near-cash instruments (money market funds, T-bills) offer the lowest risk and return. They protect capital in the short term and provide **liquidity** — the ability to redeploy into other assets quickly. In a portfolio, cash is a strategic buffer: it limits upside but ensures you can meet obligations without selling other assets at an inopportune time.","icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Strategic asset allocation","body":"Strategic **asset allocation** (SAA) is the long-run target mix of asset classes. The classic \"60/40\" portfolio holds 60% equities and 40% bonds — historically a good balance of growth and stability. The SAA is the primary driver of a portfolio's long-run return and risk. Tactical tilts (short-term deviations) matter much less than the strategic baseline.","icon":"pie-chart"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"Which asset class has historically delivered the highest long-run returns?","options":["Government bonds","Cash and money market funds","Equities","Commodities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Global equities have historically returned 7–10% per year, more than any other major asset class. The trade-off is higher volatility and periodic severe drawdowns."},{"id":"t4-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"In a diversified portfolio, bonds primarily serve to:","options":["Maximise long-run returns","Provide liquidity for day-to-day transactions","Stabilise the portfolio and often rise when equities fall","Eliminate inflation risk"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bonds act as a portfolio stabiliser. Investment-grade bonds often rise in value during equity bear markets as investors seek safety, helping to cushion overall portfolio losses."},{"id":"t4-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"Alternative assets are typically valued for their:","options":["Noticeably higher liquidity than ordinary listed equities","It offers investors guaranteed positive returns","Lower correlation to stocks and bonds, providing diversification","Its unusual simplicity and full transparency"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The main appeal of alternatives is low correlation to traditional assets. When equities and bonds both fall (as in 2022), alternatives with different return drivers can help reduce portfolio losses."},{"id":"t4-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"The classic \"60/40\" portfolio refers to:","options":["60% bonds, 40% cash","60% equities, 40% bonds","60% domestic stocks, 40% international stocks","60% large-cap, 40% small-cap equities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The 60/40 portfolio — 60% equities, 40% bonds — has been the traditional balanced portfolio for decades, balancing growth from equities with stability from bonds."},{"id":"t4-m2-l1-q5","prompt":"Strategic asset allocation (SAA) refers to:","options":["Short-term tactical bets on market direction","The long-run target mix of asset classes in a portfolio","Choosing individual stocks within an equity allocation","Rebalancing a portfolio daily"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SAA is the long-run target split between asset classes — the primary driver of portfolio risk and return. Tactical tilts around the SAA are secondary; getting the SAA right is the most important decision."}]},"t4-m2-l2":{"id":"t4-m2-l2","title":"Real Estate & REITs","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Real estate is one of the oldest asset classes — tangible, income-generating, and inflation-linked. REITs (Real Estate Investment Trusts) made it investable for everyone.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Why invest in property?","body":"Real estate offers three sources of return: rental income, capital appreciation, and inflation protection (rents and property values often rise with inflation). It also has a different return profile to equities — partially driven by local supply/demand dynamics, **interest rates**, and demographics rather than global earnings cycles. These characteristics make it a useful diversifier.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Direct vs indirect investment","body":"You can invest in real estate directly (buying a property) or indirectly via REITs or real estate funds. Direct investment offers control and **leverage** (a mortgage) but is illiquid, capital-intensive, and requires management. REITs let you own a diversified portfolio of properties through a liquid, exchange-traded security — with much lower minimums.","icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"What is a REIT?","body":"A Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) is a company that owns and typically operates income-producing real estate. By law in most countries, REITs must distribute at least 90% of taxable income to shareholders as dividends. This makes them attractive income instruments. REITs can focus on different sectors: office, retail, residential, industrial, data centres, healthcare, and more.","icon":"business"},{"heading":"Key metrics for REITs","body":"Standard earnings (**EPS**) are misleading for REITs because **depreciation** — a major non-cash charge — reduces reported profits but not cash generation. Instead, investors use Funds From Operations (FFO) = **Net Income** + Depreciation − Asset Sale Gains. Price/FFO is the REIT equivalent of P/E. Net Asset Value (NAV) is also key: it compares the REIT's market price to the estimated value of its properties.","icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Risks of real estate","body":"Real estate is sensitive to interest rates — rising rates hurt property values (higher cap rates) and increase borrowing costs. It is also sensitive to economic cycles (vacant offices in a **recession**). **Liquidity** risk is the other key concern: direct property takes weeks or months to sell, and even REIT shares can sell off sharply in a market panic, as seen in 2020 and 2022.","icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"REITs are required to distribute what proportion of taxable income as dividends?","options":["At least 50%","At least 70%","At least 90%","100%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In most jurisdictions, REITs must distribute at least 90% of taxable income as dividends to maintain their tax-advantaged status. This makes them high-yield income instruments."},{"id":"t4-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"Why is standard EPS misleading for valuing REITs?","options":["REITs do not actually have any ordinary shareholders","Large depreciation charges reduce reported profits but not cash","REITs pay no tax at all, so reported EPS has no real meaning","REITs tend to report their results in different currencies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Depreciation — a non-cash accounting charge — heavily reduces REIT net income. Since REITs own long-lived physical assets, depreciation is large. FFO (which adds back depreciation) is the standard alternative metric."},{"id":"t4-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"Which of these is a key advantage of investing in REITs over direct property?","options":["REITs always outperform direct property","REITs offer much higher leverage than a mortgage","REITs are liquid and diversified with low minimum investment","REITs are immune to interest rate changes"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"REITs trade on exchanges like stocks — they are far more liquid than direct property, offer instant diversification across many properties, and can be bought with as little as one share."},{"id":"t4-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"Rising interest rates tend to hurt real estate values because:","options":["Tenants simply pay lower rents whenever interest rates rise","Higher cap rates lower property values, and borrowing costs climb","Local property taxes always rise in step with interest rates","REITs are legally required to cut dividends when rates rise"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Property values are calculated by dividing rental income by a cap rate. When interest rates rise, cap rates rise too, pushing values down. Rising rates also increase borrowing costs, squeezing returns on leveraged property."},{"id":"t4-m2-l2-q5","prompt":"FFO (Funds From Operations) is calculated as:","options":["Net income plus depreciation, less asset-sale gains","Total revenue minus all operating expenses","EBITDA divided by the firm's total assets","Dividend per share times the total share count"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FFO = Net Income + Depreciation − Gains on Asset Sales. By adding back non-cash depreciation and removing one-off gains, FFO approximates the actual cash a REIT generates from its properties."}]},"t4-m2-l3":{"id":"t4-m2-l3","title":"Commodities","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Commodities are physical goods — oil, gold, wheat, copper — that trade on global markets. They behave differently to stocks and bonds, making them valuable portfolio diversifiers, especially during inflationary periods.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Why commodities in a portfolio?","body":"Commodities tend to perform well when other assets struggle. During inflationary periods, when stocks and bonds often fall together, commodity prices typically rise — providing a **hedge**. Their **correlation** to equities is historically low (often around 0.1–0.2), making them a genuine diversifier rather than a second equity bet.","icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Gold — the safe haven","body":"Gold is unique: it has little industrial use but is held globally as a store of value. It tends to rise when real **interest rates** fall (because the **opportunity cost** of holding a zero-yield asset decreases) and during crises when investors seek safety. Gold does not generate income — its entire return is price appreciation, making **valuation** difficult. Institutional investors often hold 5–10% of a portfolio in gold.","icon":"star"},{"heading":"Energy — oil and natural gas","body":"Oil is the most economically significant commodity. Its price reflects supply (OPEC decisions, US shale production) and demand (global **GDP** growth, Chinese industrial activity). Oil prices are highly cyclical and volatile. Energy stocks provide indirect exposure; ETFs tracking oil futures provide more direct exposure, though futures-based products can suffer from \"roll costs\" when futures are in contango.","icon":"flame"},{"heading":"Industrial metals","body":"Copper, aluminium, and iron ore are barometers of global economic activity — they are essential inputs for construction and manufacturing. Copper in particular is called \"Dr Copper\" for its ability to predict economic turning points: rising copper prices signal global growth; falling prices signal slowdowns. The energy transition is creating new demand for copper (electric vehicles, grid infrastructure).","icon":"construct"},{"heading":"How to invest in commodities","body":"You can access commodities through: (1) physical ownership (gold bars, inconvenient for most assets); (2) commodity ETFs or mutual funds (some hold physical metal, others use futures); (3) commodity producer equities (oil companies, miners); (4) commodity futures directly (for sophisticated investors). Each has different risk/return and cost profiles.","icon":"cash"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"Commodities are considered a useful portfolio diversifier primarily because:","options":["They are essentially guaranteed to have positive returns","They have low correlation to stocks and bonds, especially in inflation","They tend to be markedly less volatile than equities","They are explicitly backed by government guarantees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Commodities have historically low correlation to stocks and bonds and tend to outperform during inflationary periods — exactly when other assets struggle. This makes them valuable portfolio diversifiers."},{"id":"t4-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"Gold prices tend to rise when:","options":["The US dollar strengthens significantly","Real interest rates fall or turn negative","Corporate earnings grow strongly","Stock market volatility is at historically low levels"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When real interest rates fall, the opportunity cost of holding gold (which pays no income) decreases, making gold more attractive. Gold also rises during geopolitical crises and periods of financial stress."},{"id":"t4-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"Why is copper sometimes called \"Dr Copper\"?","options":["It was originally discovered by a practising doctor","It has important medical uses in hospital equipment","Its price often signals turning points in global activity","It is the single safest investment during a recession"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Copper is a critical industrial input. Rising prices signal expanding global manufacturing and construction activity; falling prices signal slowdowns. This predictive quality earned it the \"Dr Copper\" nickname."},{"id":"t4-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"The key distinction between a commodity ETF holding physical metal vs one using futures is:","options":["Physical ETFs are always more expensive","Futures-based ETFs can suffer roll costs and contango effects","Physical ETFs are only available to institutional investors","There is no meaningful difference in practice"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Futures contracts must be rolled (sold and replaced with longer-dated ones) as they near expiry. When the futures curve is in contango (futures priced above spot), rolling incurs a cost that erodes returns versus the spot price."},{"id":"t4-m2-l3-q5","prompt":"Which commodity is expected to see increased structural demand from the energy transition?","options":["Coal","Oil","Copper","Natural gas"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Copper is essential for electric vehicles (EVs use 3–4× more copper than combustion engines), renewable energy installations, and grid upgrades. The energy transition is a major structural tailwind for copper demand."}]},"t4-m2-l4":{"id":"t4-m2-l4","title":"Options Basics","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Options are contracts that give the buyer the right — but not the obligation — to buy or sell an asset at a fixed price before a set date. They are used for hedging, income generation, and speculation.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Calls and puts","body":"A **call option** gives the holder the right to BUY an asset at the **strike price**. If the price rises above the strike, the call has value — you can buy cheaply and sell at market price.\n\nA **put option** gives the holder the right to SELL an asset at the strike price. If the price falls below the strike, the put has value — you can sell at the strike even though the market is lower.","icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Strike price, expiry, and premium","body":"Every option has a strike price (the price at which you can buy or sell), an **expiry** date (after which the option is worthless if not exercised), and a premium (what you pay upfront to buy the option). The premium is the maximum you can lose as a buyer. The seller of an option (the \"writer\") receives the premium but takes on the obligation if the buyer exercises.","icon":"calendar"},{"heading":"In the money vs out of the money","body":"A call option is \"in the money\" (ITM) when the stock price is above the strike — it has **intrinsic value**. It is \"**out of the money**\" (OTM) when the stock is below the strike — it has only time value.\n\nA put is ITM when the stock price is below the strike. ATM (at the money) means the stock price equals the strike. OTM options are cheaper but are more likely to expire worthless.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Using options for hedging","body":"Buying put **options** on a stock you hold is like buying insurance. If the stock falls, the put gains value, offsetting your loss. This is a **protective put**. Portfolio managers use index puts (on S&P 500, for example) to **hedge** broad market exposure during uncertain periods. The cost of the put is the insurance premium — a real drag on returns when the hedge is not needed.","icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Covered calls — generating income","body":"If you hold a stock, you can sell a call option against it (a **covered call**). The buyer pays you a premium upfront; in exchange, you cap your upside at the strike price. If the stock stays below the strike, you keep the premium as income. This strategy enhances income in flat or slowly rising markets but limits your gain if the stock rallies strongly.","icon":"cash"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m2-l4-q1","prompt":"A call option gives the holder the right to:","options":["Sell an asset at the strike price before expiry","Buy an asset at the strike price before expiry","Receive dividends from the underlying stock","Borrow shares to sell short"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A call option gives the holder the right (not the obligation) to BUY the underlying asset at the strike price before or at the expiry date. Calls profit when the underlying price rises."},{"id":"t4-m2-l4-q2","prompt":"The maximum loss for an option buyer is:","options":["Unlimited — options can go to zero","The premium paid upfront","The difference between the strike price and the stock price","The full value of the underlying stock"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The most an option buyer can lose is the premium paid. If the option expires worthless (out of the money), the premium is lost in full — but nothing more."},{"id":"t4-m2-l4-q3","prompt":"A put option is \"in the money\" when:","options":["The stock price is above the strike price","The stock price equals the strike price","The stock price is below the strike price","The option has more than 30 days to expiry"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A put option gives the right to SELL at the strike. It has intrinsic value (is \"in the money\") when the stock price is below the strike — you can sell at a higher price than the market."},{"id":"t4-m2-l4-q4","prompt":"Buying a put option on a stock you already own is known as:","options":["A covered call","A protective put","A straddle","A collar"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A protective put involves buying a put on a stock you hold — like insurance. If the stock falls, the put gains value, limiting your downside. You pay the option premium as the cost of this protection."},{"id":"t4-m2-l4-q5","prompt":"A covered call strategy involves:","options":["Buying a call on a stock you do not own","Selling a call on a stock you already hold to generate income","Buying both a call and a put at the same strike","Using call options to cover short positions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A covered call: you hold the stock and sell a call option against it. You collect the premium upfront (income), but cap your upside at the strike. It enhances returns in flat markets but limits gains in strong rallies."}]},"t4-m2-l5":{"id":"t4-m2-l5","title":"Futures & Forwards","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Futures and forwards are contracts to buy or sell an asset at a fixed price on a future date. Unlike options, both parties are obligated to transact. They are the primary tools for hedging risk in commodities, currencies, and rates.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Forwards","body":"A **forward contract** is a private agreement between two parties to buy or sell an asset at a specified price on a future date. There is no exchange involved — the contract is customised and held to **maturity**. This makes forwards flexible but illiquid and subject to **counterparty** risk (the risk that the other party defaults). They are common in currency and commodity markets between large institutions.","icon":"document-text"},{"heading":"Futures","body":"**Futures** are standardised forward contracts traded on regulated exchanges (like the CME in Chicago). Because they are standardised and exchange-traded, they are more liquid and have no counterparty risk — the exchange acts as the counterparty through a clearing house. Futures require posting margin (a security deposit) rather than paying the full price upfront.","icon":"business"},{"heading":"Hedging with futures","body":"An airline worried about rising jet fuel costs might buy oil futures to lock in a price today. If oil rises, the futures gain value, offsetting the higher fuel costs. If oil falls, the airline loses on the futures but pays less for fuel. The futures eliminate uncertainty — the airline knows its fuel cost months ahead. This is the fundamental purpose of derivatives: risk transfer, not speculation.","icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Contango and backwardation","body":"Contango: futures prices are higher than the current spot price. This often happens when the cost of carrying an asset (storage, insurance) is significant. Backwardation: futures prices are below the spot price — often when there is current scarcity or high near-term demand. These curve shapes affect returns for investors who hold futures (ETFs must roll contracts, incurring costs in contango).","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Mark-to-market and margin calls","body":"Futures positions are marked-to-market daily — gains and losses are settled in cash each day. If your position moves against you, you may receive a **margin call**: a demand to post additional collateral or your position is closed. This daily settlement distinguishes futures from forwards and can create **liquidity** demands at exactly the wrong time in a crisis.","icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m2-l5-q1","prompt":"What is the key difference between a forward and a futures contract?","options":["Forwards expire within one year, while futures can run for decades","Futures are standardised and exchange-traded; forwards are private","Forwards are used only for commodities and futures only for currencies","Futures require full payment upfront, whereas forwards use margin"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Futures are standardised contracts traded on regulated exchanges with a clearing house eliminating counterparty risk. Forwards are private, customised agreements between two parties, with counterparty risk."},{"id":"t4-m2-l5-q2","prompt":"An airline buying oil futures to lock in fuel prices is an example of:","options":["Speculation","Arbitrage","Hedging","Short selling"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Hedging uses derivatives to reduce or eliminate an existing business risk. The airline faces the risk of rising fuel costs — buying oil futures locks in a price, removing this uncertainty from its cost base."},{"id":"t4-m2-l5-q3","prompt":"When futures prices are higher than the spot price, the market is said to be in:","options":["Backwardation","Inversion","Contango","Equilibrium"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Contango means futures prices exceed the spot price — typically due to storage and insurance costs, or expectations of future price rises. It creates a \"roll cost\" for futures-based investors."},{"id":"t4-m2-l5-q4","prompt":"Mark-to-market in futures means:","options":["Valuing a portfolio at the end of each quarter","Daily settlement of gains and losses in cash","Matching futures prices to forward prices","Setting futures prices at the market open each day"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Futures positions are marked-to-market daily — the exchange settles gains and losses in cash each day. This prevents losses from accumulating and reduces systemic risk, but can trigger margin calls."},{"id":"t4-m2-l5-q5","prompt":"A margin call in futures trading is:","options":["Simply a request to go and buy more futures contracts","A routine notification that your contracts are about to expire","A demand to post more collateral when the position moves against you","A special fee the exchange charges on unusually large trades"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When a futures position loses value and margin falls below the maintenance level, the broker issues a margin call — a demand to deposit more cash or securities. Failure to meet it results in the position being closed."}]},"t4-m3-l1":{"id":"t4-m3-l1","title":"Hedge Fund Strategies","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Hedge funds manage money for sophisticated investors using flexible strategies — they can go long, go short, use leverage, and trade any asset class. Understanding the major strategies demystifies a world often shrouded in mystique.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Long/short equity","body":"The most common **hedge fund** strategy: buy stocks you expect to rise (long positions) and sell short stocks you expect to fall (short positions). The goal is to profit from the spread between your long and short books while reducing exposure to overall market direction. A fund that is \"market neutral\" has equal dollar long and short exposure, aiming for zero market beta.","icon":"swap-vertical"},{"heading":"Global macro","body":"Global macro funds take directional bets on economies, **interest rates**, currencies, and commodities based on macroeconomic analysis. George Soros's famous bet against the British pound in 1992 is the archetype. These funds can deploy enormous size through liquid derivatives — currencies and rates futures — making them some of the most influential players in financial markets.","icon":"globe"},{"heading":"Merger arbitrage","body":"When a company announces it will acquire another, the target's share price jumps toward (but not all the way to) the offer price. The gap is the \"arb spread\" — compensation for deal-failure risk. Merger arbitrageurs buy the target and typically short the acquirer. If the deal completes, they earn the spread. If the deal breaks, the target typically crashes back to its pre-announcement price.","icon":"git-merge"},{"heading":"Relative value","body":"Relative value strategies exploit mispricings between related securities: buying one and selling another in the same sector when their historical relationship breaks down. Fixed income relative value (like the basis trade) arbitrages small price differences between Treasury bonds and futures. These strategies often use very high **leverage** to amplify small, frequent profits — which can amplify losses dramatically in a crisis.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Hedge fund fees","body":"**Hedge** funds traditionally charge \"2 and 20\": a 2% annual management fee and a 20% performance fee on profits above a hurdle rate. This fee structure rewards performance but also incentivises risk-taking (asymmetric: managers share profits but not losses). Under fee pressure, the industry average has fallen closer to \"1.5 and 17\". High fees mean net returns must be exceptional to justify the structure.","icon":"cash"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m3-l1-q1","prompt":"A long/short equity fund that is \"market neutral\" aims to:","options":["It should only ever hold cash and government bonds","Equal dollar long and short exposure, targeting zero market beta","It should outperform the broad market in every single month","It should avoid using any form of leverage at all"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A market-neutral long/short fund has roughly equal long and short exposure, so overall market moves cancel out. The fund profits from the spread between its winning longs and losing shorts, regardless of market direction."},{"id":"t4-m3-l1-q2","prompt":"Global macro funds primarily base their trades on:","options":["Bottom-up analysis of individual company earnings","Top-down macroeconomic views on economies, rates, and currencies","Exploiting pricing gaps between related securities","Following insider trading signals"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Global macro funds take directional bets on countries, interest rates, currencies, and commodities based on big-picture economic analysis — a top-down, macro-driven approach."},{"id":"t4-m3-l1-q3","prompt":"In merger arbitrage, the \"arb spread\" represents:","options":["The profit margin of the acquiring company","Compensation for the risk that the announced deal might not complete","The fee paid to investment banks for advising on the deal","The difference between the acquirer's and target's P/E ratios"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The arb spread is the gap between the target's market price and the deal price — it exists because there is always a risk the deal breaks before closing. Merger arbitrageurs earn this spread if the deal completes."},{"id":"t4-m3-l1-q4","prompt":"The traditional \"2 and 20\" hedge fund fee structure refers to:","options":["A 2% performance fee combined with a 20% management fee","A 2% annual management fee and 20% of profits as a performance fee","A 2% upfront entry fee together with a 20% exit fee","Investing across 2 asset classes using 20 times leverage"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"\"2 and 20\" = 2% annual management fee (regardless of performance) + 20% of any profits above a hurdle rate. This structure has come under pressure as many funds have struggled to justify the cost."},{"id":"t4-m3-l1-q5","prompt":"Relative value strategies typically require high leverage because:","options":["The individual mispricings they exploit are actually very large","The strategy only tends to work during outright bear markets","The price discrepancies are small, and leverage amplifies frequent profits","Financial regulators specifically require it for this strategy type"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Relative value mispricings are often tiny — a few basis points. High leverage (10×, 20×, or more) amplifies these small gains into meaningful returns. The same leverage amplifies losses when the strategy goes wrong."}]},"t4-m3-l2":{"id":"t4-m3-l2","title":"Private Equity","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Private equity (PE) funds buy stakes in companies not listed on public stock exchanges. By improving operations, expanding businesses, and optimising capital structures, PE aims to generate outsized returns — typically 15–25% annually.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The private equity model","body":"PE firms raise capital from institutional investors (pension funds, endowments) into funds with a fixed life — typically 10 years. In the first 3–5 years, the fund deploys capital by acquiring companies. In the following years, it manages and improves them. By year 8–10, it sells the companies (\"exits\"), returning capital plus profits to investors. PE is inherently illiquid: investors cannot exit before the fund term ends.","icon":"git-network"},{"heading":"Buyouts","body":"The classic PE transaction is a **leveraged buyout** (LBO): the PE fund acquires a company using a mix of **equity** (~30–40%) and debt (60–70%). The debt sits on the company's **balance sheet**. Cash flows generated by the business service the debt. As debt is repaid and the business grows, equity value increases. When sold, the equity return is amplified — the leverage effect.","icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Growth equity","body":"Growth equity PE takes minority stakes in established, profitable companies that need capital to scale — typically technology or healthcare businesses. Unlike LBOs, growth equity involves little or no debt. Returns come from revenue and earnings growth, not financial engineering. Growth equity sits between **venture capital** (early-stage) and buyout (mature companies) on the risk-return spectrum.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Venture capital","body":"Venture capital (VC) funds invest in early-stage startups — pre-revenue or early revenue companies with high growth potential and high failure risk. A VC fund might back 20 companies expecting 15 to fail, 4 to return the investment, and 1 to be a \"home run\" returning 50–100× the investment. VC returns are driven by outliers: missing the best investments matters enormously.","icon":"rocket"},{"heading":"PE performance metrics","body":"PE funds report returns using Internal Rate of Return (**IRR**) and Multiple on Invested Capital (MOIC). IRR annualises the return accounting for timing. A \"2× MOIC\" means you doubled your money. A \"25% IRR over 5 years\" means you earned 25% per year compounded. PE also reports DPI (Distributions to Paid-In Capital) for actual cash returned and TVPI (Total Value to Paid-In) which includes unrealised value.","icon":"calculator"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m3-l2-q1","prompt":"A typical private equity fund has a fixed life of approximately:","options":["2 years","5 years","10 years","25 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"PE funds typically have a 10-year life: ~3–5 years of investing (deployment period) followed by ~5–7 years of managing and exiting portfolio companies."},{"id":"t4-m3-l2-q2","prompt":"In a leveraged buyout (LBO), the acquisition debt sits on:","options":["The PE fund's balance sheet","The acquired company's balance sheet","The institutional investors' balance sheets","The investment bank's balance sheet"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In an LBO, the debt used to finance the acquisition is placed on the acquired company's balance sheet. The company's cash flows service the debt — this is the fundamental structure of leveraged finance."},{"id":"t4-m3-l2-q3","prompt":"What distinguishes growth equity from a classic leveraged buyout?","options":["Growth equity funds invest solely in technology companies","Growth equity takes minority stakes with little debt, relying on growth","Growth equity funds have a 20-year life versus 10 years for LBO funds","Growth equity requires no committed capital from its limited partners"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Growth equity takes minority stakes in established, fast-growing companies with minimal leverage. Returns come from revenue and earnings growth, not financial engineering — unlike the debt-heavy LBO model."},{"id":"t4-m3-l2-q4","prompt":"A 2× MOIC in PE means:","options":["The fund returned 200% annual interest","Investors doubled their invested capital over the fund's life","The fund made 2 investments that returned their capital","Management took 2× their usual fee"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MOIC (Multiple on Invested Capital) = Total Value / Invested Capital. A 2× MOIC means investors got back twice what they put in. This does not account for timing — which is why IRR is also reported."},{"id":"t4-m3-l2-q5","prompt":"In venture capital, why do a small number of \"home run\" investments matter so much?","options":["VCs simply charge much higher fees on their larger wins","Most startups fail; the rare 50–100× winner drives the whole fund return","Regulations require every fund to hold at least one very large position","The big wins are what allow VCs to go and raise their next fund"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"VC is a power-law business: most investments return zero or very little; a small number generate extreme multiples. Missing the fund's best investment can cost you the entire fund's return. This \"hit-driven\" dynamic defines the VC model."}]},"t4-m3-l3":{"id":"t4-m3-l3","title":"Factor Investing","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Factor investing is the idea that certain characteristics — value, momentum, quality, size — systematically generate excess returns over time. It bridges the gap between passive indexing and active stock-picking.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What is a factor?","body":"A factor is a characteristic that systematically explains differences in stock returns. Academic research identified the first factors starting in the 1970s. The key test: a factor must be persistent (works across decades), pervasive (works in different countries and asset classes), robust (works across different ways of measuring it), and have a logical explanation for why the premium should persist.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Value","body":"The value factor: cheap stocks (low P/E, low P/B, high **dividend yield**) tend to outperform expensive stocks over long horizons. The explanation: cheap stocks are often unloved and carry career risk for managers who own them — investors demand extra return as compensation. Value has had extended periods of underperformance (e.g., 2010–2020) and is cyclical; it typically outperforms during economic recoveries.","icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Momentum","body":"Stocks that have performed well over the past 12 months (excluding the most recent month) tend to continue outperforming over the next 3–12 months. Momentum is one of the most robust and pervasive factors — it works across asset classes and countries. Behavioural explanation: investors underreact to information initially and overreact later, creating trends.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Quality and size","body":"Quality: companies with high profitability, low debt, and stable earnings tend to outperform — counterintuitively, boring, predictable businesses often beat exciting but erratic ones. Size: smaller companies have historically outperformed larger ones over long periods (the \"small-cap premium\"), compensating for lower **liquidity** and higher business risk. Both factors are widely recognised and exploited by smart-beta ETFs.","icon":"ribbon"},{"heading":"Factor timing and crowding","body":"Factor premiums can disappear temporarily when too many investors exploit them (crowding). When momentum investors all hold the same stocks and sell simultaneously, the factor crashes. Factor timing — knowing when a factor will outperform — is extremely difficult. Most practitioners recommend holding a diversified basket of factors rather than timing them. Smart-beta ETFs make factor exposure accessible to retail investors for very low cost.","icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m3-l3-q1","prompt":"For a factor to be investable, academic researchers require it to be persistent, pervasive, robust, and:","options":["It is only ever observable within the US stock market itself","Have a logical economic reason why the premium should persist","It reliably generates returns above 20% every single year","It has been formally approved by the relevant regulatory bodies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A genuine factor must have an economic or behavioural rationale — not just a statistical anomaly. Factors without a logical explanation may be data-mining artefacts that disappear out of sample."},{"id":"t4-m3-l3-q2","prompt":"The value factor is associated with owning:","options":["High P/E, fast-growing technology and innovation companies","Stocks showing strong and sustained upward price momentum","Low P/E, low P/B stocks that look cheap versus fundamentals","Large-cap stocks that have the most stable reported earnings"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The value factor buys cheap stocks — low P/E, low price-to-book, high dividend yield. These stocks often underperform in growth-driven bull markets but tend to outperform over full market cycles."},{"id":"t4-m3-l3-q3","prompt":"The momentum factor predicts that:","options":["Past winners reliably revert to being losers over the short term","Stocks with strong 12-month performance tend to keep outperforming for 3–12 months","Volatile stocks outperform stable stocks in every market condition","Large-cap stocks are what drive the returns of smaller companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Momentum: recent strong performers tend to keep outperforming over the medium term. The behavioural explanation is that investors underreact to news initially, creating persistent trends that fade once the broader market catches up."},{"id":"t4-m3-l3-q4","prompt":"The \"quality\" factor in investing favours companies with:","options":["High revenue growth and large R&D budgets","High profitability, low debt, and stable earnings","The largest market capitalisation in their sector","The most diversified business lines"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Quality stocks are characterised by high profitability (ROE, ROA), strong balance sheets (low debt), and consistent earnings — the financial equivalent of boring but reliable businesses that tend to outperform over time."},{"id":"t4-m3-l3-q5","prompt":"Factor \"crowding\" is a risk because:","options":["Having too many factors makes portfolios impossible to manage well","When many investors crowd the same factor, simultaneous unwinding causes sharp losses","Financial regulators may step in and ban the most popular factors","Factors stop working the moment too many academics begin studying them"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When a factor becomes popular, many investors pile into the same stocks. If a shock causes them all to sell simultaneously, the resulting fire-sale can cause the factor to crash sharply — punishing investors at exactly the wrong time."}]},"t4-m3-l4":{"id":"t4-m3-l4","title":"ESG Investing","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"ESG investing integrates environmental, social, and governance factors into investment analysis. It has grown from a niche to a mainstream consideration — but also attracted criticism and controversy.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What ESG means","body":"E (Environmental): a company's **carbon footprint**, water use, waste, and exposure to climate risk. S (Social): labour practices, supply chain standards, data privacy, and community relations. G (Governance): board structure, executive pay, **shareholder rights**, and accounting quality. **ESG** scores are assigned by data providers like MSCI, Sustainalytics, and Bloomberg — but methodologies differ substantially.","icon":"information-circle"},{"heading":"Three approaches","body":"Negative screening: exclude certain industries (tobacco, weapons, fossil fuels). This was the original SRI (socially responsible investing) approach. ESG integration: incorporate ESG scores as one input into standard financial analysis — firms with poor governance or high climate exposure may be riskier than they appear. **Impact investing**: deliberately investing in companies or projects that generate measurable social or environmental benefit alongside financial returns.","icon":"filter"},{"heading":"The governance edge","body":"Of the three pillars, governance (G) has the clearest link to financial performance. Companies with strong governance — independent boards, aligned executive incentives, transparent accounting — tend to avoid the large governance failures (fraud, self-dealing, accounting manipulation) that can destroy shareholder value overnight. Governance has been accepted by mainstream investors since the 1990s; E and S are more recent and more contested.","icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"ESG controversies","body":"Critics argue: (1) ESG data is inconsistent — the same company gets very different scores from different providers; (2) ESG can be an excuse for underperformance or \"**greenwashing**\" — claiming credentials without meaningful substance; (3) excluding \"sin\" sectors may simply mean that other investors hold them, with no real-world impact on those companies' cost of capital; (4) ESG mandates may conflict with a fund manager's fiduciary duty to maximise returns.","icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Performance: ESG vs conventional","body":"Evidence on ESG performance is mixed. Over 2015–2020, many ESG indices outperformed — partly because they were overweight tech and underweight energy, which happened to be a tailwind. After 2022, with energy outperforming, ESG funds lagged. Studies suggest well-governed companies (strong G) may outperform over very long horizons, but the overall ESG premium is not consistently proven across cycles.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m3-l4-q1","prompt":"The \"G\" in ESG refers to:","options":["A focus on broad global diversification","The company's underlying growth metrics","Governance — board, pay and shareholder rights","Exposure to specific geographic risks"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Governance (G) covers how a company is managed and controlled: board independence, executive compensation alignment, shareholder rights, and the quality and transparency of accounting."},{"id":"t4-m3-l4-q2","prompt":"Negative screening in ESG investing involves:","options":["Deliberately excluding all companies with negative earnings","Removing industries like tobacco or weapons from the universe","Selling out of stocks whenever their ESG scores decline","Steering clear of companies attracting negative press coverage"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Negative screening excludes entire industries or companies based on ethical criteria — tobacco, fossil fuels, weapons. It is the original form of socially responsible investing (SRI)."},{"id":"t4-m3-l4-q3","prompt":"Which ESG pillar has the clearest link to financial performance?","options":["Environmental (E) — carbon footprint reduction","Social (S) — labour practices","Governance (G) — board quality and executive alignment","All three are equally linked to performance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Governance has the longest track record linking ESG to financial outcomes. Poor governance — fraud, misaligned incentives, weak oversight — is a major destroyer of shareholder value. Strong governance reduces this risk."},{"id":"t4-m3-l4-q4","prompt":"Greenwashing refers to:","options":["Physically cleaning up pollution from industrial facilities","Claiming strong ESG credentials without real substance behind them","Formally converting a traditional fund to an ESG mandate","The careful process of verifying a company's reported ESG data"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Greenwashing is making misleading environmental or social claims to appear more responsible than reality. It has prompted regulatory scrutiny and requirements for more rigorous ESG disclosure."},{"id":"t4-m3-l4-q5","prompt":"A key criticism of ESG is that excluding \"sin\" sectors may:","options":["They meaningfully increase the level of systemic market risk","Little real impact — other investors simply hold the excluded firms instead","They breach securities laws in the majority of countries","They always lead to underperformance versus a broad index"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If ESG funds exclude tobacco or oil companies, those companies' shares are simply held by other investors instead. The excluded companies' cost of capital may not change at all — questioning whether ESG exclusions achieve their intended real-world impact."}]},"t4-m3-l5":{"id":"t4-m3-l5","title":"Portfolio Attribution","trackId":"t4","trackTitle":"Investment Management","color":"#10B981","premium":false,"intro":"Attribution analysis breaks down a portfolio's returns to explain exactly what drove performance — sector bets, stock selection, timing, or simply riding the market. It is how investment professionals are held accountable.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Alpha vs beta","body":"A **portfolio**'s return can be decomposed into **beta** (market exposure) and **alpha** (excess return above what beta predicts). Beta return = Portfolio Beta × Market Return. Alpha = Actual Return − Beta Return − Risk-Free Rate. A manager generating a 15% return in a year when the market also returned 15% and their beta is 1 has generated zero alpha — they just rode the market.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"The Brinson attribution model","body":"The standard attribution model in institutional investing decomposes active return into three effects: Allocation (did you overweight or underweight the right sectors?), Selection (within your sector weights, did you pick better or worse stocks than the benchmark?), and Interaction (a cross-effect combining allocation and selection). This tells a manager whether outperformance came from being in the right sectors or picking the right stocks.","icon":"grid"},{"heading":"Tracking error","body":"**Tracking error** is the standard deviation of the difference between a portfolio's returns and its benchmark's returns. A portfolio with 0% tracking error is an **index fund** — it perfectly replicates the benchmark. A portfolio with 10% tracking error takes large active bets and can deviate significantly from the benchmark in any year. Higher tracking error means more active risk — and more potential for both out- and underperformance.","icon":"pulse"},{"heading":"Active share","body":"Active share measures how different a portfolio is from its benchmark: 0% = identical to the benchmark; 100% = no overlap at all. A fund with 80% active share holds mostly different stocks from the index. Research suggests that high-active-share funds outperform more consistently than \"closet index\" funds (funds with high fees but low active share — they charge for **active management** but deliver index-like results).","icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Benchmark choice matters","body":"Attribution analysis is only meaningful if the **benchmark** is appropriate. A global equity fund should be measured against a global equity index, not a domestic one. A manager can look excellent against a weak benchmark and mediocre against a tough one. Benchmark selection is a political issue in asset management: managers sometimes choose benchmarks they are likely to beat, which is why sophisticated investors scrutinise benchmark selection carefully.","icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t4-m3-l5-q1","prompt":"A manager returning 15% when the market returned 15% and their portfolio beta is 1.0 has generated:","options":["Strong positive alpha of around 15% a year","Zero alpha — the returns were entirely explained by market exposure","Negative alpha, meaning they really should have returned more","A healthy information ratio of exactly 1.0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Alpha = Actual Return − (Beta × Market Return) − Risk-Free Rate. If beta is 1.0 and the market returned what the portfolio returned, all performance is explained by market exposure. No value was added by the manager."},{"id":"t4-m3-l5-q2","prompt":"In the Brinson attribution model, the \"selection\" effect measures:","options":["Whether the manager was overweight or underweight in each sector","Within each sector weight, whether the manager's stock picks beat the benchmark","The precise timing of when the portfolio was rebalanced","The total return of the portfolio measured relative to cash"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The selection effect isolates stock-picking skill: given the sector weights the manager chose, did their specific stock picks outperform what a passive investor in those sectors would have earned?"},{"id":"t4-m3-l5-q3","prompt":"A portfolio with 0% tracking error is:","options":["A perfectly diversified portfolio","A portfolio that has never had a losing year","An index fund that perfectly replicates its benchmark","A portfolio invested entirely in cash"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Tracking error is the standard deviation of the active return (portfolio minus benchmark). 0% tracking error means the portfolio has returned exactly the benchmark every period — an index fund."},{"id":"t4-m3-l5-q4","prompt":"A \"closet index\" fund is one that:","options":["It invests in small-cap stocks absent from the major indices","Charges active management fees while holding a near-index portfolio","It replicates an index and yet charges no management fee at all","It is available only to clients with over $1 million invested"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A closet indexer has low active share — holding mostly what the benchmark holds — but charges active management fees. Investors get index-like returns while paying active fees. It is widely considered poor value for money."},{"id":"t4-m3-l5-q5","prompt":"Why does benchmark selection matter so much in attribution analysis?","options":["Attribution analysis is legally required in most countries","Managers can appear excellent against a weak benchmark and mediocre against a tough one","The benchmark determines how much the manager is paid","Attribution only works if the benchmark and portfolio have identical risk"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A manager who picks an easy-to-beat benchmark can show persistent \"outperformance\" while adding no real value. Sophisticated investors always scrutinise whether the benchmark chosen is truly appropriate for the fund's mandate."}]},"t5-m1-l1":{"id":"t5-m1-l1","title":"What Is ESG?","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"ESG stands for Environmental, Social, and Governance — a framework for assessing non-financial factors that affect a company's long-term value and risk profile. It has moved from a niche ethical concern to a core consideration in investment analysis, corporate strategy, and regulatory compliance.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Environmental (E)","body":"The E covers how a company interacts with the natural world. Key metrics include **greenhouse gas** (GHG) emissions (Scopes 1, 2, and 3), water consumption, waste generation, land use, and biodiversity impact.\n\nScope 1: Direct emissions from owned operations (e.g. factories).\nScope 2: Indirect emissions from purchased energy.\nScope 3: All other indirect emissions across the supply chain — often the largest and hardest to measure.\n\nCompanies increasingly disclose against frameworks like the TCFD (Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures) and CSRD (Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive in Europe).","bullets":["Scope 1: Direct emissions — owned or controlled sources","Scope 2: Purchased electricity and heat","Scope 3: Value chain emissions — often 70%+ of total footprint"],"icon":"leaf"},{"heading":"Social (S)","body":"The S covers a company's relationships with people — employees, suppliers, customers, and communities. Metrics include employee turnover, pay equity, health and safety incident rates, supply chain labour standards, diversity representation, and community impact.\n\nFor investors, social risk is material: a labour scandal in a supply chain (Nike in the 1990s, Boohoo in 2020) can trigger significant reputational and financial damage. Social factors are harder to quantify than environmental metrics, which makes consistent benchmarking difficult.","bullets":["Employee: pay equity, diversity, health & safety, turnover","Supply chain: labour standards, modern slavery audits","Community: local impact, data privacy, customer wellbeing"],"icon":"people"},{"heading":"Governance (G)","body":"Governance covers how a company is run — board structure, executive pay, **shareholder rights**, audit quality, and anti-corruption controls. G is the oldest pillar: governance failures (Enron, Wirecard, FTX) have always destroyed shareholder value.\n\nKey governance metrics: **board independence** (what % of directors are independent?), CEO-to-median-worker pay ratio, shareholder voting rights (dual-class shares?), and audit committee quality. Strong governance reduces the risk of fraud, mismanagement, and regulatory fines.","bullets":["Board: independence, diversity, tenure, size","Executive: pay structure, pay vs. performance alignment","Controls: audit quality, anti-bribery, whistleblower policies"],"icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Why ESG Matters to Finance","body":"**ESG** is no longer voluntary. In the EU, the SFDR (Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation) and CSRD mandate disclosure. The SEC has proposed climate disclosure rules in the US. Regulators in the UK (FCA) require TCFD alignment for listed companies.\n\nFor investors, ESG data helps identify risks not captured in financial statements: a company with high Scope 3 emissions faces carbon pricing risk; poor governance predicts fraud. For corporates, poor ESG scores increase cost of capital — ESG-screened funds may avoid you, and lenders price in ESG risk in sustainability-linked loans.","icon":"trending-up"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"A manufacturing company burns diesel in its trucks (Scope 1) and buys electricity from the grid (Scope 2). Which category covers the emissions from producing the steel it buys from a supplier?","options":["Scope 1 — it is part of the company's direct operations","Scope 2 — it is an indirect emission","Scope 3 — it is an upstream value chain emission","It is not counted because the company does not own the steel plant"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Supplier emissions fall into Scope 3 (Category 1: Purchased Goods and Services). Scope 3 covers all indirect emissions across the value chain that are not Scope 1 (direct) or Scope 2 (purchased energy). For most companies, Scope 3 represents the majority of their total footprint."},{"id":"t5-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"Which ESG pillar is most directly associated with board independence, CEO pay ratios, and anti-corruption controls?","options":["Environmental — these affect long-term operational sustainability","Social — they relate to how employees are treated","Governance — these are core board and management oversight factors","All three equally — ESG is always assessed together"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Board independence, executive pay, and anti-corruption controls are all Governance (G) factors. Governance is the oldest and most established ESG pillar — corporate scandals like Enron and Wirecard were fundamentally governance failures, not environmental or social ones."},{"id":"t5-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"Why might poor ESG scores increase a company's cost of capital?","options":["ESG scores directly affect accounting profits, which raises the required return","ESG-screened funds may exclude the company, reducing demand for its securities and raising borrowing costs","Regulators set higher tax rates for low-ESG companies","ESG scores affect only a company's public reputation, never any of its underlying financial metrics"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Poor ESG scores can reduce investor demand for a company's equity and bonds as ESG-screened funds avoid it. Less demand for securities means lower prices (higher yields on bonds, lower share price). Lenders also increasingly price ESG risk into loan terms via sustainability-linked loans, directly raising borrowing costs."},{"id":"t5-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"The TCFD framework primarily helps companies disclose what type of information?","options":["Social metrics such as workforce diversity ratios, pay equity gaps, and community engagement levels","Financial performance against sustainability-linked targets","Climate-related financial risks and opportunities, including physical and transition risks","Supply chain labour standards and human rights compliance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) specifically addresses climate risk. It asks companies to disclose physical risks (flooding, heat) and transition risks (carbon pricing, stranded assets). It has been endorsed by regulators worldwide and forms the basis for mandatory disclosure in the UK and EU."}]},"t5-m1-l2":{"id":"t5-m1-l2","title":"ESG Ratings & Data","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"ESG ratings aim to distil complex non-financial information into comparable scores. But unlike credit ratings — where Moody's and S&P largely agree — ESG ratings from different providers can diverge dramatically for the same company. Understanding why matters for anyone using them in investment analysis.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Major Rating Providers","body":"The main **ESG** data and rating providers are MSCI, Sustainalytics (owned by Morningstar), S&P Global (previously RobecoSAM), ISS, and Bloomberg ESG. Each has its own methodology, data sources, and weighting system.\n\nMSCI ESG Ratings use a relative scoring approach within industries — a company is rated AAA to CCC relative to its sector peers. Sustainalytics uses an \"unmanaged risk\" framework measuring how much ESG risk a company leaves unaddressed. The same company can score very differently on each.","bullets":["MSCI: AAA–CCC scale, relative to industry peers","Sustainalytics: \"unmanaged risk\" score (lower = better)","S&P Global: absolute scores from corporate surveys + public data"],"icon":"business"},{"heading":"Why ESG Ratings Diverge","body":"Academic research by Florian Berg, Julian Koelbel, and Roberto Rigobon found ESG rating correlations of only 0.54 across providers — far lower than the 0.99 **correlation** for credit ratings. The divergence comes from three sources:\n\n1. Scope: providers include different metrics in their assessment.\n2. Measurement: the same metric (e.g. carbon emissions) is measured differently — absolute vs intensity-based.\n3. Weights: different providers assign different importance to E, S, and G pillars.\n\nResult: Tesla scores high on MSCI (strong E) but low on Sustainalytics (governance and social concerns). Neither is wrong — they measure different things.","icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Data Limitations","body":"Most ESG data is self-reported by companies in sustainability reports, and not audited with the same rigour as financial accounts. This creates problems:\n\nGreenwashing: companies can present misleading sustainability narratives.\nInconsistency: different reporting frameworks (GRI, SASB, TCFD, ISSB) produce incomparable data.\nGaps: SMEs and private companies have far less ESG data than large-caps.\n\nThe EU's CSRD (effective 2024–2026 in phases) is designed to fix this by mandating standardised, audited sustainability reporting for large companies. The ISSB (International Sustainability Standards Board) is also converging on global standards.","icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Using ESG Data in Practice","body":"Sophisticated investors do not rely on a single ESG score. Instead they:\n\n1. Use raw data rather than composite scores where possible.\n2. Apply their own **materiality** framework — carbon data matters more for energy companies than retailers.\n3. Cross-check multiple providers to spot divergence (high divergence = uncertainty, which may itself be a risk signal).\n4. Supplement with qualitative research — reading sustainability reports, engaging management, tracking controversies.\n\nESG integration in equity analysis means asking: does this ESG factor represent a material financial risk or opportunity that the market has not priced in?","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"ESG ratings from different providers for the same company often diverge significantly. The main academic research found correlations of approximately 0.54. What is the primary cause of this divergence?","options":["Providers are biased towards companies that pay them more for ratings","Differences in scope, measurement methodology, and weighting of ESG pillars across providers","ESG data is too qualitative to be rated consistently","Companies deliberately disclose entirely different underlying data to each of the rating providers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Berg, Koelbel, and Rigobon identified three main sources: scope divergence (different metrics included), measurement divergence (same metric calculated differently), and weight divergence (different importance assigned to E, S, G). This means two providers can both be using rigorous methodology and still produce very different scores for the same company."},{"id":"t5-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"A Sustainalytics \"unmanaged risk\" score of 5 is better or worse than a score of 30?","options":["Worse — higher scores indicate more managed risk","Better — lower unmanaged risk scores indicate less residual ESG risk","They cannot be compared without knowing the sector","The same — Sustainalytics uses an absolute scale independent of the score value"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Sustainalytics scores measure how much ESG risk is left unmanaged. A score of 5 means very little residual ESG risk (well managed); a score of 30 means significant unmanaged risk. Unlike MSCI (which rates relative to peers), Sustainalytics scores are absolute — you want a lower number."},{"id":"t5-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"What is the primary data quality concern with most ESG ratings today?","options":["ESG rating agencies charge too much, discouraging small companies from participating","ESG data is mostly self-reported by companies and not independently audited to financial reporting standards","ESG metrics change too frequently for ratings to remain current","Pension funds are legally prohibited from using unaudited ratings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unlike financial statements, sustainability data is largely self-reported in annual reports or standalone sustainability reports without mandatory third-party audit. This creates greenwashing risk and comparability issues. The EU's CSRD and ISSB standards are designed to address this by standardising and mandating assurance on sustainability disclosures."},{"id":"t5-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"An asset manager wants to integrate ESG into their equity analysis. Which approach is most sophisticated?","options":["Exclude any company with an ESG rating below B from all portfolios","Use a single top-rated ESG data provider and follow their scores precisely","Apply a materiality framework, use raw data where possible, cross-check multiple providers, and supplement with qualitative research","Focus exclusively on the Governance pillar, since it is by far the most easily quantifiable of the three"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Sophisticated ESG integration uses raw data over composite scores, applies sector-specific materiality (carbon matters more for oil companies than banks), cross-checks providers to spot divergence, and supplements with qualitative analysis. Relying on a single provider's composite score ignores the significant methodological variation across the industry."}]},"t5-m1-l3":{"id":"t5-m1-l3","title":"ESG Investing Strategies","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"Not all ESG investing is the same. There is a wide spectrum of approaches, from simply excluding tobacco stocks to actively seeking measurable social impact. Understanding where a fund sits on this spectrum — and what it actually means for returns and risk — is essential for anyone working in asset management.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Negative Screening","body":"Negative screening (also called **exclusion**) is the oldest and simplest **ESG** strategy. It removes companies from the investable universe based on their business activities. Common exclusions: tobacco, weapons, gambling, adult entertainment, fossil fuels.\n\nMany pension funds operate \"ethical\" exclusion lists — the Church of England Pensions Board, for example, excludes tobacco and cluster munitions. Sovereign wealth funds like Norway's GPFG exclude companies for environmental damage, corruption, and human rights violations.\n\nCritique: exclusion reduces the investable universe, which may reduce **diversification**. It also does not change company behaviour — selling a tobacco company's shares just transfers ownership to someone else.","bullets":["Removes companies with specific activities (tobacco, weapons, fossil fuels)","Simple to implement and explain to clients","Does not engage company management or drive behaviour change"],"icon":"close-circle"},{"heading":"ESG Integration","body":"ESG integration means incorporating ESG factors into standard financial analysis — not as a values screen, but as financially material risk factors. An analyst covering an oil major would integrate carbon price risk, stranded asset probability, and regulatory risk into their **DCF** model.\n\nThis is now the dominant approach among large institutional investors. A 2023 CFA Institute survey found 85% of asset managers incorporate ESG factors in their analysis. ESG integration does not necessarily mean excluding any companies — you can integrate ESG and still hold a mining company if the risk is priced in.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Best-in-Class and Norms-Based","body":"Best-in-class screening selects the highest ESG performers within each sector — including otherwise controversial sectors like oil & gas. This maintains sector diversification while rewarding relative ESG leaders.\n\nNorms-based screening excludes companies that violate international standards: the UN Global Compact (covering human rights, labour, environment, anti-corruption), the OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, or ILO Conventions. This approach is principle-based rather than sector-based.","bullets":["Best-in-class: pick ESG leaders within each sector","Norms-based: exclude UN/OECD/ILO standard violators","Both preserve sector diversification better than blanket exclusions"],"icon":"ribbon"},{"heading":"Impact Investing","body":"**Impact investing** seeks measurable, positive social or environmental outcomes alongside financial returns. Unlike ESG integration (which focuses on risk), impact investing targets intentional change: affordable housing, clean energy infrastructure, microfinance.\n\nImpact must be intentional, additional (the outcome would not occur without the investment), and measurable. The GIIN (Global Impact Investing Network) estimates the global impact investing market at over $1.2 trillion.\n\nImpact is often achieved through private markets (**private equity**, infrastructure, private debt) rather than public equities, because ownership stakes allow investors to directly influence company operations and outcomes.","bullets":["Intentional: the impact must be the goal, not a side effect","Additional: investment makes the impact happen","Measurable: outcomes tracked against specific metrics (e.g. CO₂ avoided, homes built)"],"icon":"heart"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"A pension fund sells all its holdings in tobacco companies and invests the proceeds in tech. Which ESG strategy does this describe?","options":["ESG integration — it incorporates ESG risk factors into portfolio analysis","Negative screening — it excludes companies based on their business activities","Impact investing — it redirects capital towards positive social outcomes","Best-in-class — it selects the best ESG performers within tobacco"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Selling companies based on their business activities (tobacco, weapons, fossil fuels) is negative screening or exclusion. ESG integration would be keeping tobacco in the universe but adjusting valuations for regulatory and health liability risk. Impact investing would mean actively seeking positive health outcomes. Best-in-class would select the least harmful tobacco company."},{"id":"t5-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Best-in-class ESG screening selects the top ESG performers within each sector. What is the key advantage over blanket exclusion?","options":["It reliably guarantees materially higher financial returns than any equivalent exclusion-based strategy","It maintains sector diversification while rewarding relative ESG leaders even in controversial sectors","It avoids all companies with environmental risks","It is the only approach endorsed by the UN"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Best-in-class keeps the portfolio sector-diversified — you still hold energy, mining, and other sectors, but only the best ESG performers within each. Blanket exclusion can create sector concentrations and tracking error versus benchmarks. Best-in-class also creates competitive pressure within sectors as companies compete to be selected."},{"id":"t5-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"For an investment to qualify as impact investing, it must satisfy three criteria. Which set is correct?","options":["Sustainable, scalable, and verified by an ESG rating agency","Intentional, additional, and measurable","Environmental, social, and governed by a UN framework","Long-term, illiquid, and targeted at emerging markets"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three criteria for impact investing are: Intentional (the impact must be the stated goal, not incidental), Additional (the investment causes the impact — it would not happen otherwise), and Measurable (outcomes are tracked with specific metrics). These criteria distinguish genuine impact investing from regular ESG integration where positive outcomes may be a byproduct."},{"id":"t5-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"ESG integration in equity analysis primarily differs from negative screening in that it:","options":["Excludes more companies, resulting in a smaller investable universe","Incorporates ESG factors as financially material risks into valuation — without necessarily excluding any companies","Is only used by passive index funds, not active managers","Focuses exclusively on the Governance pillar because it happens to be the most quantifiable of the three"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"ESG integration does not require exclusion. It means an analyst factors ESG risks into their DCF, credit analysis, or portfolio risk model — carbon pricing risk into an oil major's valuation, for instance. You can integrate ESG and still hold a tobacco company if you believe the risks are already priced into the stock. This is fundamentally different from a screening approach that removes companies based on their activities."}]},"t5-m2-l1":{"id":"t5-m2-l1","title":"Green & Sustainability Bonds","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"Green bonds, social bonds, and sustainability-linked bonds are the fastest-growing segment of the fixed income market. They allow issuers to finance specific environmental or social projects — and signal commitment to sustainability. Over $900 billion in labelled sustainable bonds were issued globally in 2023.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What Is a Green Bond?","body":"A **green bond** is a standard debt instrument where the proceeds are exclusively used for eligible green projects: renewable energy, energy efficiency, clean transport, sustainable water management, or climate adaptation. The issuer must report on how the proceeds are used, providing transparency to investors.\n\nGreen bonds trade and price like conventional bonds from the same issuer. The \"greenium\" — the yield premium investors accept for green bonds over identical conventional bonds — is typically small (2-5 **basis points**) but real, reflecting investor demand from ESG-mandated funds.\n\nFirst issued by the European Investment Bank in 2007, the market has grown to over $2.5 trillion in cumulative issuance.","bullets":["Proceeds ring-fenced for eligible green projects","Issuer must report on project allocation and impact","Greenium: investors accept ~2-5bps lower yield for green bonds"],"icon":"leaf"},{"heading":"Social and Sustainability Bonds","body":"Social bonds finance projects with positive social outcomes: affordable housing, access to healthcare, education, food security, and employment generation in underserved communities. Heavily issued during COVID-19 as governments and development banks funded healthcare infrastructure.\n\nSustainability bonds combine both green and social use-of-proceeds — proceeds can fund either environmental or social projects. The ICMA (International Capital Market Association) sets the voluntary principles for all three: GBP (Green Bond Principles), SBP (Social Bond Principles), and SBP (Sustainability Bond Guidelines).","icon":"people"},{"heading":"Sustainability-Linked Bonds","body":"Sustainability-linked bonds (SLBs) are different from use-of-proceeds bonds. The proceeds can be used for any corporate purpose, but the **coupon** is tied to the issuer meeting specific Sustainability Performance Targets (SPTs) — if the company misses its target, the coupon steps up.\n\nExample: Enel issued SLBs where the coupon increases by 25bps if they fail to achieve 55% renewable capacity by 2025. This directly links the cost of capital to **ESG** performance.\n\nCritique: targets must be ambitious and independently verified. Weak targets (easily met regardless of effort) have prompted **greenwashing** concerns.","bullets":["Proceeds unrestricted — unlike green bonds","Coupon steps up if Sustainability Performance Targets are missed","Directly links cost of debt to ESG performance"],"icon":"link"},{"heading":"The Loan Market: SLLs","body":"Sustainability-linked loans (SLLs) apply the same concept to bank lending. The loan margin adjusts based on ESG KPIs — typically a margin discount if targets are met and a margin step-up if missed. Common KPIs include GHG reduction targets, renewable energy use, and gender diversity metrics.\n\nLMA (Loan Market Association) Green Loan Principles and Sustainability Linked Loan Principles provide the framework. SLLs have grown rapidly as corporates find it easier to align with sustainability goals through loans (more flexible terms, no public bond issuance required).","icon":"card"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"What fundamentally distinguishes a green bond from a sustainability-linked bond (SLB)?","options":["Green bonds are only ever issued by national governments, whereas SLBs are only issued by private corporations","Green bonds have proceeds ring-fenced for green projects; SLBs can use proceeds for any purpose but tie the coupon to ESG targets","Green bonds carry a higher credit rating than SLBs from the same issuer","SLBs are only available to investment-grade companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The key distinction is proceeds vs. structure. Green bonds require proceeds to fund specific eligible green projects (use-of-proceeds bond). SLBs have no restriction on how proceeds are used, but the coupon rate is linked to sustainability performance targets — the coupon steps up if targets are missed. This makes SLBs more flexible but also more susceptible to weak target-setting."},{"id":"t5-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"The \"greenium\" refers to what in the green bond market?","options":["The extra fee green bond verifiers charge for third-party review","The small yield premium (lower yield) investors accept on green bonds vs. equivalent conventional bonds","The higher credit spread on green bonds due to their complexity","The premium price that buyers pay for verified carbon offsets traded in the voluntary offset market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The greenium is the yield discount investors accept for green bonds — they earn slightly less (typically 2-5 basis points) than on a conventional bond from the same issuer with the same maturity and seniority. This reflects strong demand from ESG-mandated investors who need labelled green assets for their portfolios, effectively lowering the issuer's borrowing cost for green projects."},{"id":"t5-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"A company issues a sustainability-linked loan (SLL) with a margin that falls by 5bps if it reduces carbon intensity by 30% by 2026. If the company misses the target, what happens?","options":["The loan is cancelled and must be repaid immediately","The margin steps up — the company pays more interest as a penalty","Nothing changes — SLL targets are aspirational, not contractual","The company must issue a green bond to compensate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In a sustainability-linked loan, the margin adjusts based on ESG performance. If the company meets its KPI, it gets a margin discount (cheaper borrowing). If it misses, the margin steps up — it pays more interest. This creates a direct financial incentive tied to sustainability performance. The terms are contractual, not aspirational."},{"id":"t5-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"Which bond type would a government issue to fund new schools, hospitals and affordable housing?","options":["Green bond — schools and hospitals are environmental infrastructure","Sustainability-linked bond — the government sets emissions reduction targets","Social bond — the projects have defined positive social outcomes","Covered bond — they are backed by the government's tax revenues"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Schools, hospitals, and affordable housing are social projects — they target positive social outcomes (access to education, healthcare, housing). A social bond finances these via ring-fenced proceeds. A green bond would fund environmental projects (wind farms, EV charging). A sustainability-linked bond would tie a coupon to government ESG targets but without restricting how proceeds are used."}]},"t5-m2-l2":{"id":"t5-m2-l2","title":"Carbon Markets","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"Carbon markets are the financial plumbing of climate policy. They put a price on greenhouse gas emissions — creating economic incentives to reduce them. Understanding carbon markets is increasingly essential in finance: from commodity trading to corporate treasury to investment banking on energy transition deals.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Compliance Carbon Markets","body":"Compliance markets are government-mandated cap-and-trade systems. A cap is set on total emissions in a sector; participants receive or buy allowances; at year-end they must surrender enough allowances to cover their emissions.\n\nThe EU Emissions Trading System (EU ETS) is the world's largest, covering ~40% of EU **greenhouse gas** emissions across power, industry, and aviation. Carbon allowance prices (EUAs — EU Allowances) exceeded €100/tonne CO₂ in 2023.\n\nIf a company emits less than its allowances, it can sell the surplus. If it emits more, it buys allowances — the price of which creates the incentive to reduce emissions. The cap tightens over time, driving the price higher and making abatement more attractive.","bullets":["Cap sets total emissions allowed in the system","Companies that pollute less can sell surplus allowances","Price signal creates financial incentive to reduce emissions"],"icon":"document"},{"heading":"Voluntary Carbon Markets","body":"Voluntary carbon markets (VCMs) operate outside government mandates. Companies buy carbon credits to offset their emissions towards net-zero commitments — not because a regulator requires it.\n\nCarbon credits represent one tonne of CO₂ avoided or removed — e.g. protecting a rainforest (avoided deforestation) or building a wind farm in place of a coal plant. Standards bodies like Verra (VCS) and Gold Standard certify credits.\n\nVCM prices vary hugely by project type and quality: REDD+ (forestry) credits trade at $5-15/tonne; high-quality removal credits (direct air capture, biochar) can exceed $200/tonne. The VCM has faced criticism over credit quality and permanence — a forest protected today can still burn tomorrow.","bullets":["Voluntary — used by companies for net-zero claims, not regulatory compliance","Additionality: project must reduce emissions that would otherwise have occurred","Verra VCS and Gold Standard are the main certification bodies"],"icon":"leaf"},{"heading":"Carbon Price and Investment Decisions","body":"A rising carbon price changes investment economics. At €100/tonne CO₂, a gas power plant's fuel costs look very different from a wind farm's. Banks and investors model carbon price scenarios into project finance, M&A **due diligence**, and **DCF** analysis.\n\nShadow carbon pricing: many companies use an internal carbon price in **capex** decisions even when no external scheme applies — setting a price of, say, £50/tonne on internal projects to pre-position for future regulation. Shell uses $40/tonne for near-term projects and $100+/tonne for long-term ones.\n\nFor investors, high carbon prices create stranded asset risk in fossil fuel-heavy portfolios: coal plants, oil fields, and gas infrastructure may become economically unviable before their accounting useful life.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"The Net Zero Target Landscape","body":"**Net zero** means balancing greenhouse gas emissions with removals — so the net amount added to the atmosphere is zero. Over 140 countries and thousands of companies have set net-zero targets, mostly for 2050.\n\nSBTi (Science Based Targets initiative) certifies company net-zero targets as aligned with 1.5°C pathway. TCFD requires companies to disclose how their business model is consistent with a net-zero transition.\n\nKey distinction: carbon neutral vs. net zero. Carbon neutral can be achieved through offsets without reducing emissions. Net zero (under SBTi) requires deep emission cuts (90%+) with only residual emissions offset. The terminology matters in investment analysis and due diligence.","bullets":["Net zero requires ~90% actual emission cuts + offsets for residual only","SBTi certifies targets as science-aligned","Stranded assets: fossil fuel assets unviable under net-zero scenarios"],"icon":"earth"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"Under the EU ETS cap-and-trade system, a steel company reduces its emissions below its allowance allocation. What does it do with the surplus allowances?","options":["Returns them to the regulator for a rebate","Cancels them — they cannot be traded","Sells them to other companies that have exceeded their cap","Automatically rolls them forward to next year's compliance period"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In a cap-and-trade system, surplus allowances can be sold to other companies that have exceeded their cap. This creates the financial incentive to reduce emissions — every tonne avoided generates a saleable allowance. The ability to trade allowances sets an efficient market price for carbon across the economy."},{"id":"t5-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"A company buys voluntary carbon credits to offset its remaining Scope 1 emissions. Which criterion must the underlying project meet to make these credits credible?","options":["The project must be located in an emerging market country","Additionality — the emission reduction must not have occurred without the carbon credit finance","The credits must be certified by a government regulator, not a private standard","Credits must come from direct air capture technology only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Additionality is the core quality criterion: the emissions reduction (or removal) must be additional to what would have happened anyway. A wind farm built in a region where wind is already commercially viable without carbon finance would not be additional — those emissions would have been avoided regardless. Non-additional credits do not represent real climate benefit."},{"id":"t5-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"What is a \"shadow carbon price\" in corporate decision-making?","options":["The black-market price for unregistered carbon credits","An internal price companies apply to capex decisions to pre-position for future carbon regulation","The price differential between compliance and voluntary carbon markets","The official carbon-credit price set by the United Nations for international compliance markets each year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Shadow carbon pricing is an internal mechanism where companies assign a hypothetical carbon cost to investment decisions even where no external carbon price applies. It helps identify future regulatory risk — a project that is profitable at £0/tonne carbon may not be at £100/tonne. Companies like Shell and BP use shadow prices to stress-test long-lived capital investments against climate scenarios."},{"id":"t5-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"What is the key distinction between a \"carbon neutral\" and \"net zero\" claim?","options":["They are effectively identical concepts — both require exactly the same level of actual emission reductions","Carbon neutral is government-regulated; net zero is voluntary","Carbon neutral can be achieved primarily through offsets; net zero (SBTi) requires deep cuts (~90%) with only residual emissions offset","Net zero applies only to Scope 1 emissions; carbon neutral covers all three scopes"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Carbon neutral can technically be achieved by offsetting all current emissions without reducing them at all — simply buying enough credits. Net zero under SBTi standards requires deep emission reductions of ~90% across value chain emissions, with offsets only for truly unavoidable residual emissions. This distinction is critical in evaluating corporate climate commitments for greenwashing risk."}]},"t5-m2-l3":{"id":"t5-m2-l3","title":"ESG Regulation","trackId":"t5","trackTitle":"ESG & Sustainable Finance","color":"#1FA06A","premium":false,"intro":"ESG regulation has moved fast — particularly in the EU. What was voluntary is becoming mandatory. Understanding the main regulatory frameworks is essential whether you work in asset management, investment banking, corporate treasury, or advisory. Clients, funds, and counterparties are asking these questions.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"EU SFDR: Funds Classification","body":"The Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) requires EU asset managers to classify their funds:\n\nArticle 6: No sustainability characteristics — standard fund with no **ESG** integration.\nArticle 8: Promotes environmental or social characteristics (alongside financial goals).\nArticle 9: Has sustainable investment as its objective — the strictest category.\n\nGreenwashing risk is high: many funds were initially labelled Article 9 and then downgraded to Article 8 when regulators tightened definitions. From 2023, Article 9 funds must have \"sustainable investments\" as defined by the EU taxonomy — a narrower standard than many initially applied.","bullets":["Article 6: no ESG claims","Article 8: promotes E/S characteristics (most common)","Article 9: has sustainable investment as primary objective"],"icon":"folder"},{"heading":"EU Taxonomy: What Counts as Green?","body":"The EU Taxonomy is a classification system defining which economic activities are environmentally sustainable. An activity qualifies if it substantially contributes to at least one of six environmental objectives (climate mitigation, adaptation, water, circular economy, pollution, biodiversity) and does no significant harm (DNSH) to the others, while meeting minimum social safeguards.\n\nOnly Taxonomy-aligned activities can count towards Article 9 fund claims. Companies must report what percentage of their revenue, **capex**, and opex is Taxonomy-eligible and Taxonomy-aligned. This creates a standardised, comparable green metric across EU companies.","bullets":["Substantial contribution to one of six environmental objectives","Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) to the other five","Minimum social safeguards (ILO core conventions)"],"icon":"checkmark-circle"},{"heading":"CSRD: Mandatory Sustainability Reporting","body":"The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) expands the previous NFRD (Non-Financial Reporting Directive) to cover ~50,000 EU companies (vs. ~11,000 before). It requires:\n\n— Reporting under the European Sustainability Reporting Standards (ESRS)\n— **Double materiality**: both how ESG issues affect the company (financial **materiality**) AND how the company affects society/environment (impact materiality)\n— Third-party assurance (audit) of sustainability data\n— Digital XBRL tagging for machine-readability\n\nPhased in from 2024 (large listed companies) through 2026-2028 (smaller companies, non-EU companies with significant EU revenue). The CSRD is the most comprehensive sustainability reporting regime globally.","icon":"document-text"},{"heading":"TCFD and Global Standards","body":"The TCFD (Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures) framework — developed by the FSB — asks companies to disclose governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics/targets around climate risk. It distinguishes **physical risk** (flooding, heat) and **transition risk** (policy change, technology shift, **stranded assets**).\n\nTCFD is now mandatory for large UK-listed companies and financial services firms. The ISSB (International Sustainability Standards Board) has built IFRS S1 and S2 on the TCFD framework, aiming for a global baseline. Australia, Canada, Japan, and others are implementing ISSB standards, converging on interoperability with the EU's CSRD.","bullets":["TCFD: physical risk (weather) + transition risk (policy, technology)","ISSB IFRS S1/S2: global baseline built on TCFD, now mandatory in many jurisdictions","Double materiality (CSRD) vs. single materiality (ISSB) is the key difference"],"icon":"globe"}],"questions":[{"id":"t5-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"An EU fund manager classifies their fund as SFDR Article 9. What does this mean for the fund?","options":["The fund excludes all fossil fuel companies","The fund has sustainable investment as its primary objective and must meet the strictest SFDR disclosure requirements","The fund promotes ESG characteristics alongside financial returns but is not exclusively sustainable","The fund has no sustainability characteristics and does not make ESG claims"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Article 9 (also called \"dark green\") funds have sustainable investment as their primary objective. They must demonstrate that their investments qualify as sustainable under SFDR and EU Taxonomy definitions. Article 8 (\"light green\") promotes E/S characteristics. Article 6 makes no ESG claims. Many funds downgraded from Article 9 to Article 8 when regulators tightened definitions in 2022-23."},{"id":"t5-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"The EU Taxonomy defines \"environmentally sustainable\" economic activities. For an activity to qualify, it must meet how many criteria?","options":["One: it must substantially contribute to any environmental objective","Two: substantial contribution to one objective AND do no significant harm to the other five AND meet social safeguards","Three: it must score positively on climate, water, and circular economy","Six: the activity must contribute meaningfully to all six environmental objectives at the same time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"EU Taxonomy eligibility requires: (1) substantial contribution to at least one of six environmental objectives, (2) Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) to the remaining five objectives, and (3) compliance with minimum social safeguards. All three criteria must be met. An activity that benefits climate but significantly harms biodiversity would not qualify."},{"id":"t5-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"What is \"double materiality\" as required by the EU's CSRD?","options":["Companies must report their sustainability data twice — once for the regulators and once for the public","Companies must assess both how ESG issues affect them financially AND how their operations affect society and the environment","Materiality must be assessed by two independent auditors simultaneously","Companies must double-check all ESG data before publishing it"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Double materiality captures two perspectives. Outside-in (financial materiality): how do sustainability risks affect the company's financial performance? Inside-out (impact materiality): how does the company affect the environment and society? The CSRD requires both. The ISSB (IFRS S1/S2) only requires outside-in (single materiality), which is a key difference between the EU and global standards."},{"id":"t5-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"Under TCFD, what is the difference between physical risk and transition risk from climate change?","options":["Physical risk applies only to energy companies and their assets; transition risk applies only to banks and financial firms","Physical risk relates to weather events and climate impacts; transition risk relates to policy, technology, and market shifts during the move to net zero","Physical risk can be modelled; transition risk is unquantifiable","Physical risk is short-term; transition risk occurs only after 2050"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"TCFD distinguishes: Physical risk — the financial impact of climate change itself (acute: floods, storms; chronic: sea level rise, heat). Transition risk — the financial impact of moving to a low-carbon economy (policy: carbon taxes, regulations; technology: renewables displacing fossil fuels; market: shifting demand). Both can be material for any company; the mix depends on sector and geography."}]},"t6-m1-l1":{"id":"t6-m1-l1","title":"Calls & Puts","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"An option gives its holder the right — but not the obligation — to buy or sell an underlying asset at a fixed price before or on a specific date. Understanding calls and puts is foundational to derivatives, structured products, corporate finance (real options), and risk management.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Call Option","body":"A **call option** gives the buyer the right to purchase the underlying asset at the **strike price** (K) on or before expiry. The buyer pays a premium upfront for this right.\n\nIf the asset price (S) rises above K at expiry, the call is \"**in the money**\" — the holder exercises and profits: S − K − premium paid. If S stays below K, the option expires worthless — maximum loss is the premium.\n\nThe seller (writer) of a call receives the premium but faces unlimited upside risk if the stock soars — they must sell at K regardless of the market price.","bullets":["Long call: right to buy at K. Profit if S > K + premium","Max loss for buyer: premium paid","Max loss for seller: theoretically unlimited"],"icon":"arrow-up-circle"},{"heading":"The Put Option","body":"A **put option** gives the buyer the right to sell the underlying at the strike price K. Puts profit when the asset price falls.\n\nAt expiry, if S < K, the put is in the money: profit = K − S − premium. If S rises above K, the put expires worthless — the buyer loses only the premium.\n\nPuts are the classic hedge: a portfolio manager holding equities can buy puts to cap downside. If the market falls 30%, the put pays out; if it rises, the portfolio gains and only the small premium is lost.","bullets":["Long put: right to sell at K. Profit if S < K − premium","Max loss for buyer: premium paid","Max gain for buyer: K − premium (stock can only fall to zero)"],"icon":"arrow-down-circle"},{"heading":"Moneyness, Expiry & Style","body":"**Moneyness** describes where the current price is relative to the **strike**:\n\nIn the money (ITM): exercising now would be profitable (call: S > K; put: S < K).\nAt the money (ATM): S ≈ K.\nOut of the money (OTM): exercising would not be profitable.\n\nExpiry style:\nEuropean: exercisable only at **expiry**. Easier to model (Black-Scholes).\nAmerican: exercisable any time before expiry — worth at least as much as European equivalent.\n\nMost equity index options are European; most single-stock options in the US are American.","bullets":["ITM call: S > K (would profit if exercised now)","OTM put: S > K (no benefit to exercise)","American ≥ European in value — the early-exercise right has value"],"icon":"time"},{"heading":"Intrinsic Value vs Time Value","body":"Option price (premium) = **Intrinsic Value** + **Time Value**.\n\nIntrinsic value: the immediate exercise value. For a call: max(S − K, 0). For a put: max(K − S, 0). An OTM option has zero intrinsic value.\n\nTime value: the additional premium above intrinsic value, reflecting the probability that the option moves further into the money before expiry. Time value decays to zero at expiry — this decay accelerates as expiry approaches (captured by the Greek \"theta\"). An ATM option has no intrinsic value but significant time value — it could go either way before expiry.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"You buy a call option on a stock with strike £50, paying a £3 premium. At expiry, the stock trades at £58. What is your profit per share?","options":["£8","£5","£3","−£3"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The call is in the money: intrinsic value = £58 − £50 = £8. Subtract the premium paid: £8 − £3 = £5 profit per share. If the stock had finished below £50, the call would expire worthless and you would lose the full £3 premium."},{"id":"t6-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"A portfolio manager holds UK equities and is worried about a market sell-off over the next three months. Which option strategy provides direct downside protection?","options":["Sell call options on the portfolio — the premium provides income","Buy put options on the index — they profit if the market falls","Buy call options — they profit if the market rises","Sell put options — the premium cushions small declines"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buying put options is the classic portfolio hedge. If the market falls below the strike price, the puts gain in value, offsetting losses in the equity portfolio. The cost is the put premium — a form of insurance. Selling calls generates income but does not protect against large declines."},{"id":"t6-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"An option with no intrinsic value still trades at a premium. What explains this?","options":["The option is mispriced and should be arbitraged away","Time value — there is still a chance the option moves into the money before expiry","The premium always equals the strike price minus the spot price","Market makers simply add a fixed, standard spread to every option regardless of its intrinsic value"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An option's price = intrinsic value + time value. An out-of-the-money option has zero intrinsic value but still has time value, which reflects the possibility that the underlying moves in the option's favour before expiry. This time value decays to zero at expiry (theta decay), which is why OTM options become worthless if the expected move does not materialise."},{"id":"t6-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"What is the maximum loss for a buyer of a put option?","options":["Unlimited — if the stock rises sharply the losses are uncapped","The strike price minus the current stock price","The premium paid upfront","The full value of the underlying asset"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The buyer of any option (call or put) can only lose the premium paid — they are not obligated to exercise. If a put is out of the money at expiry, it expires worthless and the buyer's loss is exactly the premium. This asymmetry (capped downside, significant upside) is what makes buying options attractive as hedges and speculative instruments."}]},"t6-m1-l2":{"id":"t6-m1-l2","title":"Options Strategies","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"Buying a single call or put is the simplest option trade. But practitioners combine options to express precise directional, volatility, or income views with defined risk profiles. Understanding the major strategies — covered call, protective put, spread, straddle — is essential for anyone working in derivatives, structuring, or risk management.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Covered Call","body":"A **covered call** involves holding a long position in the **underlying** stock and selling a **call option** against it. The sold call generates premium income — but caps upside above the strike.\n\nPayoff: if the stock stays below K, the call expires worthless and the investor keeps the premium. If the stock rises above K, it gets \"called away\" at K — the investor misses gains above K but keeps the premium.\n\nIdeal for: investors who hold stock and want to generate income in a flat or mildly bullish market, accepting that they will cap their upside.","bullets":["Long stock + short call = covered call","Income: call premium received upfront","Upside capped at strike price + premium received"],"icon":"shield"},{"heading":"Bull Call Spread","body":"A **bull call spread** combines a long call at a lower **strike** (K1) and a short call at a higher strike (K2), with the same **expiry**. The short call reduces the cost of the long call at the expense of capping the upside.\n\nMax profit: K2 − K1 − net premium paid (achieved if S ≥ K2 at expiry).\nMax loss: net premium paid (if S ≤ K1 at expiry).\n\nWhy use it over a naked long call? A spread is cheaper (the short call offsets some premium cost) and defines the exact risk/reward. It is the standard way to take a moderately bullish view with limited risk.","bullets":["Long call at K1 + short call at K2 (K2 > K1)","Net cost: premium of K1 call minus premium of K2 call","Max profit achieved when S ≥ K2 at expiry"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Straddle & Strangle","body":"A straddle buys a call and a put at the same strike and expiry. The trader profits if the underlying moves significantly in either direction — they are buying volatility.\n\nMax loss: total premium paid (if the stock barely moves and both **options** expire near worthless).\nBreak-even: stock must move by more than the total premium in either direction.\n\nA strangle is similar but uses different strikes (OTM call and OTM put), making it cheaper but requiring a larger move to profit. Straddles and strangles are used around earnings announcements, **central bank** decisions, or any event expected to cause a large price move.","bullets":["Straddle: long call + long put at same strike — profits from large moves either way","Strangle: OTM call + OTM put — cheaper, needs bigger move to profit","Both are \"long volatility\" trades — they profit from high realised vol"],"icon":"swap-vertical"},{"heading":"Protective Put","body":"A **protective put** combines a long stock position with a long put at a chosen strike. It is portfolio insurance: the put floors the downside at K.\n\nPayoff: if the stock falls below K, the put pays out, limiting the loss to K − purchase price − premium. If the stock rises, the investor participates fully in the upside, minus the put premium cost.\n\nThe cost of protection is the put premium — analogous to an insurance premium. Higher strikes (deeper ITM put) give more protection but cost more. Lower strikes (OTM put) are cheaper but give less protection.","bullets":["Long stock + long put = protective put","Downside floored at strike K minus put premium","Full upside participation, minus the cost of protection"],"icon":"umbrella"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"An investor holds 1,000 shares of a company and sells a call option with strike £45. The stock currently trades at £42. What is the primary risk of this covered call strategy?","options":["Unlimited downside if the stock crashes to zero","The stock is called away above £45, missing gains beyond that level","The call premium received offsets the full downside risk of holding shares","The investor must buy more shares if the call is exercised against them"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In a covered call, the key risk is opportunity cost: if the stock surges above the strike (say to £60), the investor must deliver shares at £45. They miss the additional £15 per share of upside. The premium received provides some buffer on the downside but does not protect against a large stock decline — the investor still holds all the downside risk of the shares."},{"id":"t6-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"An options trader buys a call at strike 100 for £4 and sells a call at strike 110 for £1.50, same expiry. What is the maximum profit on this bull call spread?","options":["£10","£7.50","£2.50","£5"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Max profit = (K2 − K1) − net premium paid. Net premium = £4 − £1.50 = £2.50. Max spread value = 110 − 100 = £10. Max profit = £10 − £2.50 = £7.50, achieved if the stock finishes at or above 110 at expiry. Max loss = the net premium of £2.50 (if the stock finishes at or below 100)."},{"id":"t6-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"A trader expects a major pharmaceutical company to announce a drug trial result next week but doesn't know if it will be positive or negative. Which strategy best captures this view?","options":["Buy a call — the result will be positive and the stock will rise","Sell a put — collect premium while waiting for the announcement","Buy a straddle — profit from a large move in either direction","Buy a bull call spread — limit cost while targeting moderate upside"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A straddle (long call + long put at the same strike) profits when the stock moves significantly in either direction — exactly the situation when an outcome is binary but directionally uncertain. Drug trial results often cause +30% or −60% moves. The straddle loses only the total premium if the stock barely moves, but makes substantial gains if the move is large enough."},{"id":"t6-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"What is the key difference between a straddle and a strangle?","options":["A straddle is built from call options only, whereas a strangle is built purely from put options","A straddle shares one strike; a strangle uses OTM strikes — cheaper but needs a bigger move","A strangle is always more expensive than a straddle","A straddle is European-style; a strangle is American-style"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A straddle uses at-the-money strikes for both the call and put — both legs have intrinsic value if the stock moves. A strangle uses out-of-the-money strikes — cheaper to buy, but the stock must move further before either option gains intrinsic value. Both profit from large moves in either direction; the strangle is the lower-cost, higher-breakeven version."}]},"t6-m1-l3":{"id":"t6-m1-l3","title":"The Greeks","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"The Greeks measure how an option's price changes in response to changes in key variables. They are essential tools for options traders and risk managers — allowing them to quantify and hedge exposure to price moves, time decay, and volatility changes. Knowing the Greeks is expected in any derivatives or structuring interview.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Delta (Δ) — Price Sensitivity","body":"**Delta** measures how much an option's price changes for a £1 move in the **underlying** asset.\n\nCall delta: ranges from 0 to +1. A delta of 0.6 means the call gains £0.60 for every £1 rise in the stock.\nPut delta: ranges from −1 to 0. A delta of −0.4 means the put gains £0.40 for every £1 fall.\n\nAt-the-money options have delta ≈ 0.5 (calls) or −0.5 (puts). Deep ITM options approach delta ±1 (move almost one-for-one with the stock). Deep OTM options approach 0.\n\nDelta also approximates the probability that an option expires **in the money** — a 0.6 delta call has roughly a 60% chance of finishing ITM.","bullets":["Call delta: 0 to +1 (positive — profits from rising stock)","Put delta: −1 to 0 (negative — profits from falling stock)","ATM option delta ≈ ±0.5; deep ITM ≈ ±1; deep OTM ≈ 0"],"icon":"arrow-forward"},{"heading":"Gamma (Γ) — Rate of Change of Delta","body":"Gamma measures how delta changes as the underlying price moves — the second derivative of option price with respect to the underlying.\n\nHigh gamma means delta changes rapidly — the option's sensitivity is unstable and must be frequently rehedged. Gamma is highest for ATM options near **expiry**.\n\nFor option buyers: gamma is positive — as the stock moves in your favour, delta increases and you make more than a linear bet. For option sellers: gamma is negative — as the stock moves against you, the loss accelerates.\n\nA trader who is \"long gamma\" benefits from large moves (either direction) and pays theta (time decay). \"Short gamma\" means collecting premium but being hurt by large moves.","bullets":["Gamma: rate of change of delta per £1 move","Highest for ATM options near expiry","Long gamma = benefits from large moves; short gamma = hurt by them"],"icon":"pulse"},{"heading":"Vega (ν) — Volatility Sensitivity","body":"Vega measures how much an option's price changes for a 1% change in implied **volatility**. Vega is always positive for long options (calls and puts) — higher volatility makes options more valuable because there is more chance of a large move.\n\nImplied volatility (IV) is the market's expectation of future volatility, backed out from option prices using the Black-Scholes model. When IV rises (e.g. before earnings), options become more expensive. When IV falls after an event, options lose value — this is \"volatility crush\".\n\nA long straddle is long vega: it profits when IV rises. A **covered call** seller is short vega: they are hurt if volatility spikes.","bullets":["Vega > 0 for all long options (higher vol = higher price)","Implied vol rises before events; falls after — the \"volatility crush\"","Long straddle = long vega; covered call = short vega"],"icon":"cloud"},{"heading":"Theta (Θ) — Time Decay","body":"Theta measures how much an option loses in value per day, all else equal. Theta is negative for long option positions — time is the enemy of the option buyer.\n\nTime value decays to zero at expiry, and this decay accelerates non-linearly: an option loses relatively little **time value** early in its life, but the decay rate increases dramatically in the final weeks before expiry (visible as the steepening of the theta curve).\n\nThe Greek relationship: long **gamma** and long theta are opposed. Buyers collect gamma (they benefit from large moves) but pay theta (time decay). Sellers collect theta but face negative gamma (they are hurt by large moves). There is no free lunch.","bullets":["Theta < 0 for long options — value erodes daily","Decay accelerates near expiry — ATM options at high risk","Long gamma vs long theta: buying options is a tradeoff between the two"],"icon":"timer"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"A call option has a delta of 0.65. The underlying stock rises by £2. Approximately how much does the option price increase?","options":["£2.00","£1.30","£0.65","£0.35"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Delta approximates the change in option price for a £1 move in the underlying. A delta of 0.65 means the option gains £0.65 per £1 rise. For a £2 rise: £0.65 × 2 = £1.30. This is only an approximation because delta itself changes as the stock moves (that change is captured by gamma)."},{"id":"t6-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"A trader is \"short gamma\" after selling options. What market condition hurts them most?","options":["The underlying barely moves — low realised volatility","The underlying makes a large, sudden move in either direction","Time passes quickly — options decay faster","Implied volatility falls sharply after they sold the options"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Short gamma means the trader's losses accelerate as the underlying moves. A large move (either up or down) causes delta to shift rapidly against them, requiring costly rehedging. Short gamma sellers collect theta (time decay) but are hurt by large moves — the classic \"picking up pennies in front of a steamroller\" risk. Low volatility and time passing both help short gamma positions."},{"id":"t6-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"A pharmaceutical company announces unexpectedly bad drug trial results after market close. Options on the stock were expensive going into the announcement. After the announcement, the stock has fallen sharply but options prices have also collapsed. What explains the option price collapse?","options":["Delta fell dramatically as the stock moved out of the money","Theta accelerated because expiry is now closer","Implied volatility crushed — the uncertainty that drove high premiums resolved","Gamma increased sharply after the event, which in turn reduced the option's overall price sensitivity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is \"volatility crush.\" Before the announcement, high uncertainty drove implied volatility (and thus option prices) up. Once the event resolved — even badly — uncertainty collapsed and IV fell sharply. Vega is the exposure here: long option holders who bought before the announcement are hurt if the vol crush outweighs the directional move. This is a classic pitfall of buying options around binary events."},{"id":"t6-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"Which Greek relationship is the fundamental tradeoff every option buyer faces?","options":["Delta vs vega — directional exposure vs volatility exposure","Long gamma vs short theta — profiting from large moves costs daily time decay","Delta vs gamma — sensitivity level vs rate of change","Vega vs theta — volatility gains vs time decay are always equal and opposite"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The core tradeoff for option buyers is long gamma vs short theta. Being long gamma means you profit when the stock makes large moves — each pound of movement accelerates your gains. But you pay for this with theta: every day that passes without a big move, your option loses value. Option sellers take the opposite side: they collect theta but face negative gamma. This is why option strategies always involve a fundamental question: is the expected move worth the time-decay cost?"}]},"t6-m2-l1":{"id":"t6-m2-l1","title":"Futures & Forwards","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"Futures and forwards are agreements to buy or sell an asset at a predetermined price on a future date. They are the simplest form of derivative — no optionality, just an obligation. Understanding the difference between the two, how they are priced, and how they are used is fundamental to commodities, FX, rates, and equity derivatives.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Forwards","body":"A **forward contract** is an over-the-counter (OTC) agreement between two counterparties to exchange an asset at a fixed price (the forward price) on a future date. There is no exchange; the contract is customised.\n\nAt initiation, no money changes hands — both sides commit to the future transaction. At **maturity**, the buyer pays the forward price and receives the asset. If the spot price at maturity is above the forward price, the buyer profits; if below, they lose.\n\nForwards are used extensively in FX markets (a corporate treasurer locking in a dollar/sterling rate for a future payment) and commodity markets (an airline locking in a jet fuel price).","bullets":["OTC — customisable in size, date, and asset","No upfront payment; full settlement at maturity","Counterparty risk: no central clearing"],"icon":"handshake"},{"heading":"Futures","body":"A **futures** contract is economically similar to a forward but traded on an exchange (CME, ICE, Eurex). The key differences:\n\n1. Standardised: contract size, expiry dates, and settlement terms are fixed by the exchange.\n2. Mark-to-market daily: profits and losses are settled each day via a margin account. If your position loses value, you receive a **margin call**.\n3. Central **counterparty** clearing (CCP): the exchange becomes the counterparty to both sides, eliminating counterparty risk.\n\nFutures markets exist for equity indices (S&P 500, FTSE 100), government bonds (Bund, T-Note), FX, oil, natural gas, gold, agricultural products.","bullets":["Exchange-traded — standardised and centrally cleared","Daily mark-to-market and margin calls","No counterparty risk — the exchange clears all trades"],"icon":"bar-chart"},{"heading":"Forward Pricing","body":"The fair forward price is set by the cost-of-carry model. For a non-dividend-paying asset:\n\nF = S × e^(r × T)\n\nwhere S is the spot price, r is the risk-free rate, and T is time to maturity.\n\nIntuition: the forward price equals the spot price grown at the risk-free rate — the cost of buying the asset today and financing it until delivery. If the forward price were higher, arbitrageurs would buy spot and sell forward (cash-and-carry). If lower, they would sell spot and buy forward (reverse cash-and-carry).\n\nFor dividend-paying stocks: F = (S − PV(dividends)) × e^(rT). For commodities with storage costs and convenience yield, the formula adjusts further.","bullets":["F = S × e^(rT) for a non-dividend-paying asset","Cost-of-carry: buy spot, finance until delivery","Dividends reduce the forward price; storage costs increase it"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Basis and Convergence","body":"The basis is the difference between the futures price and the spot price: Basis = Futures − Spot.\n\nAt initiation, the basis reflects carrying costs. At expiry, the basis must converge to zero — futures and spot prices must be equal at delivery or **arbitrage** would occur.\n\nBasis risk arises when hedging with futures: you cannot always find a futures contract that exactly matches your exposure. A UK corporate hedging copper price risk with a US-listed copper future faces basis risk from **exchange rate** moves and contract specification differences. The hedge may not be perfect, but it significantly reduces — though does not eliminate — risk.","icon":"git-merge"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"An airline wants to hedge its jet fuel cost for purchases in 6 months. It enters a forward agreement to buy 10,000 barrels of oil at $80/barrel. At delivery, oil trades at $95/barrel. What is the outcome of the forward?","options":["The airline pays $95 (spot price) — the forward locks in a floor, not a ceiling","The airline pays $80 as agreed — saving $15/barrel versus the spot price","The airline receives $15/barrel from the counterparty as a cash settlement","Both B and C — physical or cash settlement both give the same economic result"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Whether the forward settles physically (airline pays $80, receives oil) or in cash ($15/barrel difference paid by counterparty), the economic result is the same: the airline effectively pays $80/barrel. In practice, many commodity forwards cash-settle — the gain/loss on the derivative offsets the higher/lower price paid in the physical market."},{"id":"t6-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"What is the key structural advantage of a futures contract over an equivalent forward contract?","options":["Futures are always cheaper because exchange fees are lower than OTC dealer spreads","Futures eliminate counterparty risk through central clearing and daily mark-to-market","Futures can be customised to any size and maturity unlike standardised forwards","Futures require no margin and no daily settlement"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The key advantage is elimination of counterparty risk. With central clearing, the exchange (CCP) becomes the counterparty to both sides. Daily mark-to-market and margin requirements mean losses are collected in real time rather than accumulating — preventing the build-up of credit exposure that characterises OTC forwards. This was highlighted during the 2008 crisis, when OTC derivative counterparty risk nearly brought down the financial system."},{"id":"t6-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"A stock trades at £100. The 1-year risk-free rate is 5%. What is the fair 1-year forward price on a non-dividend-paying stock?","options":["£100","£105","£95","£110"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"F = S × e^(rT) ≈ S × (1 + r) for small r and T = 1. F = £100 × 1.05 = £105. This reflects the cost of carry: buying the stock today at £100 and financing it for one year at 5% costs £5 in interest. An arbitrageur could lock in profit by buying the stock at £100, selling the forward at above £105, and collecting the difference at maturity."},{"id":"t6-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"What is basis risk in futures hedging?","options":["The risk that interest rates change between the trade date and settlement","The risk that the futures contract and the exposure being hedged do not move perfectly together","The risk that the exchange defaults on the futures contract","The risk that comes from using an excessively high leverage ratio across your futures positions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Basis risk arises when a futures contract does not exactly match the exposure being hedged — due to differences in underlying asset specification, settlement date, or currency. For example, hedging Brent crude exposure with WTI crude futures introduces basis risk because the two oil grades can diverge in price. The hedge reduces but does not eliminate risk."}]},"t6-m2-l2":{"id":"t6-m2-l2","title":"Interest Rate & Currency Swaps","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"A swap is an agreement to exchange sequences of cashflows over time. Swaps are the largest segment of the derivatives market — the BIS estimates over $500 trillion notional in outstanding interest rate swaps. Understanding how vanilla IR swaps and cross-currency swaps work is essential for rates, DCM, and risk management roles.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Plain Vanilla Interest Rate Swap","body":"In a plain vanilla interest rate **swap** (IRS), two parties exchange interest payments on a **notional** principal amount (which is never itself exchanged). One party pays a fixed rate; the other pays a floating rate (typically SONIA in GBP, SOFR in USD, since LIBOR is discontinued).\n\nExample: Company A borrows at floating SONIA + 100bps. It enters a 5-year swap to pay 3% fixed and receive SONIA from Bank B. Net result: A pays 3% + 100bps = 4.0% fixed — regardless of where SONIA moves.\n\nCompany A has converted floating debt into fixed debt using the swap. The notional (£100m) is never exchanged — only the interest difference is settled (net settlement).","bullets":["Fixed payer pays fixed rate, receives floating","Floating payer pays floating, receives fixed","Notional never exchanged — only net interest difference settles"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Why Swaps Exist: Comparative Advantage","body":"The classic rationale for interest rate swaps is comparative advantage. Suppose Company A (AAA-rated) can borrow at 3% fixed or SONIA + 0.1%. Company B (BBB-rated) can borrow at 4.5% fixed or SONIA + 0.6%.\n\nA has an absolute advantage in both, but a relative advantage in fixed-rate borrowing (1.5% differential vs 0.5% differential). B has a relative advantage in floating.\n\nIf A borrows fixed and B borrows floating, then they swap: total saving = 1.5% − 0.5% = 1.0%. Divided between them and the intermediating bank, both parties pay less than if they borrowed directly in their preferred market. This \"gain from trade\" in capital markets is what swap markets exploit.","icon":"people"},{"heading":"Cross-Currency Swaps","body":"A cross-currency swap (CCS) involves exchanging not just interest payments but also principal in two different currencies. A UK company that issues USD bonds (to access a deeper market or achieve cheaper funding) can use a CCS to convert the USD liability back into GBP.\n\nAt initiation: exchange principals (pay USD, receive GBP at current FX rate).\nDuring the life: exchange interest payments (pay GBP floating, receive USD fixed or floating).\nAt **maturity**: re-exchange principals at the original FX rate.\n\nThis effectively converts a USD bond into a GBP bond. The CCS eliminates FX exposure on the principal and interest — essential for issuers who want to tap foreign bond markets without taking on currency risk.","bullets":["Exchange principals at start AND maturity (unlike IRS)","Used to convert foreign currency debt into domestic currency","Basis spread: cross-currency basis reflects relative demand for currencies in swap markets"],"icon":"globe"},{"heading":"Swap Valuation and Risk","body":"At inception, a fairly priced swap has zero value — neither party should pay to enter. Over time, as **interest rates** change, the swap gains or loses value.\n\nA receive-fixed swap gains value when rates fall (the fixed leg is worth more relative to the now-lower floating leg). A pay-fixed swap gains value when rates rise.\n\nSwap duration measures the rate sensitivity. A 10-year pay-fixed swap has significant duration risk — similar to being short a 10-year bond. DV01 (dollar value of a basis point) measures how much the swap value changes for a 1bp move in rates — a key risk metric for rates desks.\n\nSwap spreads (swap rate minus **government bond** yield of the same maturity) widen in stress as banks demand more compensation for **counterparty** risk.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"A company has floating rate debt at SONIA + 150bps and wants to fix its interest cost. It enters a 5-year swap to pay 2.5% fixed and receive SONIA. What is the company's all-in fixed cost?","options":["2.5%","4.0%","1.0%","SONIA + 1.5%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The company pays SONIA + 150bps on its loan, but receives SONIA from the swap, which offsets the floating component. It then pays 2.5% fixed on the swap. Net cost = 2.5% + 150bps = 4.0% fixed. This is the advantage of a swap: the company converts its floating obligation into a fixed cost without refinancing its underlying loan."},{"id":"t6-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the notional principal is £500m. What amount is actually exchanged between counterparties at settlement?","options":["£500m — the full notional changes hands at each settlement","Only the net difference between fixed and floating interest payments","£250m — half the notional each way","Nothing — swaps never involve any cash exchange"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In a plain vanilla IRS, the notional principal is never exchanged — it is purely a reference amount for calculating interest. Only the net interest difference settles each period. If fixed rate = 3% and SONIA = 2.5%, the fixed payer owes 0.5% × £500m = £2.5m net for that period (simplified). This dramatically reduces the actual cash flows relative to the notional, making swaps capital-efficient."},{"id":"t6-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"A UK company issues a US dollar bond to access cheaper funding. It then enters a cross-currency swap to convert the proceeds. What is the primary purpose of the CCS?","options":["To benefit from lower USD interest rates on the bond","To eliminate currency risk on both the interest payments and principal repayment","To convert the fixed-rate USD bond into a floating-rate sterling bond","To reduce the tenor of the bond from 10 years to 5 years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The primary purpose of a cross-currency swap in this context is to eliminate FX risk. Without the CCS, the company would owe USD at maturity — a currency mismatch if its revenues are in GBP. The CCS converts the USD liability into GBP by exchanging principals at the start (receive USD, give GBP) and at maturity (give USD back, receive GBP back) at the original rate, locking in the exchange rate for the life of the bond."},{"id":"t6-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"Interest rates rise sharply after a company has entered a pay-fixed swap. What happens to the value of their swap position?","options":["It gains value — the fixed rate they are paying is now below market, which is advantageous","It loses value — they are locked into paying a below-market fixed rate while floating receipts rise","It remains unchanged — swaps are marked to par at all times","It gains value — rising rates always benefit fixed payers"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A pay-fixed swap gains value when rates rise. The company locked in a fixed rate (say 3%) when the market has now moved to 5%. Their fixed obligation is below market — a new counterparty entering the same swap today would pay 5% fixed. The company's existing pay-3%-fixed position is now an asset worth the present value of the 2% annual saving over the remaining life of the swap."}]},"t6-m2-l3":{"id":"t6-m2-l3","title":"Derivatives in Practice","trackId":"t6","trackTitle":"Derivatives & Risk","color":"#0891B2","premium":false,"intro":"Derivatives are used across every corner of finance — from a FTSE 100 treasurer hedging FX exposure to a macro hedge fund betting on central bank policy. Understanding how they are applied in practice, and the risk framework around them, is essential for anyone working in markets, corporate treasury, or risk management.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Corporate Hedging","body":"Corporates use derivatives to reduce the financial uncertainty of their business:\n\nFX **hedging**: A UK exporter with USD revenues uses forward contracts or USD/GBP options to fix the sterling value of future dollar receipts.\nInterest rate hedging: A company with floating-rate debt enters a pay-fixed swap to stabilise interest costs against rising rates.\nCommodity hedging: An airline buys oil futures to lock in jet fuel costs; a food manufacturer uses grain futures.\n\nKey principle: hedging reduces risk but also reduces potential upside. A perfect **hedge** transforms uncertain future cashflows into certain ones — useful for financial planning and reducing earnings **volatility**, but costly if markets move in your favour.","bullets":["FX: forwards and options lock in exchange rates","Rates: IRS converts floating debt to fixed","Commodities: futures cap input cost risk for manufacturers and airlines"],"icon":"business"},{"heading":"Speculation and Leverage","body":"Derivatives also allow speculation — taking risk with a directional view, amplified by **leverage**. A futures position requires only an initial margin (typically 5–10% of notional), giving 10–20× leverage.\n\nA macro **hedge fund** bullish on US equities might buy S&P 500 futures rather than the underlying **ETF** — the same £1m exposure requires only £50,000 in margin. If the S&P rises 5%, the futures gain £50,000 on £50,000 margin — a 100% return. But leverage cuts both ways: a 5% fall wipes out the margin entirely.\n\nRegulation (EMIR in Europe, Dodd-Frank in the US) requires most standardised derivatives to be centrally cleared and reported to trade repositories to improve market transparency and reduce systemic risk.","bullets":["Futures require only initial margin (~5-10% of notional)","Leverage amplifies both gains and losses","EMIR / Dodd-Frank: mandatory clearing and reporting for most OTC derivatives"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Value at Risk (VaR)","body":"**Value at Risk** (**VaR**) is the standard risk metric used by banks and investment managers. A 1-day 99% VaR of £10m means: there is a 1% chance the portfolio loses more than £10m in a single day.\n\nVaR is used to set trading limits, allocate economic capital, and communicate risk to management and regulators. Banks are required to hold regulatory capital against their VaR.\n\nLimitations: VaR does not tell you what the loss is in the worst 1% of cases (**tail risk**). The 2008 crisis exposed this — VaR models built on historical data from calm markets catastrophically underestimated tail risk. Expected Shortfall (ES), also called CVaR, is now preferred by regulators (Basel III/IV) because it measures the average loss in the tail, not just the boundary.","bullets":["99% 1-day VaR = threshold not exceeded 99% of days","Regulators require banks to hold capital against VaR","Expected Shortfall (CVaR): average loss in the worst X% — preferred under Basel III/IV"],"icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Risk in Practice: Delta Hedging","body":"A derivatives desk that sells options to clients must manage the resulting risk. The primary approach is delta hedging: continuously adjusting the **underlying** position to keep the portfolio delta-neutral.\n\nIf a trader sells a call with delta 0.6, they buy 0.6 shares per option to hedge. As the stock price moves, delta changes (captured by gamma) — so the hedge must be rebalanced frequently. This dynamic replication is the practical implementation of Black-Scholes.\n\nIn practice, perfect continuous hedging is impossible (transaction costs, discrete trading). The residual risk — gamma and vega exposure — must be managed through offsetting option positions or must be held within limits. This is the core of a market-making derivatives desk.","icon":"refresh-circle"}],"questions":[{"id":"t6-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"A UK exporter expects $10m from a US customer in 3 months. GBP/USD is 1.25 today. The company sells $10m forward at 1.25. At delivery, GBP/USD has moved to 1.35 — the pound has strengthened. What was the effect of hedging?","options":["Positive — the hedge protected against GBP strength; the company received £8m vs only £7.41m at spot","Negative — the company should have waited; 1.35 is a better rate for exporters","Neutral — forward rates equal the expected future spot rate, so there is no gain or loss","The hedge had no effect because GBP/USD movements cancel out over time"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"GBP/USD = 1.35 means £1 = $1.35 — the pound is stronger. For a UK exporter receiving dollars, a stronger pound is unfavorable: $10m at 1.35 converts to only £7.41m. The hedge locked in 1.25, delivering $10m / 1.25 = £8m — protecting £590k of value. This is the core purpose of FX hedging: eliminate uncertainty in both directions. If the pound had weakened instead (say GBP/USD fell to 1.10), the unhedged company would have received more sterling (£9.09m), but the hedge removes that upside too — certainty, not profit, is the goal."},{"id":"t6-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"A trader buys £1m of S&P 500 futures using £50,000 of initial margin (20× leverage). The S&P falls 5%. What is the approximate loss, and what happens to the margin account?","options":["Loss: £2,500 (5% of £50,000 margin). Margin account is intact.","Loss: £50,000 (5% of £1m notional). Margin account is wiped out.","Loss: £50,000 (5% of £1m notional). A margin call requires more capital.","Loss: £1,000 per contract. Margin is not affected."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Futures are marked-to-market on the full notional. A 5% fall on £1m notional = £50,000 loss — the full initial margin. The broker issues a margin call requiring the trader to deposit additional funds to restore the margin to the initial level. If they cannot meet the margin call, the position is forcibly closed. This is how futures leverage works: losses accrue on the full notional, not just the margin posted."},{"id":"t6-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"A bank's 1-day 99% VaR is £25m. What does this mean?","options":["The bank will lose exactly £25m on 1% of trading days","There is a 99% chance the bank loses less than £25m in a single day","The bank's average daily loss is £25m","The bank must hold £25m of capital against all derivative positions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A 99% VaR of £25m means that on 99% of days, the loss will be less than £25m. Equivalently, there is a 1% chance of a loss exceeding £25m on any given day. It does NOT say what the loss will be in the worst 1% of cases (that's Expected Shortfall). The 2008 crisis showed that VaR models systematically underestimated tail risk — leading to Basel III/IV adopting Expected Shortfall as the primary regulatory metric."},{"id":"t6-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"A derivatives trader sells options to a client and immediately delta-hedges by buying the underlying. The next day, the stock moves significantly. Why must the trader rebalance the hedge?","options":["Because the option premium has been received and the hedge is now free","Because delta changes as the stock price moves (gamma) — the original hedge is no longer delta-neutral","Because the option will expire worthless and the hedge must be unwound","Because regulatory rules explicitly require the daily rebalancing of every derivative hedge position"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Delta hedging requires continuous rebalancing because delta changes as the stock moves — this sensitivity is captured by gamma. After a large move, the option's delta has shifted, meaning the original hedge (sized at the old delta) now leaves the position with net directional exposure. The trader must buy or sell more of the underlying to restore delta-neutrality. This dynamic process is the practical implementation of the Black-Scholes replication argument."}]},"t7-m1-l1":{"id":"t7-m1-l1","title":"Credit Spreads","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"A credit spread is the extra yield an investor demands to hold a corporate bond instead of a risk-free government bond of the same maturity. It is the market's real-time price of default risk, liquidity risk, and credit uncertainty — and it moves constantly as conditions change.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"What Is a Credit Spread?","body":"If a 5-year UK gilt yields 4.0% and a 5-year investment-grade **corporate bond** yields 4.8%, the **credit spread** is 80 basis points (bps). This 80bps compensates the investor for:\n\n1. Default risk: the chance the company fails to pay.\n2. Recovery risk: how much they get back if it does default.\n3. Liquidity risk: corporate bonds are harder to trade than gilts.\n4. Uncertainty: corporate cash flows are less predictable than government revenues.\n\nSpreads are quoted in basis points (1bp = 0.01%). **Investment grade** (IG) bonds trade at tight spreads (50-200bps); high yield (HY) bonds trade wider (300-800bps+ depending on quality).","bullets":["Spread = corporate yield minus equivalent government yield","Compensation for default risk, liquidity, and uncertainty","IG: ~50-200bps; HY: ~300-800bps typical range"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"OAS, Z-Spread, and G-Spread","body":"Three spread measures are used in practice:\n\nG-spread (Government spread): yield minus the interpolated government curve at the same **maturity**. Simple, commonly used.\n\nZ-spread (Zero-volatility spread): constant spread added to each point of the zero-coupon government curve so the bond's discounted cashflows equal its price. More accurate than G-spread.\n\nOAS (Option-Adjusted Spread): adjusts Z-spread for any embedded options (callable, putable). For a callable bond, the issuer's right to redeem early is valuable — OAS strips this out to show the \"pure\" credit spread. OAS is the cleanest measure for comparing across structures.","bullets":["G-spread: simple yield minus government yield — quick approximation","Z-spread: spread over the full zero curve — more precise","OAS: removes the value of embedded options — most accurate"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"What Drives Spreads?","body":"Credit spreads move with: (1) macro environment — spreads widen in recessions as default risk rises; (2) sector dynamics — energy sector spreads spike with oil prices; (3) issuer-specific factors — leverage, cash generation, **covenant** quality; (4) technicals — **supply and demand** in credit markets.\n\nThe VIX (equity volatility) and CDX/iTraxx credit indices are closely watched leading indicators. When the VIX spikes, credit spreads typically widen as risk appetite falls. The 2008 crisis saw IG spreads exceed 600bps and HY approach 2,000bps.\n\nSpread compression: in bull markets, investors \"reach for yield\" — buying lower-rated bonds to earn more, compressing spreads. This can mask building **credit risk**.","icon":"pulse"},{"heading":"Spread Duration and Price Impact","body":"Spread **duration** measures a bond's price sensitivity to a 1bp change in its spread. A bond with spread duration of 5 loses approximately 5 × 1bp = 0.05% of price for each basis point of spread widening.\n\nIf spreads widen 100bps (a \"100bp sell-off\"), a 5-year spread duration bond loses ~5% in price — significant for a fixed income portfolio. This is why rising credit spreads hurt corporate bond holders even when **interest rates** are unchanged.\n\nDV01 (Dollar Value of a Basis Point): the absolute dollar loss per basis point of spread widening. Used by credit traders to size positions and hedges.","bullets":["Spread duration: price sensitivity to spread change (similar to interest rate duration)","100bp widening on a 5yr spread-duration bond ≈ -5% price","DV01: absolute dollar loss per basis point — used for hedging"],"icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"A 5-year government bond yields 3.5%. A 5-year investment-grade corporate bond from the same country yields 4.1%. What is the credit spread?","options":["4.1%","0.6%","3.5%","7.6%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Credit spread = corporate yield − government yield = 4.1% − 3.5% = 0.6% = 60 basis points. This 60bps compensates investors for the additional risk of holding corporate debt instead of the risk-free government bond. At 60bps, this bond would be considered investment grade (IG bonds typically trade at 50-200bps over govts in normal markets)."},{"id":"t7-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"Which spread measure is most appropriate for comparing the \"pure credit\" risk of a callable corporate bond versus a bullet bond?","options":["G-spread — simplest and most transparent","Z-spread — accounts for the full zero-coupon curve","OAS — strips out the value of the call option to isolate credit risk","Duration — measures interest rate risk, not credit risk"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"OAS (Option-Adjusted Spread) removes the value of embedded options — in this case, the call feature that allows the issuer to redeem the bond early. Without adjusting for the option, the callable bond's spread would appear artificially wide (investors demand extra yield for giving the issuer this right). OAS makes callable and bullet bonds directly comparable on credit risk alone."},{"id":"t7-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"A corporate bond has a spread duration of 6. Credit spreads widen by 50bps. Approximately what happens to the bond's price?","options":["Price rises by 3% — wider spreads mean higher yields and lower prices","Price falls by 3%","Price falls by 50bps (0.5%)","Price is unchanged — spread widening only affects yield, not price"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Price change ≈ −spread duration × spread change = −6 × 0.50% = −3%. When spreads widen, yields rise and bond prices fall (inverse relationship). A spread duration of 6 means the bond loses approximately 6% of its price for every 100bps of spread widening. This is why credit bond portfolios suffer significant mark-to-market losses during credit sell-offs."},{"id":"t7-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"During a recession, what typically happens to investment-grade corporate credit spreads?","options":["Spreads compress — investors buy safe corporate bonds as equities fall","Spreads widen — default risk rises, liquidity falls, and risk appetite retreats","Spreads are unchanged — credit spreads only react to company-specific news","Spreads compress for IG but widen for high yield only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In recessions, credit spreads widen across the board: rising unemployment and falling revenues increase default risk; reduced liquidity in corporate bond markets increases liquidity premium; risk-off sentiment reduces investor appetite. Both IG and HY spreads widen, with HY widening far more dramatically. The 2008-09 recession saw IG spreads exceed 600bps and HY approach 2,000bps — levels that implied very high default rates."}]},"t7-m1-l2":{"id":"t7-m1-l2","title":"Credit Ratings","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"Credit ratings assign a letter grade to a borrower's creditworthiness — the likelihood of timely interest and principal repayment. They divide the market into investment grade and high yield, a distinction that has enormous consequences for who can hold a bond, how it is priced, and the cost of capital for the issuer.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Rating Scale","body":"The two main rating agencies are Moody's and S&P (Fitch is a distant third). Their scales differ slightly but map as follows:\n\n**Investment Grade** (IG): S&P: AAA, AA, A, BBB (Moody's: Aaa, Aa, A, Baa). The lowest IG rung is BBB-/Baa3.\n\n**High Yield** (HY): S&P: BB, B, CCC, CC, C, D (Moody's: Ba, B, Caa, Ca, C). Also called \"speculative grade\" or \"junk.\" Default is D (S&P).\n\nThe key threshold: BBB- / Baa3. Falling below this (a \"fallen angel\") triggers forced selling from IG-only investors and significantly increases borrowing costs. Rising above it (a \"rising star\") is a positive catalyst.","bullets":["IG floor: BBB- (S&P) / Baa3 (Moody's)","HY starts at BB+ / Ba1","Fallen angel: IG issuer downgraded to HY — forced selling follows"],"icon":"list"},{"heading":"What Ratings Measure","body":"Rating agencies assess the probability of default and the expected recovery rate on default. Key factors:\n\nBusiness risk: competitive position, industry dynamics, revenue **diversification**.\nFinancial risk: leverage (Debt/**EBITDA**), interest coverage (EBIT/interest), **free cash flow** generation.\nLiquidity: access to revolving credit facilities, near-term debt maturities.\nManagement and strategy: track record, financial policy, M&A appetite.\n\nA typical IG company has Debt/EBITDA ≤ 2-3× and strong interest coverage. A typical B-rated HY company might have 5-6× leverage with tight liquidity. Ratings change slowly — agencies lag the market.","bullets":["Leverage: Debt/EBITDA — higher = more risk","Coverage: EBIT/Interest — lower = more risk","Liquidity: revolving facility headroom, near-term maturities"],"icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"Market Segmentation","body":"The IG/HY divide creates distinct investor pools:\n\nIG buyers: insurance companies, pension funds, and conservative bond funds with IG mandates. They hold bonds to maturity and focus on coupon income and capital preservation.\n\nHY buyers: high yield funds, CLO managers, hedge funds. They seek total return and tolerate higher **default risk** in exchange for higher yield.\n\nThis segmentation matters: a rating downgrade to HY forces IG-only investors to sell, creating mechanical supply in the market and compressing prices (widening spreads) — often more than the fundamental credit deterioration warrants. This \"forced selling\" dynamic is a significant source of **volatility** around the IG/HY boundary.","icon":"people"},{"heading":"Ratings vs. Market Pricing","body":"Ratings are backward-looking and change slowly. Market spreads react immediately to new information. A company rated BBB can trade at spreads more typical of BB if the market anticipates a downgrade.\n\nCDS (Credit Default **Swap**) markets provide a real-time alternative measure. A CDS spread of 200bps implies the market prices the bond as if it had a lower credit quality than its rating suggests.\n\nThe 2008 crisis exposed ratings failures: AAA-rated mortgage-backed securities defaulted at rates more consistent with CCC. Structured finance ratings proved deeply flawed because models underestimated **correlation** — when housing markets fell, all mortgages defaulted together.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"A company currently rated BBB- by S&P is downgraded to BB+. What is the primary market impact?","options":["Minimal — one notch difference in spread is negligible","Forced selling by investment-grade-only investors, mechanical spread widening","The bond is redeemed at par by the issuer under change-of-control provisions","The company's equity price rises as debt gets cheaper"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"BBB- to BB+ is the IG/HY threshold — the \"fallen angel\" crossing. IG-only investors (pension funds, insurance companies, IG bond funds) are mandated or restricted to hold only investment-grade assets. A downgrade forces them to sell regardless of price. This creates a wave of mechanical selling, widening spreads far beyond what the credit fundamentals alone would justify. Fallen angel spreads can gap out 100-200bps or more on the downgrade."},{"id":"t7-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"Which financial metric is most directly associated with a company's position on the rating scale?","options":["Revenue growth rate — higher growth means better rating","Debt/EBITDA leverage ratio — higher leverage = lower rating","Gross margin — profitability is the primary rating factor","Share price performance — market value determines creditworthiness"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Debt/EBITDA (leverage) is the single most important quantitative metric in credit analysis. IG companies typically operate at 1-3× leverage; BB-rated at 3-5×; B-rated at 5-7×. A company that takes on significant debt (e.g. through an LBO or large acquisition) will typically see its leverage spike and its rating fall. Interest coverage (EBITDA/Interest) is the second key metric."},{"id":"t7-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"Rating agencies were heavily criticised after 2008 for what failure?","options":["Downgrading government and sovereign bonds far too aggressively in the depths of the crisis","Rating structured mortgage products (MBS/CDOs) as AAA when they failed at rates more typical of CCC","Charging issuers too much for ratings, creating a conflict of interest","Being too slow to upgrade corporate bonds after the crisis ended"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The core failure was rating structured products (CDOs, MBS) as AAA when they were backed by subprime mortgages with high correlation. Rating models assumed mortgage defaults would be independent — some regions failing while others were fine. In reality, a nationwide housing price collapse caused correlated defaults across all underlying mortgages. The \"issuer-pays\" model also created conflicts: issuers paid for ratings and could shop for the highest rating. The SEC report and Dodd-Frank legislation introduced significant reforms."},{"id":"t7-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"A company rated BB by S&P has its CDS spread trading at levels consistent with a B-rated company. What does this suggest?","options":["The CDS market has simply got it wrong here, and the agency's official rating is the correct one","The market anticipates further deterioration and has priced in a lower credit quality than the current rating","CDS spreads and ratings always diverge — they measure different things","The company should immediately redeem its bonds at a discount"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CDS markets are forward-looking and react to new information in real time, while ratings are backward-looking and change slowly. When the CDS spread trades at levels more consistent with a lower rating, the market is implying it expects the company's credit quality to deteriorate and/or a downgrade. This divergence is a warning signal — credit analysts watch it closely as a leading indicator of rating action."}]},"t7-m1-l3":{"id":"t7-m1-l3","title":"Credit Default Swaps","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"A Credit Default Swap (CDS) is a derivative contract that transfers the risk of a credit event (default, restructuring) from a protection buyer to a protection seller. CDS markets provide a liquid, real-time measure of credit risk and allow investors to hedge or speculate on corporate and sovereign creditworthiness without owning the underlying bond.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"How a CDS Works","body":"In a CDS, the protection buyer pays a periodic premium (the CDS spread, in bps per annum on the **notional**) to the protection seller. In return, if the reference entity (a company or sovereign) suffers a credit event, the seller pays the buyer par minus recovery.\n\nExample: A fund owns £10m of Vodafone bonds. It buys 5-year CDS on Vodafone at 80bps. It pays £80,000/year (80bps × £10m). If Vodafone defaults with 40% recovery, the CDS pays £6m (60% × £10m) — offsetting the bond loss. If Vodafone does not **default**, the fund loses only the £400,000 of premiums paid over 5 years.\n\nCDS are OTC derivatives, now subject to mandatory central clearing under EMIR/Dodd-Frank.","bullets":["Protection buyer: pays spread, receives payout on credit event","Protection seller: receives spread, pays par minus recovery on default","Payout = (1 − recovery rate) × notional"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Credit Events","body":"ISDA (International Swaps and Derivatives Association) defines the credit events that trigger CDS settlement:\n\n1. Bankruptcy: filing for insolvency protection (Chapter 11, administration).\n2. Failure to pay: missing a **coupon** or principal payment after a grace period.\n3. Restructuring: changing bond terms in a way that disadvantages creditors (**maturity** extension, coupon reduction, change in currency).\n\nFor sovereign CDS, repudiation/moratorium of debt is also included. The ISDA Determinations Committee adjudicates whether a credit event has occurred — a role that became contentious during the Greek sovereign debt restructuring in 2012.","bullets":["Bankruptcy, failure to pay, restructuring are the three main credit events","ISDA DC determines whether an event has occurred","Restructuring is the most contested — \"soft\" events can trigger CDS"],"icon":"alert-circle"},{"heading":"CDS as a Market Signal","body":"CDS spreads are the most liquid real-time measure of **credit risk**. The CDX (US IG and HY indices) and iTraxx (European IG and crossover indices) are standardised CDS index products that allow investors to take macro credit views or **hedge** portfolios efficiently.\n\nCDS are also used \"naked\" — buying protection on a company you don't own bonds in, essentially shorting the credit. This allows price discovery that bond markets (which are harder to short) cannot provide.\n\nThe \"basis\" between a bond's spread and its CDS spread reflects technicals: if the CDS trades wider than the bond, protection sellers are being paid more for the same risk — implying the bond is cheap or CDS is expensive.","bullets":["CDX/iTraxx: standardised CDS indices for macro credit hedging","Naked CDS: buy protection without owning the underlying bond — credit short","Basis = CDS spread minus bond Z-spread; persistent basis signals technical imbalance"],"icon":"pulse"},{"heading":"Recovery Rates and Pricing","body":"CDS pricing depends on two inputs: the probability of default (PD) and the recovery rate (R). The expected annual loss = PD × (1 − R).\n\nThe fair CDS spread ≈ PD × (1 − R) (simplified). If a company has a 2% annual default probability and 40% recovery: spread ≈ 2% × 60% = 1.2% = 120bps.\n\nRecovery rates vary significantly: senior secured debt typically recovers 60-80% in default; senior unsecured 40-60%; subordinated debt 10-30%. CDS contracts specify a standard recovery assumption for pricing purposes, with physical or auction-based settlement determining actual recovery after a credit event.","bullets":["Fair CDS spread ≈ Default probability × (1 − Recovery rate)","Senior secured: 60-80% typical recovery; HY unsecured: 30-50%","Auction settlement: market determines recovery rate after credit event"],"icon":"calculator"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"A fund buys 5-year CDS protection on a £20m bond position at 120bps per annum. The company defaults after 2 years with 35% recovery. What does the fund receive from the CDS?","options":["£20m — full notional recovery","£13m — (1 − 0.35) × £20m","£7m — 0.35 × £20m recovery value","£480,000 — two years of premiums paid"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CDS payout = (1 − recovery rate) × notional = (1 − 0.35) × £20m = £13m. This compensates the fund for the loss on the defaulted bonds (which recover only £7m). The fund paid 120bps × £20m = £240,000/year for 2 years = £480,000 in premiums. Net gain: £13m received − £480,000 paid = £12.52m. Without the CDS, the fund would have lost £13m on the bond position."},{"id":"t7-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a standard ISDA credit event for corporate CDS?","options":["Bankruptcy filing","Failure to pay a coupon after the grace period","A credit rating downgrade from BBB to BB","Restructuring that disadvantages existing bondholders"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A credit rating downgrade is NOT an ISDA credit event — it does not trigger CDS settlement. The three main corporate credit events are: bankruptcy, failure to pay (missing a coupon/principal), and restructuring (changing bond terms to creditor disadvantage). A downgrade from IG to HY is extremely significant for market prices and investor mandates, but the debt itself has not been impaired — the company is still paying as agreed."},{"id":"t7-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"A company has a 3% annual default probability and a 50% expected recovery rate. What is the approximate fair CDS spread?","options":["3%","1.5%","0.5%","50bps"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fair CDS spread ≈ PD × (1 − R) = 3% × (1 − 0.50) = 3% × 50% = 1.5% = 150bps. This approximation holds for low default probabilities. The full formula accounts for the timing of defaults across the CDS term and discount rates, but the expected loss formula is the key intuition: the spread compensates for the probability-weighted loss given default."},{"id":"t7-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"An investor wants to express a bearish view on a company's credit without selling its bonds (which are illiquid). What is the most efficient instrument?","options":["Short-selling the company's equity","Buying CDS protection on the company — a \"naked\" credit short","Selling interest rate futures on the same maturity","Buying put options on the company's equity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buying CDS protection without owning the underlying bonds is the most direct credit short. It pays out specifically on credit events (default, restructuring) — the exact risk being expressed. Shorting equity gives broad negative exposure but equity can fall for non-credit reasons. Interest rate futures hedge rates, not credit. Put options provide credit exposure indirectly (equity falls when credit deteriorates) but with different payoff characteristics. Naked CDS is the cleanest credit short."}]},"t7-m2-l1":{"id":"t7-m2-l1","title":"High Yield Bonds","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"High yield (HY) bonds — also called junk bonds — are issued by companies rated below BBB-/Baa3. They offer higher coupons to compensate for elevated default risk. The HY market funds leveraged buyouts, growth companies, and fallen angels. It is a key source of capital in the leveraged finance ecosystem.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"HY Bond Characteristics","body":"**High yield** bonds are typically:\n\nFixed-rate, bullet **maturity**: a fixed coupon paid semi-annually, with full principal repayment at maturity (no **amortisation**, unlike leveraged loans). Maturities are usually 5-8 years.\n\nSenior unsecured or senior secured: most HY bonds are senior secured (first lien on assets) or senior unsecured. Subordinated HY (PIK notes) rank below and pay even higher yields.\n\nCallable: most HY bonds have call features — the issuer can redeem early (usually after a non-call period of 2-4 years). This allows refinancing if rates fall or spreads tighten, at the expense of the investor's return (callable bond = issuer has an embedded call).","bullets":["Fixed coupon, bullet maturity (5-8 years typical)","No amortisation — all principal at maturity","Usually callable after 2-4 year non-call period"],"icon":"document"},{"heading":"Covenant Packages","body":"Covenants are contractual protections for bondholders. HY bonds typically have incurrence covenants (triggered by specific actions, not ongoing financial tests):\n\nRestricted payments **covenant**: limits dividends, share buybacks, or payments to equity sponsors above a defined basket.\nDebt incurrence covenant: limits additional debt unless **leverage** stays below a set multiple (e.g. 4.5× Debt/**EBITDA**).\nChange of control put: if the company is acquired, holders can put bonds back at 101% — protecting against leveraging acquisitions.\nAsset sale covenant: proceeds from asset sales must be reinvested or used to repay debt.\n\nUnlike investment-grade bonds (which have few covenants), HY covenants are detailed and negotiated — the \"covenant quality\" is a key analytical input.","bullets":["Incurrence covenants: triggered by specific actions, not ratios","Change of control put at 101% — protects against ownership changes","Restricted payments: limits equity distributions"],"icon":"lock-closed"},{"heading":"Yield, Price and Returns","body":"HY bonds are quoted in price (like all bonds) but also in yield-to-worst (YTW) — the lowest yield scenario considering all call dates and maturities. For a callable bond, YTW might occur at an early call date if the call is deep **in the money**.\n\nTotal return in HY comes from: (1) **coupon** income (the bulk, typically 6-10% annually); (2) price appreciation if spreads tighten; (3) offset by defaults. Historical HY returns average ~7-8% annually (US market, long-run), with significant **volatility** during credit cycles.\n\nDefault rates matter: in calm periods, US HY default rates run ~2-3%. In 2009, they peaked at ~12%. Default rate × (1 − recovery) = expected credit loss, which should equal the spread premium for fair pricing.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"The HY Market Structure","body":"Investment banks run leveraged finance groups that originate and underwrite HY bonds. The process:\n\n1. Mandate: PE sponsor or corporate selects banks to underwrite.\n2. Road show: management presents to investors over 1-2 weeks.\n3. Pricing: books are built, price talk tightens as demand grows.\n4. Settlement: bonds are allocated and begin trading in the **secondary market**.\n\nHY bonds trade OTC via broker-dealers. **Liquidity** is lower than IG — bid-ask spreads are wider (50-100bps vs 5-10bps for IG). In a credit sell-off, HY liquidity can disappear rapidly — a key risk for HY fund managers running open-ended structures.","icon":"business"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"A high yield bond has a coupon of 7% and is callable at par after 3 years, with maturity in 7 years. The bond currently trades above par. Which yield measure is most relevant for investors?","options":["Yield to maturity — it captures the full return if held to maturity","Current yield — just the coupon divided by price","Yield to worst — the lowest yield across all call dates and maturity","Yield to call at year 3 — issuers always call at the first opportunity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Yield to worst (YTW) is the standard measure for callable HY bonds. It calculates the yield for each possible call scenario (year 3, year 4... maturity) and selects the worst (lowest) for the investor. If the bond trades above par and the coupon is high, the issuer is likely to call early — so the year 3 call yield may well be the worst case. Investors buying a callable bond must be compensated for this call risk."},{"id":"t7-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"A change-of-control put covenant at 101% in a high yield bond indenture protects bondholders how?","options":["The issuer must repay the bond at 101% of face value every year","If the company is acquired by a new owner, bondholders can require repurchase at 101% of par","The bond pays 101bps above market yield if credit deteriorates","The issuer cannot call the bond at below 101% of par in the call period"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A change-of-control put entitles bondholders to sell (put) their bonds back to the issuer at 101% of par if the company is acquired. This protects against a leveraging acquisition where the new owner loads the company with debt, increasing default risk for existing bondholders. Without this protection, bondholders might find the acquired company is far more leveraged — and thus riskier — than when they lent the money."},{"id":"t7-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"What is the primary difference between incurrence covenants (typical in HY bonds) and maintenance covenants (typical in leveraged loans)?","options":["Incurrence covenants are formally tested once a year, whereas maintenance covenants are tested every month","Incurrence covenants trigger on specific actions; maintenance covenants must be met continuously","Incurrence covenants apply only to interest payments; maintenance covenants apply to principal","Maintenance covenants are more common in investment grade bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Incurrence covenants in HY bonds are only triggered when the company takes a specific action (incurs more debt, pays a dividend, sells assets). If the company does nothing, it cannot breach these covenants even if leverage deteriorates. Maintenance covenants in loans require ongoing compliance — if leverage exceeds a set threshold in any quarter, the borrower is in technical default. Maintenance covenants give lenders earlier intervention rights; HY bond holders must wait for a payment failure."},{"id":"t7-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"US high yield default rates peaked at approximately 12% in 2009. If recovery rates average 40%, what was the approximate credit loss rate at the peak?","options":["12%","7.2%","4.8%","40%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Credit loss rate = default rate × (1 − recovery rate) = 12% × (1 − 0.40) = 12% × 0.60 = 7.2%. This means roughly 7.2% of the HY market's value was lost to defaults in 2009. In a normal year (~2-3% defaults, 40% recovery), the credit loss is only ~1.2-1.8% — well below the 7-9% yield that HY bonds offer, which is why the market generates positive excess returns in most years."}]},"t7-m2-l2":{"id":"t7-m2-l2","title":"Leveraged Loans","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"Leveraged loans are loans to sub-investment-grade companies — the same universe as HY bonds, but with different structural features. They are the primary debt instrument in private equity buyouts, sitting at the top of the capital structure with floating rates and tight covenants. The leveraged loan market is closely linked to CLOs (Collateralised Loan Obligations).","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Leveraged Loan Features","body":"Key structural features of leveraged loans (also called \"Term Loans\"):\n\nFloating rate: SOFR (or SONIA in GBP) + a margin (typically 300-500bps). Unlike HY bonds, coupon rises with rates — beneficial for investors when rates rise.\n\n**Amortisation**: Term Loan B (TLB) — the most common type in PE buyouts — amortises at 1% per year with a bullet at **maturity** (5-7 years). Term Loan A (TLA) amortises more quickly.\n\nSenior secured, first lien: leveraged loans rank above HY bonds in the **capital structure** and are secured against company assets. Higher recovery rates (60-80%) than unsecured HY bonds.","bullets":["Floating rate: SOFR/SONIA + margin (no fixed coupon)","TLB: 1% annual amortisation + bullet — common in LBOs","Senior secured first lien: recovers more than HY bonds in default"],"icon":"document"},{"heading":"Maintenance Covenants","body":"Leveraged loans traditionally carried maintenance covenants — quarterly financial tests that must be passed regardless of company actions:\n\n— Maximum **leverage**: Debt/**EBITDA** ≤ 5.5× (tested every quarter)\n— Minimum interest coverage: EBITDA/interest ≥ 2.0× (tested every quarter)\n\nIf breached, lenders can demand immediate repayment (\"acceleration\") or negotiate an amendment (for a fee). Maintenance covenants give lenders early warning and intervention rights.\n\nHowever, the \"**covenant**-lite\" trend: since 2012, most US TLBs are now \"cov-lite\" — no maintenance covenants, just incurrence tests (mirroring HY bonds). European loans are also trending this way. Cov-lite shifts power from lenders to sponsors.","bullets":["Maintenance covenants tested quarterly — any quarter breach = technical default","Covenant-lite (cov-lite): no maintenance tests — now standard in US TLBs","Cov-lite loans transfer power to PE sponsors, away from lenders"],"icon":"shield-checkmark"},{"heading":"The Syndication Process","body":"Leveraged loans are originated by banks but typically syndicated (sold) to institutional investors. The process:\n\n1. Underwrite: the arranging bank commits to provide the full loan.\n2. Syndicate: the bank markets the loan to institutional investors (CLO managers, loan mutual funds, insurance companies) at a spread and price.\n3. Price flex: if demand is weak, the bank \"flexes\" the margin higher to attract buyers; if oversubscribed, spreads tighten.\n4. Secondary trading: loans trade OTC in the **secondary market** at prices close to par.\n\nCLOs (Collateralised Loan Obligations) are the largest buyers of leveraged loans — they purchase diversified pools of loans, finance themselves with tranched notes, and provide a critical source of demand.","bullets":["Banks originate, then syndicate to institutional investors","Price flex: margin adjusts based on investor demand","CLOs are the dominant buyer of US leveraged loans (~65% of market)"],"icon":"people"},{"heading":"Loans vs. Bonds in an LBO","body":"In a **leveraged buyout**, the capital structure typically layers both loans and bonds:\n\nFirst lien Term Loan B (floating, SOFR + 350bps, 7yr): the largest tranche, cheapest, most institutional demand. Provided by CLOs and loan funds.\n\nSecond lien or HY Bonds (fixed, 8%, 8yr): more expensive, longer-dated, absorbs more risk. Provided by HY funds.\n\nThe mix depends on market conditions. In a \"loan-friendly\" environment, sponsors maximise TLBs (no fixed-rate risk, flexible prepayment). In a \"bond-friendly\" environment (low HY yields), they mix in fixed-rate bonds to lock in rates.\n\nPE sponsors prefer floating-rate debt when they plan to repay early (no call premium on loans vs 101-104% call premium on HY bonds in the first 2-4 years).","icon":"git-compare"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"Interest rates rise sharply. Which instrument benefits most relative to a fixed-rate high yield bond — a floating-rate leveraged loan or a fixed-rate HY bond?","options":["The fixed-rate high-yield bond, since its coupon is locked in and simply does not change with rates","Floating-rate leveraged loan — the coupon rises with rates, so the investor earns more","Both benefit equally — credit spreads compress when rates rise","Neither — leveraged borrowers default more when rates rise"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Floating-rate loans pay SOFR/SONIA + a fixed spread. When base rates rise, the coupon rises too — benefiting loan investors. Fixed-rate HY bond investors are locked into their coupon and suffer price falls as discount rates rise (duration risk). This is why loans outperformed HY bonds in 2022-23 when the Fed raised rates aggressively. Loan investors also sit above bond holders in the capital structure for additional protection."},{"id":"t7-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"A leveraged loan has a maintenance covenant: Debt/EBITDA ≤ 5.5×. The company's EBITDA drops and leverage reaches 6.0×. What happens?","options":["Nothing immediately — the covenant only triggers at the next scheduled repayment date","The company is in technical default — lenders can demand repayment or negotiate an amendment","The interest rate automatically steps up by 100bps as a penalty","The company must issue equity to pay down debt within 30 days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Breaching a maintenance covenant triggers a technical default. Lenders can then accelerate repayment (demand immediate full repayment) or, more commonly, negotiate a waiver or amendment (for a fee and possibly tighter terms). This early intervention right is the key advantage of maintenance covenants for lenders — they can restructure before the company actually misses a payment, while there is still time and value to preserve."},{"id":"t7-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"CLOs are the dominant buyer of US leveraged loans. What do CLOs do with the loans they purchase?","options":["Hold all loans to maturity and pass coupon income directly to equity investors","Pool diversified loans, then issue tranched notes (AAA down to equity) backed by the pool","Immediately syndicate the loans to retail bond investors","Convert loans into fixed-rate bonds for sale to insurance companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CLOs (Collateralised Loan Obligations) buy pools of 100-250 leveraged loans, then issue structured notes (tranches) backed by that pool: AAA-rated senior notes (lowest yield, highest protection), AA, A, BBB, BB, B, and an equity tranche (highest yield, first loss). The senior tranches are bought by banks and insurance companies; equity by hedge funds and CLO managers. CLO structures provide credit enhancement through diversification and subordination."},{"id":"t7-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"Why do PE sponsors generally prefer Term Loan B over fixed-rate HY bonds when planning to repay debt early?","options":["Term loan Bs carry lower interest rates than high-yield bonds across essentially all market conditions","TLBs have no call premium — repayable at par anytime, unlike HY bonds' early-year call premiums","TLBs are senior secured while HY bonds are always unsecured","Regulatory requirements force LBOs to use loans rather than bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Term Loan B allows prepayment at par at any time (or with a small 1% soft-call for 6 months). HY bonds have non-call periods of 2-4 years followed by declining call premiums (104, 102, 101, then par). If a PE sponsor sells the company in year 2-3, redeeming HY bonds costs 104% of face — a significant penalty. TLBs avoid this, making them preferred in shorter-hold or uncertain-exit situations."}]},"t7-m2-l3":{"id":"t7-m2-l3","title":"Structured Credit","trackId":"t7","trackTitle":"Credit Markets","color":"#DC2626","premium":false,"intro":"Structured credit transforms pools of loans or other assets into tranched securities with different risk profiles. CLOs (backed by leveraged loans), ABS (backed by consumer debt), CMBS (commercial mortgages), and RMBS (residential mortgages) are the main types. Understanding the mechanics of securitisation and tranching is essential for credit markets roles.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The Securitisation Concept","body":"**Securitisation** pools financial assets (loans, mortgages, credit card **receivables**) into a Special Purpose Vehicle (SPV), which then issues securities backed by the cashflows from that pool. The key innovation: tranching.\n\nTranching creates securities with different **seniority**. If the pool suffers losses, they are absorbed from the bottom up — first the equity tranche, then mezzanine, then senior. The senior tranches (AAA-rated) are protected by the \"credit enhancement\" provided by subordinate tranches below them.\n\nExample: a pool of 100 loans with an expected loss of 3%. If the equity tranche absorbs the first 10% of losses, the AAA senior tranche faces losses only if defaults exceed 10% — far above historical averages for a diversified pool.","bullets":["SPV: legal entity that holds the pool and issues notes","Tranching: losses absorbed bottom-up (equity → mezzanine → senior)","Credit enhancement: subordination protects senior tranches"],"icon":"layers"},{"heading":"CLO Structure","body":"A CLO (Collateralised Loan Obligation) is backed by a pool of 100-250 leveraged loans. The CLO manager actively manages the pool within agreed guidelines (industry concentration limits, minimum diversity scores, minimum weighted average spread).\n\n**Capital structure** (typical):\nAAA senior notes: ~65% of capital, SOFR + 130bps\nAA notes: ~10%, SOFR + 185bps\nA notes: ~6%, SOFR + 220bps\nBBB notes: ~5%, SOFR + 320bps\nBB notes: ~4%, SOFR + 600bps\nEquity: ~10%, residual cashflows after note payments\n\nThe equity (first-loss) tranche earns excess spread — the difference between loan income and note interest costs. In good times, this can return 15-20% annually; in a credit downturn, equity can be wiped out.","bullets":["AAA tranche: lowest risk, lowest spread, first to be repaid","Equity tranche: residual cashflow after notes — first loss, highest potential return","Manager actively buys and sells loans within agreed parameters"],"icon":"git-branch"},{"heading":"ABS and RMBS","body":"Asset-backed securities (ABS) securitise consumer debt: credit card receivables, auto loans, student loans. The structure is similar to CLOs but the **underlying** assets differ — consumer loans are more granular, shorter-dated, and less correlated than leveraged loans.\n\nRMBS (Residential Mortgage-Backed Securities) pool home loans. Agency RMBS (backed by Fannie Mae/Freddie Mac in the US) carry implicit government guarantees and are among the most liquid fixed income instruments. Non-agency RMBS (private-label) are not guaranteed — the subprime RMBS market was at the centre of the 2008 financial crisis.\n\nCMBS (Commercial Mortgage-Backed Securities) pool commercial real estate loans (offices, retail, hotels). Subordination levels are higher than RMBS because commercial properties have more concentrated risk.","icon":"home"},{"heading":"Risks in Structured Credit","body":"Key risks that 2008 exposed:\n\nCorrelation risk: securitisation models assumed **diversification** reduced risk. But in a systemic event, correlations spike — all mortgages default together. AAA tranches proved not as protected as models suggested.\n\nModel risk: rating agencies' models had flawed assumptions about house price appreciation and regional **independence**. Structured credit is highly model-dependent; small assumption changes create large rating differences.\n\nLiquidity risk: ABS markets can become completely illiquid during stress. Unlike government bonds, there is no \"buyer of last resort\" in private structured credit — bid-ask spreads can gap from 1% to 10%+ in a crisis.\n\nContagion: securitisation spreads risk widely across the financial system — both an efficiency benefit and a systemic risk amplifier.","bullets":["Correlation spike in stress: diversification fails when it's needed most","Model risk: small assumption changes → large rating/price differences","Liquidity disappears in structured credit during market stress"],"icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t7-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"In a CLO, losses from defaulted loans are absorbed in what order?","options":["Pro-rata across all tranches, including AAA","Senior AAA notes first, then mezzanine, then equity last","Equity tranche first, then mezzanine tranches, then senior notes last","Equally split between mezzanine and equity only"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Losses are absorbed from the bottom up: equity (first loss) absorbs the first 10% or so of losses. If losses exceed equity, mezzanine tranches (BB, B, BBB) are impaired. Only if losses exceed all subordinate tranches do senior AAA notes face losses. This tranching structure is the core of structured credit — it creates high-rated senior securities from a pool of lower-rated loans by concentrating expected losses in the equity tranche."},{"id":"t7-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"Why was the AAA rating on subprime RMBS tranches during 2005-2007 flawed?","options":["The rating agencies simply used the wrong discount rate when they valued the underlying securities","Models assumed low default correlation, but a national housing bust hit all regions at once","Subprime mortgages should never have been included in securitisations","The SPVs were set up fraudulently, making the underlying mortgages worthless"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The fatal flaw was correlation. Rating models assumed mortgages in Texas, Florida, Nevada, and California would not all default together — historical data showed low regional correlation. When house prices fell nationwide (an unprecedented event in the post-war era), defaults became highly correlated across all regions and borrower types. The credit enhancement designed to protect AAA tranches (assuming independent defaults) was far too thin for a correlated shock. The \"diversification\" benefit was illusory."},{"id":"t7-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"A CLO equity investor earns the \"excess spread.\" What does this mean?","options":["They receive a fixed coupon that is set at a level above the BBB-rated tranche's coupon rate","They get the residual cashflows after all note holders are paid — loan income minus note interest","They receive excess principal repayments before any other investor","They earn a spread above government bonds, like all other CLO tranches"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CLO equity sits at the bottom of the capital structure and receives whatever is left after paying interest and principal to all note tranches (AAA down to BB). If the loan pool yields SOFR + 380bps on average and the weighted cost of notes is SOFR + 165bps, the excess spread is ~215bps — which on a leveraged basis (equity is ~10% of the structure) can equate to 15-20%+ equity returns in good times. Equity is first loss but potentially highest return."},{"id":"t7-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"Agency RMBS (Fannie Mae/Freddie Mac) are considered among the safest and most liquid fixed income instruments. Non-agency RMBS are not. What is the key difference?","options":["Agency RMBS carry lower coupons purely because they hold only prime, top-quality mortgages in the pool","Agency RMBS carry an implicit US government guarantee on principal and interest; non-agency RMBS do not","Non-agency RMBS are issued by foreign banks and carry FX risk","Agency RMBS can only be purchased by US pension funds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Agency RMBS issued by Fannie Mae (FNMA) and Freddie Mac (FHLMC) carry an implicit (now explicit, post-2008 conservatorship) US government guarantee on timely payment of principal and interest. This eliminates credit risk and makes them extremely liquid — the Fed buys agency MBS as part of QE. Non-agency (private-label) RMBS have no such guarantee — credit quality depends entirely on the underlying mortgage pool, which is why subprime non-agency MBS suffered catastrophic losses."}]},"t8-m1-l1":{"id":"t8-m1-l1","title":"GDP & Economic Indicators","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the total monetary value of all goods and services produced within a country in a given period. It is the headline measure of economic size and growth — watched by investors, policymakers, and central banks as the primary indicator of economic health.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Expenditure Approach","body":"**GDP** = C + I + G + (X − M)\n\nC (Consumption): household spending on goods and services — the largest component (~70% in developed economies).\n\nI (Investment): business capital expenditure, construction, and changes in inventories. Note: \"investment\" in GDP means physical capital formation, not buying stocks or bonds.\n\nG (Government spending): public sector expenditure on goods and services (not transfer payments like benefits — those are excluded).\n\nNet exports (X − M): exports minus imports. A trade surplus adds to GDP; a **trade deficit** subtracts.","bullets":["C is ~65-70% of GDP in most developed economies","Investment includes capex, construction, inventory build — not financial investment","Government transfers (welfare, pensions) are excluded from G"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Real vs Nominal GDP","body":"**Nominal** GDP measures output in current prices. If both production and prices rise 5%, nominal GDP grows 10% — but living standards only improved by the volume growth.\n\nReal GDP adjusts for price changes using a base year, isolating true volume growth. The GDP deflator converts between the two:\n\nReal GDP = (Nominal GDP / GDP Deflator) × 100\n\nGrowth rates are almost always quoted in **real terms** to allow meaningful comparison across time and countries. A country with 10% nominal GDP growth but 8% inflation has only grown 2% in real terms — barely above stagnation.","bullets":["Nominal GDP: current prices (includes inflation)","Real GDP: constant prices (strips out inflation)","GDP deflator: broader price index than CPI (covers whole economy)"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Leading, Lagging & Coincident Indicators","body":"Economic indicators are classified by their timing relative to the cycle:\n\nLeading indicators: change before the economy turns — useful for forecasting. Examples: PMI (Purchasing Managers' Index), **yield curve** slope, housing starts, consumer confidence, equity prices.\n\nLagging indicators: confirm a trend after it has started. Examples: **unemployment** rate (rises after **recession** starts, falls after recovery is established), CPI inflation, corporate profits.\n\nCoincident indicators: move with the economy in real time. Examples: industrial production, retail sales, non-farm payrolls (monthly US employment report).\n\nThe PMI is the most widely watched leading indicator. A reading above 50 signals expansion; below 50, contraction.","bullets":["PMI > 50 = expansion; < 50 = contraction — most-watched leading indicator","Unemployment is a classic lagging indicator — it peaks after recessions","Non-farm payrolls (US) is a coincident indicator; released first Friday each month"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"GDP Per Capita and Productivity","body":"GDP per capita (GDP ÷ population) measures average living standards and allows meaningful cross-country comparisons. A country with 10% GDP growth but 10% population growth has zero improvement in living standards per person.\n\nProductivity — output per worker or per hour worked — is the foundation of long-run growth. If each worker produces more, the economy grows without needing more people or capital.\n\nTotal Factor Productivity (TFP) captures improvements from technology and innovation — the \"residual\" after accounting for labour and capital inputs. TFP growth drove the post-war economic boom in developed economies and the growth miracles of Asian economies.\n\nGDP **purchasing power parity** (PPP): adjusts for price level differences, allowing fairer comparison. China's GDP at PPP is roughly equal to the US because prices are lower in China.","icon":"people"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"Using the expenditure approach, which component of GDP is typically largest in a developed economy?","options":["Government spending (G)","Business investment (I)","Household consumption (C)","Net exports (X − M)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Household consumption (C) accounts for roughly 65-70% of GDP in most developed economies. In the US it is closer to 70%. This is why consumer confidence and retail sales data are so closely watched — a slowdown in consumer spending (which makes up most of GDP) has an outsized impact on economic growth. Government spending is typically 15-25%, investment 15-20%, and net exports can be positive or negative."},{"id":"t8-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"Nominal GDP grew 8% last year. Inflation was 5%. What was real GDP growth?","options":["13%","8%","3%","5%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Real GDP growth ≈ Nominal GDP growth − Inflation rate = 8% − 5% = 3%. (The precise calculation uses the GDP deflator: Real growth = (1.08/1.05) − 1 ≈ 2.86%.) Real GDP strips out the price level change to show actual volume growth. Investors always focus on real GDP growth because nominal growth can be inflated (literally) by rising prices without representing an improvement in economic output."},{"id":"t8-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"The PMI (Purchasing Managers' Index) falls from 52 to 47 in a single month. What does this signal?","options":["Inflation is rising quickly — the price level is now 47% higher than it was a year ago","The manufacturing or services sector is contracting — the index has crossed below 50","GDP grew at 47% last quarter — a strong reading","Employment fell by 47,000 — a modest but manageable decline"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PMI is a survey-based leading indicator with a neutral level of 50. Above 50 = expansion (more businesses reporting growth than contraction); below 50 = contraction. A drop from 52 to 47 signals the sector has shifted from growing to shrinking — a significant deterioration. PMI moves ahead of official GDP data by weeks, making it one of the most closely watched early warning signals."},{"id":"t8-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"Why is GDP per capita a more useful measure of living standards than total GDP?","options":["It carefully adjusts for the effects of inflation, unlike the standard headline GDP figure","It accounts for population size — a large country can have high GDP but low living standards per person","It excludes government spending, which does not reflect private wealth","It is measured in purchasing power parity (PPP) terms automatically"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Total GDP measures economic size but not living standards. India's total GDP is larger than Denmark's, but its GDP per capita is far lower because the output is spread across 1.4 billion people. GDP per capita (GDP / population) tells you the average output per person — a better proxy for living standards. PPP adjustment is a separate additional refinement that accounts for price level differences between countries."}]},"t8-m1-l2":{"id":"t8-m1-l2","title":"Inflation","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"Inflation is the rate at which the general price level rises over time, reducing the purchasing power of money. It is one of the most important variables in finance — driving central bank policy, bond yields, equity valuations, and currency movements. Understanding its causes, measures, and market implications is essential.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Measuring Inflation: CPI and PCE","body":"Two main **inflation** measures:\n\nCPI (Consumer Price Index): tracks a fixed basket of goods and services representative of household spending. Used for inflation-linked bonds, benefit adjustments, and public reporting. The UK uses CPIH (which includes owner-occupied housing costs).\n\nPCE (Personal Consumption Expenditure): the US Federal Reserve's preferred measure. Adjusts the basket over time to reflect actual spending (chain-weighted), so it tends to run slightly below CPI. Core PCE (excluding food and energy) is the Fed's target variable.\n\nCore inflation excludes food and energy — which are volatile and supply-driven — to capture the **underlying** demand-driven price trend. Central banks focus on **core inflation** as the policy-relevant signal.","bullets":["CPI: fixed basket — the public headline measure","PCE: adjustable basket — the Fed's preferred measure (tends to be ~0.3pp below CPI)","Core inflation: ex-food and energy — the signal central banks target"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Causes of Inflation","body":"Three main types:\n\nDemand-pull: too much money chasing too few goods. When the economy overheats — low **unemployment**, strong consumer spending — firms raise prices. \"Demand-pull\" is inflation driven by strong demand exceeding supply capacity.\n\nCost-push: rising input costs are passed through to consumers. Oil price spikes (1970s), supply chain disruption (2021-22 COVID era), or wage-push inflation in tight labour markets. This type is harder for central banks to address without triggering a **recession**.\n\nExpectations: if workers and firms expect higher future inflation, they demand higher wages and raise prices preemptively — making inflation self-fulfilling. Breaking expectations requires central banks to credibly demonstrate commitment to their inflation target.","bullets":["Demand-pull: strong demand > supply capacity → prices rise","Cost-push: higher input costs (oil, wages, materials) → passed to consumers","Expectations: self-fulfilling — why central banks obsess over credibility"],"icon":"pulse"},{"heading":"The Phillips Curve","body":"The original Phillips Curve (1958) documented an empirical inverse relationship between unemployment and inflation: lower unemployment → higher wages → higher inflation, and vice versa. This became the foundation of macroeconomic policy — the Fed could choose a point on the curve.\n\nThe stagflation of the 1970s (high inflation AND high unemployment simultaneously) broke the original curve. The \"expectations-augmented\" Phillips Curve (Friedman/Phelps) showed the trade-off only holds short-run. Long-run, there is no trade-off — **monetary policy** cannot sustainably boost employment above the \"natural rate\" (NAIRU) without accelerating inflation.\n\nNAIRU (Non-Accelerating Inflation Rate of Unemployment) is the unemployment rate consistent with stable inflation. If unemployment falls below NAIRU, inflation accelerates.","bullets":["Short-run: lower unemployment often ↔ higher inflation (trade-off exists)","Long-run: no trade-off — monetary policy is neutral in the long run","NAIRU: the unemployment floor that central banks try not to breach"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Inflation and Asset Prices","body":"Inflation has different effects across asset classes:\n\nBonds: hurt by inflation. Fixed coupon payments buy less in **real terms**; rising inflation drives up **interest rates**, reducing bond prices (duration risk).\n\nEquities: mixed. Moderate inflation is often fine — companies pass cost increases to consumers. But high inflation typically means higher rates, which compresses PE multiples (discount rate rises). Financials can benefit; utilities, tech growth stocks suffer.\n\nReal assets (property, commodities, infrastructure): hedge against inflation — their revenues or values often rise with prices. Gold is the traditional inflation hedge, though its relationship is imperfect.\n\nTIPS/Index-linked gilts: government bonds with principal linked to CPI — they guarantee real returns regardless of inflation.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"The US Federal Reserve targets \"core PCE inflation\" at 2%. Why core PCE rather than headline CPI?","options":["The CPI is considerably harder to calculate accurately and is therefore the less reliable gauge","Core PCE strips out food and energy and uses an adjustable basket, tracking underlying inflation","PCE always runs higher than CPI, giving the Fed more headroom","CPI is not published monthly, making it too infrequent for policy use"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed prefers core PCE for two reasons: (1) it excludes food and energy, which are volatile and supply-driven rather than reflecting underlying demand conditions; (2) the PCE basket is chain-weighted (updated to reflect actual spending), unlike the fixed CPI basket. Core PCE typically runs slightly below core CPI. The Fed targets 2% as their long-run inflation goal — this is the anchor for all rate decisions."},{"id":"t8-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"In 2021-22, inflation surged globally despite central banks not having significantly stimulated demand. What type of inflation was this primarily?","options":["Demand-pull — consumers spent stimulus cheques on goods","Cost-push — supply chain disruptions and energy prices raised input costs sharply","Hyperinflation — money supply expanded beyond any control","Expectations-driven — workers demanded higher wages before prices rose"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The 2021-22 inflation was primarily cost-push: COVID-19 disrupted global supply chains (semiconductors, shipping costs surged 10×), energy prices spiked (especially European gas after the Ukraine war), and food commodity prices rose. Demand-pull played a role too (COVID fiscal transfers boosted consumer spending), making it a mixed shock — but the supply disruption element was dominant and harder to address with rate hikes alone."},{"id":"t8-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"What does NAIRU represent in the Phillips Curve framework?","options":["The inflation rate at which unemployment is maximised","The unemployment rate consistent with stable, non-accelerating inflation","The national average unemployment rate published monthly","The natural interest rate that keeps the economy in equilibrium"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"NAIRU — Non-Accelerating Inflation Rate of Unemployment — is the unemployment rate below which inflation begins to accelerate. It reflects structural features of the labour market (job-matching friction, skills mismatches, geographic mobility). If the Fed pushed unemployment below NAIRU persistently, it would cause rising wages → rising prices → self-reinforcing inflation spiral. Estimating NAIRU in real-time is hard — the Fed's estimates have changed significantly over decades."},{"id":"t8-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"Inflation rises sharply to 8%. What is the likely impact on 10-year government bond prices?","options":["Bond prices rise — investors buy bonds as an inflation hedge","Bond prices fall — higher inflation drives up yields, which pushes prices down","Bond prices are unaffected — they are guaranteed by the government","Bond prices rise initially, then fall as inflation persists"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Bond prices and yields move inversely. High inflation erodes the real value of fixed coupon payments AND prompts central banks to raise interest rates (market yields rise). When yields rise, existing bond prices fall. A 10-year bond with duration of ~8 years would fall roughly 8% for every 1% rise in yields — so a 2% yield spike from inflation concerns would cause ~16% losses. This is why the 2022 bond sell-off was the worst in decades when inflation hit 8-9%."}]},"t8-m1-l3":{"id":"t8-m1-l3","title":"The Business Cycle","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"The business cycle describes the recurring fluctuation of economic activity between periods of expansion (growth) and contraction (recession). Understanding where the economy sits in the cycle — and how different assets behave at each phase — is fundamental to macro investing and economic analysis.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Four Phases","body":"The classic business cycle has four phases:\n\n1. Expansion: **GDP** growth above trend, falling **unemployment**, rising business investment and consumer confidence. Lasts months to years.\n\n2. Peak: maximum economic output. Inflation typically rising; employment at or near \"full.\" The Fed is often tightening.\n\n3. **Recession**: two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth (technical definition) or a broad contraction in economic activity. Rising unemployment, falling business investment, tightening credit.\n\n4. Trough: minimum output; the bottom of the cycle before recovery begins. Often characterised by maximum pessimism, yet historically the best time to buy risk assets.\n\nRecovery: the early expansion phase — often GDP growth accelerates sharply as pent-up demand is released.","bullets":["Recession: two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth (technical definition)","Trough: the bottom — equity markets often turn before the economy does","Full cycle length varies widely: 2-10 years typical"],"icon":"sync"},{"heading":"Cyclical vs Defensive Sectors","body":"Different equity sectors respond differently to the cycle:\n\nCyclical sectors (outperform in expansion): Consumer Discretionary (luxury, travel), Financials, Industrials, Energy, Materials. Their revenues are highly correlated with economic activity.\n\nDefensive sectors (hold up in recessions): Consumer Staples (food, household products), Healthcare, Utilities. Demand for their products is relatively inelastic — people still buy food and medicine in recessions.\n\nSector rotation strategy: shift from cyclicals to defensives as the cycle peaks; rotate back into cyclicals early in recovery. Timing this perfectly is nearly impossible, but understanding relative performance helps portfolio positioning.","bullets":["Cyclicals (discretionary, financials): amplify the cycle — big swings","Defensives (staples, healthcare, utilities): stable demand through recessions","Financials: interest rate sensitive — benefit from steepening yield curve in expansion"],"icon":"apps"},{"heading":"Yield Curve and the Cycle","body":"The **yield curve** — plotting **government bond** yields by maturity — is one of the most reliable leading indicators of recessions.\n\nNormal (upward-sloping): long-term rates above short-term rates. Signals a healthy, growing economy with inflation expectations.\n\nFlat: short and long rates are similar — often signals a late cycle with the Fed tightening.\n\nInverted (short rates > long rates): historically predicts recessions with ~12-18 month lead time. The 2-10 year spread (10yr yield minus 2yr yield) is the most watched. Every US recession since 1970 was preceded by inversion.\n\nInversion mechanism: the Fed hikes short-term rates to slow inflation; long-term growth expectations fall; investors buy long-dated bonds (driving yields down). The spread compresses or inverts.","bullets":["Inverted yield curve: 2yr > 10yr — recession indicator with ~12-18 month lead","Every US recession since 1970 was preceded by yield curve inversion","Mechanism: Fed hikes short rates; long rates fall on growth fears"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"The Credit Cycle","body":"Running alongside the business cycle is the credit cycle: the expansion and contraction of lending standards and credit availability.\n\nIn expansion: banks loosen lending standards, credit grows rapidly, **leverage** builds up across households, corporations, and financial institutions.\n\nAt the peak: debt levels are high, asset prices are elevated, credit spreads are tight. The system becomes fragile.\n\nIn recession: banks tighten, credit contracts, asset prices fall, debtors default. The \"Minsky moment\" — named after economist Hyman Minsky — describes the sudden collapse of speculative credit structures.\n\nThe credit cycle tends to amplify the business cycle. The 2008 financial crisis was a credit cycle collapse (housing debt) layered on top of a business cycle recession — hence its severity.","icon":"card"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"Which of the following best represents a \"cyclical\" sector that would outperform during economic expansion?","options":["Consumer Staples — people always need food and cleaning products","Utilities — regulated revenues are stable regardless of GDP","Consumer Discretionary — luxury spending and travel surge when incomes rise","Healthcare — demographics drive demand regardless of the cycle"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Consumer Discretionary (restaurants, travel, luxury goods, entertainment) is highly cyclical — spending in this category is the first to be cut in recessions and the first to surge in recoveries. Consumer Staples, Utilities, and Healthcare are defensive — demand is relatively inelastic to economic conditions. The sector rotation strategy involves moving from defensives to cyclicals early in expansion, and back again approaching the peak."},{"id":"t8-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"The 2-year US Treasury yield rises to 5.2% while the 10-year yield sits at 4.1%. What does this \"inverted yield curve\" signal?","options":["The Federal Reserve is cutting interest rates aggressively","The bond market anticipates a recession — it is pricing in rate cuts ahead","Short-term bonds are riskier than long-term bonds, justifying higher yields","Inflation is expected to rise sharply in the near term, boosting short rates"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An inverted yield curve (short-term yields above long-term yields) is the bond market's signal that it expects rates to fall in the future — typically because a recession is coming and the Fed will need to cut. The 2-year yield reflects expectations for the Fed funds rate over two years; the 10-year reflects long-run growth and inflation expectations. When investors expect slower growth ahead, they buy long-dated bonds (pushing 10yr yields down), while the Fed's current tight policy keeps short rates high."},{"id":"t8-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"What is a \"Minsky moment\" in the context of the credit cycle?","options":["The precise point at which overall GDP growth peaks out and then begins to slow down","The sudden collapse of speculative credit structures as asset prices fall and borrowers default","A central bank intervention to support markets during a liquidity crisis","The moment unemployment reaches its trough in the recovery phase"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hyman Minsky observed that stability breeds instability. In long expansions, investors take on progressively more risk and leverage because nothing has gone wrong. When the catalyst arrives (a rate hike, a credit default, an external shock), the speculative credit structure collapses: asset prices fall, margin calls force selling, banks tighten credit, and the self-reinforcing cycle compounds. The 2008 collapse of the housing-backed credit system is the canonical Minsky moment."},{"id":"t8-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"A portfolio manager believes the economy is approaching a cyclical peak. Which sector rotation would be consistent with this view?","options":["Increase Financials and Consumer Discretionary holdings — they reliably outperform at cycle peaks","Rotate from cyclicals into defensives (Staples, Healthcare, Utilities) ahead of the expected slowdown","Buy commodities — they always outperform when the economy peaks","Move entirely into cash — equities always fall at the peak"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As the economy approaches a peak, cyclical sectors have already outperformed significantly and are increasingly vulnerable to the forthcoming slowdown. Rotating into defensives (Consumer Staples, Healthcare, Utilities) provides earnings stability through the downturn. Cash is too defensive and gives up equity upside if the peak is misjudged. The challenge: timing the peak precisely is very difficult — rotations often happen too early or too late."}]},"t8-m2-l1":{"id":"t8-m2-l1","title":"Monetary Policy","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"Monetary policy is the use of interest rates and money supply tools by central banks to achieve their mandates — typically price stability (low inflation) and maximum employment. The Federal Reserve, ECB, Bank of England, and Bank of Japan collectively influence global financial conditions. Understanding their toolkits and signalling language is essential for anyone working in markets.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Central Bank Toolkit","body":"Central banks use several tools:\n\n1. Policy rate: the overnight interest rate at which banks lend to each other (Fed Funds rate in the US, Bank Rate in the UK). Raising rates slows borrowing and spending; cutting stimulates.\n\n2. **Quantitative Easing** (QE): the **central bank** purchases government bonds (and sometimes corporate bonds/MBS) from the market, injecting money and pushing long-term yields down. Used when the policy rate hits the zero lower bound.\n\n3. Quantitative Tightening (QT): the reverse — the central bank shrinks its **balance sheet** by selling assets or allowing them to mature without reinvestment. Removes liquidity from the system.\n\n4. Forward guidance: communicating future policy intentions (\"rates will stay lower for longer\") to shape market expectations without necessarily acting.","bullets":["Policy rate: the primary short-term tool — raises cost of borrowing economy-wide","QE: buys bonds to push long rates down when short rates are at the floor","Forward guidance: words move markets — central bank communication is itself a policy tool"],"icon":"build"},{"heading":"The Transmission Mechanism","body":"How do rate changes reach the real economy?\n\n1. Bank lending channel: higher rates → banks raise mortgage and loan rates → households and businesses borrow less, spend less.\n\n2. Asset price channel: higher rates → lower equity and property valuations (higher discount rates) → \"wealth effect\" reduces spending.\n\n3. **Exchange rate** channel: higher rates → currency appreciates (capital inflows) → exports become less competitive, imports cheaper → reduces demand and inflation.\n\n4. Expectations channel: credible rate hikes → reduce **inflation expectations** → prevent wage-price spirals.\n\nThe time lag is significant: rate hikes typically take 12-24 months to fully work through the economy. The Fed's \"long and variable lags\" mean policy mistakes are difficult to correct quickly.","bullets":["Bank lending: higher rates → more expensive mortgages/loans → less spending","Asset prices: higher rates → lower valuations → reduced wealth effect","Lags: 12-24 months for full effect — monetary policy is a slow instrument"],"icon":"git-network"},{"heading":"QE and Market Impact","body":"Quantitative Easing (QE) was introduced at scale by the Fed after 2008 and became standard crisis policy. The Fed, ECB, and BoJ all used it extensively in 2020.\n\nMechanism: by purchasing government bonds, the central bank pushes up their price (reducing yields). Investors displaced from government bonds are forced into riskier assets (equities, corporate bonds) — the \"portfolio balance\" or \"risk-on\" effect.\n\nMarket impact: QE suppressed risk-free rates, compressed credit spreads, and supported equity valuations throughout the 2010s. Critics argue it inflated asset bubbles and increased inequality (asset holders benefited most).\n\nQT (**Quantitative Tightening**) does the reverse: by reducing bond holdings, the central bank allows yields to rise organically. The Fed's 2022-23 QT cycle combined with aggressive rate hikes caused significant financial market volatility.","bullets":["QE: buy bonds → yields fall → investors take more risk → equities/credit rally","\"Portfolio balance effect\": investors pushed into riskier assets","QT: shrink balance sheet → yields rise → risk assets face headwinds"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Central Bank Independence and Credibility","body":"Central bank independence — insulating **monetary policy** from political pressure — is considered essential for credibility. Politicians prefer lower rates (stimulative before elections); independent central banks can raise rates despite political pressure.\n\nInflation targeting (2% in most developed economies) provides an anchor for expectations. The Bank of England adopted it in 1992; the Fed formally adopted 2% PCE as a target in 2012.\n\nLoss of credibility is the primary risk: if markets believe the central bank will not raise rates enough to control inflation, inflation expectations become \"unanchored\" and wage-price spirals become self-fulfilling. Paul Volcker's 1979-82 rate hikes (20% Fed Funds rate) were painful but restored Fed credibility after the 1970s inflation era.","bullets":["Independent central banks can raise rates even when politically unpopular","2% inflation target: the global standard anchor for expectations","Unanchored expectations: the nightmare scenario — make inflation self-sustaining"],"icon":"shield"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"The Bank of England raises its Bank Rate from 4.5% to 5.25%. What is the most direct effect on UK households?","options":["Government bond yields fall immediately","Variable-rate mortgage costs rise, reducing disposable income and spending","Sterling weakens, making UK exports more competitive","Corporate tax revenues fall, reducing government spending"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The most direct transmission is through the bank lending channel: variable-rate mortgages and personal loans are repriced immediately. A £300,000 mortgage at variable rate, repriced from 4.5% to 5.25%, adds ~£2,250/year (£187.50/month) to repayments. This directly reduces household disposable income and spending. The UK is particularly sensitive because many mortgages have short fixed-rate terms (2-5 years), meaning a large share reprice quickly when rates change."},{"id":"t8-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"Why did major central banks adopt Quantitative Easing (QE) in 2008 and again in 2020?","options":["Standard rate cuts had reached their limit near zero — QE provided additional stimulus by pushing down long-term yields","Government fiscal spending had become far too high, so QE was needed to help fund the growing deficit","Banks refused to lend to each other — QE replaced the interbank market","CPI inflation was too high — QE was introduced to bring it down"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"QE is deployed when the policy rate hits (or approaches) zero — the zero lower bound. With no more room to cut short-term rates, central banks turned to purchasing long-dated bonds to push down yields across the maturity spectrum and stimulate borrowing and investment. In 2020, the Fed cut rates to 0-0.25% on March 15 and simultaneously launched unlimited QE. In 2008-09, QE was similarly combined with near-zero rates. It is not a substitute for rate policy but an extension of it."},{"id":"t8-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"What is the primary reason central bank independence is considered important?","options":["Independent central banks are able to avoid paying any tax at all on the profits they generate","Politicians prefer lower rates before elections — independent banks can raise rates even when it is politically unpopular","Independent central banks have access to better economic data than governments","Central bank independence eliminates the risk of bank runs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The core rationale for independence is avoiding the \"political business cycle\": politicians prefer loose monetary policy before elections (lower rates, higher employment) and the inflationary consequences arrive after. An independent central bank can raise rates to control inflation even if it is electorally unpopular. Research shows that independent central banks achieve lower inflation on average without sacrificing economic growth — the \"free lunch\" of institutional design."},{"id":"t8-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"The Federal Reserve begins Quantitative Tightening (QT) — reducing its bond holdings. What is the likely market impact?","options":["Equity markets rally strongly, because quantitative tightening removes the risk of future inflation","Long-term Treasury yields fall — less supply as the Fed buys fewer bonds","Long-term Treasury yields tend to rise — the Fed is reducing bond purchases, requiring private investors to absorb more supply","Short-term rates fall immediately — QT targets only the overnight rate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"QT reduces the Fed's bond purchases (or lets bonds mature without reinvestment), which reduces demand for Treasuries. Private investors must absorb the supply instead, typically at higher yields — increasing the term premium. The 2022-23 QT episode contributed to the rise in long-term Treasury yields even as short-term rate hikes slowed. QT primarily affects long-term rates, while the policy rate targets short-term rates — the two tools work together."}]},"t8-m2-l2":{"id":"t8-m2-l2","title":"Fiscal Policy","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"Fiscal policy is the use of government spending and taxation to influence the economy. While monetary policy is conducted by independent central banks, fiscal policy is controlled by elected governments — making it inherently political. It is a powerful cyclical stabiliser and a major source of market risk when debt levels become unsustainable.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Budget Deficits and Surpluses","body":"A fiscal deficit occurs when government spending exceeds tax revenues in a given year. A surplus is the reverse. The deficit must be financed by borrowing — issuing government bonds.\n\nThe national debt (public debt) is the cumulative stock of all outstanding borrowing. Key metric: Debt/**GDP** ratio — this normalises debt by the size of the economy. Japan has debt/GDP >250%; the US ~120%; the UK ~100%; Germany ~65% (pre-COVID).\n\nImportant distinction: the deficit is a flow (annual shortfall); the debt is the stock (total accumulated).\n\nThe \"primary deficit\" excludes interest payments — showing whether the government could balance its books if it had no legacy debt service.","bullets":["Deficit: annual shortfall (flow); Debt: total accumulated borrowing (stock)","Debt/GDP: key sustainability metric — can the economy grow faster than debt?","Primary deficit: budget balance before interest payments"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Keynesian Stimulus","body":"Keynesian economics argues governments should increase spending (and/or cut taxes) during recessions to boost aggregate demand — the \"fiscal multiplier\" amplifies the direct effect.\n\nThe multiplier: if the government spends £1bn and GDP rises by £1.5bn, the multiplier is 1.5. Higher when: **unemployment** is high (spending goes to people who spend it); the economy is below potential; **monetary policy** is accommodative.\n\nAutomatic stabilisers reduce the need for active policy: in recessions, tax revenues fall automatically (fewer taxable incomes, profits) and welfare spending rises (unemployment benefits) — providing stimulus without legislative action.\n\nThe 2008 crisis saw the largest coordinated fiscal stimulus since WWII (G20 coordinated spending increases of ~5% of GDP). COVID in 2020 saw even larger packages — the US CARES Act was $2.2 trillion (10% of GDP).","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Debt Sustainability","body":"Government debt is sustainable if the debt/GDP ratio stabilises or falls over time. The key condition:\n\nIf nominal GDP growth (g) > interest rate on debt (r): the debt/GDP ratio falls automatically (the economy grows faster than the debt burden). Governments only need a primary surplus if r > g.\n\nIf r > g: the debt/GDP ratio spirals upward without a large primary surplus — the debt becomes unsustainable. This is why high-debt countries with weak growth (like some peripheral Eurozone nations in 2011-12) face fiscal crises.\n\nSovereign debt crises: when markets doubt a government's ability or willingness to repay, yields spike (**risk premium** widens), making the deficit harder to finance — a self-fulfilling spiral. Argentina, Greece, and Sri Lanka are recent examples.","bullets":["Sustainability: g > r → debt/GDP falls; r > g → spirals without surplus","Sovereign crisis: yield spike → harder to finance → worse spiral","The ECB's \"whatever it takes\" (Draghi, 2012) broke the Eurozone sovereign crisis"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Crowding Out and Fiscal-Monetary Interaction","body":"Crowding out: when government borrowing competes with private sector for available savings, driving up **interest rates** and reducing private investment. Classical economists argue large deficits crowd out private investment — particularly when the economy is at full capacity.\n\nIn practice, \"crowding out\" is more pronounced when the **central bank** is not accommodating (QE reduces it significantly). At the zero lower bound, there is little crowding out — fiscal multipliers are highest when monetary policy cannot respond.\n\nFiscal-monetary coordination: in extreme events (COVID, GFC), central banks buy the bonds governments issue to fund stimulus. This fiscal-monetary coordination (critics call it \"monetary financing\" or \"printing money\") blurs the line between the two arms of policy and is inflation-risk if sustained.","icon":"shuffle"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"The UK government runs a £100bn deficit this year. The existing national debt is £2.5 trillion. How does this change the debt/GDP ratio if nominal GDP is £2.2 trillion?","options":["The deficit is the same as the debt — they are both measures of government borrowing","New debt is £100bn; total debt becomes £2.6 trillion; debt/GDP rises from 114% to 118%","Debt/GDP falls because the deficit is smaller than the existing debt","The ratio is unchanged — deficits affect the deficit, not the debt"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The deficit (£100bn) is the annual flow; it adds to the stock of debt. New total debt = £2.5tn + £0.1tn = £2.6tn. Debt/GDP = £2.6tn / £2.2tn = 118%. Previously it was £2.5tn / £2.2tn = 114%. Each year of deficit running above the nominal GDP growth rate causes the debt/GDP ratio to rise. Reducing this ratio requires either running a surplus, growing the economy faster, or reducing the real value of debt through inflation."},{"id":"t8-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"During a recession, tax revenues automatically fall and welfare spending automatically rises, even without any new government legislation. What are these called?","options":["Discretionary fiscal policy — the government is actively managing the economy","Automatic stabilisers — they dampen the cycle without legislative action","Monetary policy spillovers — the fiscal effect of interest rate changes","Crowding out — the government takes resources from the private sector"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Automatic stabilisers are structural features of the fiscal system that counter economic cycles automatically: in recessions, income tax revenues fall (fewer people employed, lower corporate profits) and welfare spending rises (more unemployment claims) — providing a natural stimulus without any policy action. In expansions, the reverse: rising tax revenues and falling welfare spending act as a brake. Automatic stabilisers reduce economic volatility without the legislative delays of discretionary policy."},{"id":"t8-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"A country has nominal GDP growth of 4% but pays 6% average interest on its national debt. The government runs a balanced budget (zero primary deficit). What happens to the debt/GDP ratio?","options":["It falls — balanced budget means no new borrowing","It stays flat — the primary surplus is sufficient to stabilise the ratio","It rises — the interest rate exceeds growth, increasing debt faster than GDP","It falls — GDP growth of 4% outweighs the 6% interest rate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When r > g (interest rate > growth rate), the debt/GDP ratio rises unless the government runs a primary surplus. Even with a balanced primary budget (no new non-interest borrowing), the interest payments grow the debt stock. With r = 6% and g = 4%, debt grows 2% faster than GDP each year — the ratio spirals upward. To stabilise, the government needs a primary surplus equal to (r − g) × debt/GDP. This is the fundamental debt sustainability constraint."},{"id":"t8-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"What is \"crowding out\" in the context of fiscal policy?","options":["Government spending directly replaces private-sector spending, pound for pound, during recessions","Government borrowing competes with private firms for scarce savings, pushing up interest rates","Central bank bond purchases crowd out private creditors from the government bond market","High government debt crowds out private companies from the stock market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Crowding out occurs when government borrowing competes for the same pool of savings as private businesses seeking to invest. In a full-capacity economy, increased government borrowing drives up interest rates, making corporate borrowing more expensive — private investment is \"crowded out.\" However, crowding out is minimal when: (1) the economy has spare capacity, (2) monetary policy is accommodative (QE suppresses rates), or (3) there is a global savings glut (as in the 2000s-2010s). In recessions, fiscal multipliers tend to be high because crowding out is limited."}]},"t8-m2-l3":{"id":"t8-m2-l3","title":"Exchange Rates & FX Markets","trackId":"t8","trackTitle":"Macroeconomics","color":"#7C3AED","premium":false,"intro":"The foreign exchange (FX) market is the largest and most liquid financial market in the world — over $7 trillion traded daily. Exchange rates affect trade competitiveness, inflation, corporate earnings, and cross-border investment flows. Understanding the key theories and drivers of FX rates is fundamental to macro and international finance.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"How Exchange Rates Work","body":"An **exchange rate** is the price of one currency in terms of another. Conventions differ:\n\nDirect quote: domestic currency per unit of foreign currency (e.g. £0.79 per $1 USD).\nIndirect quote: foreign currency per unit of domestic (e.g. $1.27 per £1 GBP).\n\nSpot rate: the rate for immediate delivery (settled T+2). Forward rate: agreed today for delivery at a future date — incorporating interest rate differentials (covered interest parity).\n\nCurrency pairs: EUR/USD (most traded), GBP/USD (cable), USD/JPY, USD/CHF. The quoted rate means \"1 unit of base currency equals X units of quote currency.\" EUR/USD = 1.08 means €1 = $1.08.\n\nBid/ask: FX dealers quote a bid (buy) and ask (sell). The spread is the dealer's margin.","bullets":["EUR/USD = 1.08: \"1 euro buys 1.08 US dollars\" — euro is the base","Spot: T+2 settlement; forward: fixed price for future delivery","Most liquid pair: EUR/USD (~25% of global FX volume)"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Purchasing Power Parity","body":"**Purchasing Power Parity** (PPP) states that exchange rates should equalise the price of identical goods across countries in the long run. The classic illustration: The Economist's Big Mac Index — if a Big Mac costs $5 in the US and £4 in the UK, the implied PPP rate is $5/£4 = $1.25/£1.\n\nIf the actual exchange rate is $1.40/£1, sterling is overvalued versus PPP. Long-run reversion toward PPP is empirically supported — but the \"long run\" can be decades.\n\nPPP fails in the short run due to: non-tradeable goods (haircuts, real estate), trade barriers, and capital flow dynamics. Economists use PPP-adjusted **GDP** to compare living standards across countries (stripping out price level differences).","bullets":["PPP: identical goods should cost the same in different countries when converted","Big Mac Index: simple PPP proxy published by The Economist","Short-run: PPP fails badly; long-run: exchange rates mean-revert toward PPP"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Interest Rate Parity","body":"Interest rate parity links exchange rates and interest rate differentials.\n\nCovered Interest Parity (CIP): the forward exchange rate fully reflects the interest rate differential between two countries. If UK rates are 5% and US rates are 3%, the GBP/USD forward rate will be at a discount — the pound will be cheaper to buy forward to prevent risk-free **arbitrage**.\n\nUncovered Interest Parity (UIP): the expected change in the spot rate should equal the interest rate differential. A higher-interest currency should depreciate by enough to offset the yield advantage — so international investors earn the same return.\n\nIn practice, UIP fails reliably in the short run — the \"carry trade\" exploits this: borrow in a low-interest currency, invest in a high-interest currency, profiting from the yield differential plus (if the currency does not depreciate) the FX component.","bullets":["CIP: forward rate reflects interest differential exactly (no-arbitrage condition)","UIP: theory says high-rate currency depreciates — empirically fails short-term","Carry trade: borrow low, invest high — profitable until \"unwind\" crashes it"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"What Moves Exchange Rates","body":"Short and medium-term FX drivers:\n\n1. Interest rate differentials: higher rates attract capital inflows, strengthening the currency. Fed hikes → USD strengthens (2022).\n\n2. **Current account**: persistent trade deficits create structural selling pressure on the domestic currency (importing more than exporting means selling domestic currency to pay for foreign goods).\n\n3. Risk appetite: \"risk-off\" environments (crises, recessions) → capital flows to safe havens (USD, JPY, CHF). USD is the world's **reserve currency**.\n\n4. Political and policy uncertainty: UK sterling fell sharply after Brexit vote (2016) and Truss Budget (2022) — political risk creates currency volatility.\n\n5. Inflation differentials: higher inflation erodes purchasing power → long-run currency **depreciation** (PPP mechanism).","icon":"trending-up"}],"questions":[{"id":"t8-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"EUR/USD is quoted at 1.08. A UK fund manager converts £1m to EUR, then to USD. EUR/GBP is 0.86. How many USD does the fund receive (approximately)?","options":["$1.08m — just multiply by the EUR/USD rate","$1.26m — £1m converts to €1,162,791, then to ~$1,255,814","$860,000 — the EUR/GBP rate reduces the amount","$0.93m — the inverse of EUR/GBP times EUR/USD"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Step 1: Convert £1m to EUR. EUR/GBP = 0.86 means £0.86 = €1, so £1m = €1m / 0.86 = €1,162,791. Step 2: Convert EUR to USD at EUR/USD 1.08: €1,162,791 × 1.08 = $1,255,814 ≈ $1.26m. Currency conversions require care with direct vs indirect quotes — always check which currency is the base. In practice, FX desks handle this with cross-rate calculations."},{"id":"t8-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"The Big Mac costs $6 in the US and R50 in South Africa. The implied PPP exchange rate is ______. The actual spot rate is R18/$1. What does this suggest about the rand?","options":["The rand is overvalued at R18/$1 — it should be R8.33/$1 per PPP","The rand is undervalued at R18/$1 — it should be R8.33/$1 per PPP","The rand is correctly valued — Big Mac pricing already reflects local wages","The rand is overvalued — a higher rand/dollar rate means the rand is stronger"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PPP rate = R50 / $6 = R8.33 per $1. This means $1 should buy R8.33 at PPP. At R18/$1 (actual rate), you need R18 to buy $1 — the rand is much weaker than PPP implies. Equivalently, the South African Big Mac at R50 costs only $2.78 at spot rate vs $6 in the US — suggesting the rand is cheap. Undervalued currencies can attract investment and exports but may reflect structural weakness (inflation, political risk, current account deficits)."},{"id":"t8-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"An investor borrows JPY (at 0.1% interest), converts to AUD, and invests at 4.5%. This is called a ________ and profits from ________.","options":["Covered interest arbitrage; the profit comes purely from the forward-rate differential itself","Carry trade; the interest rate differential — as long as AUD does not depreciate enough to wipe out the yield","Currency swap; exchanging principal at inception and maturity","Basis trade; the spread between CDS and bond yields"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is a carry trade: borrow in a low-rate currency (JPY, \"funding currency\"), invest in a high-rate currency (AUD, \"carry currency\"), and pocket the yield differential (4.4%). The risk: if AUD depreciates against JPY by more than 4.4%, the trade loses money. Carry trades work well in calm, risk-on environments but unwind violently in crises — the \"carry trade unwind\" causes sharp JPY appreciation as traders close positions simultaneously (2008, 2020)."},{"id":"t8-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"The Federal Reserve signals it will raise interest rates aggressively. What is the typical short-term impact on the US dollar?","options":["The dollar weakens, because higher rates make US goods pricier abroad and reduce overall trade","USD strengthens — higher rates attract capital inflows from global investors seeking higher returns","USD weakens — higher rates hurt corporate profits and reduce equity inflows","USD is unchanged — exchange rates are determined only by trade balances"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Higher interest rates attract international capital seeking better returns — investors sell foreign currencies to buy USD-denominated assets (Treasuries, money market funds), driving USD appreciation. This is the interest rate differential channel. In 2022, the Fed's aggressive hiking cycle drove the DXY (US Dollar Index) up ~15% — the most rapid strengthening in decades. USD strength then creates a tightening of global financial conditions (dollar-denominated debt becomes harder to service in emerging markets)."}]},"t9-m1-l1":{"id":"t9-m1-l1","title":"The PE Business Model","trackId":"t9","trackTitle":"Private Equity","color":"#D97706","premium":false,"intro":"Private equity firms raise capital from institutional investors (pension funds, endowments, sovereign wealth funds) to acquire, improve, and sell companies. The fund structure — with its unique economics of management fees and carried interest — aligns the general partner's incentives with investor returns. Understanding this model is foundational for any PE career.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Fund Structure","body":"A PE fund is structured as a limited partnership:\n\nLimited Partners (LPs): institutional investors (pension funds, endowments, family offices, sovereign wealth funds) who commit capital. They have limited liability — they can only lose their committed capital.\n\nGeneral Partner (GP): the **private equity** firm itself. It manages the fund, makes investment decisions, and has unlimited liability. The GP typically commits 1-5% of the fund's capital (\"skin in the game\").\n\nFund life: typically 10 years — a 5-year investment period to deploy capital, then 5 years to exit portfolio companies and return capital. Many funds are extended.\n\nCommitted capital: LPs commit but do not immediately transfer money. The GP calls capital as needed (capital calls) and distributes proceeds from exits.","bullets":["LP: passive investor — commits capital, receives returns, limited liability","GP: active manager — deploys capital, earns fees and carry, unlimited liability","Fund life: ~10 years (5yr investment + 5yr harvest)"],"icon":"git-network"},{"heading":"Fund Economics: Fees and Carry","body":"PE firms earn money in two ways:\n\n1. Management fee: typically 2% per annum on committed capital (during investment period) or invested capital (during harvest). On a £1bn fund: £20m/year. Covers firm operating costs — salaries, offices, **due diligence**.\n\n2. Carried interest (carry): the GP's share of profits above a hurdle rate (typically 8% per annum to LPs). Standard carry is 20%. So for every £100 of profit above the hurdle: £20 to the GP, £80 to LPs. On a fund that returns £2bn on £1bn invested: £1bn profit → £200m to the GP, £800m to LPs (simplified).\n\n\"2 and 20\" is the shorthand: 2% management fee + 20% carry. Premier funds charge more (2.5%/25%). Established funds lower fees to retain large LPs.","bullets":["Management fee: 2% p.a. on committed capital — covers costs, predictable income","Carried interest: 20% of profits above the hurdle — the GP's alignment with LPs","Hurdle rate: LPs earn 8% preferred return before the GP takes carry"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"The Waterfall","body":"The distribution waterfall determines how exits are distributed:\n\n1. Return of capital: LPs get back 100% of their committed capital first.\n\n2. Preferred return (hurdle): LPs receive 8% annual return on their capital before carry kicks in.\n\n3. Catch-up: the GP receives all distributions until it has received 20% of total profits (catching up to its carried interest share).\n\n4. 80/20 split: remaining profits split 80% LPs / 20% GP (carried interest).\n\nAmerican waterfall: carry can be distributed deal-by-deal as each company is exited (faster for the GP). European waterfall: carry distributed only after all LPs have received their capital back plus hurdle — more LP-friendly. European funds typically use the European waterfall.","bullets":["Return of capital → preferred return (8%) → GP catch-up → 80/20 split","American waterfall: deal-by-deal carry — GP gets paid sooner","European waterfall: whole-fund return before carry — more LP-protective"],"icon":"layers"},{"heading":"Vintage Year and Diversification","body":"PE funds are identified by their vintage year — the year capital is first deployed. Vintage year matters because the macroeconomic environment at acquisition (and exit) significantly affects returns.\n\n2008 vintage funds bought into falling markets but exited into a long **bull market** — many generated strong returns. 2007 vintage funds bought at peak valuations and struggled with the GFC.\n\nInstitutional investors maintain PE programmes with commitments across multiple vintage years to smooth the J-curve and capture different cycles. The J-curve describes PE returns over time: early losses (fees, slow value creation) before exits generate positive returns in years 5-10.\n\nFund of funds: invest in multiple PE funds to provide LPs with vintage year **diversification** in a single vehicle — at the cost of another layer of fees.","icon":"calendar"}],"questions":[{"id":"t9-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"A PE firm raises a £500m fund. The management fee is 2% on committed capital. How much does the GP earn annually in management fees?","options":["£2m","£10m","£100m","£25m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"2% × £500m = £10m per year. This is the GP's predictable operating income regardless of fund performance — it covers salaries, rent, travel, and due diligence costs. A typical mid-market PE fund with £500m might have 20-30 investment professionals. £10m per year supports a modest team but is not the main economic upside — that comes from the 20% carried interest on successful exits."},{"id":"t9-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"A PE fund invests £1bn and exits with £2.2bn. The hurdle rate is 8% per annum over 5 years. What is the approximate profit subject to carried interest?","options":["£1.2bn — the total profit","Approximately £733m — profit above the 8% hurdle on £1bn over 5 years","£200m — the carry is 20% of total profit automatically","Zero — the hurdle requires 8× return, not 8% annual"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The hurdle return: £1bn × (1.08)^5 = £1.469bn — LPs must receive this before carry kicks in. Profit above the hurdle: £2.2bn − £1.469bn = £731m. GP carry at 20%: £731m × 20% = £146m. LPs get: £1.469bn + £731m × 80% = £2.054bn. The GP's £146m carry vastly exceeds the £50m management fees (£10m/yr × 5yr) — carry is the primary GP incentive, aligning them with LP returns."},{"id":"t9-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"Under the European waterfall, when does the GP first receive carried interest?","options":["After each portfolio company is exited, on a deal-by-deal basis as the sale proceeds arrive","Only after all LPs get their capital back plus the 8% preferred return across the whole fund","Immediately at fund closing, as an upfront carry payment","After five years, regardless of performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The European waterfall (also called \"whole fund\" waterfall) requires all LP committed capital to be returned and the preferred return (hurdle) achieved across the entire fund before any carry is distributed. This protects LPs from a scenario where early exits are profitable but later ones lose money — under the American deal-by-deal waterfall, the GP could collect carry on winning deals even if the fund as a whole underperforms. Most European and Asian PE funds use the European waterfall."},{"id":"t9-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"What is the \"J-curve\" in private equity?","options":["The typical overall shape of LBO returns — a sharp initial gain followed by a gradual decline","The early period of negative returns from fees, before portfolio exits generate positive cash flows","A graph of leverage versus return — showing diminishing returns as debt increases","The distribution of returns across a portfolio — J-shaped means a few winners drive all performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The J-curve describes the timing pattern of PE fund cash flows and returns: in years 1-3, management fees and initial investments create negative cash flows / modest valuations (the bottom of the J). As portfolio companies are grown and eventually exited in years 5-10, returns spike significantly (the upswing of the J). LPs must accept initial negative returns before realising the upside — which is why vintage year diversification is important. The J-curve effect is more pronounced for first-time funds building a portfolio from scratch."}]},"t9-m1-l2":{"id":"t9-m1-l2","title":"The LBO Model","trackId":"t9","trackTitle":"Private Equity","color":"#D97706","premium":false,"intro":"A leveraged buyout (LBO) uses a significant proportion of debt to finance the acquisition of a company. The debt magnifies equity returns — allowing PE firms to generate outsized gains relative to the equity invested. Understanding the mechanics of an LBO, including entry, debt structure, and return drivers, is a core interview topic for PE roles.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"LBO Structure","body":"In a typical **LBO**, the PE firm acquires a company using:\n\n— 30-40% equity (the PE fund's investment)\n— 60-70% debt (senior secured term loans + **high yield** bonds)\n\nExample: £500m acquisition.\n— Equity: £175m (35%)\n— Senior debt: £250m (Term Loan B, SOFR + 350bps)\n— HY bonds: £75m (8.5% fixed)\n\nThe company's existing cash flows service the debt. Over 5 years, as debt is repaid and EBITDA grows, the **equity value** compounds.\n\nLBOs require companies with: (1) stable, predictable cash flows for debt service; (2) hard assets for security; (3) proven management teams; (4) pricing power to grow earnings.","bullets":["Typical structure: ~35% equity, ~65% debt (varies by market conditions)","Debt secured against company assets — Term Loan B + sometimes high-yield bonds","Good LBO targets: stable cash flows, low capex, pricing power"],"icon":"layers"},{"heading":"LBO Return Drivers","body":"PE returns from LBOs come from three sources — the \"triangle\" of value creation:\n\n1. Financial engineering (leverage): debt amplifies equity returns. If the company grows 30% in value, an unlevered equity investor makes 30%. An LBO investor with 35% equity makes 86% on equity (if debt stays constant: £650m exit value on £175m equity).\n\n2. **EBITDA** growth: operational improvements, revenue growth, and cost cuts increase the company's earnings. Each multiple turn of EBITDA growth generates proportionate equity value.\n\n3. Multiple expansion: exit at a higher EV/EBITDA multiple than entry. Buying at 8× and selling at 10× is a significant driver, often achieved by improving the company's quality, diversifying revenue, or benefiting from sector re-rating.","bullets":["Leverage effect: amplifies equity return — debt creates the LBO \"lever\"","EBITDA growth: operational improvement grows the earnings base","Multiple expansion: exit at higher multiple than entry — market timing matters"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"IRR and MOIC","body":"PE performance is measured with two key metrics:\n\nMOIC (Multiple of Invested Capital): exit equity value ÷ equity invested. A 3.0× MOIC means £1 invested returns £3. Simple and intuitive — shows total value creation without time component.\n\nIRR (Internal Rate of Return): the annualised return, accounting for the timing of cash flows. Faster exits at the same MOIC generate higher IRRs. A 3.0× MOIC in 3 years ≈ 44% **IRR**; in 5 years ≈ 25% IRR; in 7 years ≈ 18% IRR.\n\nBoth metrics matter. Top-quartile PE funds target 2.5-3.5× MOIC and 20-25%+ IRR. IRR can be manipulated by recycling capital or using credit lines to delay capital calls — pure MOIC is harder to game.","bullets":["MOIC: total return multiple — 2.0× means you doubled your money","IRR: annualised return — same MOIC earlier = higher IRR","Top-quartile target: 2.5-3.5× MOIC, 20-25%+ IRR"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Entry and Exit Multiples","body":"LBOs are priced on EV/EBITDA multiples. Entry **multiple** discipline is crucial — overpaying at entry is the most common destroyer of PE returns.\n\nThe \"entry multiple\" is negotiated in the auction process. PE sponsors build a model showing the implied returns at different entry prices and maximum multiples they can pay while still achieving target IRR.\n\nThe exit multiple depends on: the M&A market at exit time, the company's quality improvements, comparable transaction multiples, and strategic buyer interest. PE firms improve exit multiples by strengthening management, diversifying revenue, and growing EBITDA (making the company a more attractive acquisition target).\n\nExit options: strategic sale (usually highest price — **synergies**), PE secondary sale (another PE buys), IPO (liquidity event but process is longer).","icon":"swap-horizontal"}],"questions":[{"id":"t9-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"A PE firm acquires a company for £400m (8× EV/EBITDA on £50m EBITDA) using 30% equity (£120m) and 70% debt (£280m). After 5 years, EBITDA grows to £70m and the company is sold at 9× EV/EBITDA. Ignoring debt paydown, what is the exit equity value and approximate MOIC?","options":["Exit equity = £630m; MOIC = 5.25×","Exit equity = £350m; MOIC = 2.9×","Exit equity = £170m; MOIC = 1.4×","Exit equity = £280m; MOIC = 2.3×"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Exit EV = 9 × £70m EBITDA = £630m. Subtract remaining debt (£280m, assumed unchanged for simplicity): equity = £630m − £280m = £350m. MOIC = £350m / £120m = 2.92×. This illustrates two drivers: EBITDA growth (£50m → £70m = +40%) and multiple expansion (8× → 9×). In practice, debt also amortises over 5 years (reducing faster with cash generation), which would increase equity value further."},{"id":"t9-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"Why do PE firms use high leverage in LBOs rather than funding acquisitions entirely with equity?","options":["Debt is always cheaper than equity, so funding a deal with 100% debt would maximise the returns","Leverage amplifies equity returns — enterprise-value gains are magnified on the small equity base","PE firms do not have enough capital to fund acquisitions with equity alone","Debt financing provides tax benefits that make the acquisition cheaper"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Leverage is the core mechanism of LBO returns. Example: a company is worth £100m; the PE firm invests £40m equity + £60m debt. If the company value rises to £130m and debt is unchanged, equity value = £70m — a 75% return on the £40m invested. An unlevered investor who bought a 40% equity stake for £40m (no debt) would hold a stake worth 40% × £130m = £52m at exit — just 30% return. The debt lever transformed the same £40m into a 75% return by concentrating the full upside of enterprise value growth onto the smaller equity base."},{"id":"t9-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"A PE fund returns 3× MOIC on a 5-year hold. A second fund returns 3× MOIC on a 3-year hold. Which performs better on an IRR basis?","options":["Equal — MOIC is the same so IRR must be the same","The 3-year fund — same MOIC achieved faster generates a higher IRR","The 5-year fund — longer holds generate more compounding","IRR cannot be compared without knowing the fund size"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IRR accounts for the time value of money. The same MOIC achieved faster generates a higher IRR because you get your money back sooner and can redeploy it. 3× in 3 years ≈ 44% IRR (£1 → £3 over 3 years). 3× in 5 years ≈ 25% IRR (£1 → £3 over 5 years). For LP investors, faster return of capital is better — they can redeploy into new vintage years. This is why PE firms sometimes use \"continuation vehicles\" or dividend recaps to accelerate distributions even without a full exit."},{"id":"t9-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"Which company characteristic makes it a particularly attractive LBO target?","options":["High capital expenditure requirements — the company needs continuous reinvestment","Highly cyclical revenues — the volatility creates buying opportunities","Stable, predictable, and growing free cash flow with low capital intensity","A recent large acquisition that has yet to be integrated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The ideal LBO target has: (1) stable, predictable cash flows to service the debt (LBOs fail when companies cannot make debt payments); (2) low capex requirements — cash after maintenance capex goes to debt repayment; (3) a proven, profitable business model; (4) opportunities for operational improvement or bolt-on acquisitions. High capex (like semiconductors or steel) leaves little cash for debt service. Cyclical revenues are risky — a recession could prevent debt payment. Serial acquirers with undigested deals add integration risk."}]},"t9-m1-l3":{"id":"t9-m1-l3","title":"Value Creation","trackId":"t9","trackTitle":"Private Equity","color":"#D97706","premium":false,"intro":"The original critique of PE — that returns came purely from financial engineering (leverage) — led to a shift toward operational value creation. Modern PE firms have significant operating teams focused on growing revenues, cutting costs, and improving the strategic positioning of portfolio companies. Understanding these levers is essential for PE roles.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"The 100-Day Plan","body":"After completing an acquisition, the PE firm executes a 100-day plan: an intensive operational improvement agenda identified during **due diligence**, typically including:\n\n— Management assessment: quickly identify if existing management can execute the plan, or whether replacements are needed (a major PE operational lever).\n— Quick wins: pricing analysis, procurement renegotiations, overhead removal.\n— Strategic review: confirm the investment thesis, prioritise growth initiatives.\n— Financial reporting: install better financial controls and monthly reporting.\n\nThe 100-day plan sets the cadence for the hold period. Board meetings shift to monthly (vs. quarterly for public companies). PE-owned companies typically have much more intensive financial oversight than public companies.","icon":"calendar"},{"heading":"Organic Growth Levers","body":"Revenue growth is the highest-value creation lever — each incremental pound of **EBITDA** at a 10× exit multiple is worth £10 of **enterprise value**.\n\nKey operational levers:\n\nPricing: often the fastest value creator. Data analysis of customer price sensitivity, removal of discounts, optimising price architecture. A 1% price increase on £100m revenue = £1m EBITDA (at high margins) → £10m of enterprise value at 10×.\n\nSales force effectiveness: adding salespeople, restructuring commission plans, targeting larger customers, or entering new geographies.\n\nProduct/service expansion: cross-selling or upselling to the existing customer base. Customer retention improvement: reducing churn by improving service, increasing switching costs.","bullets":["1% price increase at 10× multiple = 10× that in EV value creation","Management change: often the first and biggest PE operational lever","Cross-sell / upsell: extract more from existing customer relationships"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Cost Reduction and Efficiency","body":"Cost reduction is more predictable than revenue growth, but less valued at exit (buyers discount cost-cutting stories vs. genuine growth).\n\nTypical cost levers:\n\nProcurement: consolidating supplier contracts, leveraging PE firm relationships across the portfolio, competitive tendering. Often yields 5-15% cost savings.\n\nSG&A: removing management layers, offshoring back-office, outsourcing non-core functions.\n\nSite rationalisation: closing underperforming locations, consolidating facilities.\n\nSourcing from the portfolio: larger PE firms use their portfolio companies as suppliers to each other, sharing costs across the portfolio (\"platform advantage\").\n\nOperating **leverage**: if revenue grows faster than costs (fixed cost base), EBITDA margins expand automatically — the most powerful efficiency driver.","icon":"cut"},{"heading":"Buy-and-Build Strategy","body":"Buy-and-build (or \"bolt-on\") strategy: a PE firm acquires a platform company, then makes smaller acquisitions to add scale, capabilities, or geographic coverage.\n\nWhy it works:\n\n— **Arbitrage**: the platform is acquired at a large-cap multiple (10-12×). Smaller bolt-ons are acquired at lower multiples (5-7×). At exit, the enlarged platform is sold at a large-cap multiple — the multiple arbitrage on bolt-ons creates instant value.\n— **Synergies**: revenue synergies (cross-selling), cost synergies (procurement, SG&A), and management leverage (applying one management team across more revenue).\n— Scale: larger companies command higher exit multiples and attract more buyers (including strategics).\n\nBuy-and-build is now the dominant strategy in mid-market PE — a platform company of 3-5× EBITDA can be grown to 10× EBITDA through acquisitions, then sold as a market leader.","bullets":["Platform acquisition at large-cap multiple + bolt-ons at small-cap multiples","Multiple arbitrage: bolt-ons re-rated upward at exit with the platform","Scale creates better exit optionality — attracts larger strategic buyers"],"icon":"add-circle"}],"questions":[{"id":"t9-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"A PE firm improves the pricing of its portfolio company by 2%. Revenue was £200m with 40% EBITDA margins. If the company is exited at 10× EV/EBITDA, how much enterprise value does this pricing improvement create?","options":["£4m","£40m","£16m","£80m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Pricing improvement of 2% on £200m revenue = £4m additional revenue. At 40% margins, incremental revenue flows through at high contribution margin — assume most drops to EBITDA: £4m × ~100% (pricing is pure margin once fixed costs are covered) = £4m additional EBITDA. At 10× EV/EBITDA multiple: £4m × 10 = £40m of enterprise value created. This is why pricing is the highest-leverage value creation lever — it flows directly to margin with no incremental cost."},{"id":"t9-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"Why do PE-backed companies typically have more intensive financial oversight than public companies?","options":["PE regulations require monthly filings with the SEC, versus only quarterly for public companies","PE firms have board control and shift to monthly reporting, with hands-on GP involvement in strategy","Public companies are too large to manage closely — PE companies are always smaller","PE-backed companies must report to their banks monthly as a loan covenant"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PE firms hold board control (typically 3-5 board seats in a control buyout) and are actively incentivised to maximise returns on a 5-7 year timeline. They move financial reporting to monthly cadence, establish detailed KPIs, and are directly involved in strategic hiring and capital allocation decisions. Public companies are accountable to dispersed shareholders and typically operate on quarterly reporting with less intensive governance. This \"GP governance\" is seen as a key value creation advantage of the PE ownership model."},{"id":"t9-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"A PE firm acquires a plumbing services company (the \"platform\") at 8× EBITDA on £5m EBITDA. It then acquires three small plumbing businesses at 5× EBITDA, each with £2m EBITDA (total: £6m additional EBITDA for £30m). It exits the combined business (£11m EBITDA) at 10× EBITDA. What is the multiple arbitrage gain?","options":["£0 — there is no multiple arbitrage if the exit multiple equals the platform entry multiple","£30m — the bolt-ons were bought at 5× but exit at 10×, creating £5 per £1 of bolt-on EBITDA","The bolt-ons create £60m of EV at exit vs £30m cost — £30m gained","The platform entry multiple (8×) is already below exit (10×), so there is no bolt-on arbitrage"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The bolt-on EBITDA (£6m) is acquired at 5× = £30m total cost. At exit, this EBITDA is valued at 10× (same as the rest of the platform) = £60m of enterprise value. The arbitrage gain = £60m − £30m = £30m. This is the \"multiple re-rating\" of smaller acquisitions when combined with the larger platform and exited as part of a market leader. The PE firm effectively bought earnings at 5× and sells them at 10× — a common source of LBO returns in buy-and-build strategies."},{"id":"t9-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"A portfolio company improves its EBITDA margin from 20% to 25% on flat revenue of £100m. At exit with a 10× EV/EBITDA multiple, how much enterprise value does this margin improvement create?","options":["£5m","£50m","£25m","£100m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"EBITDA improvement: 25% − 20% = 5% margin on £100m revenue = £5m additional EBITDA. At 10× EV/EBITDA: £5m × 10 = £50m of additional enterprise value. This is the power of margin improvement in PE — every pound of EBITDA created is multiplied by the exit multiple. Combined with leverage (which amplifies the equity return on that EV increase), this demonstrates why operational improvement has such a magnified effect on PE equity returns."}]},"t9-m2-l1":{"id":"t9-m2-l1","title":"Exit Strategies","trackId":"t9","trackTitle":"Private Equity","color":"#D97706","premium":false,"intro":"The exit is when a PE firm realises its investment — converting equity ownership in a portfolio company into cash returns for its LPs. The choice of exit route depends on market conditions, the company's stage, and buyer appetite. Getting the exit right is as important as the original acquisition.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Strategic (Trade) Sale","body":"A strategic sale is the sale of the portfolio company to a corporate acquirer (a \"strategic buyer\") rather than another financial buyer. This is the most common exit route and typically achieves the highest **valuation** — strategic buyers can pay \"synergy value\" on top of standalone value.\n\n**Synergies**: a strategic buyer that can cut costs (eliminate duplicate functions, consolidate facilities) or cross-sell products values the acquisition above its standalone worth. A software company acquiring a PE-owned competitor can justify 12-15× **EBITDA** vs the 9× a standalone buyer might pay.\n\nProcess: often a controlled auction where the PE firm runs a competitive process (typically 5-10 strategic and financial bidders), creating tension and maximising price. Banks run the process (information memorandum, management presentations, best-and-final offers).","bullets":["Strategic buyer: corporate acquirer who can pay synergy premium","Highest price typically achieved in competitive auction processes","Strategic M&A rationale must be clear — buyers validate synergies in DD"],"icon":"business"},{"heading":"Secondary Buyout (SBO)","body":"A secondary buyout (SBO) is the sale of a portfolio company from one PE firm to another. It has become increasingly common as PE has grown — now ~30-40% of PE exits.\n\nAdvantages for the seller: PE buyers are familiar with the **LBO** structure, experienced with **due diligence**, and can move quickly. The process is more efficient than a strategic sale.\n\nAdvantages for the buyer: PE firms can apply fresh operational focus, new management incentives, and additional leverage capacity (refinancing the **capital structure**).\n\nCriticism: SBOs can be \"passing the parcel\" — loading more leverage without creating genuine value. However, many SBOs do create value through fresh capital for bolt-ons, new geographic expansion, or management upgrades.\n\nTertiary buyout: sold from the second PE owner to a third — less common and increasingly scrutinised.","icon":"people"},{"heading":"IPO","body":"An **IPO** (Initial Public Offering) lists the portfolio company on a stock exchange. For PE, an IPO is typically a partial exit — the PE firm sells some shares at IPO but retains a significant stake, selling down over 1-2 years via block trades.\n\nAdvantages: public markets can achieve high valuations during bull markets; creates a liquid currency for the company; establishes a public profile for the business.\n\nDisadvantages: IPOs take 12-18 months to prepare; regulatory requirements (Sarbanes-Oxley, FCA); PE firm remains locked up for 180 days post-IPO; public market valuations can be volatile. IPOs are typically used for the highest-quality portfolio companies (market leaders with strong growth profiles).\n\nPE-backed IPOs: PE firms often sell into the aftermarket (secondary offerings) at higher prices after a successful IPO debut.","icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Dividend Recapitalisation","body":"A **dividend** recapitalisation (\"div recap\") is not a full exit but a partial return of capital: the portfolio company takes on additional debt and uses the proceeds to pay a special dividend to the PE firm.\n\nAdvantages: PE firm returns capital to LPs faster (improving IRR) without giving up the asset. Allows the PE firm to retain ownership while \"de-risking\" the position.\n\nRisks: higher **leverage** increases **default risk** for the company. If performance deteriorates, the additional debt could trigger financial distress. Div recaps are viewed negatively when they leave the company over-leveraged — particularly if management or employees receive nothing.\n\nDiv recaps became controversial post-2020: some PE-backed companies paid large dividends then filed for bankruptcy within 1-2 years, leaving creditors (and employees) with losses while the PE firm had already extracted returns.","icon":"card"}],"questions":[{"id":"t9-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"Why do strategic acquirers typically pay higher prices in PE exit auctions than other PE firms (financial buyers)?","options":["Strategic buyers have more access to debt financing","Strategic buyers can pay a synergy premium — they value the combined business above standalone value","Financial buyers are constrained by carried interest calculations that cap bids","Strategic buyers do not need to model returns — they have unlimited capital"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Strategic buyers can pay above standalone value because they expect synergies: cost savings (eliminating duplicate functions), revenue synergies (cross-selling, geographic expansion), or technology acquisition. A software company acquiring a competitor might save £20m/year in costs + earn £10m in cross-sell revenue — justifying a significantly higher price than a financial buyer who can only model standalone performance plus financial engineering. This synergy premium is why PE firms often run dual-track processes to keep strategic bidders competing with financial ones."},{"id":"t9-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"A PE firm sells its portfolio company to another PE firm (a secondary buyout). What is a common criticism of SBOs?","options":["SBOs are illegal in most jurisdictions due to conflicts of interest","They can be \"passing the parcel\" — adding leverage without genuine operational value creation","Financial buyers always pay less than strategic buyers, reducing LP returns","SBOs require the selling PE firm to reinvest proceeds in the same industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SBO critics argue that when a company is sold from one PE firm to another, the new buyer often simply adds more debt (re-leveraging) and applies similar operational playbooks without genuine strategic transformation. The third or fourth buyout of the same business, each time with more leverage, increases default risk without creating proportionate economic value. However, good SBOs add value through fresh management incentives, new capital for bolt-ons, or accessing new markets that the previous PE owner was unable to execute."},{"id":"t9-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"A PE firm wants to return capital to LPs while retaining ownership of a growing portfolio company. Which route achieves this?","options":["A trade sale, meaning you sell the whole company outright to a strategic corporate buyer","Secondary buyout — sell to another PE firm","Dividend recapitalisation — take on additional debt to pay a special dividend","IPO — list on the public market and sell shares"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A dividend recapitalisation allows the PE firm to retain ownership while returning capital to LPs. The portfolio company takes on additional debt (e.g. a new term loan) and pays the proceeds as a special dividend to the equity holders (the PE fund). This increases LP IRR (faster capital return) without surrendering the asset — useful when the PE firm believes there is still significant upside remaining but LPs want capital returned. The risk is increased leverage on the company."},{"id":"t9-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"Why is an IPO typically used for only the highest-quality PE portfolio companies?","options":["IPOs are cheaper to run than an M&A sale, so only premium companies can justify the legal costs","Public markets apply higher multiples to smaller companies","IPOs take 12–18 months, need public disclosure, and face volatile pricing — only strong leaders qualify","PE firms are prohibited from taking below-average companies public by SEC rules"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"IPOs are complex, time-consuming (12-18 months of preparation), and costly (regulatory compliance, investor relations, quarterly reporting). Post-IPO, the PE firm faces a 180-day lock-up and ongoing public scrutiny. Public market valuations can disconnect from fundamentals and are volatile. Only companies with compelling growth stories, clean corporate governance, and the scale to attract institutional investor demand make good IPO candidates. Weaker or more cyclical businesses are better candidates for strategic sale or SBO."}]},"t9-m2-l2":{"id":"t9-m2-l2","title":"PE Performance Metrics","trackId":"t9","trackTitle":"Private Equity","color":"#D97706","premium":false,"intro":"Measuring PE performance is more complex than measuring public market returns — investments are illiquid, held for years, and valued using subjective marks until exit. Key metrics like IRR, MOIC, DPI, and TVPI each tell a different part of the story. Understanding them is essential for LPs allocating to PE and analysts presenting fund performance.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"IRR — Annualised Return","body":"**IRR** (Internal Rate of Return) is the **discount rate** that makes the net present value of all cash flows equal to zero. Practically, it is the annualised return on invested capital — the \"compound annual growth rate\" equivalent for irregular cash flows.\n\nFor PE: IRR accounts for the timing of capital calls and distributions — earlier returns are more valuable (they can be reinvested). A fund with a 25% IRR over 5 years generates more value than 25% IRR over 8 years at the same entry cost.\n\nLimitation: IRR can be inflated by early exits or credit line manipulation (delaying capital calls artificially). A fund might show 30% IRR on an investment that was funded on day 1, held for 6 months, and returned at a small gain — the short hold creates a high IRR on minimal absolute return.","bullets":["IRR: annualised return, time-adjusted — favours early, fast exits","Gross IRR vs Net IRR: gross is before fees and carry; LPs look at net","Can be gamed: delayed capital calls, quick exit of winners first"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"MOIC, DPI, and TVPI","body":"Three complementary metrics:\n\nMOIC (Multiple of Invested Capital): total value returned ÷ total capital invested. 2.5× means every £1 invested returns £2.50. Simple and immune to timing manipulation — captures total value created.\n\nDPI (Distributions to Paid-In): total cash distributed to LPs ÷ total capital called. DPI = 1.0× means LPs have received their capital back. DPI > 1.0× means they are in profit in cash terms. Unrealised holdings (still in the portfolio) are excluded — DPI is a \"realised\" metric.\n\nTVPI (Total Value to Paid-In): (distributions + remaining NAV) ÷ capital called. Includes both realised and unrealised value. More complete than DPI but dependent on the accuracy of unrealised valuations.\n\nA fund with high TVPI but low DPI has mostly unrealised value — the returns exist on paper but haven't been delivered yet.","bullets":["MOIC: total return multiple — immune to timing manipulation","DPI: \"cash back\" ratio — realised returns only, LP-focused","TVPI: DPI + unrealised NAV — total paper + cash return"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Benchmarking and PME","body":"How do you compare a PE fund's returns to public markets? The timing differences make direct comparison misleading.\n\nPME (Public Market Equivalent): a methodology that invests the same capital calls in a public market index and reinvests distributions. The PME IRR represents what the LP would have earned in the public market with the same cash flow timing. If the PE fund's net IRR exceeds the PME, it has \"outperformed\" the public market alternative.\n\nKS-PME (Kaplan-Schoar PME): the ratio of the PV of distributions to the PV of contributions, using public market returns as the discount rate. KS-PME > 1.0 means the PE fund outperformed the index.\n\nDifficulty: PE fund NAVs are self-reported until exit. Mature funds (large DPI) are more reliably benchmarked than young funds with high unrealised NAV.","icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"PE Fund Performance in Context","body":"The historical case for PE allocation: top-quartile PE funds have historically outperformed the S&P 500 by 3-5% per annum net of fees. However, the performance distribution is wide — bottom-quartile PE underperforms public markets. Persistence: unlike public equity managers, PE fund manager skill persists — top-quartile managers tend to stay top-quartile, making track record meaningful.\n\nFees: 2% management fee + 20% carry is significant drag. A fund earning 25% gross IRR might return 18-20% net. LPs must believe the net return exceeds what they could earn in low-cost public market funds.\n\nAccess: the best PE firms are oversubscribed — top LPs (Harvard endowment, Yale, CPP) have privileged access to top-quartile funds. For most LPs, access to the highest-performing funds is constrained.","icon":"people"}],"questions":[{"id":"t9-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"A PE fund has invested £500m and distributed £600m to LPs so far. It still holds portfolio companies valued at £400m (unrealised NAV). What are the DPI and TVPI?","options":["DPI = 2.0×; TVPI = 2.0×","DPI = 1.2×; TVPI = 2.0×","DPI = 0.6×; TVPI = 1.0×","DPI = 1.2×; TVPI = 1.4×"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DPI = Distributions / Paid-In = £600m / £500m = 1.2×. LPs have received 1.2× their invested capital back in cash. TVPI = (Distributions + Unrealised NAV) / Paid-In = (£600m + £400m) / £500m = 2.0×. The gap between TVPI (2.0×) and DPI (1.2×) = 0.8× of unrealised value — this value exists on paper but has not been converted to cash. LPs care about both: DPI shows realised returns; TVPI shows total potential if unrealised marks prove accurate."},{"id":"t9-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"A PE fund's gross IRR is 28% and net IRR is 18%. The management fee is 2% and carry is 20%. What explains the ~10pp gap between gross and net?","options":["The entire 10-point difference comes purely from the 20% carried interest charged on 28% returns","Management fees compounding over the fund life plus 20% carried interest cut the gross return to LPs","The fund is currently in the J-curve phase — gross and net will converge at exit","Net IRR includes public market index performance subtracted from gross"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The gap reflects both fee components: (1) Management fees: 2%/year on £500m over 10 years = £100m; this reduces LP capital available to invest. (2) Carried interest: 20% of profits above the 8% hurdle — on strong returns, carry is the dominant drag. Together, a fund generating 28% gross can net 18-20% to LPs. This is why LPs scrutinise gross-to-net spread carefully — high fees from smaller managers or multiple layers of funds (fund of funds) can erode returns to below public market levels."},{"id":"t9-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"What is a PME (Public Market Equivalent) analysis used for in PE performance evaluation?","options":["Calculating the maximum amount of debt a target company can support in a leveraged buyout deal","Comparing PE fund returns to what would have been earned in a public market index with the same timing of cash flows","Estimating the fair value of unrealised portfolio companies","Measuring the impact of management fees across different fund structures"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PME answers the fundamental LP question: \"Was PE worth it versus just investing in an index fund?\" It does this by simulating what would have happened if the same capital calls had been invested in a public index (e.g. S&P 500) and the same distributions had been taken out. If the PE fund's actual IRR exceeds the PME IRR, it genuinely outperformed public markets on a like-for-like timing basis. PME is now standard in LP reporting — sophisticated LPs (endowments, pension funds) use it to assess every manager they consider."},{"id":"t9-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"Research shows that PE manager performance \"persists\" — top-quartile managers tend to stay top-quartile. What are the implications for LP investors?","options":["LPs should rotate out of performing managers and into laggard managers — mean reversion will benefit them","Past performance in PE is a meaningful signal — LPs should prioritise access to managers with strong track records","The best PE returns come from the most recent vintage years regardless of manager quality","Manager persistence is a myth — PE returns are entirely driven by market timing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unlike in public equity mutual funds (where past performance does NOT predict future performance), PE fund manager skill tends to persist across fund generations. Research by Kaplan & Schoar shows that a manager in the top quartile of one fund is likely to be top quartile in the next. This means track record is meaningful for LP due diligence — unlike public market manager selection. It also explains why the best PE managers (Blackstone, KKR, Sequoia for VC) can raise oversubscribed funds: LPs rationally pay a premium for access to proven managers."}]},"t10-m1-l1":{"id":"t10-m1-l1","title":"Revenue Recognition","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Revenue is the most scrutinised — and most manipulated — line on the income statement. IFRS 15 and ASC 606 standardised revenue recognition under a single five-step model, replacing dozens of industry-specific rules. Understanding when revenue can and cannot be recognised is the foundation of financial statement analysis.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Five-Step Model (IFRS 15 / ASC 606)","body":"Both IFRS and US GAAP now use the same core framework. **Revenue** is recognised when (and only when) control of goods or services transfers to the customer:\n\n1. Identify the contract with the customer.\n2. Identify the performance obligations (distinct goods/services promised).\n3. Determine the transaction price (including variable consideration — rebates, bonuses, returns).\n4. Allocate the transaction price to each performance obligation.\n5. Recognise revenue when (or as) each performance obligation is satisfied.\n\nThe key shift: from \"risks and rewards\" (old standard) to \"control.\" Revenue is earned when the customer controls the asset — not simply when the seller has shipped it.","bullets":["Control transfer replaces risks-and-rewards as the core test","Variable consideration (discounts, bonuses) must be estimated and constrained","Multiple-element arrangements: each distinct element is a separate obligation"],"icon":"list"},{"heading":"Point-in-Time vs Over-Time Recognition","body":"A performance obligation is satisfied either at a point in time or over time — this distinction has major P&L timing implications.\n\nOver-time recognition: used when the customer simultaneously receives and consumes benefits (a cleaning service), the company creates an asset the customer controls as it is built (a custom software build), or the company has no alternative use for the asset and has an enforceable right to payment (a bespoke aircraft).\n\nPoint-in-time: everything else — typically when physical goods are delivered and the customer can direct their use. The specific \"point\" depends on which party bears the risk of loss, who has physical possession, and who has accepted the asset.\n\nConstruction contracts: long-term projects use percentage-of-completion (over-time). Previously, completed-contract method was permitted — deferring all revenue until done. IFRS 15 eliminated this, requiring over-time recognition when criteria are met.","bullets":["Over-time: customer benefits as the work is done (SaaS subscriptions, construction)","Point-in-time: control transfers at a specific moment (delivery of goods)","Percentage-of-completion: measures progress for over-time recognition"],"icon":"time"},{"heading":"Red Flags: Where Revenue Gets Manipulated","body":"Revenue manipulation is the most common form of earnings fraud. Key warning signs:\n\nChannel stuffing: pushing product to distributors before demand materialises — inflating quarterly revenue at the expense of future periods. Watch for: days sales outstanding (DSO) rising while revenue grows, unusually large Q4 shipments, high return rates in Q1.\n\nBill-and-hold: booking revenue on goods not yet shipped, on the premise the customer has \"accepted\" them. Legitimate bill-and-hold requires genuine customer request, specified risk transfer, and a separately identified asset. Frequently abused.\n\nPremature recognition: booking revenue before performance obligations are complete (e.g. recognising a multi-year software licence fee upfront when support is ongoing).\n\nRound-tripping: two companies exchange cash with each other and both book it as revenue. Common in early-stage tech companies to inflate growth metrics.","bullets":["Rising DSO with revenue growth: cash not following reported sales — warning sign","Bill-and-hold without genuine customer request: revenue not yet earned","Large Q4 spikes that reverse in Q1: channel stuffing pattern"],"icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Deferred Revenue and Revenue Reversals","body":"Deferred revenue (a liability on the **balance sheet**) represents cash received before the performance obligation is fulfilled — the company owes the customer a service. SaaS companies, subscription businesses, and magazine publishers carry large deferred revenue balances.\n\nGrowing deferred revenue is a quality signal: cash is received upfront; the P&L will recognise it as real revenue is earned. A company that shows declining deferred revenue relative to bookings may be running out of backlog.\n\nContra: aggressive revenue recognition depletes deferred revenue faster than bookings, flattering near-term P&L at the expense of future revenue. Watch the deferred revenue balance as a leading indicator of future revenue trend.\n\nRefund liabilities: under IFRS 15, companies must estimate returns and exclude them from revenue upfront — unlike old practice of booking gross revenue and provisioning separately.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"Under IFRS 15, a company signs a 3-year SaaS contract for £120,000 (£40,000/year). The customer pays the full £120,000 upfront. How should the company recognise revenue?","options":["£120,000 in year 1 — cash has been received and the contract is signed","£40,000 per year over 3 years — as the service is delivered (over-time recognition)","£120,000 at contract end — when all performance obligations are complete","£60,000 in year 1 and £60,000 in year 2 — split equally between signing and renewal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is a classic over-time recognition scenario. The customer receives and consumes the SaaS service continuously — control transfers over time, not at a single point. IFRS 15 requires recognising £40,000/year as the service is delivered. The £120,000 received upfront is recognised as a deferred revenue liability and released to the P&L as the service is provided. Recognising all £120,000 in year 1 would be a revenue recognition error (and a common early-stage company temptation)."},{"id":"t10-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"A software company's revenue grows 25% year-over-year. At the same time, days sales outstanding (DSO) jumps from 45 days to 75 days. What does this pattern suggest?","options":["The company is simply growing in a healthy way — a rising DSO is entirely normal during expansion","Possible channel stuffing — sales are outrunning cash collection, so revenue may not be genuine","The company has improved payment terms for customers, benefiting long-term retention","DSO is unrelated to revenue quality — it only affects working capital, not earnings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DSO = (Accounts Receivable / Revenue) × days. Rising DSO means the company is collecting cash more slowly than it is booking revenue. When DSO rises sharply alongside strong revenue growth, it is a major red flag: either customers are not paying (credit quality issue) or revenue is being recognised before it is genuinely earned (channel stuffing, premature recognition). Forensic accounting firms specifically flag DSO divergence as one of the most reliable early indicators of revenue fraud."},{"id":"t10-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"A construction company uses the percentage-of-completion method on a 4-year, £20m contract. After year 2, it has incurred £7m of costs against an estimated £14m total cost. How much revenue should it have recognised in total by year 2?","options":["£10m","£7m","£14m","£20m"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under percentage-of-completion, progress = costs incurred / estimated total costs = £7m / £14m = 50%. Revenue recognised = 50% × £20m contract = £10m. This method matches revenue to the work actually done rather than deferring everything until completion. If actual costs overrun (say total costs end up £16m), the percentage complete changes and future revenue recognition is adjusted — which is why construction company revenue can be volatile."},{"id":"t10-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"A company's deferred revenue balance has fallen from £50m to £20m over two years while bookings (new contracts signed) have remained flat. What does this signal?","options":["The company is collecting cash faster — a positive liquidity signal","Revenue recognition has been aggressive — backlog is being depleted without equivalent new bookings to replace it","Customers are cancelling contracts — churn risk is high","The company has shifted from subscription to perpetual licence model, which is immaterial for valuation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Deferred revenue = unrecognised future revenue that has already been billed. A shrinking deferred revenue balance when bookings are flat means the company is recognising revenue faster than it is adding new contracted backlog. This is a leading indicator of future revenue pressure: the \"queue\" of future recognised revenue is shrinking. Analysts model deferred revenue movements carefully for SaaS companies, as it signals whether reported revenue growth is being pulled forward from future periods."}]},"t10-m1-l2":{"id":"t10-m1-l2","title":"Quality of Earnings","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Reported net income is a starting point, not a conclusion. Analysts build a \"quality of earnings\" (QoE) view by stripping out one-time items, non-cash charges, accounting choices, and management adjustments to reach a clean, recurring earnings base. In PE and M&A, a full QoE process by an independent accounting firm is standard before any major acquisition.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"GAAP vs Non-GAAP Earnings","body":"Companies increasingly report \"adjusted,\" \"underlying,\" or \"pro forma\" earnings alongside GAAP/IFRS figures. Common GAAP-to-non-GAAP adjustments:\n\nAdd back: restructuring charges (labelled one-time but often recurring), stock-based compensation (SBC, a real economic cost but non-cash), **M&A** transaction costs, **amortisation** of acquired intangibles, impairment charges.\n\nRemove: gains on asset sales, favourable lawsuit settlements, government subsidies.\n\nThe problem: management chooses which items are \"one-time.\" Academic research shows that companies systematically exclude losses (marking them non-recurring) but include gains in GAAP earnings. Over time, non-GAAP **EPS** consistently exceeds GAAP EPS across the market — suggesting systematic overstatement of underlying performance.\n\nThe analyst's job: independently assess which adjustments are legitimate versus self-serving.","bullets":["Restructuring charges: often labelled \"one-time\" but recur year after year","SBC: excluded from adjusted EBITDA but is a real cost to shareholders","Management controls which items are \"adjusted out\" — conflicts of interest arise"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"Adjusted EBITDA: Legitimate and Abused","body":"**EBITDA** (Earnings Before Interest, Tax, **Depreciation** & Amortisation) is the most widely used metric in corporate finance — it approximates operating cash generation and is used for leverage ratios (Debt/EBITDA) and valuation (EV/EBITDA).\n\n\"Adjusted EBITDA\" adds back further items. Legitimate add-backs: genuinely one-time legal settlements, costs of a factory closure that won't recur, integration costs on a completed acquisition.\n\nAbused add-backs seen in PE-sponsored companies before IPO/sale:\n— \"Run-rate\" cost savings from planned but unexecuted initiatives\n— \"Management fees\" paid to the PE firm added back as if they won't exist post-exit\n— Pro forma EBITDA for bolt-on acquisitions made partway through the year (adding a full year of acquired EBITDA)\n— SBC on the basis it is \"non-cash\" (dilution is real, even if non-cash)\n\nCovenant EBITDA: leveraged finance agreements define EBITDA in the credit agreement, often allowing a broad set of add-backs. The widening of these definitions (in the cov-lite era) is a significant risk.","bullets":["EBITDA ≠ cash flow: capex and working capital movements are excluded","Run-rate synergies in EBITDA add-backs: often aspirational, never guaranteed","SBC excluded from adjusted EBITDA is the most contested adjustment for tech companies"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Cookie Jar Reserves and Earnings Smoothing","body":"Accrual accounting requires management estimates for provisions, warranties, bad debts, and restructuring. These estimates can be used to smooth earnings across periods.\n\nCookie jar reserves: in a strong year, management overprovisions (creates excessive reserves). In a weak year, they release reserves into income, buffering earnings. The result: artificially smooth EPS that masks **underlying** **volatility**.\n\nWarning signs: provisions that consistently reverse in weak periods, reserve levels that move opposite to business conditions, warranty provisions that shrink when warranty claims should logically rise (after rapid product expansion).\n\nConversely, \"big bath\" accounting occurs when a new management team takes all known and potential losses in one quarter — writing off every possible item under the guise of \"clearing the decks.\" This depresses the current period but creates a clean base for future earnings growth from the lower starting point.","bullets":["Overprovision in good years → release in bad years = artificial earnings smoothing","\"Big bath\" on CEO transition: maximise write-offs in year 1, inflate future growth","Warranty and bad debt provisions: check against historical actuals to spot manipulation"],"icon":"layers"},{"heading":"Cash Conversion and the Accruals Ratio","body":"The most fundamental quality test: does cash generation track reported earnings?\n\nCash conversion = Operating Cash Flow / EBITDA (or **Net Income**). A healthy company converts 85-100%+ of EBITDA to operating cash flow. Persistently low conversion (below 70%) indicates earnings include significant non-cash or working-capital-absorbed income that isn't turning into real cash.\n\nThe **accruals** ratio measures the portion of earnings coming from accruals (accounting estimates) rather than cash flows. Higher accruals signal lower earnings quality. Formula:\n\nAccruals ratio = (Net Income − Operating Cash Flow) / Average Total Assets\n\nA negative accruals ratio (earnings < operating cash flow) suggests conservative accounting. A high positive ratio suggests aggressive accrual build-up that may not be sustainable.\n\nAcademic research (Sloan, 1996) showed that high-accrual companies systematically underperformed low-accrual companies in subsequent stock returns — the market misprices earnings quality.","bullets":["Cash conversion = Operating CF / EBITDA — sustained below 70% is a warning","Accruals ratio = (Net Income − Operating CF) / Assets — higher = lower quality","Sloan (1996): high-accrual companies underperform — market underweights cash flow quality"],"icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"A company reports adjusted EBITDA of £50m, adding back: £5m of restructuring charges (4th consecutive year), £8m of stock-based compensation, and £3m of M&A transaction costs (deal completed 18 months ago). Which add-back is most questionable?","options":["SBC — it is always legitimate to exclude non-cash charges","Restructuring charges — four consecutive years suggests they are recurring operating costs, not one-time items","M&A transaction costs — these are always excluded regardless of timing","None of them — adjusted EBITDA already correctly excludes every genuinely non-operating item here"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Restructuring charges added back for the 4th consecutive year are the most suspect. By definition, a recurring cost is not \"one-time.\" Companies that restructure every year are effectively labelling a structural cost of their business as exceptional. An independent analyst would include these costs in normalised earnings. SBC is genuinely debated (non-cash but dilutive). M&A costs 18 months after deal close are harder to justify — those should have been fully expensed at the time."},{"id":"t10-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"A company's net income is £30m but operating cash flow is £8m. What does this likely indicate?","options":["Excellent underlying performance — high reported net income combined with conservative capital spending","Poor earnings quality — much of net income is accruals not cash, hinting at aggressive accounting","The company has high capital expenditure — normal for capital-intensive businesses","The company has paid large dividends, reducing its cash position"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cash conversion = Operating CF / Net Income = £8m / £30m = 27% — extremely low. When operating cash flow lags net income significantly, it means a large portion of profits exists on paper (accruals, receivables growth, deferred costs) rather than being collected as cash. This is the clearest signal of potential earnings quality issues. Capex affects investing cash flow, not operating cash flow. Dividends affect financing cash flow. The gap between £30m and £8m should trigger deep investigation into working capital movements and accrual items."},{"id":"t10-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"A new CEO joins a company and immediately takes a £200m write-down on goodwill, restructures the sales force (£30m charge), and writes off slow-moving inventory (£15m). This pattern is known as:","options":["Cookie jar reserve creation — building provisions for future release","\"Big bath\" accounting — maximising write-offs on arrival to set a clean earnings base going forward","Revenue smoothing — deferring income to future periods","Aggressive accrual recognition — accelerating expenses into the current year unnecessarily"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"\"Big bath\" accounting occurs when a new CEO takes maximum write-downs in their first year — on the logic that the previous management is \"blamed\" for the charges, while the new CEO's subsequent performance starts from a clean, lower cost base. Future earnings look stronger because the charges have already been taken. While some of these write-offs may be genuinely necessary, the motivation can lead to over-provisioning — creating a reserve for future earnings management. Auditors and analysts scrutinise first-year write-offs carefully."},{"id":"t10-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"Why do academics and analysts argue that stock-based compensation (SBC) should NOT be excluded from adjusted EBITDA for technology companies?","options":["Stock-based comp is really a cash expense, since it drains the company's bank account directly","SBC dilutes existing shareholders — it is a real economic cost of compensating employees, even if no cash changes hands","GAAP prohibits the exclusion of SBC from non-GAAP metrics","SBC increases revenue through employee productivity, netting to zero"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SBC is non-cash — no money leaves the company's bank account. But it dilutes existing shareholders by issuing new equity to employees. Each share issued to an employee is a share that could otherwise be issued to raise capital, or represents value transferred from existing shareholders to employees. For high-SBC tech companies, the gap between GAAP EPS (which includes SBC expense) and adjusted EPS (which excludes it) can be enormous — 50%+ at companies where SBC represents 20-30% of revenue. Excluding it paints an inflated picture of earnings power."}]},"t10-m1-l3":{"id":"t10-m1-l3","title":"Working Capital Analysis","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Working capital is the capital tied up in the day-to-day operations of the business — inventory, receivables, and payables. Efficient working capital management is a key source of cash generation (or drain). The cash conversion cycle, and changes in working capital, are essential tools for understanding whether a business really generates the cash its income statement suggests.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Three Working Capital Metrics","body":"Three ratios measure **working capital** efficiency:\n\nDays Sales Outstanding (DSO) = (Accounts Receivable / Revenue) × 365. How many days on average does it take customers to pay? A £100m company with £25m of **receivables** has DSO = 91 days. Lower is better (faster cash collection).\n\nDays Inventory Outstanding (DIO) = (Inventory / **COGS**) × 365. How many days does inventory sit before being sold? A manufacturer with £40m inventory and £200m COGS has DIO = 73 days. Lower is better (less cash tied up).\n\nDays Payable Outstanding (DPO) = (Accounts Payable / COGS) × 365. How long does the company take to pay its suppliers? Higher DPO is generally better for the company (using supplier financing), though pushing DPO too high strains supplier relationships.","bullets":["DSO: collect faster → lower → better cash conversion","DIO: sell faster → lower → less cash tied up in stock","DPO: pay later → higher → suppliers finance the business"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"The Cash Conversion Cycle","body":"CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO\n\nThe Cash Conversion Cycle measures how long cash is tied up in the operating cycle — from paying for inventory to collecting from customers.\n\nA negative CCC is a structural advantage: the company collects from customers before it has to pay suppliers. Amazon Web Services (deferred revenue), Costco (fast inventory turns + extended supplier terms), and most subscription businesses have negative CCC — they are being financed by their customers and suppliers.\n\nA high CCC is a working capital trap: cash is locked in inventory and receivables while **payables** are paid quickly. Retailers with seasonal inventory buildups or manufacturers with long production cycles often face this.\n\nWorking capital as a % of revenue: each percentage point of working capital relative to revenue that a growing company must maintain creates a proportional working capital cash drain as revenue grows. A company with 20% working capital/revenue and 20% revenue growth must fund an additional 4% of revenue in working capital each year.","bullets":["CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO — lower (or negative) is better","Negative CCC: customers and suppliers finance the business (Amazon, Costco model)","Fast growth + high CCC = large working capital cash drain"],"icon":"refresh"},{"heading":"Working Capital Changes on the Cash Flow Statement","body":"On the **cash flow statement** (operating section), working capital changes appear as:\n\n— Increase in receivables: negative cash flow (sold goods, haven't collected yet)\n— Decrease in receivables: positive cash flow (collected old invoices)\n— Increase in inventory: negative cash flow (bought stock, not yet sold)\n— Increase in payables: positive cash flow (used suppliers as financing)\n\nKey analytical move: strip out working capital movements from EBITDA to assess underlying cash quality. A company showing strong EBITDA but significant receivables growth may be funding its P&L with **balance sheet** expansion — unsustainable.\n\nThe \"receivables days\" trap: a company that accelerates revenue by offering extended payment terms (90 days vs. 30 days) boosts short-term P&L but creates a hidden cash drain. When customers eventually pay, DSO normalises — but the revenue pull-forward has been spent.","bullets":["Receivables increase = cash not yet collected = negative cash flow impact","Payables increase = suppliers financing you = positive cash flow impact","Working capital normalisation after extended payment terms = future cash drag"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Sector Benchmarks and Red Flags","body":"Working capital norms vary dramatically by industry. Comparing a company to its sector peers is the key analytical move:\n\nRetailers: DIO is critical — fashion retailers with 90+ DIO risk markdowns; grocery retailers target 15-30 DIO. DPO from large retailers can stretch 60-90 days (power over suppliers).\n\nManufacturers: high DIO is often unavoidable (production cycles); DSO varies by customer type (B2B vs. B2C).\n\nProfessional services: near-zero inventory; DSO is the key metric — consulting firms with DSO above 60 days may be struggling to bill or collect.\n\nRed flag: DSO rising faster than revenue (stretched collection), DIO rising with flat sales (building unsold inventory), DPO falling (losing pricing power with suppliers). All three moving adversely simultaneously is a serious warning of operational deterioration.","icon":"business"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"A company has DSO of 60 days, DIO of 45 days, and DPO of 30 days. What is the cash conversion cycle?","options":["75 days","135 days","45 days","15 days"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO = 60 + 45 − 30 = 75 days. This means the company takes 75 days from when it pays for raw materials to when it collects cash from customers. During those 75 days, it is funding the working capital gap from its own cash or borrowings. Reducing DIO by 10 days (selling inventory faster) or increasing DPO by 10 days (paying suppliers later) would each reduce the CCC by 10 days and release working capital cash."},{"id":"t10-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"A fast-growing software company has a cash conversion cycle of −45 days. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["The company is paying its suppliers extremely late, badly straining its key supplier relationships","Customers pay annual subscriptions upfront (creating deferred revenue) before the company incurs costs to service them","The company has negative net income, which creates a negative CCC","Inventory is being written off, reducing DIO artificially"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A negative CCC means customers pay before the company has to pay its costs — a structural cash advantage. SaaS and subscription businesses often achieve this: annual contracts paid upfront create deferred revenue (liability), while costs (cloud infrastructure, support) are incurred over the year. The company uses customer cash to fund operations. This is why high-quality subscription businesses are capital-efficient despite heavy growth investment. Negative net income does not affect the CCC formula — CCC is about operational cash timing, not profitability."},{"id":"t10-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"A company's EBITDA is £40m. Its cash from operations is only £15m. The difference is entirely explained by a £25m increase in accounts receivable. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The company has invested heavily in acquiring new customers who will pay later; growth outpaces collection","Revenue is growing strongly and all new sales are being collected — a positive sign","The firm extended payment terms to pull revenue forward, but cash hasn't followed — a quality concern","The accounts receivable increase has no impact on cash conversion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When EBITDA is strong but operating cash flow is weak due to a large receivables build, the first question is whether those receivables are genuinely collectible. Extended payment terms boost reported revenue now but defer cash collection — when the revenue was booked, the performance obligation may have been satisfied, but the economic cash has not arrived. If a growing company must extend terms to maintain growth, it may indicate demand is softer than revenue figures suggest. Always ask: why are receivables growing faster than revenue?"},{"id":"t10-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"A retailer's inventory days (DIO) jumps from 45 to 80 days while revenue is flat. What does this signal?","options":["The retailer is managing its inventory far more efficiently, and its stock turnover is clearly improving","The retailer is building inventory ahead of an expected demand surge","Unsold inventory is building up — possible demand weakness, pricing pressure, or obsolescence risk","DIO increases are always associated with revenue growth"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When DIO rises sharply with flat revenue, inventory is accumulating faster than it is being sold. For a retailer, this typically signals one or more of: demand is weaker than expected, the product mix has shifted to slower-moving items, or pricing needs to be cut to clear stock (margin risk). Inventory write-downs often follow — which then hits the income statement. A build from 45 to 80 days is a significant deterioration and would trigger analyst concern. The 2022-23 post-COVID inventory glut at many retailers is a real-world example."}]},"t10-m2-l1":{"id":"t10-m2-l1","title":"DuPont ROE Analysis","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Return on Equity (ROE = Net Income / Shareholders' Equity) is the fundamental measure of equity returns. But a high ROE can come from genuine profitability, efficient asset use, or financial leverage — which are very different business qualities. The DuPont framework decomposes ROE into its component drivers, revealing whether returns are sustainable.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"The Three-Factor DuPont Model","body":"ROE = Net Profit Margin × Asset Turnover × Financial **Leverage**\n\nROE = (**Net Income** / Revenue) × (Revenue / Total Assets) × (Total Assets / Equity)\n\nEach factor tells a distinct story:\n\nNet Profit Margin: how much profit does the company generate per pound of revenue? High-margin businesses (luxury goods, software) can generate strong ROE with moderate turnover. Low-margin businesses (grocery retail, logistics) need high turnover to compensate.\n\nAsset Turnover: how efficiently does the company use its assets to generate revenue? Revenue / Total Assets. A supermarket with 2× asset turnover generates £2 of revenue per £1 of assets.\n\nFinancial Leverage: how much is the company amplifying equity returns through debt? Total Assets / Equity. A company financed 70% by debt has assets/equity of ~3.3×, amplifying both returns and risk.","bullets":["ROE = Margin × Turnover × Leverage — three completely different drivers","High ROE from high leverage is fragile — it disappears in a downturn","High ROE from high margins is most sustainable — moat-protected business"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"The Five-Factor DuPont Extension","body":"The five-factor model further splits the profit margin into its components:\n\nROE = Tax Burden × Interest Burden × **EBIT** Margin × Asset Turnover × Leverage\n\nTax burden = Net Income / Pre-tax Income (affected by tax planning)\nInterest burden = Pre-tax Income / EBIT (shows how much of **operating profit** is consumed by interest)\nEBIT margin = EBIT / Revenue (pure operating profitability)\n\nThis allows analysts to isolate exactly where ROE is coming from or deteriorating. A company with improving EBIT margin but declining ROE may be seeing financial deleveraging (reducing the leverage factor). A company with stable operations but declining ROE may simply be paying more tax or interest.","bullets":["Interest burden = Pre-tax / EBIT — shows the debt cost drag on operating profits","Separating tax from interest reveals whether ROE decline is structural or financial","Five-factor model used in equity research to pinpoint ROE drivers"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"ROE vs ROA: Isolating the Leverage Effect","body":"Return on Assets (ROA) = Net Income / Total Assets measures profitability independent of **capital structure**. It is the underlying asset productivity before leverage amplification.\n\nROE = ROA × (Total Assets / Equity) — the leverage multiplier converts asset returns to equity returns.\n\nWhen comparing companies with different capital structures: a bank might show ROE of 15% using 10× leverage on a 1.5% ROA. A tech company shows 20% ROE with minimal debt on 15% ROA. The tech company's ROE is far more sustainable — the bank's depends entirely on maintaining high leverage.\n\nLeverage can destroy ROE just as easily as it creates it: when assets decline in value (a credit crisis), equity is first to absorb losses. A 10% fall in assets with 10× leverage wipes out all equity.","bullets":["ROA = pure asset productivity before leverage","ROE = ROA × (Assets / Equity) — leverage multiplies return in both directions","High ROE with low ROA signals leverage dependency, not genuine profitability"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Sustainable Growth Rate","body":"The sustainable growth rate (SGR) is the maximum rate a company can grow without external equity financing:\n\nSGR = ROE × (1 − Dividend Payout Ratio) = ROE × Retention Ratio\n\nIf ROE = 15% and the company retains 70% of earnings (30% payout), SGR = 15% × 70% = 10.5%. The company can grow at 10.5% per year purely by reinvesting profits, without diluting shareholders or taking on more debt.\n\nA company that grows faster than its SGR must either raise external equity (**dilution**), take on more debt (leverage), or see ROE improve. Companies that consistently grow faster than their SGR without visible external financing are often either manipulating earnings or taking on hidden leverage.\n\nThis metric also tells investors whether a company's dividend is sustainable at the current growth rate.","icon":"trending-up"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"Company A has ROE = 20%, Net Margin = 10%, Asset Turnover = 1.0×, Leverage = 2.0×. Company B has ROE = 20%, Net Margin = 5%, Asset Turnover = 2.0×, Leverage = 2.0×. Which company has the higher quality ROE?","options":["Company B, because its higher asset turnover clearly shows superior day-to-day operational efficiency","Company A — higher net margins indicate a stronger competitive position and more sustainable returns","Both equally — ROE is identical so there is no quality difference","Impossible to determine without knowing the industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both achieve 20% ROE (10% × 1.0 × 2.0 = 20% and 5% × 2.0 × 2.0 = 20%) but through different paths. Company A's 10% net margin suggests pricing power and a competitive moat — it earns more from each pound of revenue. Company B's 5% margin means it is a lower-quality business competing on volume. High-margin businesses are more resilient in downturns, have more flexibility to invest, and are less vulnerable to cost inflation. A margin-driven ROE is generally considered higher quality than a turnover-driven one at the same leverage."},{"id":"t10-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"A company's ROE has improved from 12% to 18% over 3 years. DuPont analysis shows: Net Margin unchanged at 8%, Asset Turnover unchanged at 1.0×, Leverage up from 1.5× to 2.25×. What is the correct interpretation?","options":["The underlying business has genuinely improved — a rising ROE always reflects better real performance","ROE rose purely on higher leverage, not real improvement — more risk without more profitability","The company has become more efficient — leverage is a sign of confidence","The ROE improvement is immaterial because margins did not change"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Using DuPont: 8% × 1.0 × 1.5 = 12% (before), 8% × 1.0 × 2.25 = 18% (after). The entire ROE improvement came from borrowing more. Operating profitability (margin) and efficiency (turnover) were unchanged. Leverage amplifies returns but also amplifies losses and risk. An analyst would note that the ROE improvement is financial engineering, not business improvement — and would flag that the higher leverage increases vulnerability to an earnings decline or rate rise."},{"id":"t10-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"A company has ROE of 18% and a dividend payout ratio of 40%. What is its sustainable growth rate?","options":["18%","7.2%","10.8%","40%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SGR = ROE × (1 − payout ratio) = 18% × (1 − 0.40) = 18% × 0.60 = 10.8%. The company retains 60% of its earnings and generates 18% ROE on equity — so it can grow at 10.8% per year without external financing. If the company is growing faster than 10.8%, it must either borrow more, issue equity, or cut the dividend. This is a useful sanity check when analysts see companies projecting aggressive growth without visible financing plans."},{"id":"t10-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"Bank A has ROE = 14% and ROA = 0.7%, financed with 20× leverage. Bank B has ROE = 14% and ROA = 7%, financed with 2× leverage. Which bank's ROE is more sustainable?","options":["Bank A, because banks are fundamentally designed to operate safely with very high levels of leverage","Bank B — the same ROE is generated with much stronger underlying asset profitability, making it far more resilient to stress","Equal — both deliver 14% ROE to shareholders","Bank A — regulatory capital requirements make high leverage mandatory for banks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both deliver 14% ROE, but through very different structures. Bank A: 0.7% ROA × 20× leverage = 14%. Bank B: 7% ROA × 2× leverage = 14%. Bank A's ROE is entirely leverage-dependent: a 5% fall in asset values on 20× leverage wipes out all equity. Bank B can absorb a 50% asset loss before equity is impaired. High-ROA businesses with modest leverage are fundamentally more resilient. The 2008 crisis destroyed banks that operated on thin ROA/high-leverage models. Bank B's ROE quality is incomparably superior."}]},"t10-m2-l2":{"id":"t10-m2-l2","title":"ROIC and Economic Profit","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Return on Invested Capital (ROIC) — and its comparison to the cost of capital (WACC) — is the most rigorous measure of whether a business is truly creating or destroying shareholder value. A company that earns ROIC above its WACC creates economic profit; below WACC, it destroys it regardless of what the income statement shows.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"ROIC: The Formula and Its Logic","body":"ROIC = NOPAT / Invested Capital\n\nNOPAT (Net **Operating Profit** After Tax) = EBIT × (1 − Tax Rate). This is the after-tax operating profit available to all capital providers — both debt and equity — stripping out the financing structure.\n\nInvested Capital = Total Equity + Total Debt − Cash (or equivalently: Net **Working Capital** + PP&E + Intangibles). This represents the capital deployed in the business to generate returns.\n\nROIC is superior to ROE for comparing businesses because:\n1. It strips out the effect of financial leverage (ROE is distorted by debt)\n2. It uses operating profit (NOPAT) rather than accounting **net income**\n3. It is calculated on capital actually deployed in operations\n\nBerkshire Hathaway, consulting firms, and premium consumer brands typically generate ROIC of 20-40%. Capital-intensive infrastructure or basic materials companies might generate 5-8%.","bullets":["ROIC = NOPAT / Invested Capital — strips out leverage effects","NOPAT = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) — available to both debt and equity holders","Invested Capital = equity + debt − cash: what is actually deployed in operations"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"ROIC vs WACC: Economic Value Creation","body":"**WACC** (Weighted Average Cost of Capital) is the blended required return of all capital providers — debt and equity weighted by their proportions in the **capital structure**.\n\nThe key relationship:\n\nROIC > WACC: the company earns more on its invested capital than its capital costs. Every pound reinvested creates value above the hurdle — this is genuine value creation. These businesses should reinvest aggressively.\n\nROIC < WACC: the company destroys value on every pound invested. Its returns are below what investors could earn elsewhere at equivalent risk. These businesses should return capital rather than reinvest.\n\nROIC = WACC: the company earns exactly what capital costs — zero economic profit. Steady state, no value creation or destruction.\n\nA company with ROIC − WACC spread of 10% and growing reinvestment will compound **intrinsic value** rapidly. Terminal values in DCF models are heavily sensitive to the assumed long-run ROIC/WACC spread.","bullets":["ROIC > WACC: invest more — every pound creates economic profit","ROIC < WACC: return capital or restructure — reinvestment destroys value","DCF terminal value: sensitive to ROIC/WACC spread at reinvestment rate"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Economic Value Added (EVA)","body":"EVA (Economic Value Added), popularised by Stern Stewart, translates the ROIC/WACC relationship into a pound amount:\n\nEVA = NOPAT − (Invested Capital × WACC)\n\nOr equivalently: EVA = (ROIC − WACC) × Invested Capital\n\nExample: A company has £500m invested capital, ROIC = 12%, WACC = 8%.\nEVA = (12% − 8%) × £500m = 4% × £500m = £20m per year.\n\nThe company is creating £20m of economic profit annually above its cost of capital. Summing discounted future EVA gives an estimate of market value added (MVA) — the premium above invested capital that a business commands.\n\nSome PE firms and corporates use EVA as an internal management metric and compensation driver — aligning management with true economic value creation rather than just accounting profit.","bullets":["EVA = (ROIC − WACC) × Invested Capital — pounds of economic profit created","MVA: present value of future EVA = value premium above book invested capital","EVA-linked compensation: aligns managers with genuine value creation"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"ROIC in Practice: Adjustments and Pitfalls","body":"Common adjustments analysts make to ROIC:\n\nOperating vs. total capital: some analysts exclude **goodwill** from invested capital to see \"**underlying**\" ROIC (pre-acquisition returns). This answers: is the core business good, even if acquisitions destroyed value?\n\nIncluding goodwill: post-goodwill ROIC shows whether management paid the right price. A company with 15% pre-goodwill ROIC that paid 3× book might have post-goodwill ROIC of only 7% — below WACC, a value-destructive acquisition.\n\nCapitalized R&D: for R&D-intensive businesses, some analysts capitalise R&D expenditure and amortise it — adjusting both NOPAT and invested capital to treat R&D as an investment rather than an expense.\n\nROIC trends matter more than levels: a company improving ROIC from 8% to 14% over 5 years tells a better story than one stable at 12%. Improving ROIC signals growing competitive advantage; declining ROIC warns of eroding moat.","icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"A company has EBIT of £60m, a 25% tax rate, and invested capital of £400m. What is its ROIC?","options":["15%","11.25%","10%","25%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"NOPAT = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) = £60m × (1 − 0.25) = £60m × 0.75 = £45m. ROIC = NOPAT / Invested Capital = £45m / £400m = 11.25%. Note: we use EBIT (not net income) because ROIC measures returns before financing — interest is not subtracted. The tax adjustment converts operating profit to an after-tax basis so ROIC and WACC are comparable on the same post-tax basis."},{"id":"t10-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"Company X has ROIC = 20% and WACC = 12%. Company Y has ROIC = 8% and WACC = 12%. Both have £1bn of invested capital. What is the EVA for each?","options":["X: £200m created and Y: £80m created — both genuinely add economic value","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m — X creates economic value; Y destroys it","X: £120m, Y: £96m"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"EVA = (ROIC − WACC) × Invested Capital. Company X: (20% − 12%) × £1bn = 8% × £1bn = +£80m of economic profit. Company Y: (8% − 12%) × £1bn = −4% × £1bn = −£40m of economic loss. Despite Y having positive reported profits (8% ROIC generates positive NOPAT), it is destroying value because its returns fall short of capital costs. Every pound Y reinvests reduces economic value. Y should return capital to shareholders rather than grow."},{"id":"t10-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"A PE firm acquires a business for £300m. The business had pre-acquisition invested capital of £100m and pre-goodwill ROIC of 22%. After the acquisition, including £200m of goodwill, what is the post-goodwill ROIC if NOPAT is £22m?","options":["22%","7.3%","11%","15%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Post-goodwill invested capital = pre-acquisition capital + goodwill = £100m + £200m = £300m. Post-goodwill ROIC = NOPAT / total invested capital = £22m / £300m = 7.3%. The acquisition price was 3× book value. The underlying business is excellent (22% pre-goodwill ROIC) but the PE firm paid such a high premium that the blended return falls to 7.3% — potentially below WACC. This is the \"winner's curse\" in M&A: a great business acquired at too high a price becomes a poor investment."},{"id":"t10-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"A company's ROIC has declined from 18% to 11% over five years while its WACC remains 9%. What is the most important strategic implication?","options":["The company should accelerate capital investment to recover ROIC through scale","ROIC remains above WACC so value is still being created — no action required","A falling ROIC nearing WACC signals eroding advantage — investigate the cause and consider returning capital","The company should reduce its WACC by taking on more debt to restore the ROIC/WACC spread"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"At 11%, ROIC is still above WACC (9%) — value is being created, but less efficiently than before. The trend is more alarming than the current level: declining ROIC typically signals intensifying competition, pricing pressure, or capital misallocation. If the trend continues and ROIC falls below WACC, the company becomes a value destroyer. Management must investigate the root cause — is it a structural threat to margins or a temporary issue? If the former, a capital return strategy (buybacks, dividends) may be better than continued reinvestment at declining returns."}]},"t10-m2-l3":{"id":"t10-m2-l3","title":"Goodwill, Intangibles & Impairment","trackId":"t10","trackTitle":"Financial Statement Analysis","color":"#0F766E","premium":false,"intro":"Goodwill and intangible assets are among the most misunderstood items on corporate balance sheets — and among the most significant sources of value destruction when things go wrong. Understanding acquisition accounting, amortisation rules, and impairment testing is essential for reading the financial statements of any company that has made acquisitions.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"How Goodwill Arises","body":"When a company acquires another for more than the fair value of its identifiable net assets, the excess is recorded as **goodwill**. In a purchase price allocation (PPA), the acquirer must:\n\n1. Identify all tangible assets and liabilities at fair value (not book value).\n2. Identify and value intangible assets separately: customer relationships, technology, brand names, licences — each given a value and **amortisation** period.\n3. Record goodwill as the residual: Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets.\n\nExample: a company buys a software business for £500m. Fair value of tangible assets: £100m (net of liabilities). Identified intangibles: technology (£80m), customer relationships (£70m), brand (£30m) = £180m. Goodwill = £500m − £100m − £180m = £220m.\n\nGoodwill represents: **synergies**, the assembled workforce, brand strength not separately identifiable, and sometimes acquisition premium (overpayment).","bullets":["Goodwill = purchase price minus fair value of net identifiable assets","PPA: acquirer must separately value all identifiable intangibles before recording goodwill","Goodwill embeds synergies AND the risk of acquisition overpayment"],"icon":"add-circle"},{"heading":"Amortisation: GAAP vs IFRS","body":"US GAAP (ASC 350) and IFRS (IAS 36) differ fundamentally on goodwill treatment:\n\nIFRS: goodwill is NOT amortised. Instead, it is subject to an annual impairment test. As long as the business is performing, goodwill stays on the balance sheet indefinitely. This inflates balance sheets of serial acquirers.\n\nUS GAAP: same — no amortisation, impairment-only model post-2001 (prior to 2001, goodwill was amortised over 40 years). Private companies under GAAP can elect to amortise over 10 years.\n\nSeparately identifiable intangibles (technology, customer relationships, trade names): these ARE amortised over their useful lives (5-15 years is typical). This amortisation flows through the **income statement** as an expense — and is a significant cost for acquisitive companies. Analysts often add back \"amortisation of acquired intangibles\" to EBITDA, arguing it is a non-cash accounting charge unrelated to the current business.","bullets":["Goodwill: not amortised (IFRS and US GAAP) — impairment-only model","Identified intangibles: amortised over useful life (5-15 years typical)","Amortisation of acquired intangibles is often stripped out by analysts to reach \"clean\" earnings"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Impairment Testing","body":"Under both IFRS and US GAAP, goodwill is tested for impairment annually (or when indicators arise). Under IFRS (IAS 36), goodwill is allocated to Cash Generating Units (CGUs) — the smallest group of assets generating independent cash flows.\n\nIFRS impairment test: the recoverable amount of the CGU is compared to its carrying value (book value including goodwill). Recoverable amount = the higher of fair value less costs to sell, and value-in-use (**DCF** of expected cash flows). If carrying value > recoverable amount, an impairment loss is recognised.\n\nImpairment is irreversible: once goodwill is written down, it cannot be written back up (unlike other assets).\n\nKey inputs to DCF impairment test: growth rates, discount rates, and terminal values — all subject to management judgement. Companies can delay impairment by using optimistic assumptions. Regulators and auditors scrutinise these.","bullets":["IFRS: goodwill allocated to CGUs, tested at least annually","Impairment = carrying value > recoverable amount (higher of FV or value-in-use)","Impairment is permanent — cannot be reversed once taken under IFRS/GAAP"],"icon":"warning"},{"heading":"Reading Goodwill Impairments as an Analyst","body":"A large goodwill impairment is an admission that management overpaid — or that the acquired business is performing below acquisition-case expectations. How to analyse impairments:\n\nWhen impairment happens: impairments tend to cluster in recessions (falling cash flow projections) and after serial acquisitions (multiple deals priced at peak cycle multiples). 2020 saw record impairment charges across airlines, retail, and energy.\n\nGoodwill as a % of total assets: a company with 60%+ of assets as goodwill is heavily acquisition-funded. Any disappointment in the acquired businesses flows through to large impairment risk. Check goodwill/total assets for serial acquirers.\n\nEarnings impact: an impairment reduces **net income** but does not affect cash. From a cash flow perspective, nothing changes. However, it often signals operational deterioration that WILL affect future cash flows.\n\nManagement credibility: repeated impairments on the same acquisitions raise questions about **due diligence** quality, integration capability, and management's willingness to acknowledge problems early.","bullets":["Impairment = admission of overpayment or underperformance versus acquisition case","Goodwill / total assets: high ratio signals impairment concentration risk","Non-cash charge: P&L is hit but cash flow unaffected — watch underlying business trends"],"icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t10-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"A company acquires a business for £400m. Net identifiable assets at fair value are £120m (including £60m of separately identified intangibles). How much goodwill is recorded?","options":["£400m","£280m","£340m","£60m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Goodwill = Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets = £400m − £120m = £280m. The £120m includes the separately identified intangibles (technology, customer relationships, brand) already allocated to specific line items. Goodwill is the residual — what the acquirer paid above and beyond everything that could be specifically identified and valued. It represents synergies, assembled workforce, and market position. Separately identified intangibles (£60m) will be amortised; the £280m goodwill will not."},{"id":"t10-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"Under IFRS, how is goodwill treated after it is initially recorded?","options":["It is amortised over a fixed 40-year period on a straight-line basis, like other long-life assets","Amortised over its estimated useful life, typically 10-20 years","Not amortised — tested for impairment at least annually and written down only if recoverable amount falls below carrying value","Written off immediately against retained earnings in the year of acquisition"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under IFRS (IAS 36), goodwill is not amortised — it stays on the balance sheet indefinitely at cost unless impaired. Annually, the goodwill is allocated to CGUs and tested: if the recoverable amount (higher of fair value or value-in-use) falls below the carrying value, an impairment charge is taken. The rational is that goodwill from a successful acquisition may retain its value indefinitely (a strong brand or technology can appreciate). The practical problem: this inflates balance sheets of acquisitive companies and delays recognition of overpayment."},{"id":"t10-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"A company takes a £300m goodwill impairment charge. Which of the following correctly describes the impact?","options":["Net income falls by £300m, cash falls by £300m, and total equity also falls by the full £300m","Net income falls by approximately £300m × (1 − tax rate), equity falls by the same amount, but cash is unaffected","Net income is unaffected — impairments bypass the P&L and go directly to equity","Net income falls £300m, equity falls £300m, cash flow from operations falls £300m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Goodwill impairments flow through the income statement: they reduce pre-tax profit. After applying the tax rate (note: goodwill impairments are often not tax-deductible, so the effective tax impact may be zero), net income falls by approximately the full £300m. This reduces retained earnings (equity) by the same amount. However, it is a non-cash charge: no money leaves the company. Operating cash flow is unaffected. This is why analysts add back impairment charges to reach \"clean\" earnings — the impairment tells you about the past (overpayment or underperformance) but doesn't directly affect current cash generation."},{"id":"t10-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"A company has total assets of £2bn, of which £1.3bn is goodwill from 12 acquisitions made over the past 8 years. What risk does this balance sheet structure present?","options":["None at all — goodwill is a genuinely productive asset that continues to generate real returns","High concentration risk: 65% of assets are goodwill, so any deterioration could trigger big write-downs","Tax risk — goodwill cannot be deducted for tax purposes, creating a deferred tax liability","Liquidity risk — goodwill cannot be sold to meet short-term obligations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When goodwill is 65% of total assets, the balance sheet is almost entirely built on acquisition premiums. If the acquired businesses underperform their acquisition case, impairment tests will fail and large write-downs follow — directly hitting reported earnings. Serial acquirers with heavy goodwill balances are particularly vulnerable in recessions when discount rates rise and cash flow projections fall simultaneously (both reduce recoverable amounts). Analysts track goodwill/total assets as a metric of impairment risk concentration, and impairment risk is typically highlighted in equity research when this ratio exceeds 40-50%."}]},"t11-m1-l1":{"id":"t11-m1-l1","title":"Bond Pricing & Yield Measures","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"A bond's price and its yield move in opposite directions — this inverse relationship is the most fundamental concept in fixed income. But \"yield\" can mean several different things depending on how you measure it. Understanding the difference between coupon rate, current yield, yield to maturity, and yield to worst is essential for anyone analysing bonds.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Bond Pricing: The Discounted Cash Flow","body":"A bond's fair price equals the present value of all its future payments — every **coupon** plus the **face value** returned at **maturity** — each discounted at the current market yield.\n\nThe intuition: the bond locks in fixed cash flows. When market rates rise, those fixed flows are worth less in present-value terms, so the price must fall to make the bond competitive with newly issued bonds offering higher yields. When rates fall, the price rises.\n\nExample: £1,000 bond, 4% coupon, 5-year. Market yield 4% → price is £1,000 (at par). Market yield rises to 6% → price falls to ~£916. Market yield falls to 2% → price rises to ~£1,092.","formula":"Price = C/(1+r) + C/(1+r)² + … + (C+F)/(1+r)ⁿ\nC = coupon  ·  F = face value  ·  r = yield  ·  n = periods","bullets":["Price = present value of all coupon + principal cash flows, discounted at market yield","Coupon rate = contractual rate on face value — fixed for the life of the bond","Market yield > coupon: bond trades below par (discount); yield < coupon: above par (premium)"],"icon":"cash"},{"heading":"Yield to Maturity vs Other Yield Measures","body":"Four yield measures, used in different contexts:\n\nCoupon rate: the stated annual coupon as a % of face value. Fixed at issuance. A 5% coupon on £1,000 = £50/year regardless of market price.\n\nCurrent yield: annual coupon / current market price. If the bond trades at £950, current yield = £50 / £950 = 5.26%. Simple but ignores capital gain/loss to maturity.\n\n**Yield to maturity** (YTM): the single discount rate that equates the bond's current price to the present value of all future cash flows. The IRR of buying the bond at today's price and holding to maturity. The most complete single measure of return.\n\nYield to worst (YTW): the lowest yield across all possible call/put dates and maturity. Used for callable bonds — the yield if the issuer calls at the earliest possible date. Standard for high-yield bonds.","bullets":["YTM: the \"complete\" return measure — assumes reinvestment at YTM, held to maturity","Current yield: quick approximation, ignores price gain/loss","YTW: the conservative measure for callable bonds — used in high yield"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Clean vs Dirty Price","body":"When a bond is quoted in the market, it is usually quoted as the \"clean price\" — the price excluding accrued interest. But when you buy the bond, you pay the \"dirty price\" (also called invoice price): clean price + accrued interest.\n\nAccrued interest: since the last coupon date, interest has been accumulating. The seller is entitled to the interest earned during their holding period. The buyer pays for it upfront and then receives the full next coupon.\n\nExample: a bond pays a £50 coupon semi-annually. If you buy it 3 months after the last coupon date, accrued interest = £50 × 90/180 = £25. You pay clean price + £25. At the next coupon date, you receive the full £50 — effectively getting your £25 back plus £25 for your 3 months of holding.\n\nWhy it matters: Bloomberg quotes clean prices; settlement systems use dirty prices. Confusing the two causes mispricings in fixed income analytics.","bullets":["Clean price: quoted in market — excludes accrued interest","Dirty price = clean + accrued interest — what you actually pay","Accrued interest: seller's share of the next coupon for their holding period"],"icon":"receipt"},{"heading":"The Par / Premium / Discount Framework","body":"Three states a bond can be in relative to par (face value):\n\nAt par (price = 100): coupon rate = market yield. The bond is fairly priced relative to current rates.\n\nAt a premium (price > 100): coupon rate > market yield. Investors pay more than face value because the coupon is above-market — they will receive a capital loss at maturity (as the bond pulls to par), offset by the higher coupons.\n\nAt a discount (price < 100): coupon rate < market yield. Investors pay less than face value — they gain a capital profit at maturity (as price pulls to par), but receive below-market coupons.\n\nPull to par: as a bond approaches maturity, its price converges to par regardless of the coupon rate — at maturity, the issuer repays exactly the face value. This price convergence is mechanical and predictable.","icon":"swap-horizontal"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m1-l1-q1","prompt":"A bond with a 5% coupon and face value of £1,000 currently trades at £920. What is the current yield?","options":["5.0%","5.43%","4.6%","6.25%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Current yield = annual coupon / market price = £50 / £920 = 5.43%. The current yield is above the coupon rate (5%) because the bond trades at a discount — you're paying less than face value for the same £50 coupon. Note: the YTM would be even higher than 5.43% because it also captures the capital gain from buying at £920 and receiving £1,000 at maturity."},{"id":"t11-m1-l1-q2","prompt":"Market interest rates rise sharply from 4% to 7%. What happens to the price of existing 4% coupon bonds?","options":["Price rises — existing bonds become more valuable as rates rise","Price is unchanged — the coupon rate is fixed in the bond contract","Price falls — new bonds now offer 7%, making existing 4% bonds less attractive unless they are cheaper","Price falls to zero — the bond is worth nothing if market rates exceed the coupon"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bond prices fall when market yields rise. Existing bonds are fixed at 4% coupon — but new bonds being issued offer 7%. For an investor to want to hold the old bond, its price must fall enough that the effective yield (YTM) equals 7%. The price must drop until the combination of coupon income and capital gain/loss to maturity equals the 7% market rate. This is the core inverse relationship: price and yield move in opposite directions."},{"id":"t11-m1-l1-q3","prompt":"You buy a bond at a \"clean price\" of £98 on a date exactly halfway between coupon payments. The bond pays a semi-annual coupon of £4 (£8 annual). What is the dirty price you actually pay?","options":["£98","£100","£102","£94"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest. Accrued interest halfway through a semi-annual coupon period = £4 × 0.5 = £2. Dirty price = £98 + £2 = £100. At the next coupon date, you receive the full £4 coupon — effectively recovering the £2 of accrued interest you paid, plus £2 of \"new\" income for your half-period of holding. The clean price (£98) is what's quoted; the dirty price (£100) is what settles."},{"id":"t11-m1-l1-q4","prompt":"A bond trades at a premium (above par). As it approaches maturity, what happens to its price, all else equal?","options":["The price stays comfortably above par right up until the final coupon, then drops to par at maturity","The price gradually declines toward par — \"pulling to par\" — as the redemption at face value approaches","The price rises further above par as investors appreciate its high coupon","The price is unchanged until the final year, then falls rapidly to par"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"\"Pull to par\" is the mechanical convergence of bond price to face value as maturity approaches. A premium bond has a coupon rate above market yields, making it valuable — but at maturity, the issuer repays only face value. As time passes, the remaining premium erodes linearly. A bond trading at £105 with 5 years left might trade at £103, £101, and finally £100 at maturity. This gradual premium amortisation is why premium bond current yields overstate total return (the capital loss is not captured in current yield)."}]},"t11-m1-l2":{"id":"t11-m1-l2","title":"Duration & Convexity","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"Duration is the most important risk measure in fixed income — it tells you how much a bond's price will move for a given change in yields. Convexity refines this estimate for large yield moves. Understanding duration and convexity is essential for bond portfolio management, hedging interest rate risk, and fixed income interview questions.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Macaulay Duration","body":"Macaulay **Duration** is the weighted average time (in years) to receive a bond's cash flows, where the weights are the present value of each cash flow as a share of the bond's total price.\n\nA zero-coupon bond has Macaulay Duration = its **maturity** (no interim cash flows — all value comes at the end). A coupon bond has Macaulay Duration < its maturity because coupons pull forward some value.\n\nIntuition: a bond with duration of 6 years behaves like a portfolio with an \"average\" cash flow at year 6. The further in the future the average cash flow, the more sensitive the price is to yield changes.\n\nKey properties: duration increases with maturity; duration decreases with coupon rate (higher coupons bring cash flows forward); duration decreases as yield rises (higher discount rates reduce the relative weight of distant cash flows).","bullets":["Zero-coupon bond: Macaulay Duration = maturity (all cash flows at end)","Higher coupon → shorter duration (cash brought forward)","Longer maturity → longer duration (cash received later)"],"icon":"time"},{"heading":"Modified Duration: The Price Sensitivity Tool","body":"Modified Duration converts Macaulay Duration into a direct price-sensitivity number. A Modified Duration of 7 means: for every 1% (100bps) move in yields, the bond's price moves approximately 7% in the opposite direction. Yields up → price down by 7%. Yields down → price up by 7%.\n\nDV01 (Dollar Value of a Basis Point): the cash P&L from a 1bp yield move. For a £1m position with duration 7: DV01 = 7 × £1m × 0.0001 = £700. Every basis point rates move, the position gains or loses £700. Traders use DV01 to size positions and calculate **hedge** ratios.","formula":"%ΔPrice  ≈  −Modified Duration × Δyield\nModified Duration = Macaulay Duration ÷ (1 + yield/n)","bullets":["Duration of 7 → ~7% price change per 100bp yield move (opposite direction)","DV01 = Modified Duration × Price × 0.0001 — the cash value of 1bp","Used for position sizing, hedging, and portfolio risk management"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Convexity: The Curvature Correction","body":"Duration gives a linear approximation of price changes. But the true price-yield relationship is curved — the bond's price doesn't fall as much as duration predicts when yields rise, and rises more than duration predicts when they fall. This curvature is convexity, and it's always a benefit for standard bonds: you gain more on downward moves than you lose on upward moves of the same size.\n\nFor small moves (50-100bps), the duration approximation is close enough. For large moves — like the 2020 COVID rate shock (300bp cut) or the 2022 hiking cycle — convexity materially changes the outcome.\n\nNegative convexity: callable bonds and MBS work against the investor when yields fall. Homeowners prepay, issuers call — the investor loses the high-coupon bond and must reinvest at lower rates. This caps price upside and makes these instruments less attractive when **volatility** is high.","formula":"%ΔPrice  ≈  −MD × Δyield  +  ½ × Convexity × (Δyield)²","bullets":["Convexity > 0 for standard bonds: beneficial curvature — gain more than you lose","Matters most for large yield moves (200bps+); minor for small moves","Negative convexity (callable bonds, MBS): price upside capped by prepayment/call risk"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"Portfolio Duration and Immunisation","body":"**Portfolio** duration is the market-value-weighted average of the individual bond durations. Example: a £100m portfolio with 60% in 5-year bonds (duration 4.5) and 40% in 10-year bonds (duration 8.5) has portfolio duration = 0.6 × 4.5 + 0.4 × 8.5 = 6.1.\n\nDuration management: portfolio managers target a specific duration vs the benchmark. \"Long duration\" (above benchmark) profits when yields fall; \"short duration\" profits when yields rise.\n\nImmunisation: pension funds and insurers match the duration of their assets to their liabilities. When both sides have equal duration, a parallel **yield curve** shift hits assets and liabilities equally — immunising funded status from rate moves.","formula":"Portfolio Duration = Σ (weight × bond duration)","bullets":["Portfolio duration = value-weighted average of component durations","Long duration = bullish on rates (expects yields to fall)","Immunisation: match asset duration to liability duration — pension fund strategy"],"icon":"shield"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m1-l2-q1","prompt":"A bond has a Modified Duration of 6.5. If yields rise by 50bps, what is the approximate change in the bond's price?","options":["+3.25%","−3.25%","−6.5%","+6.5%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"%ΔPrice ≈ −Modified Duration × Δyield = −6.5 × 0.50% = −3.25%. The negative sign reflects the inverse relationship: yields up → price down. Modified Duration of 6.5 means the bond is approximately 6.5 times as sensitive to yield changes as a 1-year zero-coupon note. For a 50bp rise (a modest but not unusual move), the bond loses 3.25% of its value."},{"id":"t11-m1-l2-q2","prompt":"Which of the following bonds has the HIGHEST duration?","options":["5-year bond with 8% coupon, yielding 5%","10-year zero-coupon bond yielding 5%","7-year bond with 6% coupon, yielding 5%","5-year zero-coupon bond yielding 5%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Duration increases with maturity and decreases with coupon rate. A 10-year zero-coupon bond has the longest duration: its Macaulay Duration equals its maturity (10 years) because all cash flows arrive at the end. The 5-year zero-coupon bond has duration 5. The coupon bonds (which make regular coupon payments, shortening effective duration) have durations less than their stated maturities. The 10-year zero-coupon bond is the most sensitive to interest rate changes of the four options."},{"id":"t11-m1-l2-q3","prompt":"A portfolio manager holds a £50m bond position with Modified Duration of 8. They want to reduce duration to 5 by selling some of the bonds and replacing with 3-month T-bills (duration ≈ 0). How much must they sell (approximately)?","options":["£18.75m","£50m","£25m","£37.5m"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Current portfolio duration: £50m × 8 = 400 duration-years. Target duration: £50m × 5 = 250 duration-years. Need to reduce by 150 duration-years. Selling £X of 8-duration bonds reduces duration-years by 8X. Set 8X = 150: X = £18.75m. After selling £18.75m of bonds (replacing with T-bills at duration 0): remaining £31.25m × 8 = 250 duration-years → 250 / £50m = 5 duration. Correct. This is exactly how portfolio managers actively manage duration exposure."},{"id":"t11-m1-l2-q4","prompt":"Why do mortgage-backed securities (MBS) sometimes exhibit \"negative convexity\" when interest rates fall?","options":["Mortgage-backed securities are issued at a discount, which structurally limits their price appreciation","When rates fall, homeowners refinance and prepay — the investor reinvests at lower rates and upside is capped","MBS have shorter duration than government bonds, so they benefit less from rate falls","Central banks sell MBS when rates fall, creating supply that caps prices"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When rates fall, homeowners rush to refinance — prepaying the mortgages that back the MBS. The MBS investor, who bought expecting years of high-coupon payments, instead receives their principal back early and must reinvest at lower yields. The beneficial price appreciation that should accompany falling rates is offset by prepayment acceleration — capping the price gain. This is negative convexity: unlike standard bonds, MBS prices rise less than duration predicts when yields fall. This prepayment risk makes MBS analysis complex and requires dedicated prepayment models (CPR, PSA)."}]},"t11-m1-l3":{"id":"t11-m1-l3","title":"The Yield Curve","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"The yield curve — plotting government bond yields against maturities from 3 months to 30 years — is one of the most information-dense charts in finance. Its shape reflects expectations about growth, inflation, and monetary policy. Understanding the theories behind it and the trades built around it is fundamental for macro investing and rates desks.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"Reading the Curve","body":"The three main shapes:\n\nNormal (upward-sloping): long-term yields above short-term. The most common state — investors demand a premium for lending long (locking up capital, uncertainty about future). Signals a healthy, growing economy.\n\nFlat: short and long yields are similar. Occurs in late-cycle transitions — the Fed has hiked short rates while long rates have not risen proportionately. Often signals uncertainty about growth outlook.\n\nInverted (downward-sloping): short rates exceed long rates. The bond market is pricing in future rate cuts — typically because it expects a **recession** and lower policy rates ahead. The most watched recession predictor: every US recession since 1970 was preceded by inversion of the 2s10s spread (10-year yield minus 2-year yield).\n\nThe 2s10s spread: the most commonly quoted **yield curve** metric. When positive, the curve is normal. When negative (2yr > 10yr), the curve is inverted.","bullets":["2s10s spread = 10yr yield − 2yr yield: the most-watched curve metric","Negative 2s10s: inverted curve — recession predictor with ~12-18 month lead","Every US recession since 1970 preceded by 2s10s inversion"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Theories of the Yield Curve","body":"Three main theories explain why yields differ across maturities:\n\n1. Pure Expectations Theory: long-term yields purely reflect expected future short-term rates. A 10-year yield of 4% implies the market expects the average 1-year rate over the next 10 years will be 4%. Inversion means the market expects future short rates to be lower — i.e. rate cuts ahead.\n\n2. **Liquidity** Premium Theory: investors demand extra yield for longer maturities (liquidity risk, uncertainty). This adds an upward slope to the curve even when rate expectations are flat — explaining why the curve is normally upward-sloping even without a growth premium.\n\n3. Market Segmentation Theory: different investors have specific **maturity** preferences (pension funds want long bonds to match liabilities; banks prefer short). **Supply and demand** in each segment determines yields independently. This explains why different parts of the curve can move independently.","bullets":["Expectations theory: long rates = average expected future short rates","Liquidity premium: extra yield demanded for uncertainty over longer horizons","Market segmentation: different investors dominate different maturities"],"icon":"library"},{"heading":"Curve Trades: Steepeners and Flatteners","body":"Fixed income traders take positions on the shape of the yield curve, not just the direction of rates:\n\nSteepener: a trade that profits when the yield curve steepens (long-short spread widens). Bull steepener: short rates fall faster than long rates (rate cuts). Bear steepener: long rates rise faster than short rates (inflation fears, fiscal concerns).\n\nFlattener: profits when the spread narrows. Bull flattener: long rates fall as growth slows (flight to safety). Bear flattener: short rates rise faster (Fed hiking — the most common late-cycle curve move).\n\nButterfly: involves three maturity points (2yr, 10yr, 30yr) — typically long the belly (10yr) and short the wings (2yr and 30yr) or vice versa. Profits from the relative movement of medium-term yields versus the short and long ends.\n\nRoll-down return: in a normal upward-sloping curve, a 10-year bond \"rolls down\" to become a 9-year bond after one year — benefiting from the lower yield (higher price) without any change in the overall curve.","bullets":["Steepener: long spreads (30yr/2yr) — profit if curve steepens","Flattener: short spreads — profit if curve flattens (typical late-cycle Fed hike trade)","Roll-down: in normal curve, bonds gain as they move down the slope over time"],"icon":"swap-vertical"},{"heading":"What Moves Different Parts of the Curve","body":"The short end (0-2 years): almost entirely controlled by **central bank** policy rate expectations. When the Fed signals rate hikes, 2-year yields jump immediately. The front end is forward-looking — it prices in what the market expects the Fed funds rate to be over the next 1-2 years.\n\nThe belly (5-7 years): transitional — a mix of policy expectations and macro growth/inflation outlook. Heavily influenced by the \"neutral rate\" concept — where the policy rate settles in the long run.\n\nThe long end (10-30 years): driven by long-run **inflation expectations**, fiscal concerns (bond supply), and global **capital flows**. The \"term premium\" — extra yield for holding duration risk — lives here. When governments run large deficits, increased Treasury supply can push up 10-30yr yields even when the Fed holds rates flat.\n\nKey distinction from front end: the Fed directly controls only overnight rates. Long-end yields are market-determined — sometimes working against the Fed (tightening long-term financial conditions despite rate cuts, as in 2024).","bullets":["Short end: central bank policy expectations — almost mechanical","Long end: inflation expectations + term premium + fiscal supply dynamics","Fed controls overnight rates only — long end is the market's independent judgment"],"icon":"pulse"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m1-l3-q1","prompt":"The US 2-year yield rises to 5.3% while the 10-year stays at 4.1%. What is the 2s10s spread, and what does it signal?","options":["+1.2% (120bps) — a normal, healthy upward-sloping curve","−1.2% (−120bps) — an inverted curve, historically a reliable recession predictor","+9.4% — the sum of both yields, signalling strong growth","Exactly 0% — the yield curve is considered flat whenever both of the yields are above 4%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"2s10s spread = 10yr − 2yr = 4.1% − 5.3% = −1.2% (−120bps). A negative spread means the curve is inverted — short rates are above long rates. The bond market is pricing in future rate cuts (expected recession → Fed eases). This specific inversion (2yr > 10yr) preceded every US recession since 1970, with a lead time of roughly 12-18 months. The depth of inversion also matters — −120bps is a significantly deep inversion, historically associated with more severe recessions."},{"id":"t11-m1-l3-q2","prompt":"According to the Pure Expectations Theory, if the current 1-year rate is 4% and the expected 1-year rate next year is 6%, what is the fair 2-year rate today?","options":["6%","4%","5%","10%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Pure Expectations Theory, the 2-year rate = geometric average of expected future 1-year rates: (1 + r₂)² = (1.04) × (1.06), so r₂ = √(1.04 × 1.06) − 1 = √1.1024 − 1 ≈ 5.0%. Approximately: (4% + 6%) / 2 = 5%. Intuitively, investing for 2 years should give the same return as investing for 1 year at 4% and rolling over into another year at 6% — otherwise arbitrage would push rates into alignment."},{"id":"t11-m1-l3-q3","prompt":"A macro trader believes the Federal Reserve will begin cutting rates aggressively over the next 12 months. Which yield curve trade best expresses this view?","options":["A bear flattener trade: go short the 30-year bonds while going long the 2-year bonds","Bull steepener: long 2-year bonds, short 30-year bonds","Bull steepener: long 2-year bonds, which rally as rates are cut, and short or neutral the 30-year","Curve flattener: short 2-year bonds, long 30-year bonds"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When the Fed cuts rates, short-end yields fall fastest and furthest — 2-year yields are most directly tied to near-term policy rate expectations. A bull steepener (rates fall, curve steepens) is the classic Fed-cutting trade: long the front end (2yr bonds rally significantly), neutral or short the long end (30yr is driven more by inflation and term premium, which may not fall as much or may rise if rate cuts reflate the economy). This is called a \"bull steepener\" because rates are falling (bond bull market) and the curve is steepening (front end rallying more than the long end)."},{"id":"t11-m1-l3-q4","prompt":"What is \"roll-down return\" in the context of fixed income?","options":["The return earned from rolling maturing bonds into brand-new, longer-dated bonds each period","The gain from a bond rolling to shorter maturities down an upward-sloping curve, into lower yields","The return generated by rotating from short-duration to long-duration bonds","The loss incurred when a bond's coupon is below the risk-free rate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Roll-down return exploits the normal upward-sloping yield curve. A 10-year bond bought at 4.5% yield \"rolls\" to become a 9-year bond after one year. If the curve is unchanged, it is now priced at the 9-year yield (say 4.3%). The fall in yield means the price rises — generating a return beyond the coupon. In a steep, stable curve, roll-down can add 0.5-1.0% annually to total return. Active bond managers target high roll-down points on the curve (where the slope is steepest) to maximise this effect."}]},"t11-m2-l1":{"id":"t11-m2-l1","title":"Inflation-Linked Bonds","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"Inflation-linked bonds — TIPS (Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities) in the US and index-linked gilts in the UK — protect investors against inflation by linking their principal and coupons to a price index. The real yield, nominal yield, and breakeven inflation rate form a three-way relationship that is central to macro fixed income analysis.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"How Inflation-Linked Bonds Work","body":"A standard (nominal) bond pays fixed coupon and principal — **inflation** erodes their real value. An inflation-linked bond adjusts both:\n\nPrincipal adjustment: the **face value** is multiplied by the inflation index ratio (CPI today / CPI at issuance). If CPI has risen 15% since issuance, a £1,000 bond's adjusted principal = £1,150.\n\nCoupon: paid on the adjusted (inflation-uplifted) principal. A 1.5% real coupon on a £1,150 adjusted principal = £17.25, vs £15 at issuance.\n\nAt maturity: the investor receives the inflation-adjusted principal (or the original face value, whichever is higher — **deflation** floor in most structures).\n\nReal vs nominal return: the linker investor earns the real coupon (fixed) plus inflation compensation on the principal. The nominal bond investor earns the nominal coupon but bears inflation risk.","bullets":["Principal uplifted by CPI index: face value grows with inflation","Coupon paid on uplifted principal: real income also grows with inflation","Deflation floor: most TIPS pay at least the original face value at maturity"],"icon":"shield"},{"heading":"Real Yields and the Fisher Equation","body":"The Fisher equation describes the relationship between nominal **interest rates**, real rates, and inflation. The real yield on a TIPS is directly observable — it's the yield quoted in the market, already stripped of inflation compensation. You don't need to estimate inflation separately.\n\nPositive real yields: the bond genuinely beats inflation. Through most of post-WWII history, savers earned a positive real return.\n\nNegative real yields (as in 2020–21, when the Fed held rates near zero while inflation rose): holding bonds guarantees a loss in purchasing-power terms. Investors rationally rotate into equities, property, and commodities that can deliver positive real returns — the \"TINA\" dynamic (There Is No Alternative). When real yields turned positive in 2022, those assets corrected sharply.","formula":"Nominal yield ≈ Real yield + Expected inflation\nReal yield = Nominal yield − Expected inflation","bullets":["TIPS real yield: directly observable — no need to estimate inflation","Negative real yields: bonds lose purchasing power → investors shift to real assets","2020–21: deeply negative TIPS real yields (−1.5% to −2%) drove equity and property surge"],"icon":"calculator"},{"heading":"Breakeven Inflation Rate","body":"The breakeven inflation rate is the implied market expectation of future inflation, derived from the yield difference between nominal and inflation-linked bonds of the same maturity.\n\nExample: 10-year Treasury yields 4.5%; 10-year TIPS real yield is 2.0%. Breakeven = 2.5% — the market prices in 2.5% average annual inflation over the next 10 years. If actual inflation runs above 2.5%, TIPS win. Below 2.5%, nominal Treasuries win.\n\nThe Fed watches the 5yr/5yr forward breakeven (inflation expected 5–10 years from now) as a measure of whether expectations are \"anchored.\" When it spikes above 3%, the market is doubting **central bank** **credibility**.","formula":"Breakeven inflation = Nominal yield − TIPS real yield","bullets":["Breakeven = nominal yield − TIPS yield — the market's inflation forecast","TIPS outperform if actual inflation > breakeven; nominals outperform if below","5yr5yr forward breakeven: Fed's preferred measure of long-run inflation expectations"],"icon":"git-compare"},{"heading":"When to Prefer TIPS vs Nominals","body":"The decision framework for inflation-linked vs **nominal** bonds:\n\nBuy TIPS (inflation protection) when:\n— Inflation is likely to exceed market expectations (breakeven)\n— Real yields are historically low or negative (attractive entry for institutional buyers with real liability)\n— Central bank credibility is in question (risk of unanchored inflation)\n— Commodity prices are rising sharply (supply-driven inflation)\n\nBuy nominals when:\n— Inflation is likely to be below breakeven (disinflation or deflation)\n— Economy is slowing significantly (flight to safety pushes nominal yields down)\n— Real yields are historically high (nominals offer attractive absolute return)\n\nStrategic allocations: pension funds and insurance companies with real liabilities (inflation-linked pensions) hold linkers to match their liability profile — regardless of the inflation outlook.","icon":"analytics"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m2-l1-q1","prompt":"A TIPS bond is issued with a £1,000 face value and 1% real coupon. After 2 years, the CPI index has risen by 10%. What is the adjusted principal and annual coupon payment?","options":["Principal: £1,000; Coupon: £10","Principal: £1,100; Coupon: £11","Principal: £1,100; Coupon: £10","Principal: £1,000; Coupon: £11"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Adjusted principal = £1,000 × (1 + 10% CPI uplift) = £1,100. Annual coupon = 1% real coupon rate × £1,100 adjusted principal = £11. Both the principal AND the coupon have grown with inflation. If the investor had held a nominal bond with 1% coupon instead, they would receive only £10/year and £1,000 at maturity — the inflation protection from TIPS has delivered £100 of real principal preservation and £1 of additional annual income."},{"id":"t11-m2-l1-q2","prompt":"The 10-year US Treasury yields 4.8% and the 10-year TIPS real yield is 2.1%. What is the breakeven inflation rate, and what does it mean?","options":["6.9% — the sum of both yields represents total expected return","2.7% — the market expects average inflation of 2.7% over the next 10 years","2.1% — the real yield equals the inflation expectation","4.8% — nominal yield equals expected total return"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Breakeven inflation = nominal yield − TIPS real yield = 4.8% − 2.1% = 2.7%. This means the market prices in 2.7% average CPI inflation annually over the next decade. If actual inflation over that period averages more than 2.7%, TIPS investors outperform. If it averages less, nominal Treasury investors outperform. The Fed's 2% target is well below this breakeven — at 2.7%, the market is implying inflation remains somewhat above target for an extended period."},{"id":"t11-m2-l1-q3","prompt":"TIPS real yields turn deeply negative (−1.5%). What is the likely impact on real asset prices (equities, property, gold)?","options":["Real assets fall in value, because deeply negative real yields signal underlying economic weakness","Real assets rise — when bonds pay negative real returns, investors shift into real assets for positive ones","Real assets are unaffected — only nominal yields affect asset valuations","Real assets fall — negative yields mean the economy is deflating"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When real yields are negative, bonds guarantee a loss in purchasing power terms. Investors rationally seek alternatives that offer positive real returns: equities (especially those with pricing power), real estate, commodities, and gold. The 2020-2021 period of deeply negative TIPS real yields (−1% to −2%) coincided with surging equity valuations, record property prices, and gold touching all-time highs — exactly this \"TINA\" (There Is No Alternative) dynamic. When the Fed began hiking rates in 2022, real yields turned positive and these real assets corrected sharply."},{"id":"t11-m2-l1-q4","prompt":"A pension fund has liabilities that are linked to inflation (index-linked pension payments). Which bond holding best matches this liability profile?","options":["Short-dated nominal Treasury bills, since they carry the very lowest credit and default risk of all","Long-duration nominal government bonds — high duration matches long liabilities","Long-duration inflation-linked bonds — both duration and inflation linkage match index-linked pension liabilities","Corporate bonds — higher yield offsets the inflation mismatch"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An index-linked pension liability has two dimensions: it is long-dated (payments stretch decades into the future) and inflation-linked (benefits rise with CPI). To immunise this liability, the fund needs assets with matching characteristics: long duration (to match the interest rate sensitivity of the long-dated liability) AND inflation linkage (to offset the CPI exposure). Long-duration linkers (index-linked gilts, long TIPS) are the only instrument that directly addresses both. Nominal bonds leave the fund exposed to inflation risk — if inflation exceeds expectations, liability grows while the asset's real value erodes."}]},"t11-m2-l2":{"id":"t11-m2-l2","title":"Repo and Bond Financing","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"The repo (repurchase agreement) market is the plumbing of the global financial system. It allows banks, hedge funds, and dealers to fund bond positions overnight or short-term at near-risk-free rates. Understanding repo is essential for understanding how fixed income markets actually function — and what happens when that plumbing seizes.","xp":20,"sections":[{"heading":"How Repo Works","body":"A repo (repurchase agreement) is an agreement to sell a security and simultaneously agree to buy it back at a higher price on a future date. From the seller's perspective, it is secured borrowing — they raise cash using the security as collateral.\n\nExample: a **hedge fund** holds £100m of UK gilts. It \"repos\" them overnight: sells the gilts to a **counterparty** for £99.9m cash, agreeing to repurchase them tomorrow for £99.9m + £30,000 interest (at a 3% repo rate: £99.9m × 3% / 365 = ~£8,200/day, but illustratively).\n\nThe interest rate on a repo is the \"repo rate.\" For the lender of cash (the counterparty), a repo is a \"reverse repo\" — lending cash secured against the **gilt**.\n\nHaircut: the difference between the market value of collateral and the cash lent. A 1% haircut on £100m gilts: only £99m cash lent. Haircuts protect the cash lender if the collateral falls in value.","bullets":["Repo: sell security + promise to buy back = secured cash borrowing","Repo rate: interest on the cash borrowed (typically close to risk-free rate for government bond collateral)","Haircut: protection for cash lender — market value of collateral > cash lent"],"icon":"swap-horizontal"},{"heading":"GC vs Special Repo","body":"Two types of repo based on the collateral:\n\nGeneral collateral (GC) repo: the borrower delivers any eligible security (any gilt, any Treasury). GC repo rates are close to the **policy rate** — the focus is cash, not the specific security.\n\nSpecific (Special) repo: the lender specifically needs a particular security — typically because they are short that bond and need to borrow it to deliver on a short sale. Demand for a specific bond as collateral makes its repo rate fall below GC. The \"specialness\" of a bond reflects how in-demand it is for short sellers or as preferred collateral.\n\nWhen a bond \"goes special\": its repo rate falls significantly below GC. Owning an on-the-run (newly issued, most liquid) Treasury that is \"on special\" generates a financing benefit — you can repo it out at a below-market rate while reinvesting the cash at GC rate, earning the spread.","bullets":["GC repo: any eligible collateral — rates close to policy rate","Special repo: specific bond in demand — repo rate falls below GC","\"Specialness\": owning a special bond generates financing income from the GC-special spread"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Leverage Through Repo","body":"Repo is the primary mechanism through which fixed income investors lever up. With a 2% haircut, an investor can lever a bond position 50:1 by repeatedly repoeing the same collateral:\n\nBuy £1m gilts → repo for £980k → buy more gilts → repo again → ... continuing until haircut is the binding constraint.\n\nIn practice, **hedge** funds and broker-dealers use repo to maintain large bond positions with relatively small equity capital. **Leverage** ratios of 10-20× are common in fixed income relative value strategies.\n\nRisk: repo is typically overnight or short-term. If markets are stressed and counterparties refuse to roll (renew) the repo, the fund must sell bonds to repay cash — a forced deleveraging that amplifies market moves. The 2019 repo market crisis (when overnight US repo rates spiked to 10%) and the 2020 COVID Treasury sell-off illustrated what happens when repo markets seize.","bullets":["Repo enables leverage: borrow against securities, buy more, repeat","Funding risk: repo must be rolled daily — counterparties can refuse in stress","2019 repo spike: overnight rates hit 10% as bank reserves fell below minimum comfort level"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"The Repo Market and Financial Stability","body":"The repo market is $4-5 trillion in the US alone — larger than the federal funds market. When it malfunctions, financial conditions tighten immediately across the entire system.\n\n2008 GFC: Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers were heavily repo-funded. When counterparties refused to roll their repos (worried about collateral quality and counterparty risk), these firms were forced to sell assets at fire-sale prices — accelerating their collapse.\n\n\"Run on repo\" (Gary Gorton): the GFC was essentially a bank run on the shadow banking system's repo funding. Just as retail depositors can withdraw bank deposits, repo counterparties can refuse to roll overnight — creating a **liquidity** crisis even for solvent institutions.\n\nFed repo facility: the Fed stands ready to lend cash against high-quality collateral via its repo facility (SOFR rate), providing a backstop to prevent repo market seizing. Post-2019, the Fed dramatically expanded its standing repo facility to prevent a repeat of the overnight rate spike.","bullets":["Repo market: $4-5tn in US — critical plumbing for financial system","GFC: \"run on repo\" forced fire sales and accelerated Lehman/Bear Stearns collapse","Fed repo facility: backstop to prevent overnight rate spikes becoming systemic"],"icon":"warning"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m2-l2-q1","prompt":"A hedge fund repos £50m of gilts overnight at a 2% repo rate with a 1% haircut. How much cash does it receive, and what does it pay back tomorrow?","options":["Receives £50m; repays £50m + £27,397 interest","Receives £49.5m; repays £49.5m + £2,712 interest","Receives £49m; repays £49m + £2,685 interest","Receives £50m; repays £50.5m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"1% haircut on £50m: cash received = £50m × (1 − 0.01) = £49.5m. Overnight interest at 2%: £49.5m × 2% / 365 = £2,712. Total repayment: £49.5m + £2,712 = £49,502,712. The haircut protects the cash lender: if the gilts fall from £50m to £49.5m overnight, the cash lender is fully protected. The fund pays back the £49.5m cash plus one day of interest to get its gilts back."},{"id":"t11-m2-l2-q2","prompt":"A specific gilt is \"on special\" in the repo market with a repo rate of 0.5%, while GC repo is 3.5%. What is the annual \"specialness benefit\" for a holder of £100m of this gilt?","options":["£3.5m — the full GC repo rate is earned","£3m — the holder earns the 3% spread between GC and special rates","£0.5m — only the special repo rate is earned","No benefit — specialness only affects the borrower"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Specialness benefit = (GC rate − special repo rate) × position = (3.5% − 0.5%) × £100m = 3% × £100m = £3m/year. The holder repos out the on-special gilt at only 0.5% while reinvesting the cash at GC (3.5%), earning the 3% spread. This is pure financing income from holding a bond the market desperately needs. On-the-run (recently issued) Treasuries and gilts often go special around auction dates. Specialness is one reason the on-the-run bond trades at a lower yield (and higher price) than adjacent off-the-run bonds."},{"id":"t11-m2-l2-q3","prompt":"In September 2008, Lehman Brothers' repo counterparties refused to roll its overnight repo funding. What was the immediate consequence?","options":["Lehman was forced to raise fresh equity capital in a hurry to replace the repo funding it had lost","Lehman was forced to sell assets rapidly to repay the repo cash, creating fire-sale prices and accelerating its collapse","The Federal Reserve stepped in and provided repo funding directly to Lehman","Lehman activated contingency credit lines from commercial banks to replace repo funding"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When repo counterparties refuse to roll (renew) short-term funding, the borrower must immediately repay the cash — which requires selling the underlying securities. Lehman was holding large positions in illiquid mortgage-related securities funded through short-term repo. As counterparties refused to roll, Lehman had to sell these assets at deeply distressed prices, crystallising losses and accelerating its insolvency. This \"run on repo\" dynamic — analogous to a bank run — is exactly what Gary Gorton described as the mechanism of the 2008 crisis. Fire-sale prices then spread losses across the system as others marked similar assets down."},{"id":"t11-m2-l2-q4","prompt":"A fixed income hedge fund buys £10m of gilts and repos them with a 2% haircut. It uses the proceeds to buy more gilts and repos them again, repeating the cycle 3 times. Approximately how much gilt exposure does it control?","options":["£10m","£20.4m","£28.8m","£50m"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Each repo cycle: the fund keeps 2% of equity in each position. Round 1: £10m gilts, repo for £9.8m (98% of £10m). Round 2: £9.8m gilts, repo for £9.604m. Round 3: £9.604m gilts. Total gilts owned ≈ £10m + £9.8m + £9.604m ≈ £29.4m (roughly £28.8m after the third cycle). Using a 2% haircut, maximum theoretical leverage is 1/2% = 50×. In practice, risk limits, counterparty constraints, and capital charges limit this — but the principle shows how repo enables significant leverage from a small equity base."}]},"t11-m2-l3":{"id":"t11-m2-l3","title":"Fixed Income Portfolio Management","trackId":"t11","trackTitle":"Fixed Income & Rates","color":"#1D4ED8","premium":false,"intro":"Managing a fixed income portfolio requires decisions across duration, curve positioning, credit allocation, and sector rotation. Whether managing against a benchmark or running absolute return, the tools — tracking error, information ratio, factor decomposition — apply across styles. This lesson covers how professional bond managers think about and construct portfolios.","xp":25,"sections":[{"heading":"Benchmarks and Tracking Error","body":"Most institutional bond mandates are managed against a **benchmark** — typically a broad market index (Bloomberg Global Aggregate, Bloomberg US Agg, FTSE Actuaries Gilts). The benchmark defines:\n\nIndex composition: what bonds (and what weights) form the reference **portfolio**. Bonds enter when issued and exit when they mature — the index is rules-based.\n\n**Tracking error**: the standard deviation of the portfolio's returns relative to the benchmark. A tracking error of 1% means the portfolio tends to deviate ~1% from the benchmark in any given year. Low tracking error = closely hugging the index; high tracking error = active positioning.\n\nActive risk budget: the CIO or investment committee sets maximum tracking error. A typical core bond mandate allows 50-100bps of tracking error. Multi-sector or unconstrained mandates allow much more (300-500bps).\n\nInformation ratio = active return / tracking error. A manager generating +0.5% excess return with 1% tracking error has an IR of 0.5 — broadly considered good for fixed income.","bullets":["Tracking error: std dev of portfolio return vs benchmark — budget for active risk","Information ratio = excess return / tracking error — quality of active management","Index rebalancing: bond indices change monthly as new bonds are issued and old ones mature"],"icon":"analytics"},{"heading":"Active Fixed Income Strategies","body":"Portfolio managers take active positions along several dimensions:\n\nDuration positioning: the most impactful single decision. Long duration (above benchmark) profits from yield falls; short duration profits from yield rises. Duration bets account for ~80% of active fixed income return variance.\n\nCurve positioning: a steepener (long short end, short long end) profits from curve steepening without requiring a directional call on rates. Butterfly positions exploit the belly vs wings relationship.\n\nSector allocation: overweighting corporate bonds (for spread income) vs. government bonds — the most common credit risk trade. In a credit **bull market**, **corporate bond** overweights outperform; in recessions, they underperform.\n\nCountry allocation: in a global mandate, overweighting higher-yielding sovereign markets (Italy, Spain) vs core (Germany, US). Carries sovereign and currency risk.\n\nSecurity selection: choosing specific bonds within a sector — typically the smallest source of active return but still meaningful in credit.","bullets":["Duration: the biggest single driver of active fixed income P&L","Curve: express relative views without full directional exposure","Sector allocation (credit vs govts): the credit risk premium trade"],"icon":"trending-up"},{"heading":"Fixed Income Factor Investing","body":"Factor-based fixed income applies the logic of equity factor investing to bonds. The main fixed income risk premia (factors):\n\nTerm premium: the extra return for holding long duration vs. short — rewarded over long holding periods for bearing reinvestment and inflation uncertainty.\n\nCredit premium: extra return from holding corporate bonds over government bonds. Compensation for **default risk** and liquidity risk. Historical premium: ~0.5-1% p.a. for investment-grade (IG) bonds, 2-3% for high-yield (HY) bonds.\n\nLiquidity premium: less liquid bonds (off-the-run Treasuries, smaller corporate issues) tend to trade at wider spreads than equivalent but more liquid bonds. Patient capital can harvest this.\n\nCarry: the systematic strategy of owning higher-yielding bonds (longer duration or lower quality) and funding with lower-yielding short bonds. In fixed income, carry is a powerful **risk premium** but suffers sharp drawdowns in crises.\n\nValue: buying bonds that are cheap relative to fair value (based on credit models, **yield curve** models) and selling those that are expensive.","bullets":["Term premium: duration earns a risk premium over time — the reward for bearing rate risk","Credit premium: corporate bonds earn above government bonds for bearing default risk","Carry: higher yield minus funding cost — rewarded long-run but subject to crash risk"],"icon":"git-network"},{"heading":"Liability-Driven Investing (LDI)","body":"For pension funds and insurance companies, the purpose of fixed income is not to maximise return but to match liabilities. Liability-Driven Investing (LDI) matches the interest rate sensitivity (duration) and inflation linkage of assets to liabilities:\n\nA pension fund with £1bn of liabilities (duration 15, 40% inflation-linked) needs assets with matching characteristics: duration 15 bonds (predominantly long-dated gilts and linkers) weighted to mirror the liability profile.\n\nHedge ratio: the proportion of interest rate and inflation risk that is hedged. A 100% **hedge ratio** perfectly matches assets and liabilities — funded status is immune to rate and inflation moves. Below 100%, the fund retains some sensitivity.\n\nDerivatives overlay: pension funds often use interest rate swaps and inflation swaps to hedge duration and inflation without buying physical bonds — allowing the growth assets (equities, alternatives) to remain in the portfolio for return-seeking.\n\nLDI crisis (UK, 2022): UK pension funds using leveraged LDI strategies faced margin calls when **gilt** yields spiked 150bps in 3 days after the Truss mini-budget. Forced gilt selling amplified the yield spike — the Bank of England intervened to stabilise the market.","bullets":["LDI: match asset duration and inflation sensitivity to pension/insurance liabilities","Hedge ratio: 100% = fully immunised; below 100% retains some rate/inflation risk","2022 UK LDI crisis: leveraged gilt positions unwound rapidly, requiring BoE intervention"],"icon":"shield"}],"questions":[{"id":"t11-m2-l3-q1","prompt":"A bond fund generates +0.8% excess return versus its benchmark with a tracking error of 1.6%. What is the information ratio, and how should it be interpreted?","options":["IR = 0.5 — a good result; the manager generates positive active return per unit of active risk","IR = 2.0 — exceptional; the manager takes too little risk for the alpha generated","IR = 0.8 — active return alone determines manager quality","IR = 1.6 — tracking error is the correct measure of manager skill"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information ratio = active return / tracking error = 0.8% / 1.6% = 0.5. In fixed income, an IR above 0.5 is considered good; above 0.75 is excellent. The IR tells you how much active return is generated per unit of active risk taken. A high IR means the manager's active positions are generating consistent excess returns relative to the risk they take. An IR of 0.5 suggests a skilled manager — but this needs to be evaluated over multiple years to distinguish skill from luck."},{"id":"t11-m2-l3-q2","prompt":"A portfolio manager expects the economy to slow and interest rates to fall. Which positioning would be most consistent with this view?","options":["Run a short duration below the benchmark while going meaningfully overweight on credit spreads","Long duration (above benchmark) and underweight credit — adding rate-sensitive positions while reducing spread risk","Neutral duration and overweight high yield bonds","Short duration and overweight inflation-linked bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An economic slowdown typically means: (1) central bank cuts rates → government bond yields fall → long-duration bonds gain; (2) credit spreads widen as default risk rises → corporate bonds underperform. The correct positioning is long duration (benefit from yield fall) and underweight credit (avoid spread widening). High-yield bonds would be particularly exposed in a slowdown. This is the classic defensive fixed income trade: move to duration (government bonds) from credit when growth deteriorates."},{"id":"t11-m2-l3-q3","prompt":"What happened to UK pension funds using leveraged LDI strategies when gilt yields spiked 150bps in September 2022?","options":["They actually benefited from the move, because LDI strategies tend to profit whenever yields rise","They were unaffected — liabilities fell proportionally as yields rose, immunising the fund","They faced margin calls on gilt and swap positions, forcing rapid gilt sales — needing Bank of England action","They activated their hedge ratios and were fully protected from the move"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Leveraged LDI worked like this: pension funds held long-dated gilts and inflation swaps to hedge liabilities, often financed through repo (leverage). When gilt yields spiked 150bps in days after the September 2022 mini-budget, the value of their gilt positions collapsed and the margin calls on their swap positions surged. To meet margin calls, they had to sell gilts — but in a falling market with many sellers simultaneously. The fire-sale dynamic pushed yields even higher, triggering more margin calls. The Bank of England launched a temporary gilt-buying programme on 28 September to break the feedback loop — one of the most dramatic UK financial market interventions in decades."},{"id":"t11-m2-l3-q4","prompt":"The \"credit premium\" in fixed income refers to:","options":["The extra yield that government bonds offer over cash — the reward investors get for taking duration risk","The extra return from corporate bonds over equal-maturity government bonds — pay for default and liquidity risk","The yield advantage of inflation-linked bonds over nominal bonds","The financing benefit from holding on-the-run Treasury bonds that go special in repo"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The credit premium (also called credit risk premium or credit risk premia) is the long-run excess return of corporate bonds over government bonds of the same maturity. It compensates investors for: (1) default risk (the probability the issuer fails to pay); (2) liquidity risk (corporate bonds are harder to sell at short notice); and (3) uncertainty about credit quality over time. Historically, investment-grade credit has earned 0.5-1% above Treasuries and high yield has earned 2-3% above — but these returns come with meaningful drawdown risk in recessions when spreads widen sharply."}]}};
